const DAYS = {"1":{"day":1,"week":"01","themeRu":"The one exact word","themeEn":"Сно́ва вме́сте: то́чное сло́во","intro":"Last month you learned to choose the <b>appropriate</b> word — the one that fits the room. This month asks for something finer. Picture ten words that all fit the same room: all neutral, all correct, all meaning roughly «to walk» or «a mistake». A native still lands on <b>one</b> — the single word that carries the exact shade they feel. That last inch — «почти́ то сло́во» versus the word itself — is the accent of C2. Эмма is a literary translator now, and her whole day is that hunt. Сно́ва вме́сте — and this time we chase the one exact word.","introAudio":"Last month you learned to choose the appropriate word — the one that fits the room. This month asks for something finer. Picture ten words that all fit the same room: all neutral, all correct, all meaning roughly 'to walk' or 'a mistake'. A native still lands on one — the single word that carries the exact shade they feel. That last inch — 'pochti to slovo', almost the word, versus the word itself — is the accent of C2. Emma is a literary translator now, and her whole day is that hunt. Snova vmeste, together again — and this time we chase the one exact word.","goals":["Feel the C2 leap: from the <b>appropriate</b> word (register — last month) to the <b>one exact</b> word (its shade of meaning)","Hear apart near-synonyms that share a register but differ by a shade: <b>идти́ · шага́ть · плести́сь · семени́ть · брести́</b>","Pick the exact noun from a family of «mistake»: <b>оши́бка · про́мах · огре́х · опло́шность</b>","Ask of any word: what does it <b>carry</b> beyond its dictionary meaning?"],"learned":["Felt the leap from the appropriate word to the one exact word","Told near-synonyms apart by shade, not register (идти́ / шага́ть / плести́сь / семени́ть / брести́)","Chose the exact word from a family of «mistake» (оши́бка / про́мах / огре́х / опло́шность)","Asked what a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new leap, one minute on last month's C1 ear — style and register, choosing the word that fits the room. Hold that; today we go one inch finer.","introAudio":"Before the new leap, one minute on last month's C1 ear — style and register, choosing the word that fits the room. Hold that; today we go one inch finer.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is C1 register — an old friend.","q":"In an official report, “money” should be —","opts":["деньжа́та","де́нежные сре́дства","ба́бки"],"answer":1,"explain":"Official register → <b>де́нежные сре́дства</b>. деньжа́та is colloquial, ба́бки is slang. That was C1: the word that fits the room."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что вопро́с остаётся откры́тым» — which style?","opts":["нау́чный","разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й"],"answer":0,"explain":"A term, an impersonal verb, a nominal phrase → the <b>нау́чный</b> (scientific) style. You still read the markers instantly."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the official notice.","q":"Build an <b>official</b> notice: “I hereby inform you of the meeting.”","want":"Настоя́щим уведомля́ю вас о встре́че","answer":"Настоя́щим уведомля́ю вас о встре́че"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · From fitting to exact","title":"Last month: the <em>right</em> word. Now: the <em>one exact</em> word","paras":["Last month was <b>register</b>: don't write «деньжа́та» in a report, don't say «де́нежные сре́дства» to a friend. You learned to pick the word that <b>fits the room</b>. C2 starts exactly where that ends.","Now picture ten words that all fit the same room — all neutral, all correct, all meaning roughly the same thing. A native still lands on <b>one</b>, because each carries a slightly different picture: a different speed, a different feeling, a different <i>why</i>. The dictionary calls them synonyms; the ear hears ten different words.","This is the accent of C2. B1 picks a word that's <b>correct</b>, C1 one that's <b>appropriate</b>, C2 the one that's <b>exact</b> — and knows what it carries beyond the dictionary. «Почти́ то сло́во» — <i>almost</i> the word — is still a foreign accent."],"audio":"Last month was register: don't write 'denzhata' in a report, don't say 'denezhnye sredstva' to a friend — you learned to pick the word that fits the room. C2 starts exactly where that ends. Now picture ten words that all fit the same room — all neutral, all correct, all meaning roughly the same thing. A native still lands on one, because each carries a slightly different picture: a different speed, a different feeling, a different why. The dictionary calls them synonyms; the ear hears ten different words. This is the accent of C2. B1 picks a word that's correct, C1 one that's appropriate, C2 the one that's exact — and knows what it carries beyond the dictionary. 'Pochti to slovo', almost the word, is still a foreign accent.","table":{"rows":[["ве́рно (пра́вильно) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"верно, правильно\">🔊</button>","correct — the grammar works (A1–B1)"],["уме́стно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уместно\">🔊</button>","appropriate — it fits the style and the room (C1)"],["то́чно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"точно\">🔊</button>","exact — the one word that carries the right shade (C2)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Every one of these is “correct” — only one is exact.","en":"Все э́ти слова́ ве́рны — то́чное то́лько одно́.","say":"Все эти слова верны — точное только одно."},{"ru":"A native doesn't translate the thought — they feel the exact word for it.","en":"Носи́тель не перево́дит мысль — он чу́вствует для неё то́чное сло́во.","say":"Носитель не переводит мысль — он чувствует для неё точное слово."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Едва́ живо́й от уста́лости, он шага́л домо́й».","right":"«Едва́ живо́й от уста́лости, он плёлся домо́й».","why":"<b>шага́ть</b> is to stride briskly and on purpose — wrong for someone barely alive with fatigue. The exact verb is <b>плести́сь</b>, to drag yourself along. Both mean “walk”; only one carries “exhausted”. Settling for the merely-adequate synonym is the C2-level slip."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"At C2, from several words that all fit, a native picks the one that is —","opts":["still just correct grammar","exact — it carries the right shade","longer than the alternatives"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct and appropriate are already behind you. C2 chooses the <b>exact</b> word — the one whose shade matches what you mean."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Едва́ живо́й от уста́лости, он … домо́й.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["плёлся","шага́л","семени́л"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barely-alive fatigue → <b>плёлся</b> (плести́сь, to drag oneself along). шага́л is brisk striding; семени́л is tiny quick steps — right pictures, wrong moment."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Ten ways to walk","title":"<em>Идти́</em> is the default — the shade is <em>how</em>","paras":["Take the plainest verb: <b>идти́</b>, to go on foot. Around it stands a whole family, every one neutral, standard Russian — no slangier, no more bookish than идти́. What separates them isn't register (that was last month). It's the picture: how the feet move, how the body feels, why the person walks.","<b>шага́ть</b> — to stride: big, deliberate, purposeful steps (a soldier, someone with somewhere to be). <b>плести́сь</b> — to drag yourself along: slow, weary, reluctant, lagging behind. <b>семени́ть</b> — to mince: tiny, quick steps (a hurried child, a frail old lady). <b>брести́</b> — to plod or wander: slow and aimless, worn down, with no clear goal.","English reaches for adverbs — “walk briskly”, “walk wearily”. Russian packs all of it into the verb itself. Choosing идти́ where a native would reach for <b>плёлся</b> isn't wrong — it just isn't exact, and that inch is what an ear notices."],"audio":"Take the plainest verb: 'idti', to go on foot. Around it stands a whole family, every one neutral, standard Russian — no slangier, no more bookish than 'idti'. What separates them isn't register; that was last month. It's the picture: how the feet move, how the body feels, why the person walks. 'Shagat' is to stride: big, deliberate, purposeful steps. 'Plestis' is to drag yourself along: slow, weary, reluctant, lagging behind. 'Semenit' is to mince: tiny, quick steps. 'Bresti' is to plod or wander: slow and aimless, worn down, with no clear goal. English reaches for adverbs — walk briskly, walk wearily. Russian packs all of it into the verb itself. Choosing 'idti' where a native would reach for 'plyolsya' isn't wrong — it just isn't exact, and that inch is what an ear notices.","table":{"rows":[["идти́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"идти\">🔊</button>","to go on foot — the neutral default"],["шага́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"шагать\">🔊</button>","to stride — big, deliberate, purposeful steps"],["плести́сь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"плестись\">🔊</button>","to trudge — slow, weary, reluctant, lagging"],["семени́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"семенить\">🔊</button>","to mince — tiny, quick steps"],["брести́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брести\">🔊</button>","to plod, wander — slow and aimless, worn out"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The soldiers strode in step across the square.","en":"Солда́ты бо́дро шага́ли в но́гу по пло́щади.","say":"Солдаты бодро шагали в ногу по площади."},{"ru":"After the night shift, Лена trudged home, barely lifting her feet.","en":"По́сле ночно́й сме́ны Ле́на плела́сь домо́й, едва́ поднима́я но́ги.","say":"После ночной смены Лена плелась домой, едва поднимая ноги."},{"ru":"The little boy minced along beside his father.","en":"Ма́льчик семени́л ря́дом с отцо́м.","say":"Мальчик семенил рядом с отцом."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ста́рушка бо́дро шага́ла по льду».","right":"«Ста́рушка осторо́жно семени́ла по льду».","why":"<b>шага́ть</b> is confident, big strides — wrong for a frail old woman on ice. The exact verb is <b>семени́ть</b> — tiny, careful steps. Same “walk”, opposite picture."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Солда́ты … в но́гу по пло́щади.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["плели́сь","шага́ли","семени́ли"],"answer":1,"explain":"Marching in step, big purposeful strides → <b>шага́ли</b> (шага́ть). плели́сь is weary trudging; семени́ли is tiny steps."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"One verb for “to walk with big, deliberate, purposeful steps” — a soldier's walk. Type it:","accept":["шагать","шагает","шагал"],"placeholder":"шага…","explain":"<b>шага́ть</b> — to stride. The step is big and on purpose; that's the whole shade."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Five kinds of “mistake”","title":"One English word, <em>a whole shelf</em> of Russian ones","paras":["It works the same for nouns. English has basically one everyday word for getting something wrong: <b>a mistake</b>. Russian keeps a shelf, and a native takes down the exact one.","<b>оши́бка</b> — the general word, any error (the safe default). <b>про́мах</b> — a blunder, a wrong move: an error of judgment or action, like a shot that misses («маркетинговый про́мах»). <b>огре́х</b> — a flaw left by careless or hasty work, the little rough spots in a finished job — the kind Эмма hunts in a translation. <b>опло́шность</b> — a careless slip, an oversight from plain inattention.","And <b>ля́псус</b> — an embarrassing slip of the tongue or pen — carries something extra: a bookish, faintly ironic wink. That “extra” a word carries beyond its meaning is <i>tomorrow's</i> whole subject (коннота́ция — connotation). For today: five words, five different pictures of “wrong”."],"audio":"It works the same for nouns. English has basically one everyday word for getting something wrong: a mistake. Russian keeps a shelf, and a native takes down the exact one. 'Oshibka' is the general word, any error — the safe default. 'Promakh' is a blunder, a wrong move, an error of judgment, like a shot that misses. 'Ogrekh' is a flaw left by careless or hasty work, the little rough spots in a finished job — the kind Emma hunts in a translation. 'Oploshnost' is a careless slip, an oversight from plain inattention. And 'lyapsus' is an embarrassing slip of the tongue or pen, and it carries something extra: a bookish, faintly ironic wink. That extra a word carries beyond its meaning is tomorrow's whole subject, connotation. For today: five words, five different pictures of 'wrong'.","table":{"rows":[["оши́бка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ошибка\">🔊</button>","a mistake — the general word, any error"],["про́мах <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"промах\">🔊</button>","a blunder — a wrong move, an error of judgment"],["огре́х <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"огрех\">🔊</button>","a flaw — a rough spot left by careless work"],["опло́шность <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оплошность\">🔊</button>","a slip — a careless oversight, from inattention"],["ля́псус <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ляпсус\">🔊</button>","a lapsus — an embarrassing slip (bookish, ironic)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Launching without a test was a serious blunder.","en":"Запусти́ть без прове́рки бы́ло серьёзным про́махом.","say":"Запустить без проверки было серьёзным промахом."},{"ru":"The editor found a few rough spots in the translation.","en":"Реда́ктор нашла́ не́сколько огре́хов в перево́де.","say":"Редактор нашла несколько огрехов в переводе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В твоём перево́де па́ра серьёзных ля́псусов».","right":"«В твоём перево́де па́ра серьёзных огре́хов».","why":"<b>ля́псус</b> is an embarrassing, jokey slip and rings bookish; for plain rough spots in a translation the exact, neutral word is <b>огре́х</b>. The near-synonym is understood — but it isn't the word a native editor reaches for."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Стратеги́ческий … сто́ил компа́нии ры́нка.» — which noun is exact?","opts":["огре́х","про́мах","опло́шность"],"answer":1,"explain":"A wrong strategic move, an error of judgment → <b>про́мах</b> (a blunder). огре́х is a craft flaw; опло́шность is a small careless slip."},{"type":"choice","q":"Beyond its meaning, which word also carries a bookish, slightly ironic wink?","opts":["оши́бка","огре́х","ля́псус"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ля́псус</b> carries tone beyond its meaning — a wink. That “charge” a word carries is exactly tomorrow's topic (коннота́ция)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · How to find it — and the week ahead","title":"Ask what a word <em>carries</em> — then a week of precision","paras":["The method is one question. When several words fit, don't ask “which is correct” (all are) or even “which is appropriate” (they share a register). Ask: <b>what does each one carry</b> — what picture, what speed, what feeling, what judgment — beyond its dictionary line? The exact word is the one whose extra baggage matches what you actually mean.","It's slow at first and instant later; it's exactly what a native does without noticing, and exactly what Эмма does all day over a translation. The “good enough” синоним is the last accent to lose.","This week installs precision from every side. Tomorrow: the hidden charge a word carries (коннота́ция). Then: words that keep company (collocations — <b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b>, not «сде́лать»), expressive word-building (a whole English clause packed into one Russian word), the finest grain of aspect (<b>посиде́ть</b> — to sit a pleasant little while), and the tiny particles that add the last shade. By Sunday you'll stop settling for “almost”."],"audio":"The method is one question. When several words fit, don't ask which is correct — all are — or even which is appropriate — they share a register. Ask what each one carries: what picture, what speed, what feeling, what judgment, beyond its dictionary line. The exact word is the one whose extra baggage matches what you actually mean. It's slow at first and instant later — exactly what a native does without noticing, and what Emma does all day over a translation. The 'good enough' synonym is the last accent to lose. This week installs precision from every side. Tomorrow: the hidden charge a word carries, connotation. Then: words that keep company — collocations, 'okazat vliyaniye', not 'sdelat'; expressive word-building, a whole English clause packed into one Russian word; the finest grain of aspect, 'posidet', to sit a pleasant little while; and the tiny particles that add the last shade. By Sunday you'll stop settling for 'almost'.","examples":[{"ru":"Not “which is correct” — “which carries exactly what I mean”.","en":"Не «что ве́рно», а «что несёт и́менно мой смысл».","say":"Не что верно, а что несёт именно мой смысл."},{"ru":"Neutral: a request. Official: a petition filed at an office.","en":"Нейтра́льно: про́сьба. Официа́льно: проше́ние.","say":"Нейтрально: просьба. Официально: прошение."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он извини́лся за оши́бку — за то, что забы́л о встре́че».","right":"«Он извини́лся за опло́шность — за то, что забы́л о встре́че».","why":"Forgetting the meeting is a small careless slip, not an error in the general sense — the exact word is <b>опло́шность</b>. оши́бка is understood, but it's the flat, near word, not the one a native reaches for."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"When several words all fit, the C2 question is —","opts":["which carries the exact shade I mean","which is grammatically correct","which is the rarest and most bookish"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct and appropriate are settled. The remaining question is what each word <b>carries</b> — pick the one whose shade is yours."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it aloud, letting the verb sound as tired as it means.","target":"По́сле до́лгой доро́ги мы уста́ло брели́ домо́й.","sub":"let «брели́» sound slow and heavy — the word is doing the work","want":"после долгой дороги мы устало брели домой"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The tiny difference in meaning that separates two near-synonyms is its … . Type the Russian noun (hint: отт…):","accept":["оттенок","оттенки"],"placeholder":"отт…","explain":"<b>отте́нок</b> — a shade, a nuance. The whole level lives in these оттенки."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is editing a novel at Ле́на's café. Ива́н drops by and finds her stuck on a single word. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Всё сиди́шь над одни́м абза́цем? Ты его́ уже́ час пра́вишь.","ru":"Still stuck on one paragraph? You've been fixing it for an hour."},{"who":"A","en":"Не пра́влю — ищу́. Геро́й идёт по у́лице, но «идёт» здесь непра́вда.","ru":"Not fixing — searching. The hero walks down the street, but “walks” is a lie here."},{"who":"B","en":"Почему́ непра́вда? Он же идёт.","ru":"Why a lie? He is walking."},{"who":"A","en":"Он е́ле живо́й от го́ря. Он не идёт — он плетётся. Одно́ сло́во, а вся сце́на друга́я.","ru":"He's barely alive with grief. He doesn't walk — he trudges. One word, and the whole scene is different."},{"who":"B","en":"А ра́ньше ты вы́брала бы про́сто «идёт»?","ru":"And before, you'd have just picked “walks”?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше — да. Тепе́рь слы́шу, что «идёт» — почти́ то, но не то.","ru":"Before — yes. Now I hear that “walks” is almost it, but not it."},{"who":"B","en":"«Почти́ то, но не то»… зву́чит как про весь ру́сский.","ru":"“Almost it, but not it”… sounds like it's about the whole Russian language."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и́менно. На э́том у́ровне ва́жно то́лько «то».","ru":"Exactly. At this level only “it” matters."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the hunt: the vocabulary of precision, plus the two families you met today. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"то́чный","ru":"exact, precise","ex":"Носи́тель выбира́ет то́чное сло́во."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"shade, nuance (of meaning)","ex":"Ме́жду сино́нимами — то́нкий отте́нок."},{"en":"сино́ним","ru":"synonym","ex":"У «идти́» мно́жество сино́нимов."},{"en":"разли́чие","ru":"difference, distinction","ex":"Ме́жду ни́ми то́нкое разли́чие."},{"en":"шага́ть","ru":"to stride (big purposeful steps)","ex":"Он бо́дро шага́л по у́лице."},{"en":"плести́сь","ru":"to trudge, drag oneself along","ex":"Уста́лый, он плёлся домо́й."},{"en":"семени́ть","ru":"to mince (tiny quick steps)","ex":"Ребёнок семени́л за ма́мой."},{"en":"брести́","ru":"to plod, wander (slow, aimless)","ex":"Мы бесце́льно брели́ по па́рку."},{"en":"про́мах","ru":"a blunder, a wrong move","ex":"Э́то был серьёзный про́мах."},{"en":"огре́х","ru":"a flaw (of careless work)","ex":"В перево́де оста́лись огре́хи."},{"en":"опло́шность","ru":"a careless slip, an oversight","ex":"Прости́, э́то моя́ опло́шность."},{"en":"ля́псус","ru":"an embarrassing slip (bookish)","ex":"В докла́де вы́шел смешно́й ля́псус."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each verb of walking to the shade it carries — the picture, not the register.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each verb on the left onto its picture — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["шага́ть","stride — big, deliberate, purposeful steps"],["плести́сь","trudge — drag yourself along, slow and weary"],["семени́ть","mince — tiny, quick steps"],["брести́","plod, wander — slow and aimless"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"At C2, from several words that all fit, a native picks the one that is —","opts":["exact — it carries the right shade","the longest one","the most formal one"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Ста́рушка осторо́жно … по льду.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["шага́ла","семени́ла","брела́"],"answer":1},{"q":"The general, neutral word for any error is —","opts":["оши́бка","ля́псус","про́мах"],"answer":0},{"q":"A wrong strategic move, an error of judgment, is a —","opts":["про́мах","огре́х","опло́шность"],"answer":0},{"q":"The C2 question about a word is not “is it correct” but “what does it …?”","opts":["carry beyond the dictionary","rhyme with","cost"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Three characters, three different walks — and one flat verb, «идти́», that none of them quite is. Read each aloud and feel the exact word land.","title":"Почти́ то сло́во — и то са́мое","sentences":["Оди́н геро́й бо́дро шага́ет навстре́чу судьбе́ — в ка́ждом ша́ге реши́мость.","Друго́й, разби́тый го́рем, едва́ плетётся по пусто́й у́лице.","Тре́тий про́сто бредёт куда́ глаза́ глядя́т, сам не зна́я куда́.","Все тро́е «иду́т» — но ни одного́ нельзя́ назва́ть про́сто «идёт».","В э́том и рабо́та ма́стера: найти́ не бли́зкое сло́во, а то са́мое."],"translation":"One character strides briskly to meet his fate — resolve in every step. Another, broken by grief, barely trudges down the empty street. A third simply wanders wherever his eyes lead, not knowing where himself. All three “walk” — but none of them can be called merely “walking”. And that is the master's work: to find not the near word, but the very one."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to hunt the exact word.","prompt":"Take one plain action — someone walking, or someone getting something wrong — and describe the SAME moment in Russian TWICE, changing only the key word: once with the neutral default (идти́ / оши́бка), once with the exact near-synonym that carries the real shade (плести́сь, семени́ть; про́мах, огре́х). Then name, in a word or two, the shade each choice carries. 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Ask the C2 question of each choice: what does the word carry beyond the dictionary — a speed, a feeling, a judgment? The neutral word states the fact; the exact one paints the picture 🙂","example":"Нейтра́льно: По́сле сме́ны Ле́на шла домо́й. То́чно: По́сле сме́ны Ле́на не шла, а плела́сь — е́ле поднима́я но́ги от уста́лости. «Плела́сь» несёт то, чего́ нет в «шла»: тя́жесть, уста́лость, неохо́ту. Одно́ то́чное сло́во — и чита́тель ви́дит всю сце́ну."}},"2":{"day":2,"week":"01","themeRu":"The hidden charge of a word","themeEn":"Коннота́ция и оце́нка в сло́ве","intro":"Yesterday, in that shelf of words for «mistake», one carried a little extra — <b>ля́псус</b>, with its bookish, ironic wink. I promised that <i>extra</i> would be today's whole subject. It has a name: <b>коннота́ция</b> (connotation — the charge a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning). Two words can point at the exact same thing and still pass opposite verdicts on it. Call a careful spender <b>бережли́вый</b> and you admire them; call the same person <b>скупо́й</b> and you've just called them a miser. Same fact, opposite judgment — hidden inside the word. Today Эмма learns to hear the charge before it goes off.","introAudio":"Yesterday, in that shelf of words for 'mistake', one carried a little extra — lyapsus, with its bookish, ironic wink. I promised that extra would be today's whole subject. It has a name: konnotatsiya, connotation — the charge a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning. Two words can point at the exact same thing and still pass opposite verdicts on it. Call a careful spender berezhlivyj and you admire them; call the same person skupoj and you've just called them a miser. Same fact, opposite judgment — hidden inside the word. Today Emma learns to hear the charge before it goes off.","goals":["Meet <b>коннота́ция</b> — the hidden charge a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning","Hear same-fact, opposite-verdict pairs: <b>бережли́вый / скупо́й</b>, <b>упо́рный / упря́мый</b>, <b>уве́ренный / самоуве́ренный</b>","Catch the words that come pre-loaded with irony: <b>пресло́вутый · так называ́емый · небезызве́стный</b>","Ask of any word: what <b>verdict</b> does it smuggle in — and did I mean it?"],"learned":["Met коннота́ция — the charge a word carries beyond its meaning","Heard same-fact, opposite-verdict pairs (бережли́вый / скупо́й, упо́рный / упря́мый)","Caught the pre-loaded ironic markers (пресло́вутый, так называ́емый, небезызве́стный)","Learned to ask what verdict a word smuggles in — and whether it was intended"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday: the <b>one exact</b> word from a family that all «fit». Hold that — today we go inside the word itself.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday: the one exact word from a family that all 'fit'. Hold that — today we go inside the word itself.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's exact word — an old friend already.","q":"«Едва́ живо́й от го́ря, он … по пусто́й у́лице.» — yesterday's exact verb for barely-moving weariness?","opts":["плёлся","шага́л","семени́л"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barely alive with grief → <b>плёлся</b> (плести́сь, to drag oneself along). шага́л strides; семени́л minces. That was day 1: the one exact word."},{"type":"choice","q":"A rough spot left in a finished translation is a —","opts":["про́мах","огре́х","ля́псус"],"answer":1,"explain":"A flaw from careless work → <b>огре́х</b>. про́мах is a blunder of judgment; ля́псус is an embarrassing slip. Yesterday's shelf of «mistake»."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Yesterday's word for the tiny difference that separates two near-synonyms — type it (отт…):","accept":["оттенок","оттенки","отенок"],"placeholder":"отт…","explain":"<b>отте́нок</b> — a shade, a nuance. Today the whole shade is a hidden verdict."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Beyond the dictionary","title":"A word <em>means</em> something — and <em>says</em> something about it","paras":["Every word has two layers. The first is <b>denotation</b> — the thing it points at, the dictionary line. The second is <b>коннота́ция</b> (connotation) — the charge it carries on top: a feeling, a judgment, a whiff of approval or contempt. All those «mistake» words share a denotation; <b>ля́псус</b>'s wink was pure connotation.","Denotation is what a word <i>means</i>; connotation is what it <i>says about</i> what it means. <b>дом</b> and <b>жили́ще</b> both denote a place you live — but жили́ще is cold and official, the word on a form, while <b>оча́г</b> (a hearth) denotes the same place and glows with warmth and belonging.","At C2 you stop hearing only the dictionary. You hear the charge riding on top — and you never fire it by accident."],"audio":"Every word has two layers. The first is denotation — the thing it points at, the dictionary line. The second is konnotatsiya, connotation — the charge it carries on top: a feeling, a judgment, a whiff of approval or contempt. All those 'mistake' words share a denotation; lyapsus's wink was pure connotation. Denotation is what a word means; connotation is what it says about what it means. 'Dom' and 'zhilishche' both denote a place you live — but zhilishche is cold and official, the word on a form, while 'ochag', a hearth, denotes the same place and glows with warmth and belonging. At C2 you stop hearing only the dictionary. You hear the charge riding on top — and you never fire it by accident.","table":{"rows":[["дом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом\">🔊</button>","home — neutral, the everyday default"],["жили́ще <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жилище\">🔊</button>","a dwelling — cold, official, the word on a form"],["оча́г <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"очаг\">🔊</button>","a hearth — warmth, belonging, home as a feeling"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"«дом» and «жили́ще» point at the same place — one is warm, one is a form.","en":"«Дом» и «жили́ще» — одно́ и то́ же ме́сто, но одно́ сло́во тёплое, друго́е — казённое.","say":"Дом и жилище — одно и то же место, но одно слово тёплое, другое — казённое."},{"ru":"The dictionary gives the meaning; the ear catches the charge.","en":"Слова́рь даёт значе́ние — слух ло́вит заря́д.","say":"Словарь даёт значение — слух ловит заряд."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«По́сле сва́дьбы они́ ста́ли жить в одно́м жили́ще».","right":"«По́сле сва́дьбы они́ ста́ли жить под одно́й кры́шей».","why":"<b>жили́ще</b> is the chilly word from a housing form — its official coldness is wrong for a newlyweds' home. The warm idiom is <b>под одно́й кры́шей</b> (under one roof). The denotation fits; the connotation clashes."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"<b>жили́ще</b> and <b>оча́г</b> denote the same place. What differs between them is —","opts":["their denotation (dictionary meaning)","their connotation (the charge each carries)","their grammar"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both mean «a place you live» — same denotation. The gap is <b>connotation</b>: жили́ще is cold and official, оча́г is warm."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word denotes «home» but glows with warmth and belonging?","opts":["жили́ще","дом","оча́г"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>оча́г</b> (a hearth) — same place as дом, but soaked in warmth. дом is neutral; жили́ще is a form."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Same fact, opposite verdict","title":"One person, two words — and you've <em>judged</em> them","paras":["The sharpest proof that connotation is real: pairs of words that describe the <b>identical</b> behaviour and disagree only about whether it's good. Nothing in the facts changes — only the verdict you smuggle in.","Someone who spends little is <b>бережли́вый</b> (thrifty — admiring) or <b>скупо́й</b> (stingy — contemptuous). Someone who won't back down is <b>упо́рный</b> (persistent — a compliment) or <b>упря́мый</b> (stubborn — a complaint). Someone sure of themselves is <b>уве́ренный</b> (confident — good) or <b>самоуве́ренный</b> (cocky — bad).","There is no neutral ground here: choose the word and you've taken a side. Reach for <b>скупо́й</b> when you only meant «careful with money», and you've insulted someone you meant to praise."],"audio":"The sharpest proof that connotation is real: pairs of words that describe the identical behaviour and disagree only about whether it's good. Nothing in the facts changes — only the verdict you smuggle in. Someone who spends little is berezhlivyj, thrifty and admiring, or skupoj, stingy and contemptuous. Someone who won't back down is upornyj, persistent, a compliment, or upryamyj, stubborn, a complaint. Someone sure of themselves is uverennyj, confident and good, or samouverennyj, cocky and bad. There is no neutral ground here: choose the word and you've taken a side. Reach for skupoj when you only meant 'careful with money', and you've insulted someone you meant to praise.","table":{"rows":[["бережли́вый — скупо́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бережливый, скупой\">🔊</button>","thrifty (good) vs stingy (bad) — spends little"],["упо́рный — упря́мый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"упорный, упрямый\">🔊</button>","persistent (good) vs stubborn (bad) — won't yield"],["уве́ренный — самоуве́ренный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уверенный, самоуверенный\">🔊</button>","confident (good) vs cocky (bad) — sure of self"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Call him thrifty and you praise him; call him stingy and you sneer.","en":"Назови́ его́ бережли́вым — э́то похвала́; назови́ скупы́м — э́то насме́шка.","say":"Назови его бережливым — это похвала; назови скупым — это насмешка."},{"ru":"She isn't stubborn — she's persistent. Same trait, kinder word.","en":"Она́ не упря́мая, а упо́рная. То же ка́чество — сло́во добре́е.","say":"Она не упрямая, а упорная. То же качество — слово добрее."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"(о лу́чшем дру́ге) «Он тако́й скупо́й — никогда́ не тра́тит зря».","right":"(о лу́чшем дру́ге) «Он тако́й бережли́вый — никогда́ не тра́тит зря».","why":"You meant to praise his good sense with money, but <b>скупо́й</b> (stingy) fires a verdict of meanness. The admiring word for the very same habit is <b>бережли́вый</b> (thrifty). The fact is identical; the connotation insults."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You want to <b>praise</b> a friend for standing his ground. Which word?","opts":["упря́мый","упо́рный","самоуве́ренный"],"answer":1,"explain":"Praise → <b>упо́рный</b> (persistent). упря́мый (stubborn) and самоуве́ренный (cocky) both carry a complaint — the same behaviour, judged badly."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The <b>admiring</b> word for someone careful with money (not «скупо́й») — type it (береж…):","accept":["бережливый","бережлив","бережливый человек"],"placeholder":"береж…","explain":"<b>бережли́вый</b> — thrifty, and it's a compliment. скупо́й is the same habit seen with contempt."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Words that come pre-loaded","title":"<em>Пресло́вутый</em>: the irony baked into the word","paras":["Some words don't wait for you to judge — they arrive with the verdict already inside. Slip one in and you've announced your attitude whether you meant to or not. These are the words a native uses to signal, quietly, «I don't buy this».","<b>пресло́вутый</b> — «that notorious, much-touted so-and-so»: it looks like «famous», but it drips scorn. «пресло́вутый план» is that overhyped plan nobody believes in. <b>так называ́емый</b> — «so-called»: air-quotes folded into a word, «their так называ́емый expert». <b>небезызве́стный</b> — literally «not-unknown», but it's a sly wink: «a certain well-known figure», nudge-nudge.","There's a matching trick with lofty words. <b>пове́дать</b> (to relate, solemnly) for a scrap of gossip, or <b>осо́ба</b> (a personage) for an ordinary person — the grand word on a trivial thing lands as irony. The size mismatch <i>is</i> the joke."],"audio":"Some words don't wait for you to judge — they arrive with the verdict already inside. Slip one in and you've announced your attitude whether you meant to or not. These are the words a native uses to signal, quietly, 'I don't buy this'. Preslovutyj is 'that notorious, much-touted so-and-so': it looks like 'famous', but it drips scorn. 'Preslovutyj plan' is that overhyped plan nobody believes in. Tak nazyvayemyj is 'so-called': air-quotes folded into a word, 'their tak nazyvayemyj expert'. Nebezyzvestnyj is literally 'not-unknown', but it's a sly wink: 'a certain well-known figure', nudge-nudge. There's a matching trick with lofty words. Povedat, to relate solemnly, for a scrap of gossip, or osoba, a personage, for an ordinary person — the grand word on a trivial thing lands as irony. The size mismatch is the joke.","table":{"rows":[["пресло́вутый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пресловутый\">🔊</button>","that notorious / much-touted — scorn, not fame"],["так называ́емый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"так называемый\">🔊</button>","so-called — air-quotes, quiet doubt"],["небезызве́стный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"небезызвестный\">🔊</button>","a certain well-known… — a sly, ironic wink"],["пове́дать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"поведать\">🔊</button>","to relate (solemnly) — grand word, often ironic"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«their so-called plan» — the word так называ́емый already says you doubt it.","en":"«Их так называ́емый план» — само́ сло́во говори́т, что вы в него́ не ве́рите.","say":"Их так называемый план — само слово говорит, что вы в него не верите."},{"ru":"That notorious «reform» again — everyone's sick of it.","en":"Опя́ть э́та пресло́вутая «рефо́рма» — все от неё уста́ли.","say":"Опять эта пресловутая реформа — все от неё устали."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Мы горди́мся на́шим пресло́вутым университе́том».","right":"«Мы горди́мся на́шим знамени́тым университе́том».","why":"<b>пресло́вутый</b> isn't «famous» — it's «notorious, overhyped», soaked in scorn. Praising your own university with it says the opposite of what you mean. For real pride use <b>знамени́тый</b> (renowned)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«пресло́вутый» looks like «famous», but a native hears —","opts":["neutral fame","scorn: notorious, overhyped","warm affection"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>пресло́вутый</b> arrives pre-loaded with contempt — «that overhyped one nobody believes in». Never a compliment."},{"type":"choice","q":"You want to hint, with a wink, at «a certain well-known person». Which word?","opts":["небезызве́стный","знамени́тый","изве́стный"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>небезызве́стный</b> («not-unknown») is the sly, ironic wink. знамени́тый and изве́стный are plain «famous / well-known», with no wink."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Did you mean the verdict? — and the method","title":"The charge fires <em>whether you aimed it or not</em>","paras":["Here's the trap for an English ear. You reach for a word by its dictionary meaning, not knowing it comes charged — and it goes off on its own. <b>амбицио́зный</b> feels like English «ambitious» (a virtue), but in Russian it often tilts toward «pushy, self-serving». Praise a colleague as амбицио́зный and half the room hears a dig.","The charge doesn't switch off because you didn't intend it. A word's connotation is public property — the listener hears the verdict the word carries, not the one in your head. «Почти́ то сло́во» from yesterday now has a second danger: the near word may carry a judgment you never meant.","So the method is one more question, stacked on yesterday's. First: which word is <b>exact</b>? Then: <b>what verdict does it smuggle in — and did I mean it?</b> Tomorrow we look at which words simply refuse to stand next to each other (сочета́емость — collocation): «оказа́ть влия́ние», never «сде́лать»."],"audio":"Here's the trap for an English ear. You reach for a word by its dictionary meaning, not knowing it comes charged — and it goes off on its own. Ambitsioznyj feels like English 'ambitious', a virtue, but in Russian it often tilts toward 'pushy, self-serving'. Praise a colleague as ambitsioznyj and half the room hears a dig. The charge doesn't switch off because you didn't intend it. A word's connotation is public property — the listener hears the verdict the word carries, not the one in your head. 'Pochti to slovo' from yesterday now has a second danger: the near word may carry a judgment you never meant. So the method is one more question, stacked on yesterday's. First: which word is exact? Then: what verdict does it smuggle in, and did I mean it? Tomorrow we look at which words simply refuse to stand next to each other, sochetayemost, collocation: 'okazat vliyaniye', never 'sdelat'.","examples":[{"ru":"«ambitious» sounds like praise; амбицио́зный can quietly mean «pushy».","en":"«Амбицио́зный» звучи́т как похвала́, но ча́сто намека́ет на «напо́ристый, себе́ на уме́».","say":"Амбициозный звучит как похвала, но часто намекает на напористый, себе на уме."},{"ru":"Ask twice: is it exact — and does its charge match what I mean?","en":"Спроси́ два́жды: то́чное ли сло́во — и совпада́ет ли его́ заря́д с мои́м смы́слом?","say":"Спроси дважды: точное ли слово — и совпадает ли его заряд с моим смыслом?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"(в рекоменда́ции) «Э́то весьма́ амбицио́зный сотру́дник».","right":"(в рекоменда́ции) «Э́то целеустремлённый сотру́дник».","why":"In a warm reference, <b>амбицио́зный</b> can read as «pushy, out for himself» — a charge you never intended. The clean, positive word is <b>целеустремлённый</b> (goal-driven). Same drive, no hidden dig."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The C2 question about a word, stacked on «is it exact?», is —","opts":["what verdict does it carry, and did I mean it","how many letters it has","whether it's rare and bookish"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exact was yesterday. Today: what charge rides on top — and is that the verdict you actually intend?"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it so the irony lives in your voice.","target":"Их так называ́емый «специали́ст» опя́ть всё перепу́тал.","sub":"let «так называ́емый» drip with quiet doubt — the charge is in the word and in your tone","want":"их так называемый специалист опять всё перепутал"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя shows Эмма a caption she wrote for the café's page. One word makes Эмма smile — it's carrying a verdict Ка́тя never intended. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Посмотри́, я написа́ла: «Наш пресло́вутый десе́рт сно́ва в меню́!»","ru":"Look, I wrote: «Our пресло́вутый dessert is back on the menu!»"},{"who":"A","en":"Краси́во — но «пресло́вутый» здесь то́чно не то, что ты хоте́ла сказа́ть.","ru":"Nice — but «пресло́вутый» here really isn't what you meant to say."},{"who":"B","en":"А что оно́ зна́чит? Я ду́мала — «знамени́тый», «изве́стный».","ru":"What does it mean? I thought «famous», «well-known»."},{"who":"A","en":"Почти́ — но с презре́нием. «Пресло́вутый» — э́то «тот са́мый, надое́вший, разреклами́рованный».","ru":"Almost — but with contempt. «Пресло́вутый» is «that same tiresome, overhyped one»."},{"who":"B","en":"То есть я написа́ла, что наш же со́бственный десе́рт всем надое́л?!","ru":"So I wrote that our own dessert has bored everyone?!"},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и́менно. Сло́во несёт оце́нку, кото́рую ты не заклада́ла. Поста́вь «знамени́тый» — и всё.","ru":"Exactly. The word carries a verdict you didn't load into it. Put «знамени́тый» — and done."},{"who":"B","en":"С ума́ сойти́. Одно́ сло́во — и смысл наоборо́т.","ru":"Incredible. One word — and the meaning flips."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то и есть коннота́ция. Она́ сраба́тывает, да́же когда́ ты не це́лилась.","ru":"That's connotation. It fires even when you weren't aiming."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the hidden charge: the vocabulary of connotation, plus the pairs and markers you met today. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"коннота́ция","ru":"connotation (the charge beyond the meaning)","ex":"У ка́ждого сло́ва есть коннота́ция."},{"en":"оце́нка","ru":"an evaluation, a verdict (carried in a word)","ex":"Сло́во несёт скры́тую оце́нку."},{"en":"заря́д","ru":"a charge (of feeling or judgment)","ex":"У э́того сло́ва си́льный заря́д."},{"en":"бережли́вый","ru":"thrifty (careful with money — a compliment)","ex":"Он бережли́вый, а не скупо́й."},{"en":"скупо́й","ru":"stingy, miserly (the contemptuous twin)","ex":"Скупо́й дрожи́т над ка́ждой копе́йкой."},{"en":"упо́рный","ru":"persistent (won't give up — a compliment)","ex":"Упо́рный всегда́ дохо́дит до це́ли."},{"en":"упря́мый","ru":"stubborn (won't yield — a complaint)","ex":"С упря́мым тру́дно договори́ться."},{"en":"самоуве́ренный","ru":"cocky, over-sure (the negative twin of «confident»)","ex":"Самоуве́ренный не слу́шает сове́тов."},{"en":"пресло́вутый","ru":"notorious, much-touted (ironic scorn)","ex":"Опя́ть э́тот пресло́вутый вопро́с."},{"en":"так называ́емый","ru":"so-called (air-quotes, quiet doubt)","ex":"Их так называ́емый экспе́рт молчи́т."},{"en":"небезызве́стный","ru":"a certain well-known… (an ironic wink)","ex":"Э́то сказа́л небезызве́стный вам челове́к."},{"en":"пове́дать","ru":"to relate, recount (solemn, often ironic)","ex":"Позво́ль пове́дать тебе́ но́вость."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each charged word to the hidden verdict it carries.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word on the left onto the verdict it smuggles in — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["скупо́й","stingy — contempt (spends little, seen as mean)"],["бережли́вый","thrifty — praise (spends little, seen as wise)"],["пресло́вутый","notorious — scorn (much-touted, not believed)"],["упря́мый","stubborn — complaint (won't yield)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"<b>Коннота́ция</b> is —","opts":["a word's dictionary meaning","the charge a word carries beyond its meaning","a spelling rule"],"answer":1},{"q":"You admire a friend's care with money. The right word is —","opts":["бережли́вый","скупо́й","жа́дный"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Их пресло́вутый прое́кт» tells the listener you —","opts":["admire the project","don't believe in it","have never heard of it"],"answer":1},{"q":"Same behaviour, opposite verdict — the negative twin of «упо́рный» is —","opts":["надёжный","уве́ренный","упря́мый"],"answer":2},{"q":"The C2 question stacked on «is it exact?» is —","opts":["does it rhyme","what verdict does it carry, and did I mean it","is it short"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Two neighbours' view of one man and one habit with money — and two words that judge it in opposite directions. Read each aloud and feel the verdict land.","title":"Одно́ ка́чество — два пригово́ра","sentences":["Анто́н живёт скро́мно и счита́ет ка́ждую копе́йку.","Сосе́ди зову́т его́ бережли́вым — и в э́том сло́ве слы́шно уваже́ние.","А за глаза́ те же сосе́ди говоря́т: «скупо́й».","Фа́кты те же — привы́чка одна́, — но пригово́р противополо́жный.","В э́том и си́ла коннота́ции: не что́ ты говори́шь, а как ты э́то оце́ниваешь."],"translation":"Anton lives modestly and counts every kopeck. The neighbours call him thrifty — and you can hear respect in that word. But behind his back the same neighbours say: «stingy». The facts are the same — one single habit — yet the verdict is opposite. That is the power of connotation: not what you say, but how you judge it."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to hear the charge before it goes off.","prompt":"Take one neutral fact about a person — how they spend money, how they hold an opinion, how sure of themselves they are — and describe them in Russian TWICE, changing only the key word: once with the <b>admiring</b> connotation (бережли́вый, упо́рный, уве́ренный), once with the <b>contemptuous</b> one for the same fact (скупо́й, упря́мый, самоуве́ренный). Then name, in a word or two, the verdict each choice smuggles in. 3–4 sentences.","hint":"Remember: the facts don't change — only the charge does. The listener hears the verdict the word carries, not the one in your head 🙂","example":"Оце́нка со зна́ком плюс: О́льга упо́рная — она́ не отступа́ет, пока́ не добьётся своего́. Оце́нка со зна́ком ми́нус: О́льга упря́мая — с ней невозмо́жно договори́ться. Фа́кт оди́н: она́ не меня́ет реше́ний. Но «упо́рная» хва́лит, а «упря́мая» осужда́ет — вся ра́зница в коннота́ции."}},"3":{"day":3,"week":"01","themeRu":"Words that keep company","themeEn":"Сочета́емость: сло́ва, кото́рые дру́жат","intro":"Yesterday a single word could leak a verdict. Today the danger moves into the <i>space between</i> words. Your grammar is flawless, every word is correct — and a native still hears a foreigner, because you paired words that Russian keeps apart. English says «make a mistake»; Russian will not «make» one — it <b>допуска́ет</b> (admits) or <b>соверша́ет</b> (commits) it. This is <b>сочета́емость</b> (collocation — which words keep company). It's the last, quietest accent, and the one Эмма polishes hardest in a translation.","introAudio":"Yesterday a single word could leak a verdict. Today the danger moves into the space between words. Your grammar is flawless, every word is correct — and a native still hears a foreigner, because you paired words that Russian keeps apart. English says 'make a mistake'; Russian will not 'make' one — it dopuskayet, admits, or sovershayet, commits, it. This is sochetayemost, collocation — which words keep company. It's the last, quietest accent, and the one Emma polishes hardest in a translation.","goals":["Meet <b>сочета́емость</b> (collocation) — which words Russian keeps together, and which it keeps apart","Use the right light verb: <b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b>, <b>одержа́ть побе́ду</b>, <b>допусти́ть оши́бку</b> — never «сде́лать»","Lock the fixed pairs: <b>проливно́й до́ждь</b>, <b>закады́чный друг</b>, <b>ка́рие глаза́</b>","Catch the word-by-word calque that breaks a Russian pairing — the No. 1 near-native tell"],"learned":["Met сочета́емость — the partnerships Russian fixes between words","Chose the right light verb (оказа́ть влия́ние, одержа́ть побе́ду, допусти́ть оши́бку)","Locked fixed pairs (проливно́й до́ждь, закады́чный друг, ка́рие глаза́)","Learned to spot the calque that breaks a collocation — grammar perfect, pairing wrong"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on yesterday's hidden charge (коннота́ция), then into the space between words.","introAudio":"One minute on yesterday's hidden charge, konnotatsiya, then into the space between words.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's hidden charge.","q":"You want to <b>praise</b> a friend's care with money. The word is —","opts":["скупо́й","бережли́вый","жа́дный"],"answer":1,"explain":"Praise → <b>бережли́вый</b> (thrifty). скупо́й and жа́дный carry contempt — same habit, a verdict of meanness. That was yesterday's connotation."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Их пресло́вутый прое́кт» tells the listener you —","opts":["don't believe in it","admire it","invented it"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>пресло́вутый</b> is pre-loaded with scorn — «that overhyped thing nobody buys». Never admiration."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build, with yesterday's ironic marker: «I don't believe in their so-called plan.»","want":"Я не ве́рю в их так называ́емый план","answer":"Я не ве́рю в их так называ́емый план"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Words keep company","title":"Grammar lets you; <em>usage</em> won't","paras":["Take «to make a mistake». Every word is right, the grammar is spotless — and «сде́лать оши́бку» is still wrong. Russian doesn't «make» a mistake; it <b>допуска́ет</b> (admits) or <b>соверша́ет</b> (commits) one. Nothing is ungrammatical; the words simply don't keep company.","This is <b>сочета́емость</b> — collocation. Every language quietly fixes which words stand together. You can't «сде́лать влия́ние»; you <b>ока́зываете</b> it. You don't «сде́лать побе́ду»; you <b>оде́рживаете</b> it. The partner is fixed by habit, not logic.","This is the last accent to lose. Your grammar can be perfect and your word charged exactly right — and one wrong partner still says «not a native». It's the tell Эмма hunts line by line."],"audio":"Take 'to make a mistake'. Every word is right, the grammar is spotless — and 'sdelat oshibku' is still wrong. Russian doesn't 'make' a mistake; it dopuskayet, admits, or sovershayet, commits, one. Nothing is ungrammatical; the words simply don't keep company. This is sochetayemost — collocation. Every language quietly fixes which words stand together. You can't 'sdelat vliyaniye'; you okazyvayete it. You don't 'sdelat pobedu'; you oderzhivayete it. The partner is fixed by habit, not logic. This is the last accent to lose. Your grammar can be perfect and your word charged exactly right — and one wrong partner still says 'not a native'. It's the tell Emma hunts line by line.","table":{"rows":[["допусти́ть оши́бку <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"допустить ошибку\">🔊</button>","to make (lit. admit / commit) a mistake — not «сде́лать»"],["оказа́ть влия́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оказать влияние\">🔊</button>","to have (lit. render) an influence — not «сде́лать»"],["одержа́ть побе́ду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"одержать победу\">🔊</button>","to win (lit. gain) a victory — not «сде́лать»"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"You don't «make» a mistake in Russian — you «admit» one.","en":"По-ру́сски оши́бку не «де́лают» — её «допуска́ют».","say":"По-русски ошибку не делают — её допускают."},{"ru":"Perfect grammar, wrong partner — and the sentence sounds foreign.","en":"Грамма́тика безупре́чна, но па́ра не та — и фра́за звучи́т не по-ру́сски.","say":"Грамматика безупречна, но пара не та — и фраза звучит не по-русски."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он сде́лал большо́е влия́ние на мою́ рабо́ту».","right":"«Он оказа́л большо́е влия́ние на мою́ рабо́ту».","why":"«влия́ние» keeps company with <b>оказа́ть</b> (to render), never «сде́лать». Every word in the wrong version is correct on its own — only the partnership is broken, and that alone rings foreign."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Он … си́льное влия́ние на прое́кт.» — which verb keeps company with «влия́ние»?","opts":["сде́лал","оказа́л","дал"],"answer":1,"explain":"«влия́ние» marries <b>оказа́ть</b> → оказа́л. «сде́лал влия́ние» is the English calque; «дал влия́ние» is no better."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which pairing is native Russian?","opts":["сде́лать оши́бку","положи́ть оши́бку","допусти́ть оши́бку"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>допусти́ть оши́бку</b> (also соверши́ть). «сде́лать оши́бку» calques English «make»; «положи́ть» makes no sense here."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The light-verb partners","title":"Each noun has its <em>own</em> verb — «сде́лать» won't cover them","paras":["English leans on a few all-purpose verbs — <i>make, do, have, give</i> — and lets them marry almost any noun. Russian assigns each abstract noun its own particular verb, and swapping in a general one is the classic calque.","Learn the noun <b>with</b> its verb, as one unit. <b>оказа́ть</b> влия́ние / по́мощь / подде́ржку / давле́ние. <b>одержа́ть</b> побе́ду / верх. <b>нанести́</b> уда́р / визи́т / уще́рб. <b>допусти́ть</b> оши́бку / опло́шность. <b>приня́ть</b> реше́ние / ме́ры / уча́стие. <b>дать</b> сло́во / обеща́ние.","And beware the famous mix-up: it's <b>игра́ть роль</b> (to play a role) and <b>име́ть значе́ние</b> (to have importance) — never «игра́ть значе́ние» or «име́ть роль». Cross the wires and every native winces."],"audio":"English leans on a few all-purpose verbs — make, do, have, give — and lets them marry almost any noun. Russian assigns each abstract noun its own particular verb, and swapping in a general one is the classic calque. Learn the noun with its verb, as one unit. Okazat: vliyaniye, pomoshch, podderzhku, davleniye. Oderzhat: pobedu, verkh. Nanesti: udar, vizit, ushcherb. Dopustit: oshibku, oploshnost. Prinyat: resheniye, mery, uchastiye. Dat: slovo, obeshchaniye. And beware the famous mix-up: it's igrat rol, to play a role, and imet znacheniye, to have importance — never 'igrat znacheniye' or 'imet rol'. Cross the wires and every native winces.","table":{"rows":[["оказа́ть по́мощь / влия́ние / давле́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оказать помощь\">🔊</button>","render help / influence / pressure"],["нанести́ уда́р / визи́т / уще́рб <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нанести удар\">🔊</button>","deal a blow / pay a visit / do damage"],["приня́ть реше́ние / ме́ры / уча́стие <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принять решение\">🔊</button>","take a decision / measures / part"],["игра́ть роль · име́ть значе́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"играть роль, иметь значение\">🔊</button>","play a role · have importance — never crossed"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"The paramedics gave the victim first aid.","en":"Врачи́ оказа́ли пострада́вшему пе́рвую по́мощь.","say":"Врачи оказали пострадавшему первую помощь."},{"ru":"Your opinion really matters here — it plays no small role.","en":"Ва́ше мне́ние здесь мно́го зна́чит и игра́ет нема́лую роль.","say":"Ваше мнение здесь много значит и играет немалую роль."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́тот фа́ктор игра́ет большо́е значе́ние».","right":"«Э́тот фа́ктор име́ет большо́е значе́ние».","why":"The wires are crossed. Russian fixes <b>игра́ть роль</b> and <b>име́ть значе́ние</b> — never «игра́ть значе́ние». Say «фа́ктор име́ет значе́ние» or «фа́ктор игра́ет роль»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́тот фа́ктор … большу́ю роль.» — which verb?","opts":["име́ет","игра́ет","де́лает"],"answer":1,"explain":"«роль» goes with <b>игра́ть</b> → игра́ет. «име́ет роль» crosses the famous pair; «де́лает» is the English calque."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (past tense). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"First aid was given to the victim: «Пострада́вшему … пе́рвую по́мощь.» Type the verb (ок…):","accept":["оказали","оказал","оказала"],"placeholder":"ок…","explain":"<b>оказа́ть по́мощь</b> — help keeps company with оказа́ть, never «сде́лать» or «дать»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The one-and-only partner","title":"<em>Ка́рие</em> can only be eyes; <em>проливно́й</em> only rain","paras":["Some adjectives have exactly one noun they'll marry. A downpour is <b>проливно́й до́ждь</b> — «си́льный до́ждь» is fine, but проливно́й is the word a native reaches for, and проливно́й attaches to nothing else. A hard frost is <b>треску́чий моро́з</b>; a bosom friend, <b>закады́чный друг</b>; a sworn enemy, <b>закля́тый враг</b>; pitch dark, <b>кроме́шная тьма</b>.","Colour splits the same way. Brown eyes are <b>ка́рие</b> — and only eyes are ка́рие. A brown horse is <b>гнедо́й</b>, brown hair <b>ру́сый</b> or <b>кашта́новый</b>, but a brown jacket is plain <b>кори́чневый</b>. One English «brown»; a shelf of Russian words, each with its own partner.","There's no rule to derive these — they're fixed by usage, learned as whole phrases. Say «кори́чневые глаза́» and you'll be understood and instantly marked as foreign."],"audio":"Some adjectives have exactly one noun they'll marry. A downpour is prolivnoj dozhd — 'silnyj dozhd' is fine, but prolivnoj is the word a native reaches for, and prolivnoj attaches to nothing else. A hard frost is treskuchij moroz; a bosom friend, zakadychnyj drug; a sworn enemy, zaklyatyj vrag; pitch dark, kromeshnaya tma. Colour splits the same way. Brown eyes are karie — and only eyes are karie. A brown horse is gnedoj, brown hair rusyj or kashtanovyj, but a brown jacket is plain korichnevyj. One English 'brown'; a shelf of Russian words, each with its own partner. There's no rule to derive these — they're fixed by usage, learned as whole phrases. Say 'korichnevyye glaza' and you'll be understood and instantly marked as foreign.","table":{"rows":[["проливно́й до́ждь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"проливной дождь\">🔊</button>","a downpour — проливно́й attaches to nothing but rain"],["закады́чный друг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"закадычный друг\">🔊</button>","a bosom friend — закады́чный marries only «друг»"],["ка́рие глаза́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"карие глаза\">🔊</button>","brown eyes — ка́рий is for eyes only"],["кори́чневый пиджа́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"коричневый пиджак\">🔊</button>","a brown jacket — кори́чневый for things, never eyes"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"We got caught in a downpour without an umbrella.","en":"Мы попа́ли под проливно́й до́ждь без зонта́.","say":"Мы попали под проливной дождь без зонта."},{"ru":"Her brown eyes and his brown jacket — two different «browns».","en":"Её ка́рие глаза́ и его́ кори́чневый пиджа́к — два ра́зных «кори́чневых».","say":"Её карие глаза и его коричневый пиджак — два разных коричневых."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«У неё краси́вые кори́чневые глаза́».","right":"«У неё краси́вые ка́рие глаза́».","why":"«кори́чневый» is brown for things — jackets, walls. Eyes are <b>ка́рие</b>, and only eyes. Understood, yes; native, no. The partner is fixed."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A sudden heavy rain is a «… до́ждь». Which adjective?","opts":["кори́чневый","проливно́й","закады́чный"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>проливно́й до́ждь</b> — a downpour. закады́чный is only for «друг»; кори́чневый is brown for objects."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Brown eyes» in Russian are —","opts":["кори́чневые","ка́рие","ру́сые"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ка́рие глаза́</b> — ка́рий is brown for eyes only. ру́сые is for light-brown hair; кори́чневые is for things."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The calque, and how to beat it","title":"Don't translate the words — <em>recall the phrase</em>","paras":["Every broken collocation comes from the same move: you translated word by word out of English. «make a decision» → «сде́лать реше́ние», «pay attention» → «плати́ть внима́ние», «strong rain» → «си́льный до́ждь». Some land, most limp. The calque is the single loudest tell once your grammar is perfect.","The fix is to stop assembling phrases from single words and instead <b>recall the whole partnership</b>. A native doesn't build «приня́ть» + «реше́ние»; they store <b>приня́ть реше́ние</b> as one lump and pour it out whole. Learn nouns married to their verbs, adjectives married to their nouns.","So the day's question: not «is each word right?» (they are) but «<b>do these words keep company in Russian?</b>» When unsure, don't reason from English — reach for the fixed phrase you've heard. Tomorrow: how Russian packs a whole English clause into a single freshly-built word (экспресси́вное словообразова́ние)."],"audio":"Every broken collocation comes from the same move: you translated word by word out of English. 'make a decision' becomes 'sdelat resheniye', 'pay attention' becomes 'platit vnimaniye', 'strong rain' becomes 'silnyj dozhd'. Some land, most limp. The calque is the single loudest tell once your grammar is perfect. The fix is to stop assembling phrases from single words and instead recall the whole partnership. A native doesn't build 'prinyat' plus 'resheniye'; they store prinyat resheniye as one lump and pour it out whole. Learn nouns married to their verbs, adjectives married to their nouns. So the day's question: not 'is each word right?' — they are — but 'do these words keep company in Russian?' When unsure, don't reason from English — reach for the fixed phrase you've heard. Tomorrow: how Russian packs a whole English clause into a single freshly-built word, ekspressivnoye slovoobrazovaniye.","examples":[{"ru":"«pay attention» isn't «pay» in Russian — it's «turn» attention: обрати́ть внима́ние.","en":"«Обрати́ть внима́ние», а не «плати́ть» его́ — вот ру́сская па́ра.","say":"Обратить внимание, а не платить его — вот русская пара."},{"ru":"Don't build the phrase from English — recall the Russian one whole.","en":"Не собира́й фра́зу из англи́йского — вспо́мни ру́сскую целико́м.","say":"Не собирай фразу из английского — вспомни русскую целиком."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ну́жно плати́ть бо́льше внима́ния к дета́лям».","right":"«Ну́жно обраща́ть бо́льше внима́ния на дета́ли».","why":"«pay attention» calques into «плати́ть внима́ние» — but Russian «turns» attention: <b>обраща́ть внима́ние на</b>. Word-for-word from English breaks the partnership; recall the fixed phrase instead."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The day's collocation question is —","opts":["is each word grammatically correct","do these words keep company in Russian","which word is the rarest"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each word is already correct. The remaining question is whether the words <b>keep company</b> in Russian — the fixed partnership."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the native phrase.","q":"Build the native Russian for «to pay attention to the details»:","want":"обраща́ть внима́ние на дета́ли","answer":"обраща́ть внима́ние на дета́ли"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н is drafting a cover letter in Russian and asks Эмма to check it. His grammar is perfect — his word-partners aren't. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Я написа́л: «Я хочу́ сде́лать большо́е влия́ние на разви́тие компа́нии».","ru":"I wrote: «I want to make a big influence on the company's growth»."},{"who":"A","en":"Мысль отли́чная, но «сде́лать влия́ние» так не говоря́т.","ru":"Great thought, but «make an influence» isn't how it's said."},{"who":"B","en":"А как? Ка́ждое сло́во же пра́вильное.","ru":"How, then? Every word is correct."},{"who":"A","en":"В том и де́ло. Слова́ ве́рные, но не дру́жат. Влия́ние «ока́зывают».","ru":"That's exactly it. The words are right but don't keep company. Influence is «rendered»."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, «оказа́ть влия́ние». А «приня́ть уча́стие» — то́же така́я па́ра?","ru":"So «render an influence». And «take part» — is that the same kind of pair?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Их не выво́дят из ло́гики — их про́сто зна́ют наизу́сть.","ru":"Exactly. You don't derive them from logic — you just know them by heart."},{"who":"B","en":"То есть на́до запомина́ть глаго́л вме́сте с существи́тельным?","ru":"So I should memorise the verb together with the noun?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Учи́ не сло́во, а па́ру. Э́то после́дний акце́нт, кото́рый ухо́дит.","ru":"Yes. Learn not the word but the pair. It's the last accent to fade."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the company words keep: the light verbs and the one-partner adjectives you met today. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"сочета́емость","ru":"collocability (which words go together)","ex":"У ка́ждого сло́ва своя́ сочета́емость."},{"en":"оказа́ть","ru":"to render (help, influence, pressure)","ex":"Он оказа́л мне большу́ю по́мощь."},{"en":"одержа́ть","ru":"to gain (a victory, the upper hand)","ex":"Кома́нда одержа́ла побе́ду."},{"en":"нанести́","ru":"to deal / pay / inflict (a blow, a visit, damage)","ex":"Урага́н нанёс большо́й уще́рб."},{"en":"допусти́ть","ru":"to make / allow (a mistake)","ex":"Я допусти́л глу́пую оши́бку."},{"en":"проливно́й","ru":"pouring, torrential (of rain)","ex":"Начался́ проливно́й до́ждь."},{"en":"закады́чный","ru":"bosom (of a friend)","ex":"Э́то мой закады́чный друг."},{"en":"треску́чий","ru":"crackling, hard (of frost)","ex":"На у́лице треску́чий моро́з."},{"en":"закля́тый","ru":"sworn (of an enemy)","ex":"Они́ закля́тые враги́."},{"en":"ка́рий","ru":"brown (of eyes only)","ex":"У неё ка́рие глаза́."},{"en":"значе́ние","ru":"importance, meaning (име́ть значе́ние)","ex":"Э́то не име́ет значе́ния."},{"en":"усто́йчивый","ru":"fixed, set (усто́йчивое сочета́ние = a set phrase)","ex":"«Оказа́ть по́мощь» — усто́йчивое сочета́ние."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each light verb to the nouns it renders — the fixed company it keeps.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each verb on the left onto the nouns it partners — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["оказа́ть","influence, help, pressure (render)"],["одержа́ть","a victory, the upper hand (gain)"],["нанести́","a blow, a visit, damage (deal / pay)"],["допусти́ть","a mistake, an oversight (make / admit)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"<b>Сочета́емость</b> (collocation) is about —","opts":["a word's dictionary meaning","which words keep company with which","how words are spelled"],"answer":1},{"q":"«to make a mistake» in native Russian is —","opts":["сде́лать оши́бку","допусти́ть оши́бку","положи́ть оши́бку"],"answer":1},{"q":"«to have an influence» pairs «влия́ние» with —","opts":["оказа́ть","сде́лать","дать"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Brown eyes» are ка́рие; a «brown jacket» is —","opts":["ка́рий","гнедо́й","кори́чневый"],"answer":2},{"q":"The near-native tell this lesson targets is —","opts":["a grammar error","a word-by-word calque that breaks a pairing","a spelling slip"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's editing note to a young author: every word is right, yet three pairings betray a non-native hand. Read it aloud and hear the partnerships snap into place.","title":"Слова́, кото́рые не дру́жат","sentences":["В твоём те́ксте нет ни одно́й граммати́ческой оши́бки.","И всё же слы́шно, что писа́л не носи́тель.","Ты «де́лаешь» оши́бки — а по-ру́сски их «допуска́ют».","Ты «де́лаешь» влия́ние — а на́до «оказа́ть».","Грамма́тика безупре́чна; про́сто слова́ поста́влены не в ту компа́нию."],"translation":"There isn't a single grammatical mistake in your text. And yet you can hear it wasn't written by a native. You «make» mistakes — but in Russian one «admits» them. You «make» an influence — but it should be «render». The grammar is flawless; the words have simply been put in the wrong company."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to catch a broken partnership.","prompt":"Write 3–4 Russian sentences about someone's day — a win, a mistake, some help given, and the weather. Use the RIGHT collocations on purpose: <b>одержа́ть побе́ду</b>, <b>допусти́ть оши́бку</b>, <b>оказа́ть по́мощь</b>, <b>проливно́й до́ждь</b>. Then take ONE of them, break it the English way («сде́лать побе́ду»), and say in a word or two why it rings foreign.","hint":"Store the phrase as one lump, not two words. A native pours out «приня́ть реше́ние» whole; they never assemble «приня́ть» + «реше́ние» from scratch 🙂","example":"Сего́дня Анто́н одержа́л ва́жную побе́ду на ша́хматном турни́ре, хотя́ в нача́ле па́ртии допусти́л оби́дную оши́бку. По́сле игры́ он оказа́л по́мощь ново́му уча́стнику, а домо́й шёл под проливно́й до́ждь. Ска́жешь «сде́лал побе́ду» — и сра́зу слы́шен иностра́нец: побе́ду не «де́лают», её «оде́рживают»."}},"4":{"day":4,"week":"01","themeRu":"Coining and colouring","themeEn":"Экспресси́вное словообразова́ние","intro":"For three days we've been choosing among words that already exist — the exact one, the charged one, the one that keeps company. Today Russian does something English almost can't: it <b>builds a new word on the spot</b>, and a native understands it instantly. Where English strings out a whole clause — «to overthink it and mess it up» — Russian sculpts one verb, <b>перемудри́ть</b>, from a root and a prefix. A single <b>недо-</b> or <b>пере-</b> or a suffix carries a shade you'd otherwise spell out in a sentence. Эмма's work as an editor is partly this: to read a freshly-coined word and feel exactly what it's doing.","introAudio":"For three days we've been choosing among words that already exist — the exact one, the charged one, the one that keeps company. Today Russian does something English almost can't: it builds a new word on the spot, and a native understands it instantly. Where English strings out a whole clause — 'to overthink it and mess it up' — Russian sculpts one verb, peremudrit, from a root and a prefix. A single nedo- or pere- or a suffix carries a shade you'd otherwise spell out in a sentence. Emma's work as an editor is partly this: to read a freshly-coined word and feel exactly what it's doing.","goals":["See the C2 move: Russian builds a <b>word</b> where English builds a <b>phrase</b> (<b>перемудри́ть</b> = «to overthink it and mess it up»)","Read the degree prefixes <b>пере-</b> (overdo) and <b>недо-</b> (fall short): <b>пересоли́ть</b>, <b>недооцени́ть</b>","Feel expressive suffixes: augmentative <b>-ищ-</b> (<b>доми́ще</b>) and coinage nouns <b>зазна́йка</b>, <b>белору́чка</b>","Decode an <b>окказионали́зм</b> (a word coined for the moment) by its root + model, without a dictionary"],"learned":["Saw Russian build a word where English builds a phrase (перемудри́ть)","Read the degree prefixes пере- (overdo) and недо- (fall short)","Felt expressive suffixes and coinage nouns (доми́ще, зазна́йка, белору́чка)","Learned to decode a nonce word by root + model, no dictionary needed"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on yesterday's partnerships (сочета́емость), then Russian starts inventing words.","introAudio":"One minute on yesterday's partnerships, sochetayemost, then Russian starts inventing words.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's collocations.","q":"«to make a mistake» in native Russian is —","opts":["сде́лать оши́бку","допусти́ть оши́бку","положи́ть оши́бку"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>допусти́ть оши́бку</b> (also соверши́ть). «сде́лать» is the English calque. Yesterday's fixed partnership."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Brown eyes» in Russian are —","opts":["кори́чневые","ру́сые","ка́рие"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ка́рие</b> — brown for eyes only. кори́чневый is for things; ру́сый is for light-brown hair."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Two days back: the word for the hidden charge a word carries beyond its meaning (конн…):","accept":["коннотация","конотация"],"placeholder":"конн…","explain":"<b>коннота́ция</b> — the charge beyond the dictionary line. Today a suffix or prefix adds that charge itself."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A word, not a phrase","title":"English says it in a clause; Russian in <em>one built word</em>","paras":["Here's a move Russian makes constantly and English almost never can: it takes a root and, with a prefix or a suffix, <b>builds a brand-new word</b> that carries a whole English clause inside it. <b>перемудри́ть</b> — «to be too clever for your own good and mess it up». <b>зачита́ться</b> — «to get so lost in a book you lose track of time». One word each.","The pieces are productive — they combine on demand — so a native both <i>coins</i> new words and <i>understands</i> freshly-coined ones without a beat. You don't look up <b>недосоли́ть</b>; you parse it: <b>недо-</b> (not enough) + <b>соли́ть</b> (to salt) = «to under-salt». The meaning is built, not memorised.","For an English ear this is a mindset shift. Stop translating the whole phrase; learn to <b>read the parts</b>. The root tells you <i>what</i>, the prefix and suffix tell you <i>how much, how, and how I feel about it</i>."],"audio":"Here's a move Russian makes constantly and English almost never can: it takes a root and, with a prefix or a suffix, builds a brand-new word that carries a whole English clause inside it. Peremudrit — 'to be too clever for your own good and mess it up'. Zachitatsya — 'to get so lost in a book you lose track of time'. One word each. The pieces are productive — they combine on demand — so a native both coins new words and understands freshly-coined ones without a beat. You don't look up nedosolit; you parse it: nedo-, not enough, plus solit, to salt, equals 'to under-salt'. The meaning is built, not memorised. For an English ear this is a mindset shift. Stop translating the whole phrase; learn to read the parts. The root tells you what, the prefix and suffix tell you how much, how, and how I feel about it.","table":{"rows":[["перемудри́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"перемудрить\">🔊</button>","to overthink it and mess it up — one verb"],["недосоли́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"недосолить\">🔊</button>","to under-salt — недо- (too little) + соли́ть"],["зачита́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зачитаться\">🔊</button>","to get lost in reading — за-…-ся (absorbed)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«overthink it and ruin it» is one Russian verb: перемудри́ть.","en":"«Перемудри́ть» — э́то це́лое англи́йское предложе́ние в одно́м глаго́ле.","say":"Перемудрить — это целое английское предложение в одном глаголе."},{"ru":"You don't look up недосоли́ть — you read it by its parts.","en":"«Недосоли́ть» не и́щут в словаре́ — его́ чита́ют по частя́м.","say":"Недосолить не ищут в словаре — его читают по частям."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он ду́мал сли́шком до́лго, всё усложни́л и в ито́ге всё испо́ртил».","right":"«Он перемудри́л».","why":"English needs a whole clause — «he thought too long, overcomplicated it and ruined it». Russian folds all of it into one built verb: <b>перемудри́ть</b> (пере- «past the mark» + мудри́ть «to be clever»). Reaching for the long paraphrase is the tell; the native reaches for the one word."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Russian's C2 move today is to —","opts":["build a whole word where English builds a phrase","borrow the English phrase directly","always use the shortest root"],"answer":0,"explain":"Russian packs a whole English clause into one <b>built word</b> — root + affixes. That's the move to read, and to make."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"One verb for «to get so lost in a book you lose track of time» (за…):","accept":["зачитаться","зачитался","зачиталась"],"placeholder":"зачит…","explain":"<b>зачита́ться</b> — за-…-ся marks getting absorbed. English needs a clause; Russian, one verb."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Too much, too little","title":"<em>Пере-</em> goes past the mark; <em>недо-</em> falls short","paras":["Two prefixes carry a built-in verdict of degree, and English usually needs «too» plus an adjective to match them. <b>пере-</b> means «past the right point, overdone»: <b>пересоли́ть</b> (over-salt), <b>перестара́ться</b> (overdo it, try too hard), <b>перехвали́ть</b> (over-praise), <b>перемудри́ть</b> (over-clever it).","<b>недо-</b> is the mirror: «short of the mark, not enough». <b>недосоли́ть</b> (under-salt), <b>недооцени́ть</b> (underestimate), <b>недоспа́ть</b> (not get enough sleep), <b>недоговори́ть</b> (leave something unsaid). Notice недо- is glued on: it's one word, not «не» + a verb.","The prefix is the judgment. «Он перестара́лся» isn't «he tried» — it's «he tried <i>too hard</i> and it backfired». That «too much / too little», an adverb in English, is a single syllable in Russian."],"audio":"Two prefixes carry a built-in verdict of degree, and English usually needs 'too' plus an adjective to match them. Pere- means 'past the right point, overdone': peresolit, over-salt; perestaratsya, overdo it, try too hard; perekhvalit, over-praise; peremudrit, over-clever it. Nedo- is the mirror: 'short of the mark, not enough'. Nedosolit, under-salt; nedootsenit, underestimate; nedospat, not get enough sleep; nedogovorit, leave something unsaid. Notice nedo- is glued on: it's one word, not 'ne' plus a verb. The prefix is the judgment. 'On perestaralsya' isn't 'he tried' — it's 'he tried too hard and it backfired'. That 'too much, too little', an adverb in English, is a single syllable in Russian.","table":{"rows":[["пересоли́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пересолить\">🔊</button>","to over-salt — пере- «past the mark»"],["перестара́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"перестараться\">🔊</button>","to overdo it, try too hard"],["недооцени́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"недооценить\">🔊</button>","to underestimate — недо- «short of the mark»"],["недоспа́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"недоспать\">🔊</button>","to not get enough sleep"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"He tried too hard and overdid it — one verb: перестара́лся.","en":"Он перестара́лся — оди́н глаго́л вме́сто «сли́шком си́льно стара́лся».","say":"Он перестарался — один глагол вместо слишком сильно старался."},{"ru":"Don't underestimate her — the verb already packs in «too little».","en":"Не недооце́нивай её — в э́том глаго́ле уже́ спря́тано «сли́шком ма́ло».","say":"Не недооценивай её — в этом глаголе уже спрятано слишком мало."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я не досоли́л суп» (в смы́сле «положи́л ма́ло со́ли»).","right":"«Я недосоли́л суп».","why":"Split as «не досоли́л», it reads like a denied action. The degree prefix is glued: <b>недосоли́ть</b> is one word meaning «to put in too little salt». One built verb replaces «I added too little salt»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«перестара́ться» means —","opts":["to try a little","to try too hard and overdo it","to stop trying"],"answer":1,"explain":"пере- «past the mark» → <b>перестара́ться</b> is to overdo it, to try so hard it backfires."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which verb means «to underestimate»?","opts":["переоцени́ть","недооцени́ть","оцени́ть"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>недооцени́ть</b> — недо- «too little». переоцени́ть is the opposite (over-value); оцени́ть is plain «to value»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Suffixes that colour","title":"<em>-ищ-</em> makes it huge; a suffix can name a whole character","paras":["Suffixes carry feeling, not just size. The augmentative <b>-ищ-</b> blows a thing up, often roughly: from <b>дом</b> (house) → <b>доми́ще</b> (a great hulking house); from <b>рука́</b> (hand) → <b>ручи́ща</b> (a huge paw). You met the affectionate diminutives earlier; -ищ- is their loud cousin.","More striking, Russian coins whole <b>character nouns</b> from a behaviour plus a suffix. <b>зазна́йка</b> — someone who's got a swelled head (from зазна́ться, to get conceited). <b>белору́чка</b> — someone who won't dirty their hands (literally «white-hands»). <b>всезна́йка</b> — a know-it-all (all + know). One word paints the whole type.","English needs «a person who…»; Russian mints a noun. And the suffix already tells you how the speaker feels — <b>-ка</b> here is faintly mocking. The judgment is baked into the shape of the word."],"audio":"Suffixes carry feeling, not just size. The augmentative -ishch- blows a thing up, often roughly: from dom, house, to domishche, a great hulking house; from ruka, hand, to ruchishcha, a huge paw. You met the affectionate diminutives earlier; -ishch- is their loud cousin. More striking, Russian coins whole character nouns from a behaviour plus a suffix. Zaznajka — someone who's got a swelled head, from zaznatsya, to get conceited. Beloruchka — someone who won't dirty their hands, literally 'white-hands'. Vseznajka — a know-it-all, all plus know. One word paints the whole type. English needs 'a person who…'; Russian mints a noun. And the suffix already tells you how the speaker feels — -ka here is faintly mocking. The judgment is baked into the shape of the word.","table":{"rows":[["доми́ще <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"домище\">🔊</button>","a huge (hulking) house — augmentative -ищ-"],["зазна́йка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зазнайка\">🔊</button>","a show-off, swelled head — from зазна́ться"],["белору́чка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"белоручка\">🔊</button>","one who won't dirty their hands («white-hands»)"],["всезна́йка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"всезнайка\">🔊</button>","a know-it-all — all + know"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"That's not a house, it's a great hulking доми́ще.","en":"Э́то не дом, а це́лый доми́ще.","say":"Это не дом, а целый домище."},{"ru":"One word for «a person who won't do dirty work»: белору́чка.","en":"Одно́ сло́во вме́сто «тот, кто чура́ется гря́зной рабо́ты» — белору́чка.","say":"Одно слово вместо тот, кто чурается грязной работы — белоручка."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он тако́й челове́к, кото́рый всё зна́ет и всех поуча́ет».","right":"«Он тако́й всезна́йка».","why":"The long relative clause is exactly what Russian compresses: <b>всезна́йка</b> (all-knower) names the whole type in one mildly mocking word. The suffix <b>-ка</b> even carries the light disdain for free."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«доми́ще» differs from «дом» by —","opts":["being smaller and cuter","being huge, with a rough augmentative -ищ-","being more formal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augmentative <b>-ищ-</b> makes дом into a great hulking <b>доми́ще</b> — big, and a bit rough."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"One mocking word for «a person who won't dirty their hands» (бел…):","accept":["белоручка","белоручкой"],"placeholder":"белор…","explain":"<b>белору́чка</b> — «white-hands». A whole character folded into one coined noun."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Words made on the spot","title":"Meet a word that isn't in any dictionary — and read it anyway","paras":["Because the parts are productive, Russians freely mint <b>окказионали́змы</b> (occasionalisms — words coined for a single occasion, never meant to enter the dictionary). A parent calls a small child «разбойни́ще!»; a columnist writes «преднового́дняя суета́». You won't find these coinages listed anywhere, and a native doesn't blink.","The trap for an English ear is to freeze at an unfamiliar word and assume you missed some vocabulary. Usually you didn't — the word was <i>built</i> in front of you. The move is to <b>parse, not panic</b>: strip the prefix and suffix, find the root, reassemble the meaning. <b>подзабы́л</b>? под- (a bit) + забы́л (forgot) = «half-forgot».","So today's question, next to «exact / charged / keeps company», is: <b>what did this word's parts build?</b> Read the root for the <i>what</i> and the affixes for the <i>how much and how I feel</i>. Tomorrow we go inside the verb itself — the finest shades of aspect, where a prefix tunes not the meaning but the very <i>grain</i> of an action (то́нчайший вид)."],"audio":"Because the parts are productive, Russians freely mint okkazionalizmy — occasionalisms, words coined for a single occasion, never meant to enter the dictionary. A parent calls a small child 'razbojnishche!'; a columnist writes 'prednovogodnyaya sueta'. You won't find these coinages listed anywhere, and a native doesn't blink. The trap for an English ear is to freeze at an unfamiliar word and assume you missed some vocabulary. Usually you didn't — the word was built in front of you. The move is to parse, not panic: strip the prefix and suffix, find the root, reassemble the meaning. Podzabyl? Pod-, a bit, plus zabyl, forgot, equals 'half-forgot'. So today's question, next to exact, charged, keeps company, is: what did this word's parts build? Read the root for the what and the affixes for the how much and how I feel. Tomorrow we go inside the verb itself — the finest shades of aspect, where a prefix tunes not the meaning but the very grain of an action, tonchajshij vid.","examples":[{"ru":"«подзабы́л» = под- (a bit) + забы́л (forgot) = «half-forgot».","en":"«Подзабы́л» — э́то «немно́го забы́л», всё в одно́м сло́ве.","say":"Подзабыл — это немного забыл, всё в одном слове."},{"ru":"Don't panic at a new word — parse it: root plus affixes.","en":"Не пуга́йся но́вого сло́ва — разбери́ его́: ко́рень плюс приста́вки.","say":"Не пугайся нового слова — разбери его: корень плюс приставки."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"(услы́шав «зачита́лась») «Не зна́ю тако́го сло́ва, поле́зу в словарь».","right":"(услы́шав «зачита́лась») «Поня́тно: увлекла́сь чте́нием».","why":"There's nothing to look up — <b>зачита́ться</b> is built from за-…-ся «getting absorbed» + чита́ть «to read». Parse it and the meaning appears: «got lost in reading». Panic is the foreign move; parsing is the native one."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You hit a word that's in no dictionary. The C2 move is to —","opts":["assume you missed vocabulary and stop","parse it: root + affixes rebuild the meaning","ignore the whole sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The word was likely <b>built</b> in front of you. Strip the affixes, find the root, reassemble — parse, don't panic."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it warmly.","target":"Ну ты и приду́мщик — це́лое сло́во сочини́л на ходу́!","sub":"«приду́мщик» is a coined, playful noun for «an inventor of things» — let it sound admiring","want":"ну ты и придумщик целое слово сочинил на ходу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At the café, Ле́на invents a word on the spot to describe a customer, and Эмма lights up — that's exactly today's lesson in action. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"У нас сего́дня был тако́й ко́фе-зану́да: ню́хал, кружи́л, три ра́за переспра́шивал.","ru":"We had such a coffee-bore today: sniffed, swirled, asked three times over."},{"who":"A","en":"«Ко́фе-зану́да»? Ты то́лько что сочини́ла сло́во.","ru":"«Coffee-bore»? You just made up a word."},{"who":"B","en":"Ну да, случа́йно. А так мо́жно?","ru":"Well, yes, by accident. Is that allowed?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не то́лько мо́жно — э́то са́мый ру́сский приём. Я сра́зу по́няла, о ком речь.","ru":"Not only allowed — it's the most Russian move there is. I got exactly who you meant."},{"who":"B","en":"Стра́нно: сло́ва нет в словаре́, а всё поня́тно.","ru":"Strange: the word isn't in any dictionary, yet it's all clear."},{"who":"A","en":"Потому́ что ты собрала́ его́ из поня́тных часте́й. Ко́рень плюс отноше́ние — и гото́во.","ru":"Because you built it from parts everyone knows. A root plus an attitude — and done."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, я неча́янно заговори́ла как писа́тель?","ru":"So I accidentally spoke like a writer?"},{"who":"A","en":"Как носи́тель. Носи́тель ле́пит слова́, а не то́лько выбира́ет их.","ru":"Like a native. A native sculpts words, not just picks them."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for coining and colouring: the machinery of word-building and the vivid words it makes. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"словообразова́ние","ru":"word-formation (building words from parts)","ex":"Ру́сское словообразова́ние о́чень бога́то."},{"en":"окказионали́зм","ru":"a nonce word (coined for the moment)","ex":"Поэ́т лю́бит свои́ окказионали́змы."},{"en":"приста́вка","ru":"a prefix","ex":"Приста́вка меня́ет смысл ко́рня."},{"en":"су́ффикс","ru":"a suffix","ex":"Су́ффикс несёт отноше́ние говоря́щего."},{"en":"перемудри́ть","ru":"to overthink it and mess it up","ex":"Не перемудри́ с отве́том."},{"en":"пересоли́ть","ru":"to over-salt (to overdo)","ex":"Ка́жется, я пересоли́л суп."},{"en":"недооцени́ть","ru":"to underestimate","ex":"Его́ ле́гко недооцени́ть."},{"en":"недоспа́ть","ru":"to not get enough sleep","ex":"Я всю неде́лю недосыпа́ю."},{"en":"зачита́ться","ru":"to get lost in reading","ex":"Она́ зачита́лась и забы́ла о вре́мени."},{"en":"зазна́йка","ru":"a show-off (swelled head)","ex":"Не будь зазна́йкой."},{"en":"белору́чка","ru":"one who shuns dirty work","ex":"Он не белору́чка, рабо́ты не бои́тся."},{"en":"доми́ще","ru":"a huge (hulking) house — augmentative","ex":"Они́ постро́или на́стоящий доми́ще."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each built word to the whole English phrase it folds up.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word on the left onto the phrase it packs — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["перемудри́ть","to overthink it and mess it up"],["недооцени́ть","to value someone too little, to underrate"],["белору́чка","a person who won't do dirty work"],["зачита́ться","to get so lost in a book you forget the time"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Russian's C2 word-building move is to —","opts":["build a word where English builds a phrase","always borrow from English","avoid prefixes"],"answer":0},{"q":"«пересоли́ть» vs «недосоли́ть» — the prefixes mark —","opts":["past tense vs future","too much vs too little","polite vs rude"],"answer":1},{"q":"«всезна́йка» is —","opts":["a huge house","a know-it-all","a kind of soup"],"answer":1},{"q":"You meet a word that's in no dictionary. You should —","opts":["parse it: root + affixes","assume it's a typo","skip the whole text"],"answer":0},{"q":"An <b>окказионали́зм</b> is —","opts":["an official term","a word coined for one occasion","a grammar rule"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма reads a children's writer who coins words the way children do — and every made-up word lands instantly. Read it aloud and parse each one on the fly.","title":"Слова́, кото́рых нет в словаре́","sentences":["Ма́льчик был не про́сто шалу́н, а настоя́щий шалуни́ще.","Он перемудри́л с пла́ном побе́га и сам себя́ запу́тал.","Сестрёнка-белору́чка наотре́з отказа́лась мыть посу́ду.","К ве́черу о́ба так уста́ли, что засну́ли пря́мо за столо́м.","Ни одного́ из э́тих слов нет в словаре́ — а поня́тно ка́ждое."],"translation":"The boy wasn't just a rascal — he was a real rascal-and-a-half. He overthought his escape plan and tangled himself up. His little sister, a won't-dirty-her-hands type, flatly refused to wash the dishes. By evening both were so tired they fell asleep right at the table. Not one of these words is in the dictionary — yet every one is clear."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to build a word.","prompt":"Describe someone in 3–4 Russian sentences, and make TWO of your words <b>built ones</b>, not looked-up: use a degree prefix (<b>пере-</b> / <b>недо-</b>: перемудри́ть, недооцени́ть, недоспа́ть) and one coinage or augmentative (<b>зазна́йка</b>, <b>белору́чка</b>, <b>доми́ще</b>). Then, for one of them, write out the whole English phrase it replaces — so you feel how much a single Russian word carries.","hint":"Read your own coinage the way a native reads a new word: the root for the «what», the prefix/suffix for «how much and how I feel» 🙂","example":"Макси́м — тала́нтливый инжене́р, но иногда́ он перемудри́т с реше́нием там, где хвата́ло бы просто́го. При э́том он совсе́м не зазна́йка и не белору́чка: пе́рвым берётся за са́мую гря́зную рабо́ту. «Перемудри́т» — э́то це́лая англи́йская фра́за «overcomplicates it and trips himself up» в одно́м ру́сском глаго́ле."}},"5":{"day":5,"week":"01","themeRu":"The finest grain of aspect","themeEn":"То́нчайший вид: отте́нки де́йствия","intro":"Yesterday a prefix could <i>change</i> a word — «недо-» made a whole new verb. Today a prefix does something even subtler: it leaves the meaning alone and tunes only the <b>grain</b> of the action — how long, how it begins, how much, how you feel about it. «Сиде́ть» is «to sit». «Посиде́ли» is «we sat a nice little while» — the warmth, the coziness, the just-for-a-bit, all in the two letters of по-. English would need a whole adverbial; Russian hears the shade inside the verb. These are <b>спо́собы де́йствия</b> (aktionsart — the manner of an action). By tonight you'll hear what по-, за-, на-…-ся are quietly saying.","introAudio":"Yesterday a prefix could change a word — 'nedo-' made a whole new verb. Today a prefix does something even subtler: it leaves the meaning alone and tunes only the grain of the action — how long, how it begins, how much, how you feel about it. 'Sidet' is 'to sit'. 'Posideli' is 'we sat a nice little while' — the warmth, the coziness, the just-for-a-bit, all in the two letters of po-. English would need a whole adverbial; Russian hears the shade inside the verb. These are sposoby dejstviya, aktionsart — the manner of an action. By tonight you'll hear what po-, za-, na-…-sya are quietly saying.","goals":["Hear <b>спо́собы де́йствия</b> (aktionsart) — a prefix that tunes not the meaning but the <b>grain</b> of an action","Feel the delimitative <b>по-</b>: a bounded, cozy little while (<b>посиде́ть</b>, <b>погуля́ть</b>, <b>поболта́ть</b>)","Catch the inceptive <b>за-</b> (a sudden start: <b>запе́ть</b>) and the saturative <b>на-…-ся</b> (to one's fill: <b>наговори́ться</b>)","Read the softening <b>под-/при-</b> (just a touch: <b>поднакопи́ть</b>, <b>приле́чь</b>)"],"learned":["Heard спо́собы де́йствия — the prefix tuning the grain of an action, not its meaning","Felt the delimitative по- (посиде́ть — a cozy little while)","Caught the inceptive за- (запе́ть) and saturative на-…-ся (наговори́ться)","Read the softening под-/при- (приле́чь — to lie down for a bit)"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on yesterday's built words (словообразова́ние), then inside the verb to the finest grain of aspect.","introAudio":"One minute on yesterday's built words, slovoobrazovaniye, then inside the verb to the finest grain of aspect.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's degree prefixes.","q":"«недосоли́ть» vs «пересоли́ть» — the prefixes mean —","opts":["before vs after","too much vs too little","too little vs too much"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>недо-</b> = too little (under-salt), <b>пере-</b> = too much (over-salt). The prefix is the verdict of degree."},{"type":"choice","q":"You meet a word that's in no dictionary. You —","opts":["parse it by its parts","give up on the text","assume it's a typo"],"answer":0,"explain":"The word was likely <b>built</b> in front of you — strip the affixes, find the root, reassemble. Parse, don't panic."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Two days back: «to make a mistake» — the native verb, «… оши́бку» (доп…):","accept":["допустить","допустил","допускать"],"placeholder":"доп…","explain":"<b>допусти́ть оши́бку</b> — the fixed partner. «сде́лать» is the English calque."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The grain of an action","title":"Same verb, same meaning — a prefix tunes the <em>grain</em>","paras":["You've long known perfective and imperfective — whether an action is whole or in progress. C2 hears one layer finer: <b>спо́собы де́йствия</b> (aktionsart — «manners of action»). Here a prefix keeps the core meaning intact and colours only the <i>grain</i>: how long it lasts, how it starts, how much of it, and how the speaker feels about it.","Take <b>сиде́ть</b> (to sit). Add по- and you get <b>посиде́ть</b> — «to sit a little while», usually a pleasant, unhurried one. Nothing about «sitting» changed; what changed is the <i>frame</i> around it: bounded in time, cozy in tone. «Посиде́ли, поговори́ли» is an evening of warmth folded into two verbs.","English carries this in adverbs — «for a bit», «all of a sudden», «to my heart's content». Russian carries it in the prefix, so a native feels the shade instantly and an English ear, translating only the root, walks right past it."],"audio":"You've long known perfective and imperfective — whether an action is whole or in progress. C2 hears one layer finer: sposoby dejstviya, aktionsart, 'manners of action'. Here a prefix keeps the core meaning intact and colours only the grain: how long it lasts, how it starts, how much of it, and how the speaker feels about it. Take sidet, to sit. Add po- and you get posidet — 'to sit a little while', usually a pleasant, unhurried one. Nothing about 'sitting' changed; what changed is the frame around it: bounded in time, cozy in tone. 'Posideli, pogovorili' is an evening of warmth folded into two verbs. English carries this in adverbs — 'for a bit', 'all of a sudden', 'to my heart's content'. Russian carries it in the prefix, so a native feels the shade instantly and an English ear, translating only the root, walks right past it.","table":{"rows":[["сиде́ть → посиде́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сидеть, посидеть\">🔊</button>","to sit → to sit a cozy little while (по-)"],["петь → запе́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"петь, запеть\">🔊</button>","to sing → to burst into song (за-, onset)"],["говори́ть → наговори́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорить, наговориться\">🔊</button>","to talk → to talk one's fill (на-…-ся)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«We sat a while and talked» — the coziness lives in по-.","en":"«Посиде́ли, поговори́ли» — вся теплота́ в приста́вке по-.","say":"Посидели, поговорили — вся теплота в приставке по."},{"ru":"The root says «sit»; по- says «for a nice little while».","en":"Ко́рень говори́т «сиде́ть», а по- добавля́ет «недо́лго и в удово́льствие».","say":"Корень говорит сидеть, а по добавляет недолго и в удовольствие."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Дава́й сиде́ть в кафе́ по́сле рабо́ты» (в смы́сле „немно́го, для удово́льствия“).","right":"«Дава́й посиди́м в кафе́ по́сле рабо́ты».","why":"Bare <b>сиде́ть</b> is open-ended, almost a chore. For «let's sit a nice little while», Russian reaches for the delimitative <b>посиде́ть</b> — по- bounds the time and warms the tone. English would add «for a bit»; Russian just adds по-."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"<b>Спо́собы де́йствия</b> (aktionsart) tune —","opts":["the core meaning of the verb","the grain: duration, onset, degree, attitude","the tense only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The meaning stays put; the prefix tunes the <b>grain</b> — how long, how it starts, how much, and the speaker's feel."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Посиде́ли, поговори́ли» carries, beyond «sat and talked», a sense of —","opts":["anger and hurry","a cozy, unhurried little while","official formality"],"answer":1,"explain":"The delimitative <b>по-</b> caps the time and warms the tone — an easy, cozy, bounded little while."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · A little while","title":"The delimitative <em>по-</em>: bounded, unhurried, often cozy","paras":["The most useful способ действия is the <b>delimitative по-</b>: «to do something for a limited while», and usually a relaxed, pleasant one. <b>погуля́ть</b> (take a little walk), <b>почита́ть</b> (read a while), <b>поболта́ть</b> (have a little chat), <b>поспа́ть</b> (get some sleep), <b>помолча́ть</b> (be quiet for a bit).","The по- does two things at once: it <i>caps the time</i> (not forever, just a while) and it <i>softens the tone</i> (casual, easy, no pressure). «Дава́й погуля́ем» isn't a hike — it's «let's stroll a bit». That gentle, bounded feel is why it fills everyday warmth: «посиди́ со мной», «дава́й поговори́м».","Miss the по- and you lose the coziness. «Гуля́ть» is «to walk / to be out»; <b>погуля́ть</b> is «to go out for a nice little while». Same action, a whole mood in one prefix."],"audio":"The most useful sposob dejstviya is the delimitative po-: 'to do something for a limited while', and usually a relaxed, pleasant one. Pogulyat, take a little walk; pochitat, read a while; poboltat, have a little chat; pospat, get some sleep; pomolchat, be quiet for a bit. The po- does two things at once: it caps the time — not forever, just a while — and it softens the tone — casual, easy, no pressure. 'Davaj pogulyayem' isn't a hike — it's 'let's stroll a bit'. That gentle, bounded feel is why it fills everyday warmth: 'posidi so mnoj', 'davaj pogovorim'. Miss the po- and you lose the coziness. 'Gulyat' is 'to walk, to be out'; pogulyat is 'to go out for a nice little while'. Same action, a whole mood in one prefix.","table":{"rows":[["погуля́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"погулять\">🔊</button>","to take a little (unhurried) walk"],["почита́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"почитать\">🔊</button>","to read for a while"],["поболта́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"поболтать\">🔊</button>","to have a little chat"],["помолча́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"помолчать\">🔊</button>","to stay quiet for a bit"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Let's stroll a bit before dinner.","en":"Дава́й немно́го погуля́ем пе́ред у́жином.","say":"Давай немного погуляем перед ужином."},{"ru":"Sit with me a little while.","en":"Посиди́ со мной немно́жко.","say":"Посиди со мной немножко."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"(предлага́я лёгкую прогу́лку) «Хо́чешь гуля́ть в па́рке?»","right":"(предлага́я лёгкую прогу́лку) «Хо́чешь погуля́ть в па́рке?»","why":"Bare <b>гуля́ть</b> sounds open-ended, even like «to be out on the town». For a light, bounded «shall we take a little walk?», Russian wants the delimitative <b>погуля́ть</b> — the по- makes it short and easy."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The delimitative «погуля́ть» adds, over «гуля́ть», a sense of —","opts":["a bounded, easy little while","an official duty","great physical effort"],"answer":0,"explain":"по- caps the time and softens the tone → <b>погуля́ть</b> is «to stroll a bit», not an open-ended «to be out»."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"One verb for «to have a little chat» (побол…):","accept":["поболтать","поболтаем","поболтали"],"placeholder":"побол…","explain":"<b>поболта́ть</b> — по- makes it a light, bounded, friendly little chat."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Bursting out, and to the full","title":"<em>За-</em> flips the switch on; <em>на-…-ся</em> fills you up","paras":["Two more способы, opposite in feel. The <b>inceptive за-</b> marks the <i>sudden onset</i> of an action — the moment it kicks in. <b>запе́ть</b> (to burst into song), <b>заговори́ть</b> (to start talking, to pipe up), <b>заплака́ть</b> (to break into tears), <b>забе́гать</b> (to start rushing about). The за- is the switch flipping on.","The <b>saturative на-…-ся</b> is the far end: to do something <i>to the full, to your heart's content, until you've had enough</i>. <b>наговори́ться</b> (to talk one's fill), <b>нагуля́ться</b> (to walk until satisfied), <b>наспа́ться</b> (to sleep one's fill), <b>нае́сться</b> (to eat one's fill). The на- piles it up; the -ся turns it back on you: you're sated.","Each is a full English clause in a prefix-frame. «Они́ наконе́ц наговори́лись» = «they finally talked as much as they'd been longing to». The onset, the fill, the satisfaction — all inside the verb."],"audio":"Two more sposoby, opposite in feel. The inceptive za- marks the sudden onset of an action — the moment it kicks in. Zapet, to burst into song; zagovorit, to start talking, to pipe up; zaplakat, to break into tears; zabegat, to start rushing about. The za- is the switch flipping on. The saturative na-…-sya is the far end: to do something to the full, to your heart's content, until you've had enough. Nagovoritsya, to talk one's fill; nagulyatsya, to walk until satisfied; naspatsya, to sleep one's fill; naestsya, to eat one's fill. The na- piles it up; the -sya turns it back on you: you're sated. Each is a full English clause in a prefix-frame. 'Oni nakonets nagovorilis' equals 'they finally talked as much as they'd been longing to'. The onset, the fill, the satisfaction — all inside the verb.","table":{"rows":[["запе́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"запеть\">🔊</button>","to burst into song — за- (sudden onset)"],["заговори́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"заговорить\">🔊</button>","to start talking, to pipe up — за-"],["наговори́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"наговориться\">🔊</button>","to talk one's fill — на-…-ся (to satiety)"],["нагуля́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нагуляться\">🔊</button>","to walk about until fully satisfied"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Suddenly, out of nowhere, she burst into song.","en":"Вдруг, ни с того́ ни с сего́, она́ запе́ла.","say":"Вдруг, ни с того ни с сего, она запела."},{"ru":"The sisters hadn't seen each other for a year and talked their fill till dawn.","en":"Сёстры не ви́делись год и наговори́лись до рассве́та.","say":"Сёстры не виделись год и наговорились до рассвета."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«По́сле до́лгой разлу́ки они́ мно́го говори́ли и наконе́ц останови́лись».","right":"«По́сле до́лгой разлу́ки они́ наконе́ц наговори́лись».","why":"«talked a lot and then stopped» misses the point. The saturative <b>наговори́ться</b> says it in one verb: they talked <i>until fully satisfied</i>, filled up on it. на-…-ся carries the whole «to one's heart's content»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«запе́ть» differs from «петь» by marking —","opts":["singing quietly","singing badly","the sudden start of singing"],"answer":2,"explain":"The inceptive <b>за-</b> is the switch flipping on → <b>запе́ть</b> = to burst into song, to strike it up."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Они́ наговори́лись» means they talked —","opts":["a little","to their heart's content, till satisfied","unwillingly"],"answer":1,"explain":"The saturative <b>на-…-ся</b> means to the full → they talked their fill, until sated."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · A touch more — and the week's close","title":"The softening <em>под-/при-</em>, and the shade you'd have lost","paras":["Gentler still are the <b>softening под-/при-</b>: «a little, a touch, on the side». <b>поднакопи́ть</b> (to save up a bit extra), <b>подзабы́ть</b> (to half-forget), <b>приле́чь</b> (to lie down for a short while), <b>приоткры́ть</b> (to open just a crack), <b>приуста́ть</b> (to get a touch tired). Not the full action — a measured, modest dose of it.","Put the week's shades side by side on one root and you feel the system: <b>поговори́ть</b> (chat a while), <b>заговори́ть</b> (start talking), <b>наговори́ться</b> (talk one's fill), <b>подговори́ть</b> (a different, lexical turn — «to put someone up to it»). The prefix is a dial for time, onset, degree, and mood.","So the C2 question about a verb isn't only «perfective or imperfective?» — that was B2 — but «<b>what does the prefix say about the grain: how long, how it starts, how much, how I feel?</b>» This closes week one: from the exact word to its charge, its company, its build, and now its finest aspect. Tomorrow, the last shade of all — the tiny particles (части́цы) that colour a whole sentence."],"audio":"Gentler still are the softening pod- and pri-: 'a little, a touch, on the side'. Podnakopit, to save up a bit extra; podzabyt, to half-forget; prilech, to lie down for a short while; priotkryt, to open just a crack; priustat, to get a touch tired. Not the full action — a measured, modest dose of it. Put the week's shades side by side on one root and you feel the system: pogovorit, chat a while; zagovorit, start talking; nagovoritsya, talk one's fill; podgovorit, a different, lexical turn, 'to put someone up to it'. The prefix is a dial for time, onset, degree, and mood. So the C2 question about a verb isn't only 'perfective or imperfective?' — that was B2 — but 'what does the prefix say about the grain: how long, how it starts, how much, how I feel?' This closes week one: from the exact word to its charge, its company, its build, and now its finest aspect. Tomorrow, the last shade of all — the tiny particles, chastitsy, that colour a whole sentence.","examples":[{"ru":"I'll lie down for a little while before the road.","en":"Я приля́гу ненадо́лго пе́ред доро́гой.","say":"Я прилягу ненадолго перед дорогой."},{"ru":"Open the window just a crack — it's stuffy.","en":"Приоткро́й окно́ — ду́шно.","say":"Приоткрой окно — душно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Пе́ред у́жином я хочу́ лечь на дива́н на де́сять мину́т».","right":"«Пе́ред у́жином я хочу́ приле́чь на дива́н на де́сять мину́т».","why":"«лечь» is to lie down fully, to sleep; for a brief, light «lie down for a bit», Russian softens it with при-: <b>приле́чь</b>. The prefix measures out a small, cozy dose of the action."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The softening «приле́чь» means to lie down —","opts":["for good, to sleep the night","for a short, light while","and refuse to get up"],"answer":1,"explain":"при- measures out a small dose → <b>приле́чь</b> is to lie down briefly, for a bit — not to bed down for the night."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it warmly and unhurriedly.","target":"Дава́й посиди́м, поговори́м — сто лет не ви́делись!","sub":"let «посиди́м, поговори́м» sound cozy and easy — the по- is doing the warm, bounded work","want":"давай посидим поговорим сто лет не виделись"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м, the engineer, prides himself on precision, so Эмма teases him with the one place Russian is precise in a way his diagrams can't be — the shade inside по-. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты написа́ла «посиде́ли и поговори́ли». Почему́ не про́сто «сиде́ли и говори́ли»?","ru":"You wrote «we sat a while and talked». Why not just «sat and talked»?"},{"who":"A","en":"Потому́ что э́то ра́зные ве́щи. «Сиде́ли» — про́сто факт. «Посиде́ли» — ую́тно, недо́лго, в удово́льствие.","ru":"Because they're different things. «Сиде́ли» is a plain fact. «Посиде́ли» is cozy, brief, for pleasure."},{"who":"B","en":"И всё э́то — в двух бу́квах «по»?","ru":"And all of that is in the two letters «по»?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Приста́вка не меня́ет де́йствие — она́ задаёт его́ отте́нок.","ru":"Exactly. The prefix doesn't change the action — it sets its shade."},{"who":"B","en":"Как до́пуск на черте́же: разме́р тот же, а поса́дка друга́я.","ru":"Like a tolerance on a drawing: same dimension, different fit."},{"who":"A","en":"Лу́чше не ска́жешь. «Запе́л» — включи́лось. «Наговори́лись» — напо́лнились до краёв.","ru":"Couldn't put it better. «Запе́л» — it switched on. «Наговори́лись» — filled up to the brim."},{"who":"B","en":"То есть англича́нин ска́жет це́лую фра́зу, а ты — оди́н глаго́л.","ru":"So an Englishman says a whole phrase, and you — one verb."},{"who":"A","en":"И слы́шу отте́нок сра́зу. Вот э́то и есть то́нчайший вид.","ru":"And I hear the shade at once. That's the finest grain of aspect."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the finest grain: the machinery of aktionsart and the verbs that carry each shade. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"вид","ru":"aspect (of a verb)","ex":"У ру́сского глаго́ла два ви́да."},{"en":"спо́соб де́йствия","ru":"aktionsart (the manner of an action)","ex":"У ка́ждой приста́вки свой спо́соб де́йствия."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"a shade, a nuance","ex":"Приста́вка даёт то́нкий отте́нок."},{"en":"погуля́ть","ru":"to take a little walk","ex":"Пойдём погуля́ем в па́рке."},{"en":"посиде́ть","ru":"to sit a (cozy little) while","ex":"Дава́й посиди́м немно́го."},{"en":"поболта́ть","ru":"to have a little chat","ex":"Мы люби́ли поболта́ть за ча́ем."},{"en":"запе́ть","ru":"to burst into song (sudden onset)","ex":"Она́ вдруг запе́ла."},{"en":"заговори́ть","ru":"to start talking, to pipe up","ex":"Малы́ш то́лько-то́лько заговори́л."},{"en":"наговори́ться","ru":"to talk one's fill (to satiety)","ex":"Друзья́ наконе́ц наговори́лись."},{"en":"нагуля́ться","ru":"to walk about until satisfied","ex":"Де́ти нагуля́лись и усну́ли."},{"en":"приле́чь","ru":"to lie down for a short while","ex":"Я приля́гу на полчаса́."},{"en":"поднакопи́ть","ru":"to save up a bit extra","ex":"На́до поднакопи́ть де́нег."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each verb to the shade its prefix adds — the grain, not the meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each verb on the left onto the shade it carries — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["посиде́ть","for a cozy, bounded little while (по-)"],["запе́ть","the sudden start of an action (за-)"],["наговори́ться","to one's heart's content, till full (на-…-ся)"],["приле́чь","a small, light dose of it (при-)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"<b>Спо́собы де́йствия</b> (aktionsart) tune —","opts":["the verb's core meaning","the grain: duration, onset, degree, mood","the spelling"],"answer":1},{"q":"«посиде́ть» (vs «сиде́ть») adds a sense of —","opts":["a cozy, bounded little while","great effort","anger"],"answer":0},{"q":"The saturative «наговори́ться» means to talk —","opts":["a little","to one's heart's content, till full","never"],"answer":1},{"q":"The inceptive «за-» in «запе́ть» marks —","opts":["the end of singing","the sudden start of it","quiet singing"],"answer":1},{"q":"The softening «приле́чь» is to lie down —","opts":["forever","for a short, light while","standing up"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"One evening, one root — «говори́ть» — bent through the day's shades. Read it aloud and hear each prefix set the grain.","title":"Оди́н ко́рень, ты́сяча отте́нков","sentences":["Го́сти пришли́, се́ли за стол и снача́ла ти́хо посиде́ли.","Пото́м кто́-то пошути́л — и все разо́м заговори́ли.","Сёстры, не ви́девшиеся год, наговори́лись за весь пропу́щенный год.","К полу́ночи ба́бушка приле́гла на дива́н отдохну́ть.","Оди́н глаго́л — а ско́лько отте́нков вре́мени, поры́ва и ме́ры в ка́ждой приста́вке."],"translation":"The guests arrived, sat down at the table and at first sat quietly a while. Then someone made a joke — and all at once everyone started talking. The sisters, who hadn't seen each other in a year, talked their fill for the whole year they'd missed. Toward midnight grandmother lay down on the sofa for a little rest. One verb — yet how many shades of time, of impulse, and of measure live in each prefix."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to tune the grain.","prompt":"Describe a small get-together in 3–4 Russian sentences, and let the verbs carry the day's shades: a delimitative <b>по-</b> (посиде́ли, поговори́ли, погуля́ли), an inceptive <b>за-</b> (запе́ли, заговори́ли), and a saturative <b>на-…-ся</b> (наговори́лись, нагуля́лись). Then take one, swap in the bare verb (говори́ли instead of наговори́лись), and say in a word or two what shade you lost.","hint":"Ask what the prefix says about the grain: how long, how it starts, how much. The bare verb states the fact; the prefix paints the mood 🙂","example":"Ве́чером мы посиде́ли на ку́хне и споко́йно поговори́ли о жи́зни. Пото́м Анто́н вдруг запе́л, и мы наговори́лись так, как не говори́ли уже́ год. Скажи́ про́сто «говори́ли» — и пропадёт са́мое ва́жное: что мы напо́лнились э́тим ве́чером до краёв, наговори́лись всласть."}},"6":{"day":6,"week":"01","themeRu":"Particles for the last shade","themeEn":"Части́цы для после́днего отте́нка","intro":"Yesterday one prefix — <b>по-</b>, <b>за-</b> — folded a whole shade of action into a single verb. Today the shade rides even lighter: it sits on one unstressed particle you could almost skip. <b>Уж</b>, <b>-та́ки</b>, <b>аж</b> — drop them and every sentence is still correct, still perfectly fine. Keep them, and the sentence suddenly has an attitude, a breath, a native's fingerprint. This is the top shelf of Russian particles — the last shade — and the whole art is the <b>dose</b>.","introAudio":"Yesterday one prefix — 'po-', 'za-' — folded a whole shade of action into a single verb. Today the shade rides even lighter: it sits on one unstressed particle you could almost skip. 'Uzh', '-taki', 'azh' — drop them and every sentence is still correct, still perfectly fine. Keep them, and the sentence suddenly has an attitude, a breath, a native's fingerprint. This is the top shelf of Russian particles — the last shade — and the whole art is the dose.","goals":["Add the top-shelf particles that turn a correct sentence into a native one: <b>уж · -та́ки · аж · небо́сь</b>","Feel what each carries: <b>уж</b> softens or presses, <b>-та́ки</b> means «after all, despite it», <b>аж</b> marks a jolt","Reach for the folk-guess <b>небо́сь / чай</b> («I bet you're…») and the emphatic <b>во́все не</b>","Learn the dose: one particle breathes, three make a caricature"],"learned":["Added the top-shelf particles that turn a correct sentence native (уж / -та́ки / аж / небо́сь)","Felt what each carries — уж presses, -та́ки means «after all», аж marks a jolt","Used the folk-guess небо́сь / чай and the emphatic во́все не","Learned the dose: one particle breathes, three make a caricature"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on this week before the last new shade. Yesterday's finest grain of aspect, and the collocations from Wednesday — hold them, then we add the particle layer.","introAudio":"One minute back on this week before the last new shade. Yesterday's finest grain of aspect, and the collocations from Wednesday — hold them, then we add the particle layer.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's finest grain of aspect.","q":"«Мы сла́вно … часо́к в саду́ и разошли́сь.» — the delimitative «for a pleasant little while» is —","opts":["засиде́лись","посиде́ли","насиде́лись"],"answer":1,"explain":"Delimitative <b>по-</b> = did it a little, and pleasantly → <b>посиде́ли</b>. засиде́лись = stayed too long; насиде́лись = sat to the point of «enough». One prefix, a whole shade."},{"type":"choice","q":"Wednesday's collocations: «to have an influence» is —","opts":["сде́лать влия́ние","оказа́ть влия́ние","соверши́ть влия́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"The word that keeps company with влия́ние is <b>оказа́ть</b> — «оказа́ть влия́ние». Word-for-word «сде́лать/соверши́ть влия́ние» breaks the collocation and reads foreign."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The delimitative verb for «to walk a little, for pleasure» (a stroll) — one word, from гуля́ть. Type it:","accept":["погулять","погуляю","погуляем","погуляли"],"placeholder":"погу…","explain":"<b>погуля́ть</b> — to walk a little while, for the pleasure of it. The delimitative по- carries the whole «a bit, and nicely»."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The last shade","title":"A correct sentence — and then its <em>breath</em>","paras":["You already own the workhorse particles — <b>же, ведь, -то, мол</b> — from C1. They point, they remind, they mark reported speech. Today's set does something lighter and harder to name: it lays a thin film of <b>attitude</b> over a sentence that's already complete. «Он пришёл» is a fact. «Он-та́ки пришёл» is the same fact, plus a little sigh of «after everything».","These are the particles a native drops in without thinking and a foreigner leaves out without noticing — which is exactly why leaving them out reads as a faint accent. Nothing is <i>wrong</i>; the sentence is just a shade flatter than a native would let it be. This is pure C2: not correctness, not even register, but the last film of feeling.","One warning up front, because it is the whole craft: these particles work in <b>tiny doses</b>. One «уж» or «-та́ки» breathes; three in a row turn a sentence into a caricature of a folksy grandmother. The native gift isn't knowing them — it's knowing when <i>not</i> to reach for one."],"audio":"You already own the workhorse particles — 'zhe', 'ved', '-to', 'mol' — from C1. They point, they remind, they mark reported speech. Today's set does something lighter and harder to name: it lays a thin film of attitude over a sentence that's already complete. 'On prishyol', he came, is a fact. 'On-taki prishyol' is the same fact, plus a little sigh of 'after everything'. These are the particles a native drops in without thinking and a foreigner leaves out without noticing — which is exactly why leaving them out reads as a faint accent. Nothing is wrong; the sentence is just a shade flatter than a native would let it be. This is pure C2: not correctness, not even register, but the last film of feeling. One warning up front, because it is the whole craft: these particles work in tiny doses. One 'uzh' or '-taki' breathes; three in a row turn a sentence into a caricature of a folksy grandmother. The native gift isn't knowing them — it's knowing when not to reach for one.","table":{"rows":[["<b>же</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я же говорил\">🔊</button>","C1: emphasis, insistence — «Я же говори́л» (I <i>told</i> you)"],["<b>ведь</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты ведь обещал\">🔊</button>","C1: «after all, you know» — «Ты ведь обеща́л»"],["<b>уж</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уж прости\">🔊</button>","C2: softens or presses — «Уж прости́» (do forgive me)"],["<b>-та́ки</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"успел-таки\">🔊</button>","C2: «after all, despite it» — «Успе́л-та́ки»"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"He did come, after all — for all the doubt.","en":"Он-та́ки пришёл — вопреки́ всем сомне́ниям.","say":"Он-таки пришёл — вопреки всем сомнениям."},{"ru":"Do forgive me — I honestly forgot.","en":"Уж прости́ — я и впра́вду забы́л.","say":"Уж прости — я и вправду забыл."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он уж-та́ки аж всё-та́ки пришёл небо́сь».","right":"«Он-та́ки пришёл».","why":"Piling particle on particle is the classic overcorrection. One «-та́ки» carries the whole shade of «after all»; stacking «уж», «аж», «небо́сь» on top turns a real sentence into a parody. The C2 skill is the single, well-placed particle — and the silence around it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Compared with C1's же / ведь, today's particles mainly add —","opts":["a new grammatical case","a thin film of attitude over a complete sentence","a formal, official tone"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sentence is already complete and correct. These particles lay a last film of <b>attitude</b> on top — a sigh, a jolt, a guess."},{"type":"choice","q":"The native skill with these particles is mostly —","opts":["the dose — one breathes, three is a caricature","using as many as possible for colour","always placing them first in the sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"One well-placed particle sounds native; a handful sounds like a parody. The gift is knowing when <i>not</i> to add one."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · «Уж» and «-та́ки»","title":"The <em>after-all</em> layer: pressed, and hard-won","paras":["<b>Уж</b> is a leaner — it presses on the word beside it. Before an imperative it softens into a resigned plea: «Уж прости́» (do forgive me, come on). Before an adjective it intensifies: «уж о́чень стра́нно» (awfully strange). And «уж не зна́ю» is «honestly, I'm not sure» — a shrug in one syllable. It is not the time-word «уже́» (already); it's the toneless «уж».","<b>-та́ки</b> — always hyphenated onto a verb or adverb — carries «after all, in the end, despite everything». «Дошёл-та́ки» = he got there, and you can hear the struggle behind it. «Всё-та́ки» = «still, all the same», the everyday one you already half-know. The shade is: expectation said no, reality said yes.","Put them where the feeling lives. «Уж» leans on the word you most mean; «-та́ки» clings to the verb that finally happened. Miss them and the sentence still parses — it just loses the sigh."],"audio":"'Uzh' is a leaner — it presses on the word beside it. Before an imperative it softens into a resigned plea: 'uzh prosti', do forgive me. Before an adjective it intensifies: 'uzh ochen stranno', awfully strange. And 'uzh ne znayu' is 'honestly, I'm not sure' — a shrug in one syllable. It is not the time-word 'uzhe', already; it's the toneless 'uzh'. '-taki' — always hyphenated onto a verb or adverb — carries 'after all, in the end, despite everything'. 'Doshyol-taki' is he got there, and you can hear the struggle behind it. 'Vsyo-taki' is 'still, all the same', the everyday one you already half-know. The shade is: expectation said no, reality said yes. Put them where the feeling lives. 'Uzh' leans on the word you most mean; '-taki' clings to the verb that finally happened. Miss them and the sentence still parses — it just loses the sigh.","table":{"rows":[["уж прости́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уж прости\">🔊</button>","do forgive me — softened, resigned plea"],["уж о́чень <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уж очень\">🔊</button>","awfully, really — intensifies what follows"],["дошёл-та́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дошёл-таки\">🔊</button>","got there after all — despite the struggle"],["всё-та́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"всё-таки\">🔊</button>","still, all the same — the everyday one"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"He was late after all — the traffic won.","en":"Он опозда́л-та́ки — про́бки победи́ли.","say":"Он опоздал-таки — пробки победили."},{"ru":"Honestly, I don't know why she said that.","en":"Уж не зна́ю, заче́м она́ так сказа́ла.","say":"Уж не знаю, зачем она так сказала."},{"ru":"Do forgive me, I got it wrong.","en":"Уж прости́, я оши́бся.","say":"Уж прости, я ошибся."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он пришёл уже́, несмотря́ на всё».","right":"«Он-та́ки пришёл, несмотря́ на всё».","why":"«уже́» is the time-adverb «already» — a fact about the clock. The shade you want here is «after all, despite everything», and that's the toneless particle «-та́ки» hung on the verb: «пришёл-та́ки» / «Он-та́ки пришёл». Nearly the same letters, a different word."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Доро́га была́ тру́дной, но он … добра́лся.» — which carries «after all, despite it»?","opts":["уже́ добра́лся","добра́лся-та́ки","то́лько добра́лся"],"answer":1,"explain":"«after all, despite the hard road» → <b>добра́лся-та́ки</b>. «уже́ добра́лся» is just «already»; «то́лько добра́лся» is «only just now»."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle (2 letters). Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The particle that softens an imperative into a resigned plea — «… прости́» (do forgive me):","accept":["уж"],"placeholder":"у…","explain":"<b>уж</b> — it presses gently on «прости́», turning a bare command into a sigh. Not «уже́» (already)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · «Аж», «эк», «то́-то»","title":"The <em>jolt</em>: a reaction in one syllable","paras":["<b>Аж</b> marks a jolt — a reaction so strong it surprises even the speaker. «Он аж подпры́гнул» (he actually jumped). «Аж дух захвати́ло» (it took your breath away, that much). It reaches for the far edge of a feeling: even to <i>that</i> point. Purely colloquial, warm, alive — never in a report.","<b>Эк</b> is an exclamation of surprise, often with a raised eyebrow: «Эк тебя́ разобра́ло!» (my, you're worked up!), «Эк куда́ хвати́л!» (well, that's a stretch!). It's folksy and a touch archaic — a spice used knowingly. <b>То́-то</b> is the «aha» particle: «То́-то он молчи́т» (no wonder he's quiet), «То́-то же!» (there you go — told you!).","All three live in the mouth, not on the page. They're how a native lets a sentence <i>react</i> instead of merely reporting. Read «Он аж подпры́гнул» aloud and you can hear the jump — that is the whole point of them."],"audio":"'Azh' marks a jolt — a reaction so strong it surprises even the speaker. 'On azh podprygnul', he actually jumped. 'Azh dukh zakhvatilo', it took your breath away, that much. It reaches for the far edge of a feeling: even to that point. Purely colloquial, warm, alive — never in a report. 'Ek' is an exclamation of surprise, often with a raised eyebrow: 'Ek tebya razobralo!', my, you're worked up! 'Ek kuda khvatil!', well, that's a stretch! It's folksy and a touch archaic — a spice used knowingly. 'To-to' is the 'aha' particle: 'To-to on molchit', no wonder he's quiet; 'To-to zhe!', there you go, told you. All three live in the mouth, not on the page. They're how a native lets a sentence react instead of merely reporting. Read 'On azh podprygnul' aloud and you can hear the jump — that is the whole point of them.","table":{"rows":[["аж <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он аж подпрыгнул\">🔊</button>","even to that point — marks a jolt / extreme reaction"],["эк <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"эк тебя разобрало\">🔊</button>","my!, well! — folksy exclamation of surprise"],["то́-то <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"то-то он молчит\">🔊</button>","aha, no wonder — confirms a guess"],["то́-то же <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"то-то же\">🔊</button>","there you go! — told you so"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"He was so startled he actually jumped.","en":"Он аж подпры́гнул от неожи́данности.","say":"Он аж подпрыгнул от неожиданности."},{"ru":"No wonder he's silent — he lost.","en":"То́-то он молчи́т — проигра́л.","say":"То-то он молчит — проиграл."},{"ru":"My, what an imagination you've got going!","en":"Эк у тебя́ фанта́зия разыгра́лась!","say":"Эк у тебя фантазия разыгралась!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Заседа́ние аж затяну́лось до ве́чера, о чём соста́влен протоко́л».","right":"«Заседа́ние затяну́лось до ве́чера».","why":"«аж» is warm, spoken colouring — a jolt of feeling — and it clashes with the dry official sentence around it. In a protocol you drop it entirely. Register still rules: a C2 particle in the wrong style is as off as slang in a report."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Она́ … всплесну́ла рука́ми от удивле́ния.» — which particle fits the jolt?","opts":["мол","аж","ведь"],"answer":1,"explain":"A sudden strong reaction → <b>аж</b> («even to that point»). мол marks reported speech; ведь is «after all, you know» — neither is a jolt."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and let «То́-то же!» land like a small triumph.","target":"То́-то же! Я так и знал.","sub":"«То́-то же!» is «there you go — told you so»; say it with a little grin","want":"то-то же я так и знал"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · «Небо́сь», «во́все» — and the dose","title":"The <em>I-bet</em> guess, flat negation — and when to stop","paras":["<b>Небо́сь</b> and its folksier twin <b>чай</b> mean «I bet, I suppose, probably» — a warm guess about someone: «Небо́сь проголода́лся?» (bet you're hungry?), «Ты, чай, уста́л?» (you're tired, I reckon?). They carry familiarity, a village kindness. Here «чай» has nothing to do with tea — it's the guessing particle.","<b>Во́все</b> sharpens a negation: «во́все не тру́дно» (not hard at all), «во́все нет» (not in the least). It's the emphatic «at all», neutral enough for most speech. And the rare, folksy <b>ан</b> flips an expectation: «Ду́мал — легко́, ан нет» (thought it'd be easy — but no).","So the discipline, one last time, is the dose: one particle, placed where the feeling lives, sounds native; a handful sounds like a parody. Tomorrow is a review — you'll gather the whole week, from the one exact word to these last-shade particles. Then week two turns from single words to whole meanings left <i>unsaid</i>: subtext, irony, the pun. The particles were the training ground — a shade carried by something you could almost skip."],"audio":"'Nebos' and its folksier twin 'chai' mean 'I bet, I suppose, probably' — a warm guess about someone: 'Nebos progolodalsya?', bet you're hungry? 'Ty, chai, ustal?', you're tired, I reckon? They carry familiarity, a village kindness. Here 'chai' has nothing to do with tea — it's the guessing particle. 'Vovse' sharpens a negation: 'vovse ne trudno', not hard at all; 'vovse net', not in the least. It's the emphatic 'at all', neutral enough for most speech. And the rare, folksy 'an' flips an expectation: 'Dumal — legko, an net', thought it'd be easy, but no. So the discipline, one last time, is the dose: one particle, placed where the feeling lives, sounds native; a handful sounds like a parody. Tomorrow is a review — you'll gather the whole week, from the one exact word to these last-shade particles. Then week two turns from single words to whole meanings left unsaid: subtext, irony, the pun. The particles were the training ground — a shade carried by something you could almost skip.","table":{"rows":[["небо́сь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"небось проголодался\">🔊</button>","I bet, I suppose — a warm guess"],["чай <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты чай устал\">🔊</button>","I reckon (not the drink!) — folk guess"],["во́все не <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вовсе не трудно\">🔊</button>","not at all — emphatic negation"],["ан нет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"думал легко ан нет\">🔊</button>","but no — flips an expectation"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Bet you're hungry after the road?","en":"Небо́сь проголода́лся с доро́ги?","say":"Небось проголодался с дороги?"},{"ru":"It's not hard at all, honestly.","en":"Э́то во́все не тру́дно, че́стно.","say":"Это вовсе не трудно, честно."},{"ru":"Thought it'd be simple — but no.","en":"Ду́мал, бу́дет про́сто, — ан нет.","say":"Думал, будет просто, — ан нет."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Небо́сь во́все аж уста́л, чай?».","right":"«Уста́л, небо́сь?».","why":"Four guessing and intensifying particles in one short question is the overcorrection again — it reads as a parody of folk speech. One «небо́сь» does the whole job: «Уста́л, небо́сь?». The mark of C2 is subtraction, not addition."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«…, проголода́лся с доро́ги?» — which single particle makes it a warm «I bet you're…» guess?","opts":["Ан","Небо́сь","Во́все"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Небо́сь</b> = «I bet, I suppose» — a kindly guess. Ан flips an expectation; во́все sharpens a negation — neither guesses."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle (5 letters). Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The emphatic particle for «not … at all» — «… не тру́дно» (not hard at all):","accept":["вовсе"],"placeholder":"во…","explain":"<b>во́все не</b> = not at all — it sharpens the negation past a plain «не»."},{"type":"choice","q":"The real C2 skill with all these particles is —","opts":["using several together for extra flavour","the dose — one, placed where the feeling lives","reserving them for official writing"],"answer":1,"explain":"One particle, exactly placed, sounds native. A handful sounds like a caricature. The question is «does this sentence want one at all — and which single one?»"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма reads a line of her translation aloud to Ива́н; the English is flat, and she's hunting the particle that will make the Russian breathe. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Слу́шай: «Он пришёл». Пра́вильно — но пу́сто. Чего́-то не хвата́ет.","ru":"Listen: “He came.” Correct — but empty. Something's missing."},{"who":"B","en":"А что в оригина́ле? Он до́лго не мог — и всё же пришёл?","ru":"What's in the original? He couldn't for a long time — and yet came?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Он до́лго не реша́лся — и пришёл вопреки́ всему́.","ru":"Exactly. He hesitated for a long time — and came in spite of everything."},{"who":"B","en":"Тогда́ не «пришёл», а «пришёл-та́ки». Оди́н слог — и слы́шно всю борьбу́.","ru":"Then not “came” but “came after all”. One syllable — and you hear the whole struggle."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот! «-та́ки» несёт то, чего́ нет в го́лом глаго́ле. А англича́нин его́ про́сто не поста́вит.","ru":"There! “-taki” carries what the bare verb doesn't. And an English speaker just wouldn't add it."},{"who":"B","en":"То́лько не переборщи́. Поста́вишь три части́цы — вы́йдет паро́дия.","ru":"Just don't overdo it. Put in three particles — and you'll get a parody."},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́ю. Одна́, на своём ме́сте. В э́том вся хи́трость.","ru":"I know. One, in its place. That's the whole trick."},{"who":"B","en":"Уж э́то ты сказа́ла по-на́шему.","ru":"Now <i>that</i> you said like one of us."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the last shade: the particle family, plus the words to talk about them. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"уж","ru":"do, really — softening / pressing particle","ex":"Уж прости́, я забы́л."},{"en":"-та́ки","ru":"after all, in the end","ex":"Он дошёл-та́ки до це́ли."},{"en":"аж","ru":"even to that point (marks a jolt)","ex":"Он аж подпры́гнул."},{"en":"эк","ru":"my!, well! — folksy surprise","ex":"Эк тебя́ разобра́ло!"},{"en":"то́-то","ru":"aha, no wonder; there you go","ex":"То́-то он молчи́т."},{"en":"небо́сь","ru":"I bet, I suppose","ex":"Небо́сь уста́л с доро́ги?"},{"en":"чай","ru":"I reckon (here — not «tea»!)","ex":"Ты, чай, проголода́лся?"},{"en":"во́все","ru":"at all (emphatic negation)","ex":"Э́то во́все не тру́дно."},{"en":"ан","ru":"but no — flips an expectation","ex":"Ду́мал легко́, ан нет."},{"en":"части́ца","ru":"a particle (grammatical word)","ex":"Одна́ части́ца ме́няет тон."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"a shade, a nuance","ex":"Части́ца несёт то́нкий отте́нок."},{"en":"переборщи́ть","ru":"to overdo it, go too far","ex":"С части́цами легко́ переборщи́ть."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each last-shade particle to what it carries — the feeling, not the dictionary meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each particle on the left onto its shade — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["-та́ки","after all, despite everything — the verb finally happened"],["аж","a jolt — a reaction so strong it surprises you"],["небо́сь","I bet, I suppose — a warm guess about someone"],["то́-то","aha, no wonder — confirms what you suspected"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Compared with C1's же / ведь, today's last-shade particles mainly add —","opts":["a required grammatical case","an official, formal tone","a thin film of attitude to a complete sentence"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Доро́га была́ тру́дной, но он … добра́лся.» — which carries «after all, despite it»?","opts":["уже́ добра́лся","добра́лся-та́ки","то́лько добра́лся"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which particle marks a jolt — a reaction so strong it surprises the speaker?","opts":["аж","уж","ан"],"answer":0},{"q":"«…, проголода́лся с доро́ги?» — the warm «I bet you're…» guess is —","opts":["Во́все","Небо́сь","Уж"],"answer":1},{"q":"The real C2 skill with these particles is —","opts":["stacking several together for flavour","the dose — one, placed where the feeling lives","keeping them for official documents"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"The same plain fact, said six ways — and the one syllable that makes it breathe. Read each aloud and feel the shade land.","title":"Оди́н слог — и фра́за ды́шит","sentences":["«Он пришёл» — су́хо и то́чно, но без дыха́ния.","«Он-та́ки пришёл» — и за глаго́лом слы́шно всю борьбу́ и до́лгое ожида́ние.","«Уж прости́» — не прика́з, а ти́хая, уста́лая про́сьба.","Носи́тель роня́ет таку́ю части́цу не ду́мая — и и́менно её иностра́нец забыва́ет поста́вить.","Но три части́цы в одно́й фра́зе — уже́ не речь, а её паро́дия.","Весь секре́т в ме́ре: одна́ части́ца ды́шит, а це́лая горсть — смеши́т."],"translation":"“He came” — dry and exact, but without breath. “He came after all” — and behind the verb you hear the whole struggle and the long wait. “Do forgive me” — not an order but a quiet, tired request. A native drops such a particle without thinking — and it's exactly the one a foreigner forgets to add. But three particles in one phrase are no longer speech, but its parody. The whole secret is in the measure: one particle breathes, a whole handful makes you laugh."},"essay":{"intro":"Your ear for the last shade.","prompt":"Take one plain, correct sentence — «Он пришёл», «Э́то не тру́дно», «Ты уста́л?» — and rewrite it TWICE, each time adding exactly ONE last-shade particle (уж, -та́ки, аж, небо́сь, во́все, то́-то). After each, name in a word or two the shade it adds — a sigh, a jolt, a warm guess. Then write ONE deliberately over-salted version (three particles at once) and say why it fails. 4–5 sentences total.","hint":"The C2 test isn't «do I know the particle» — it's «does this sentence want one at all, and if so, which single one?» One breathes; three make a caricature 🙂","example":"Нейтра́льно: Он пришёл. С отте́нком: Он-та́ки пришёл — слы́шно, как до́лго не реша́лся. Ина́че: Уж он-то придёт — в го́лосе увере́нность. А вот перебо́р: Он уж аж та́ки небо́сь пришёл — здесь части́цы деру́тся, и фра́за преврати́лась в паро́дию. Одна́ части́ца на своём ме́сте — и до́вольно."}},"7":{"day":7,"week":"01","themeRu":"Review 1","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 1","intro":"Six days, one idea seen from six sides: at C2 a word isn't just correct, or even appropriate — it's <b>exact</b>, and it carries a shade beyond the dictionary. This week you chased that shade through the one true synonym, the hidden charge a word holds, the words that keep company, the whole clause folded into a single verb, the finest grain of aspect, and the last-shade particles. Today nothing new — just the whole week in your hand, and a longer quiz to prove the ear is set.","introAudio":"Six days, one idea seen from six sides: at C2 a word isn't just correct, or even appropriate — it's exact, and it carries a shade beyond the dictionary. This week you chased that shade through the one true synonym, the hidden charge a word holds, the words that keep company, the whole clause folded into a single verb, the finest grain of aspect, and the last-shade particles. Today nothing new — just the whole week in your hand, and a longer quiz to prove the ear is set.","goals":["Retrieve the C2 leap: from <b>correct</b> to <b>appropriate</b> to the <b>one exact</b> word — and its connotation","Re-feel collocations (<b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b>) and the clause packed into one word (<b>зачита́ться</b>)","Recall the finest grain of aspect (<b>посиде́ть</b>, <b>запе́ть</b>) and the last-shade particles (<b>уж</b>, <b>-та́ки</b>)","Prove the ear in a longer, eight-question review"],"learned":["Retrieved the leap from correct to appropriate to the one exact word — with its connotation","Re-felt collocations (оказа́ть влия́ние) and the clause packed into one verb (зачита́ться)","Recalled the finest grain of aspect (посиде́ть, запе́ть) and the last-shade particles","Proved the ear across a longer, eight-question review"],"review":{"intro":"The morning minute, backward over the week — the exact verb, the charged word, the coined one. Then three rules gather it all.","introAudio":"The morning minute, backward over the week — the exact verb, the charged word, the coined one. Then three rules gather it all.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Monday's exact verb of walking.","q":"«Солда́ты … в но́гу по пло́щади.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["плели́сь","шага́ли","семени́ли"],"answer":1,"explain":"Big, purposeful strides in step → <b>шага́ли</b> (шага́ть). плели́сь is weary trudging; семени́ли is tiny quick steps."},{"type":"choice","q":"Tuesday's connotation: which verb quietly adds solemnity — «to recount» — where «сказа́ть» is neutral?","opts":["пове́дать","пробормота́ть","ля́пнуть"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>пове́дать</b> carries a lofty, solemn charge beyond «сказа́ть». пробормота́ть is «to mutter»; ля́пнуть is «to blurt» — different charges entirely."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Thursday's coinage: one verb for «to put too little salt in» (prefix недо- = not enough). Type it:","accept":["недосолить","недосолил"],"placeholder":"недо…","explain":"<b>недосоли́ть</b> — to under-salt. The prefix недо- folds «not enough» into the verb; English needs a whole phrase."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Review · Precision & charge","title":"The exact word — and what it <em>carries</em>","paras":["Monday's ladder: <b>ве́рно</b> (correct — the grammar works), <b>уме́стно</b> (appropriate — it fits the room, that was C1), <b>то́чно</b> (exact — the one word that carries the right shade, C2). Ten verbs all mean «to walk»; only <b>плести́сь</b> carries «barely alive with fatigue». Ten nouns all mean «error»; only <b>про́мах</b> is a wrong move of judgment.","Tuesday added the second layer: <b>connotation</b>. A word can smuggle in a verdict. <b>Пресло́вутый</b> isn't «famous» — it's «notorious, that so-called…», a sneer built in. <b>Пове́дать</b> isn't «told» — it's solemn, lofty, where <b>рассказа́ть</b> is flat. Pick the charged word in a neutral place and you leak an opinion you never meant.","So the standing C2 question, restated: not «is it correct» (all the options are), not «is it appropriate» (they share a register), but <b>«what does this word carry beyond the dictionary — and is that my meaning?»</b>"],"audio":"Monday's ladder: 'verno', correct — the grammar works; 'umestno', appropriate — it fits the room, that was C1; 'tochno', exact — the one word that carries the right shade, C2. Ten verbs all mean 'to walk'; only 'plestis' carries 'barely alive with fatigue'. Ten nouns all mean 'error'; only 'promakh' is a wrong move of judgment. Tuesday added the second layer: connotation. A word can smuggle in a verdict. 'Preslovutyi' isn't 'famous' — it's 'notorious, that so-called', a sneer built in. 'Povedat' isn't 'told' — it's solemn, lofty, where 'rasskazat' is flat. Pick the charged word in a neutral place and you leak an opinion you never meant. So the standing C2 question, restated: not 'is it correct' — all the options are; not 'is it appropriate' — they share a register; but 'what does this word carry beyond the dictionary, and is that my meaning?'","table":{"rows":[["то́чно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"точно\">🔊</button>","exact — the one word carrying the right shade (day 1)"],["плести́сь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"плестись\">🔊</button>","to trudge — «walk» + exhaustion, a shade (day 1)"],["пресло́вутый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пресловутый\">🔊</button>","notorious, so-called — a sneer built in (day 2)"],["пове́дать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"поведать\">🔊</button>","to recount — lofty, solemn vs neutral рассказа́ть (day 2)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The dictionary calls them synonyms; the ear hears ten different words.","en":"Слова́рь зовёт их сино́нимами — у́хо слы́шит де́сять ра́зных слов.","say":"Словарь зовёт их синонимами — ухо слышит десять разных слов."},{"ru":"«Recounted» isn't just «said» — it carries solemnity.","en":"«Пове́дал» — не про́сто «сказа́л»: в нём торже́ственность.","say":"Поведал — не просто сказал: в нём торжественность."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Дире́ктор пове́дал нам, где буфе́т».","right":"«Дире́ктор сказа́л нам, где буфе́т».","why":"<b>пове́дать</b> carries solemnity — it's for grand tidings, not directions to the cafeteria. The charge clashes with the trivial content; the neutral, unloaded <b>сказа́ть</b> is exact here."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Едва́ живо́й от уста́лости, он … домо́й.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["шага́л","плёлся","семени́л"],"answer":1,"explain":"Barely alive with fatigue → <b>плёлся</b> (плести́сь). шага́л is brisk striding; семени́л is tiny quick steps — wrong picture."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word carries a built-in sneer — «notorious, that so-called…»?","opts":["изве́стный","знамени́тый","пресло́вутый"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>пресло́вутый</b> smuggles in disdain. изве́стный (well-known) and знамени́тый (famous) are neutral-to-positive — no sneer."}]},{"eyebrow":"Review · Company & coinage","title":"Words that keep company — and a clause folded into one","paras":["Wednesday: <b>collocations</b>, the words that keep company. A native doesn't «сде́лать влия́ние» — they <b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b>. Not «сде́лать побе́ду» but <b>одержа́ть побе́ду</b>. Rain isn't just «си́льный» but <b>проливно́й</b>; eyes are <b>ка́рие</b>, not «кори́чневые». Break the fixed pair and the grammar's perfect but the ear flinches.","Thursday: <b>expressive word-building</b>, where English builds a phrase and Russian builds a <i>word</i>. <b>Зачита́ться</b> = to get so lost in reading you forget the time. <b>Недосоли́ть</b> = to put too little salt. <b>Перемудри́ть</b> = to outsmart yourself. One prefix folds in a whole clause — and a native reads a freshly-coined one on the fly.","Both are the pure native ear: not a grammar rule you can look up, but a feel for which words belong together and how much meaning one word can carry."],"audio":"Wednesday: collocations, the words that keep company. A native doesn't 'sdelat vliyaniye' — they 'okazat vliyaniye'. Not 'sdelat pobedu' but 'oderzhat pobedu'. Rain isn't just 'silnyi' but 'prolivnoi'; eyes are 'karie', not 'korichnevye'. Break the fixed pair and the grammar's perfect but the ear flinches. Thursday: expressive word-building, where English builds a phrase and Russian builds a word. 'Zachitatsya' is to get so lost in reading you forget the time. 'Nedosolit' is to put too little salt. 'Peremudrit' is to outsmart yourself. One prefix folds in a whole clause — and a native reads a freshly-coined one on the fly. Both are the pure native ear: not a grammar rule you can look up, but a feel for which words belong together and how much meaning one word can carry.","table":{"rows":[["оказа́ть влия́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оказать влияние\">🔊</button>","to exert influence — not «сде́лать» (day 3)"],["одержа́ть побе́ду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"одержать победу\">🔊</button>","to win a victory — the fixed pair (day 3)"],["зачита́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зачитаться\">🔊</button>","to get lost in reading — one word, a clause (day 4)"],["недосоли́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"недосолить\">🔊</button>","to under-salt — недо- = «not enough» (day 4)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Heavy rain: not «сильный» — a native says «проливной».","en":"Проливно́й дождь — так ска́жет носи́тель, не про́сто «си́льный».","say":"Проливной дождь — так скажет носитель, не просто сильный."},{"ru":"He got so lost in the book he forgot the time.","en":"Он так зачита́лся, что забы́л о вре́мени.","say":"Он так зачитался, что забыл о времени."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Кома́нда сде́лала побе́ду в фина́ле».","right":"«Кома́нда одержа́ла побе́ду в фина́ле».","why":"Победа keeps company with <b>одержа́ть</b>, never «сде́лать». Word-for-word from English breaks the collocation — the single loudest marker of «foreign but grammatical»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Учи́тель … большо́е влия́ние на де́тей.» — which verb keeps company with влия́ние?","opts":["оказа́л","сде́лал","провёл"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b> is the fixed pair. «сде́лать / провести́ влия́ние» are word-for-word calques that break the collocation."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary or past form). Stress marks are just for you.","q":"One verb for «to get so absorbed in reading that you lose track of time». Type it:","accept":["зачитаться","зачитался","зачиталась"],"placeholder":"зачи…","explain":"<b>зачита́ться</b> — the prefix за- + -ся fold a whole English clause into one Russian verb."}]},{"eyebrow":"Review · The folded shade","title":"A shade in a <em>prefix</em>, a shade in a <em>particle</em>","paras":["Friday: the <b>finest grain of aspect</b>. A prefix folds a way-of-acting into the verb. Delimitative <b>по-</b>: <b>посиде́ть</b> = to sit a pleasant little while. Inceptive <b>за-</b>: <b>запе́ть</b> = to break into song. Saturative <b>на-…-ся</b>: <b>наговори́ться</b> = to talk one's fill. English needs «for a little while», «to start», «to one's heart's content»; Russian folds each into one prefix.","Saturday: the <b>last-shade particles</b>. <b>Уж</b> presses or softens, <b>-та́ки</b> means «after all, despite it», <b>аж</b> marks a jolt, <b>небо́сь</b> is a warm «I bet you're…». The thinnest film of attitude on a finished sentence — and the whole craft is the <b>dose</b>: one breathes, three make a caricature.","Prefix and particle are the same C2 lesson from two ends: a shade English spells out in a phrase, Russian carries in a syllable — and losing it is the last accent to go."],"audio":"Friday: the finest grain of aspect. A prefix folds a way-of-acting into the verb. Delimitative 'po-': 'posidet' is to sit a pleasant little while. Inceptive 'za-': 'zapet' is to break into song. Saturative 'na-...-sya': 'nagovoritsya' is to talk one's fill. English needs 'for a little while', 'to start', 'to one's heart's content'; Russian folds each into one prefix. Saturday: the last-shade particles. 'Uzh' presses or softens, '-taki' means 'after all, despite it', 'azh' marks a jolt, 'nebos' is a warm 'I bet you're...'. The thinnest film of attitude on a finished sentence — and the whole craft is the dose: one breathes, three make a caricature. Prefix and particle are the same C2 lesson from two ends: a shade English spells out in a phrase, Russian carries in a syllable — and losing it is the last accent to go.","table":{"rows":[["посиде́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"посидеть\">🔊</button>","to sit a pleasant little while — delimitative по- (day 5)"],["запе́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"запеть\">🔊</button>","to break into song — inceptive за- (day 5)"],["-та́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дошёл-таки\">🔊</button>","after all, despite it — a particle's shade (day 6)"],["небо́сь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"небось устал\">🔊</button>","I bet you're… — a warm guess (day 6)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"We sat a little while in the garden and went our ways.","en":"Мы посиде́ли в саду́ и разошли́сь.","say":"Мы посидели в саду и разошлись."},{"ru":"He got there after all, despite the road.","en":"Он-та́ки добра́лся, несмотря́ на доро́гу.","say":"Он-таки добрался, несмотря на дорогу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он уж аж та́ки пришёл небо́сь».","right":"«Он-та́ки пришёл».","why":"The week's last lesson, one more time: the <b>dose</b>. Four particles at once is a parody; one «-та́ки», exactly placed, carries the whole shade of «after all». C2 is subtraction."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Мы сла́вно … часо́к и разошли́сь.» — the delimitative «a pleasant little while» is —","opts":["посиде́ли","засиде́лись","просиде́ли"],"answer":0,"explain":"Delimitative <b>по-</b> = a little, and pleasantly → <b>посиде́ли</b>. засиде́лись = stayed too long; просиде́ли = sat through the whole time."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic; let «аж» carry the jolt and «то́-то» the little «there!»","target":"Он аж запе́л от ра́дости — то́-то ста́ло легко́!","sub":"«аж» = even to that point; «то́-то» = there, that's why — a small release","want":"он аж запел от радости — то-то стало легко"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ле́на notices Эмма's Russian has shifted this week — sharper, somehow. Эмма tries to name what changed. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты э́ту неде́лю говори́шь ина́че. Не пойму́ чем, но ина́че.","ru":"You've been speaking differently this week. Can't say how, but differently."},{"who":"A","en":"Слова́ те же. Про́сто я переста́ла выбира́ть «почти́ то».","ru":"Same words. I've just stopped settling for “almost the right one”."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то как?","ru":"Meaning?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше сказа́ла бы «си́льный дождь». Тепе́рь — «проливно́й». Ме́лочь, а слы́шно.","ru":"Before I'd say “heavy rain”. Now — “pouring”. A trifle, but you hear it."},{"who":"B","en":"И всё? Одно́ сло́во?","ru":"That's all? One word?"},{"who":"A","en":"И заря́д сло́ва, и части́ца к ме́сту. «Он-та́ки пришёл» — и слы́шно, как до́лго ждал.","ru":"The charge of a word, too, and a particle in its place. “He came after all” — and you hear how long he waited."},{"who":"B","en":"А не бои́шься переборщи́ть?","ru":"Aren't you afraid of overdoing it?"},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень бою́сь. Поэ́тому — одна́ части́ца, одно́ то́чное сло́во. В ме́ре вся суть.","ru":"Very much. So — one particle, one exact word. The whole point is in the measure."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words to carry out of week one — the exact, the charged, the coined, the folded. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"то́чный","ru":"exact, precise","ex":"Носи́тель и́щет то́чное сло́во."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"shade, nuance","ex":"Всё де́ло в отте́нке."},{"en":"пресло́вутый","ru":"notorious, so-called (a sneer)","ex":"Э́тот пресло́вутый отчёт всех изму́чил."},{"en":"пове́дать","ru":"to recount (lofty, solemn)","ex":"Он пове́дал стра́нную исто́рию."},{"en":"оказа́ть","ru":"to exert, render (with влия́ние, по́мощь)","ex":"Оказа́ть влия́ние — не «сде́лать»."},{"en":"одержа́ть","ru":"to gain (with побе́ду)","ex":"Одержа́ть побе́ду в спо́ре нелегко́."},{"en":"проливно́й","ru":"pouring, torrential (of rain)","ex":"Проливно́й дождь ли́л всю ночь."},{"en":"зачита́ться","ru":"to get lost in reading","ex":"Он зачита́лся до утра́."},{"en":"посиде́ть","ru":"to sit a little while (pleasantly)","ex":"Дава́й посиди́м здесь пять мину́т."},{"en":"запе́ть","ru":"to break into song","ex":"Она́ вдруг запе́ла."},{"en":"небо́сь","ru":"I bet, I suppose","ex":"Небо́сь замёрз на у́лице?"},{"en":"переборщи́ть","ru":"to overdo it, go too far","ex":"С части́цами легко́ переборщи́ть."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each idea from the week to the word that shows it.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word on the left onto the idea it demonstrates — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["плести́сь","the exact word — «walk» plus exhaustion (day 1)"],["пресло́вутый","connotation — a built-in sneer (day 2)"],["оказа́ть влия́ние","collocation — words that keep company (day 3)"],["посиде́ть","the finest grain of aspect — a little while (day 5)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — the whole week. If one bites, the rule above it will remind you.","items":[{"q":"At C2, from several words that all fit, a native picks the one that is —","opts":["the most formal","exact — it carries the right shade","the longest"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ста́рушка осторо́жно … по льду.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["шага́ла","брела́","семени́ла"],"answer":2},{"q":"A word that smuggles in a built-in negative verdict («notorious, so-called») is —","opts":["изве́стный","знамени́тый","пресло́вутый"],"answer":2},{"q":"«to win a victory» keeps company as —","opts":["одержа́ть побе́ду","сде́лать побе́ду","получи́ть побе́ду"],"answer":0},{"q":"«зачита́ться» packs into one verb —","opts":["to get so absorbed in reading you lose track of time","to read a text aloud","to finish a book"],"answer":0},{"q":"The delimitative «посиде́ть» means —","opts":["to sit down abruptly","to sit far too long","to sit a pleasant little while"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Доро́га была́ тру́дной, но он … добра́лся.» — «after all, despite it» is —","opts":["уже́ добра́лся","добра́лся-та́ки","то́лько добра́лся"],"answer":1},{"q":"The native skill with last-shade particles is —","opts":["the dose — one, well placed","stacking several for extra flavour","keeping them for official use only"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"The week in one breath — from «correct» to «the very word». Read each line aloud and feel how far the ear has come.","title":"Неде́ля то́чного сло́ва","sentences":["За шесть дней сло́во переста́ло быть про́сто «пра́вильным».","Снача́ла мы научи́лись слы́шать еди́нственно то́чный сино́ним, пото́м — скры́тый заря́д в сло́ве.","Узна́ли, каки́е слова́ дру́жат, и как це́лая мысль скла́дывается в оди́н глаго́л.","Почу́вствовали тонча́йший отте́нок ви́да и лёгкое дыха́ние части́цы.","Тепе́рь вопро́с оди́н: не «ве́рно ли», а «то ли э́то са́мое сло́во, что вы́брал бы носи́тель»."],"translation":"In six days the word stopped being merely “correct”. First we learned to hear the one exact synonym, then the hidden charge inside a word. We found out which words keep company, and how a whole thought folds into a single verb. We felt the finest grain of aspect and the light breath of a particle. Now there's one question: not “is it correct”, but “is this the very word a native would have chosen”."},"essay":{"intro":"One paragraph to prove the week.","prompt":"Write 4–5 sentences describing the SAME small moment — someone arriving, someone reading, a rainy evening — and pack this week's precision into it: reach for the ONE exact verb (not the flat default), one word with the right connotation, one true collocation, and exactly one last-shade particle. Then, in a line, point to each choice and name what it carries. Show the whole week working at once — without over-salting.","hint":"The proof of the week is a single natural paragraph where every word is the exact one — and where you stopped at ONE particle 🙂","example":"Проливно́й дождь ли́л весь ве́чер, и Ле́на не шла домо́й, а плела́сь. Она́ так зачита́лась в метро́, что чуть не прое́хала свою́ ста́нцию. «Уж э́ти кни́ги», — вздохну́ла она́, но кни́гу-та́ки не закры́ла. Здесь ка́ждое сло́во то́чное: «проливно́й» — коллока́ция, «плела́сь» — уста́лость, «зачита́лась» — це́лая мысль в одно́м сло́ве, «-та́ки» — оди́н то́чный отте́нок, и бо́льше ни одно́й ли́шней части́цы."}},"8":{"day":8,"week":"02","themeRu":"Reading between the lines","themeEn":"Подте́кст: смысл ме́жду строк","intro":"«Я про́сто спра́шиваю.» Read that at face value and it's an innocent question. But nine times out of ten it isn't a question at all — it's a reproach: «why are you hiding this from me?» The words are spotless; the meaning sits underneath them. Last week you made words <b>exact</b> — what a sentence <i>says</i>. This week is about what a sentence <i>means</i> without ever saying it: <b>подте́кст</b>, subtext. It's the last thing an English ear learns to hear.","introAudio":"'Ya prosto sprashivayu', I'm just asking. Read that at face value and it's an innocent question. But nine times out of ten it isn't a question at all — it's a reproach: why are you hiding this from me? The words are spotless; the meaning sits underneath them. Last week you made words exact — what a sentence says. This week is about what a sentence means without ever saying it: 'podtekst', subtext. It's the last thing an English ear learns to hear.","goals":["Hear the second layer: what a line <i>means</i> under what it <i>says</i> (<b>подте́кст</b>)","Name the machinery — <b>пресуппози́ция</b> (what's taken for granted) and <b>импликату́ра</b> (what's inferred)","Read the small carriers of a barb: <b>про́сто · коне́чно · да нет · интере́сно, кто…</b>","Answer the meaning, not the words — without <i>inventing</i> subtext where there is none"],"learned":["Heard the second layer — what a line means under what it says (подте́кст)","Named the machinery: пресуппози́ция (taken for granted) and импликату́ра (inferred)","Read the small carriers of a barb (про́сто, коне́чно, да нет, интере́сно кто…)","Answered the meaning, not the words — without over-reading a neutral line"],"review":{"intro":"Half a minute back on Saturday's last-shade particles before we drop under the surface. The dose, then the depths.","introAudio":"Half a minute back on Saturday's last-shade particles before we drop under the surface. The dose, then the depths.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Saturday's particles.","q":"«Доро́га была́ тру́дной, но он … добра́лся.» — «after all, despite it» is —","opts":["уже́ добра́лся","то́лько добра́лся","добра́лся-та́ки"],"answer":2,"explain":"«after all, despite the hard road» → <b>добра́лся-та́ки</b>. «уже́» is «already»; «то́лько» is «only just»."},{"type":"choice","q":"The native skill with last-shade particles is —","opts":["the dose — one, well placed","stacking as many as possible","using them only in writing"],"answer":0,"explain":"One particle breathes; three make a caricature. The gift is knowing when <i>not</i> to add one."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle (5 letters). Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The warm folk-guess for «I bet you're…», as in «…, уста́л с доро́ги?». Type it:","accept":["небось"],"placeholder":"неб…","explain":"<b>небо́сь</b> — «I bet, I suppose». A kindly guess about someone, pure spoken warmth."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The second layer","title":"The line says one thing, means <em>another</em>","paras":["You've spent a week on the exact word — what a sentence <b>says</b>. Now: what it <b>means</b> without saying it. «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» — «I'm just asking» — is usually not a question but a reproach, «why are you hiding this from me?» The words are innocent; the meaning sits under them. That underneath is <b>подте́кст</b> (subtext).","Every language does this, but Russian conversation leans on it hard — much of what's meant is only implied. Two words name the machinery. <b>Пресуппози́ция</b> (presupposition) — what a sentence takes for granted: «Ты <i>опя́ть</i> опозда́л» quietly assumes you're always late. <b>Импликату́ра</b> (implicature) — what it lets you infer unsaid: «Здесь хо́лодно» can simply mean «shut the window».","For an English speaker at C2 this is the last big trap. Your grammar is flawless, so you read every line at face value — and miss the half of the conversation that was never spoken. Learning to hear <b>what isn't said</b> is this week's whole work."],"audio":"You've spent a week on the exact word — what a sentence says. Now: what it means without saying it. 'Ya prosto sprashivayu', I'm just asking, is usually not a question but a reproach, why are you hiding this from me? The words are innocent; the meaning sits under them. That underneath is 'podtekst', subtext. Every language does this, but Russian conversation leans on it hard — much of what's meant is only implied. Two words name the machinery. 'Presuppozitsiya', presupposition — what a sentence takes for granted: 'Ty opyat opozdal', you're late again, quietly assumes you're always late. 'Implikatura', implicature — what it lets you infer unsaid: 'Zdes kholodno', it's cold in here, can simply mean shut the window. For an English speaker at C2 this is the last big trap. Your grammar is flawless, so you read every line at face value — and miss the half of the conversation that was never spoken. Learning to hear what isn't said is this week's whole work.","table":{"rows":[["Я про́сто спра́шиваю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я просто спрашиваю\">🔊</button>","surface: just asking · subtext: a reproach"],["Де́лай как зна́ешь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"делай как знаешь\">🔊</button>","surface: do as you like · subtext: I disapprove"],["Нет, всё норма́льно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нет всё нормально\">🔊</button>","surface: it's fine · subtext: something's wrong"],["Спаси́бо, я сам. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спасибо я сам\">🔊</button>","surface: thanks, I've got it · subtext: don't help me"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«I'm just asking» is rarely a question — it's a reproach.","en":"«Я про́сто спра́шиваю» — ре́дко вопро́с, ча́ще упрёк.","say":"Я просто спрашиваю — редко вопрос, чаще упрёк."},{"ru":"«Do as you like» means «I think you're making a mistake».","en":"«Де́лай как зна́ешь» зна́чит «по-мо́ему, ты ошиба́ешься».","say":"Делай как знаешь значит по-моему, ты ошибаешься."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Нет, всё норма́льно» — зна́чит, всё хорошо́.","right":"«Нет, всё норма́льно» — ча́сто зна́чит, что что́-то не так.","why":"Taking the surface for the meaning is the C2 slip. A flat, wounded «всё норма́льно» usually signals the opposite; the native hears the subtext, not the dictionary."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Подте́кст» is —","opts":["a grammatical case","the literal dictionary sense","the meaning implied but not stated"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Подте́кст</b> is the meaning that runs <i>under</i> the words — implied, not spoken."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты опя́ть опозда́л» takes for granted (presupposes) that you —","opts":["are usually on time","are late often","arrived early"],"answer":1,"explain":"The little «опя́ть» (again) is a <b>presupposition</b> — it assumes lateness is a pattern, without stating it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · How the hint is built","title":"Small words that carry a <em>barb</em>","paras":["Subtext isn't magic — it's ordinary words used at a slant. Watch the little ones. <b>«Про́сто»</b> and <b>«всего́ лишь»</b> pretend to shrink a thing while pointing right at it: «Я про́сто хоте́л помо́чь» = «and look how you took it». <b>«Коне́чно»</b> concedes in order to accuse: «У тебя́, коне́чно, свои́ дела́…» — «of course you've your own affairs» — means «you've no time for me anymore».","Then the <b>unfinished sentence</b>. Russian loves the trailing «…»: «Я, коне́чно, ничего́ не говорю́, но…» leaves the blow unspoken and all the louder for it. And the <b>rhetorical wonder</b>: «Интере́сно, кто́ не вы́мыл посу́ду» isn't curiosity — it's an accusation with the name already attached.","None of these words is doing its dictionary job. «Про́сто» isn't «simply»; «интере́сно» isn't «interesting». They're carriers — the exact-word instinct from last week, now aimed at a meaning that stays under the surface."],"audio":"Subtext isn't magic — it's ordinary words used at a slant. Watch the little ones. 'Prosto' and 'vsego lish' pretend to shrink a thing while pointing right at it: 'Ya prosto khotel pomoch', I only wanted to help, means and look how you took it. 'Konechno', of course, concedes in order to accuse: 'U tebya, konechno, svoi dela', of course you've your own affairs, means you've no time for me anymore. Then the unfinished sentence. Russian loves the trailing dots: 'Ya, konechno, nichego ne govoryu, no...', I'm not saying anything, but..., leaves the blow unspoken and all the louder for it. And the rhetorical wonder: 'Interesno, kto ne vymyl posudu', I wonder who didn't wash the dishes, isn't curiosity — it's an accusation with the name already attached. None of these words is doing its dictionary job. 'Prosto' isn't simply; 'interesno' isn't interesting. They're carriers — the exact-word instinct from last week, now aimed at a meaning that stays under the surface.","table":{"rows":[["про́сто / всего́ лишь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я просто хотел помочь\">🔊</button>","shrinks a thing while pointing at it — «and look…»"],["коне́чно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у тебя конечно свои дела\">🔊</button>","concedes in order to accuse"],[", но… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я ничего не говорю но\">🔊</button>","the unspoken blow, louder for the silence"],["Интере́сно, кто… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интересно кто не вымыл посуду\">🔊</button>","a rhetorical wonder = a named accusation"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«I only wanted to help» — with a reproach inside it.","en":"«Я всего́ лишь хоте́л помо́чь» — а внутри́ упрёк.","say":"Я всего лишь хотел помочь — а внутри упрёк."},{"ru":"«Of course you've got your own affairs» — meaning «you've no time for me».","en":"«У тебя́, коне́чно, свои́ дела́» — то́ есть «на меня́ вре́мени нет».","say":"У тебя, конечно, свои дела — то есть на меня времени нет."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я про́сто хоте́л помо́чь» — челове́к вы́разил жела́ние помо́чь.","right":"«Я про́сто хоте́л помо́чь» — ча́сто скры́тый упрёк: «а ты не оцени́л».","why":"Here «про́сто» isn't «simply» — it's the barb, shrinking the good deed to enlarge your ingratitude. Read it literally and you miss the reproach entirely."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In «Я про́сто спра́шиваю», the word «про́сто» works to —","opts":["state the manner literally","shrink the act while pointing a reproach","ask a genuine question"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Про́сто» here isn't «simply» — it shrinks «asking» to make the hidden reproach louder."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The Russian for «subtext» — the meaning that runs under the words (hint: подт…). Type it:","accept":["подтекст"],"placeholder":"подт…","explain":"<b>подте́кст</b> — literally «under-text»: the meaning beneath the line."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Reading the unsaid","title":"The signals that a second layer is <em>on</em>","paras":["How do you know subtext is running? First, <b>too-polite or too-flat</b>: a clipped «Спаси́бо, я сам» or a toneless «Нет, всё норма́льно» is almost never neutral. Second, the answer that <b>dodges</b>: ask «Ты не серди́шься?» and hear «Да нет, всё в поря́дке» — that little «да нет» is a soft yes-dressed-as-no, usually «I am, a little».","Third, <b>intonation</b> — tomorrow's tool for irony, but already here: «Ну-ну» or «Молоде́ц…» said flat can bless you or bury you. Fourth, the <b>mismatch</b>: when the words are kind but the situation is tense, trust the situation. «Мо́жешь не спеши́ть» to someone chronically late isn't permission — it's a jab.","Reading subtext is choosing the exact word run backwards: you already know the ten shades a phrase <i>could</i> carry, so you ask which one the moment actually holds. A native does it without noticing — which is why they answer the reproach, not the question."],"audio":"How do you know subtext is running? First, too-polite or too-flat: a clipped 'Spasibo, ya sam', thanks, I've got it, or a toneless 'Net, vsyo normalno', no, it's fine, is almost never neutral. Second, the answer that dodges: ask 'Ty ne serdishsya?', you're not angry? and hear 'Da net, vsyo v poryadke', no no, all's well — that little 'da net' is a soft yes dressed as no, usually I am, a little. Third, intonation — tomorrow's tool for irony, but already here: 'Nu-nu' or 'Molodets' said flat can bless you or bury you. Fourth, the mismatch: when the words are kind but the situation is tense, trust the situation. 'Mozhesh ne speshit', you needn't hurry, to someone chronically late isn't permission — it's a jab. Reading subtext is choosing the exact word run backwards: you already know the ten shades a phrase could carry, so you ask which one the moment actually holds. A native does it without noticing — which is why they answer the reproach, not the question.","table":{"rows":[["Да нет, всё в поря́дке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"да нет всё в порядке\">🔊</button>","the soft «да нет» — usually a quiet yes"],["Спаси́бо, я сам <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спасибо я сам\">🔊</button>","clipped = «back off», not real thanks"],["Мо́жешь не спеши́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"можешь не спешить\">🔊</button>","to the always-late — a jab, not permission"],["Ну-ну… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ну-ну\">🔊</button>","flat = disbelief or warning, not cheer"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«No, no, all's well» — the «да нет» hides a quiet «yes».","en":"«Да нет, всё в поря́дке» — за «да нет» пря́чется ти́хое «да».","say":"Да нет, всё в порядке — за да нет прячется тихое да."},{"ru":"When the words are kind but the moment is tense, trust the moment.","en":"Когда́ слова́ до́брые, а моме́нт напряжённый — верь моме́нту.","say":"Когда слова добрые, а момент напряжённый — верь моменту."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"На «Ты не серди́шься?» отве́т «Да нет» — по́лное «нет, всё хорошо́».","right":"«Да нет» здесь — мя́гкое «да, немно́го»: подте́кст говори́т, что челове́к всё-та́ки заде́т.","why":"«Да нет» is rarely a clean refusal. The subtext is a hedged «yes» — the person is hurt but softening it. Read it as a flat «no» and you walk right past the feeling."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Мо́жешь не спеши́ть», said to someone always late, most likely means —","opts":["genuine permission to relax","a jab about their lateness","they should hurry up"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kind words, tense situation → trust the situation. To the chronically late, this is a <b>jab</b>, not permission."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic; let the flat «да нет» sound like a quiet «yes, a little».","target":"Да нет, всё в поря́дке… про́сто уста́л.","sub":"the «да нет» should sound soft and unconvincing — the subtext is «yes, a bit»","want":"да нет всё в порядке… просто устал"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Answering the unsaid — and the week","title":"Reply to the <em>meaning</em>, not the words","paras":["The native move is to answer the subtext, not the surface. To «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» you don't defend the fact — you address the reproach: «Не серди́сь, я не скрыва́л». To «У тебя́, коне́чно, свои́ дела́» you answer the hurt, not your calendar. Miss the layer and you reply, politely and fluently, to the wrong sentence.","The trap runs the other way too: don't <i>invent</i> subtext where there's none. Not every «всё норма́льно» is a wound — sometimes it's just fine. The skill is the same taste as the dose of particles: read the signals, weigh the situation, don't over-read. A paranoid ear is as foreign as a deaf one.","This week is the layer under the words. Tomorrow subtext sharpens into its most pointed form — <b>irony</b>, where «Ну ты ге́ний!» means the opposite. Then the pun, the allusion, the whole art of saying one thing and meaning another. You spent a week making words exact; now watch them carry meanings they never state."],"audio":"The native move is to answer the subtext, not the surface. To 'Ya prosto sprashivayu' you don't defend the fact — you address the reproach: 'Ne serdis, ya ne skryval', don't be angry, I wasn't hiding it. To 'U tebya, konechno, svoi dela' you answer the hurt, not your calendar. Miss the layer and you reply, politely and fluently, to the wrong sentence. The trap runs the other way too: don't invent subtext where there's none. Not every 'vsyo normalno' is a wound — sometimes it's just fine. The skill is the same taste as the dose of particles: read the signals, weigh the situation, don't over-read. A paranoid ear is as foreign as a deaf one. This week is the layer under the words. Tomorrow subtext sharpens into its most pointed form — irony, where 'Nu ty geniy', well aren't you a genius, means the opposite. Then the pun, the allusion, the whole art of saying one thing and meaning another. You spent a week making words exact; now watch them carry meanings they never state.","table":{"rows":[["Не серди́сь, я не скрыва́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не сердись я не скрывал\">🔊</button>","answer the reproach, not the question"],["Не всё — подте́кст. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не всё подтекст\">🔊</button>","don't invent a hint where there is none"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Answer the hurt, not the schedule.","en":"Отвеча́й на оби́ду, а не на расписа́ние.","say":"Отвечай на обиду, а не на расписание."},{"ru":"Not every «it's fine» is a wound — don't read in too much.","en":"Не ка́ждое «всё норма́льно» — ра́на: не вычи́тывай ли́шнего.","say":"Не каждое всё нормально — рана: не вычитывай лишнего."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"На «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» отвеча́ть: «Да, ты спроси́л».","right":"На «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» отвеча́й на упрёк: «Прости́, я не хоте́л скрыва́ть».","why":"Answering the surface leaves the real message hanging in the air. The native answers the subtext — the reproach — and defuses it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The native way to handle «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» is to —","opts":["answer only the literal question","address the reproach underneath","ignore it entirely"],"answer":1,"explain":"The line is a reproach in question's clothing. A native answers the <b>reproach</b>, not the surface question."},{"type":"choice","q":"Once you can hear subtext, the opposite danger is —","opts":["reading a hidden barb into every neutral line","speaking too formally","forgetting your vocabulary"],"answer":0,"explain":"A paranoid ear is as foreign as a deaf one. Don't over-read: sometimes «всё норма́льно» is simply true."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The idiom «to read between the …» — чита́ть ме́жду … (hint: стр…). Type the missing word:","accept":["строк"],"placeholder":"стр…","explain":"<b>чита́ть ме́жду строк</b> — to read between the lines: to hear the meaning that isn't printed."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма arrives late; Ле́на greets her a shade too kindly. Эмма hears the second layer and answers <i>it</i>, not the words. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"О, ты всё-та́ки пришла́. А я уж и не жда́ла.","ru":"Oh, you did come after all. I'd rather given up."},{"who":"A","en":"Слы́шу подте́кст. Ты не «не жда́ла» — ты оби́делась, что я опозда́ла.","ru":"I hear the subtext. You didn't “give up” — you're hurt that I was late."},{"who":"B","en":"Да нет, всё норма́льно. Сиди́, ко́фе уже́ осты́л, ничего́.","ru":"No, no, it's fine. Sit down, the coffee's gone cold, never mind."},{"who":"A","en":"«Ничего́», за кото́рым — «ты меня́ подвела́». Я по́няла. Прости́, застря́ла в метро́.","ru":"“Never mind”, with “you let me down” underneath. I get it. Sorry, I was stuck in the metro."},{"who":"B","en":"Ну вот, ты уже́ отвеча́ешь на то, чего́ я не сказа́ла.","ru":"There — now you're answering what I didn't even say."},{"who":"A","en":"В э́том и фо́кус. Я перево́дчик — я живу́ ме́жду строк.","ru":"That's the whole trick. I'm a translator — I live between the lines."},{"who":"B","en":"А ра́ньше приняла́ бы «всё норма́льно» за чи́стую моне́ту.","ru":"And before, you'd have taken “it's fine” at face value."},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше — да. Тепе́рь слы́шу не слова́, а то, что за ни́ми.","ru":"Before — yes. Now I hear not the words but what's behind them."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the layer under the words — the hint, the reproach, the unsaid. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"подте́кст","ru":"subtext, implied meaning","ex":"В ка́ждой её фра́зе есть подте́кст."},{"en":"намёк","ru":"a hint","ex":"Он сра́зу по́нял намёк."},{"en":"упрёк","ru":"a reproach","ex":"В её слова́х скво́зит упрёк."},{"en":"недоска́занность","ru":"the unsaid, understatement","ex":"В письме́ мно́го недоска́занности."},{"en":"пресуппози́ция","ru":"presupposition (taken for granted)","ex":"«Опя́ть» несёт пресуппози́цию."},{"en":"импликату́ра","ru":"implicature (what's inferred)","ex":"Импликату́ра ва́жнее са́мих слов."},{"en":"намека́ть","ru":"to hint, to imply","ex":"Она́ намека́ет, но пря́мо не говори́т."},{"en":"подразумева́ть","ru":"to imply, to mean underneath","ex":"Он подразумева́л совсе́м друго́е."},{"en":"заде́ть","ru":"to hurt, to touch a nerve","ex":"Э́ти слова́ его́ заде́ли."},{"en":"вы́читать","ru":"to read into, glean from a text","ex":"Он вы́читал в письме́ намёк."},{"en":"ме́жду строк","ru":"between the lines","ex":"Гла́вное чита́й ме́жду строк."},{"en":"чи́стая моне́та","ru":"face value (idiom «за чи́стую моне́ту»)","ex":"Не принима́й э́то за чи́стую моне́ту."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each innocent phrase to the meaning hiding under it.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each phrase on the left onto its subtext — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я про́сто спра́шиваю.","a reproach — «why are you hiding it?»"],["Де́лай как зна́ешь.","disapproval — «I think you're wrong»"],["Спаси́бо, я сам.","«back off» — I don't want your help"],["Да нет, всё норма́льно.","a quiet «yes, something's wrong»"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Подте́кст» is —","opts":["a grammatical case","the literal dictionary sense","the meaning implied but not stated"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Ты опя́ть опозда́л» presupposes that you —","opts":["are usually on time","are late often","arrived early"],"answer":1},{"q":"In «Я про́сто хоте́л помо́чь», the word «про́сто» mainly —","opts":["states the manner literally","carries a reproach underneath","asks a real question"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Мо́жешь не спеши́ть», said to a chronically late friend, most likely is —","opts":["a jab about their lateness","genuine permission","a request to run"],"answer":0},{"q":"Once you can hear subtext, the opposite danger is —","opts":["reading a hidden barb into every neutral line","speaking too formally","forgetting your vocabulary"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Half a conversation happens in the words; the other half happens under them. Read each line aloud and hear both.","title":"Полразгово́ра — ме́жду строк","sentences":["«Я про́сто спра́шиваю», — говори́т она́, и э́то не вопро́с, а упрёк.","«Де́лай как зна́ешь», — отвеча́ет он, то́ есть «ты соверша́ешь оши́бку».","Полразгово́ра прохо́дит ме́жду строк, и слова́ здесь — то́лько ве́рхний слой.","Иностра́нец слы́шит бу́кву и кива́ет; носи́тель слы́шит намёк и отвеча́ет на него́.","Поня́ть ру́сский до конца́ — зна́чит услы́шать и то, что не́ было ска́зано."],"translation":"“I'm just asking,” she says, and it's not a question but a reproach. “Do as you like,” he answers — that is, “you're making a mistake”. Half the conversation runs between the lines, and the words here are only the top layer. A foreigner hears the letter and nods; a native hears the hint and answers <i>that</i>. To understand Russian to the end is to hear also what was never said."},"essay":{"intro":"Your ear for the unsaid.","prompt":"Write a SHORT exchange (3–4 short lines of dialogue) where at least two lines mean something other than what they say — a «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» that reproaches, a «всё норма́льно» that isn't. Then, underneath, unpack the subtext of each: what does the line really mean, and what signalled it? Finish with the reply that answers the meaning, not the words.","hint":"The exact-word instinct run backwards: you know the ten things a line could mean — name the one this moment holds. But don't over-read: not every «fine» is a wound 🙂","example":"— Ты, коне́чно, за́нят свои́ми дела́ми. — Да нет, всё норма́льно, иди́. Подте́кст пе́рвой ре́плики — не про дела́, а «ты совсе́м забы́л про меня́»; знак — «коне́чно» и незако́нченный упрёк. Подте́кст второ́й — «мне оби́дно», хотя́ ска́зано «норма́льно». Отве́т по существу́: «Прости́, я закружи́лся — дава́й посиди́м ве́чером»."}},"9":{"day":9,"week":"02","themeRu":"Saying the opposite","themeEn":"Иро́ния и сарка́зм","intro":"Someone floods the kitchen, and you hear: «Ну ты молоде́ц». The words say «well done». The flood says otherwise. Yesterday the real meaning hid <i>under</i> the words; today it stands as their exact <b>opposite</b>. That's <b>иро́ния</b> — praise said backwards — and its sharper cousin <b>сарка́зм</b>, the same reversal aimed to wound. Taking either one at face value is the single most classic C2 stumble. Today you learn to hear the flip — and, carefully, to make it.","introAudio":"Someone floods the kitchen, and you hear: 'Nu ty molodets', well done, you. The words say well done. The flood says otherwise. Yesterday the real meaning hid under the words; today it stands as their exact opposite. That's 'ironiya', irony — praise said backwards — and its sharper cousin 'sarkazm', sarcasm, the same reversal aimed to wound. Taking either one at face value is the single most classic C2 stumble. Today you learn to hear the flip — and, carefully, to make it.","goals":["Hear <b>антифра́зис</b> — a word standing for its own opposite («Хорошо́ приду́мал!» = «what a mess»)","Tell <b>иро́ния</b> (can be fond, even self-aimed) from <b>сарка́зм</b> (sharpened to wound)","Read the ready-made ironic phrases: <b>то́же мне · как же · ну-ну · спаси́бо, удружи́л</b>","Catch irony's marks — ironic quotes and flat, sing-song tone — and answer the real meaning"],"learned":["Heard антифра́зис — a word standing for its own opposite","Told иро́ния (fond, even self-aimed) from сарка́зм (sharpened to wound)","Read the ready-made ironic phrases (то́же мне, как же, ну-ну, спаси́бо, удружи́л)","Caught irony's marks — quotes and tone — and answered the real meaning, not the words"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday's subtext before we flip the words outright. The meaning under, then the meaning reversed.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday's subtext before we flip the words outright. The meaning under, then the meaning reversed.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's subtext.","q":"«Я про́сто спра́шиваю» is usually not a question but —","opts":["a polite greeting","a reproach","a real request for facts"],"answer":1,"explain":"The innocent words carry a reproach underneath — «why are you hiding this from me?» That's <b>подте́кст</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Once you can hear subtext, the opposite danger is —","opts":["reading a hidden barb into every neutral line","speaking too slowly","using no particles"],"answer":0,"explain":"A paranoid ear is as foreign as a deaf one. Don't over-read — sometimes «всё норма́льно» is simply true."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The idiom for hearing the unspoken meaning — чита́ть ме́жду … (hint: стр…). Type the missing word:","accept":["строк"],"placeholder":"стр…","explain":"<b>чита́ть ме́жду строк</b> — to read between the lines."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Saying the opposite","title":"«Well done!» that means <em>what a mess</em>","paras":["Yesterday's subtext ran <i>under</i> the words. Today it flips them outright. <b>Иро́ния</b> (irony) is saying one thing and meaning its opposite: «Хорошо́ ты приду́мал!» — «nice thinking!» — hissed flat over a disaster means «what a mess you've made». The device has a name: <b>антифра́зис</b> — a word standing for its own reverse.","How do you know it's irony and not praise? The same two signals as subtext, turned up. <b>Intonation</b>: real praise lifts; irony goes flat, sing-song, or over-sweet. <b>Mismatch</b>: sunny words on a grim situation. Someone shatters a cup and hears «Ну ты молоде́ц» — the words say «well done», everything else says «disaster».","Two cousins to keep apart. <b>Иро́ния</b> can be light, even fond, and it can turn on yourself (самоиро́ния — self-irony). <b>Сарка́зм</b> is irony sharpened to wound: «Ну ты, коне́чно, ге́ний», meant to cut. Same reversal, opposite intent — a smile versus a knife."],"audio":"Yesterday's subtext ran under the words. Today it flips them outright. 'Ironiya', irony, is saying one thing and meaning its opposite: 'Khorosho ty pridumal!', nice thinking!, hissed flat over a disaster means what a mess you've made. The device has a name: 'antifrazis' — a word standing for its own reverse. How do you know it's irony and not praise? The same two signals as subtext, turned up. Intonation: real praise lifts; irony goes flat, sing-song, or over-sweet. Mismatch: sunny words on a grim situation. Someone shatters a cup and hears 'Nu ty molodets' — the words say well done, everything else says disaster. Two cousins to keep apart. 'Ironiya' can be light, even fond, and it can turn on yourself, 'samoironiya', self-irony. 'Sarkazm' is irony sharpened to wound: 'Nu ty, konechno, geniy', meant to cut. Same reversal, opposite intent — a smile versus a knife.","table":{"rows":[["Хорошо́ ты приду́мал! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хорошо ты придумал\">🔊</button>","flat = «what a mess you've made» (antiphrasis)"],["Ну ты молоде́ц… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ну ты молодец\">🔊</button>","over the disaster = the opposite of praise"],["иро́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ирония\">🔊</button>","saying the opposite — can be light, even fond"],["сарка́зм <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сарказм\">🔊</button>","irony sharpened to wound — a knife, not a smile"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«Nice thinking!» over a disaster means the exact opposite.","en":"«Хорошо́ приду́мал!» над катастро́фой зна́чит ро́вно наоборо́т.","say":"Хорошо придумал! над катастрофой значит ровно наоборот."},{"ru":"Irony can smile; sarcasm means to cut.","en":"Иро́ния мо́жет улыба́ться, а сарка́зм — ра́нить.","say":"Ирония может улыбаться, а сарказм — ранить."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ну ты ге́ний!» — челове́к хва́лит меня́ за ум.","right":"«Ну ты ге́ний!», ска́занное над разби́той ча́шкой, — насме́шка, а не похвала́.","why":"Taking irony at face value is the classic C2 failure. A flat tone plus a broken cup flip the «praise» into mockery — the words and the moment point opposite ways, and the moment wins."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Антифра́зис» is a word used to mean —","opts":["exactly what it says","its own opposite","something more formal"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Антифра́зис</b> is the engine of irony: a word set to carry the reverse of its dictionary sense."},{"type":"choice","q":"The difference between иро́ния and сарка́зм is mainly —","opts":["irony can be fond; sarcasm means to wound","sarcasm is more polite","there is no difference"],"answer":0,"explain":"Same reversal, different intent. Irony can smile or turn on yourself; <b>сарка́зм</b> is aimed to cut."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Ready-made irony","title":"Set phrases that flip on their own — <em>«то́же мне»</em>","paras":["Russian keeps a rack of phrases that are ironic by default — you barely need tone. <b>«То́же мне…»</b> deflates whatever follows: «То́же мне геро́й!» = «some hero you are». <b>«Как же!»</b> is «yeah, right»: «Он изви́нится? Как же!» means he certainly won't. <b>«Ну-ну»</b>, said flat, is polite disbelief — «sure, go on then».","A whole set turns thanks into a jab. <b>«Спаси́бо, удружи́л»</b> — «thanks, you did me a fine favour» — means you made things worse. <b>«Вот спаси́бо»</b>, flat, is the same sting. And <b>«нашёл чем горди́ться»</b> — «found something to be proud of» — mocks the very boast it echoes.","These aren't tone-of-voice tricks; they're lexical — the phrase carries the reversal. Learn them as units, the way you learned collocations: «то́же мне» simply <i>is</i> a sneer, whoever says it and however they say it."],"audio":"Russian keeps a rack of phrases that are ironic by default — you barely need tone. 'Tozhe mne' deflates whatever follows: 'Tozhe mne geroy!', some hero you are. 'Kak zhe!' is yeah, right: 'On izvinitsya? Kak zhe!' means he certainly won't. 'Nu-nu', said flat, is polite disbelief — sure, go on then. A whole set turns thanks into a jab. 'Spasibo, udruzhil', thanks, you did me a fine favour, means you made things worse. 'Vot spasibo', flat, is the same sting. And 'nashyol chem gorditsya', found something to be proud of, mocks the very boast it echoes. These aren't tone-of-voice tricks; they're lexical — the phrase carries the reversal. Learn them as units, the way you learned collocations: 'tozhe mne' simply is a sneer, whoever says it and however they say it.","table":{"rows":[["То́же мне… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тоже мне герой\">🔊</button>","«some … you are» — deflates what follows"],["Как же! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он извинится как же\">🔊</button>","«yeah, right» — it certainly won't happen"],["Ну-ну. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ну-ну\">🔊</button>","flat = polite disbelief, «sure, go on»"],["Спаси́бо, удружи́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спасибо удружил\">🔊</button>","«thanks a lot» — you only made it worse"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«Some hero!» — the phrase mocks all by itself.","en":"«То́же мне геро́й!» — фра́за насмеха́ется сама́ по себе́.","say":"Тоже мне герой! — фраза насмехается сама по себе."},{"ru":"«He'll apologize? Yeah, right!»","en":"«Он изви́нится? Как же!»","say":"Он извинится? Как же!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Спаси́бо, удружи́л!» — челове́к и́скренне благодари́т за по́мощь.","right":"«Спаси́бо, удружи́л!» — е́дкая насме́шка: ты то́лько всё испо́ртил.","why":"This «спаси́бо» is sarcastic thanks — it means you made things worse, not better. The set phrase carries the reversal on its own, whatever the tone."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«То́же мне специали́ст!» means —","opts":["a genuine expert","some expert you are (a sneer)","a certified specialist"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>То́же мне…</b> deflates the word after it — «call that an expert?» A ready-made sneer."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two-word phrase. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The flat «yeah, right / as if» that denies what was just said — «Он изви́нится? …!» (hint: как…). Type it:","accept":["как же"],"placeholder":"как…","explain":"<b>как же!</b> — «yeah, right», it dismisses the idea as never going to happen."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Quotes and voice","title":"Irony you can <em>see</em> and <em>hear</em>","paras":["Irony leaves marks. In writing, <b>ironic quotation marks</b>: наш «специали́ст» or он мне «помо́г» — the quotes mean «so-called», a printed sneer. You met this charge already in «пресло́вутый»; now you can aim it with punctuation.","In speech, <b>intonation</b> does the flipping. A stretched, sing-song «Ну-у, коне́чно-о» drips doubt; a flat, toneless «Молоде́ц» buries the praise it pretends to give. This is the C2 pronunciation slot: the same words, opposite meanings, decided entirely by the melody — «на са́мом де́ле нет», carried by voice alone.","Sarcasm is this same machinery pointed to hurt. «Ну ты, коне́чно, гига́нт мы́сли» — «a giant of thought, obviously» — is the ironic quote and the flat tone together, aimed at a person. Reading it straight, as a compliment, is exactly the accent this week exists to erase."],"audio":"Irony leaves marks. In writing, ironic quotation marks: 'nash spetsialist', our specialist, in quotes, or 'on mne pomog', he helped me, in quotes — the quotes mean so-called, a printed sneer. You met this charge already in 'preslovutyi'; now you can aim it with punctuation. In speech, intonation does the flipping. A stretched, sing-song 'Nu-u, konechno-o' drips doubt; a flat, toneless 'Molodets' buries the praise it pretends to give. This is the C2 pronunciation slot: the same words, opposite meanings, decided entirely by the melody — 'na samom dele net', actually no, carried by voice alone. Sarcasm is this same machinery pointed to hurt. 'Nu ty, konechno, gigant mysli', a giant of thought, obviously, is the ironic quote and the flat tone together, aimed at a person. Reading it straight, as a compliment, is exactly the accent this week exists to erase.","table":{"rows":[["наш «специали́ст» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"наш специалист\">🔊</button>","ironic quotes = «so-called», a printed sneer"],["он мне «помо́г» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он мне помог\">🔊</button>","quotes flip «helped» into «made it worse»"],["Ну-у, коне́чно-о… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ну конечно\">🔊</button>","sing-song tone = dripping disbelief"],["Молоде́ц. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"молодец\">🔊</button>","flat tone buries the praise it pretends"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"In quotes, «helped» means the opposite — he made it worse.","en":"В кавы́чках «помо́г» зна́чит наоборо́т — то́лько навреди́л.","say":"В кавычках помог значит наоборот — только навредил."},{"ru":"The same «well done» praises or mocks — the tone decides.","en":"Одно́ и то́ же «молоде́ц» хва́лит и́ли высме́ивает — реша́ет тон.","say":"Одно и то же молодец хвалит или высмеивает — решает тон."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он написа́л: наш «специали́ст» — зна́чит, он уважа́ет его́ как специали́ста.","right":"Кавы́чки здесь ирони́ческие: наш «специали́ст» зна́чит «так называ́емый», с насме́шкой.","why":"Ironic quotes signal «so-called», not emphasis or respect. Read them as sincere and you invert the meaning — the writer is sneering, not praising."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ironic quotation marks around a word (наш «геро́й») signal —","opts":["extra respect","«so-called», a sneer","a direct quotation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The quotes are a printed sneer — «so-called». Same charge you met in «пресло́вутый», now aimed with punctuation."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic; stretch it and go flat — the tone must say «yeah, sure».","target":"Ну-у, коне́чно, ты у нас гла́вный специали́ст…","sub":"sing-song and unconvinced — the melody carries «на са́мом де́ле нет»","want":"ну конечно ты у нас главный специалист"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Hearing it, using it — and the week","title":"Take the <em>opposite</em>, and handle with care","paras":["The rule for hearing irony: when the sunny words don't fit the grim moment, believe the moment. Answer the real meaning, not the flipped words — to a sarcastic «Молоде́ц» you don't say «спаси́бо», you own the mess. Taking irony literally is the single most classic C2 stumble; a native never does.","Using it needs a light hand and a <b>shared frame</b>. Irony works only if both of you see the gap between word and truth; aimed at the wrong person or moment it just reads as rude — or, worse, as sincere. And sarcasm cuts, so reach for it knowingly, not by reflex. The taste is the same dose you learned with particles.","This is subtext at its sharpest. Next comes the <b>pun</b> and word-play (каламбу́р), where meaning splits inside a single word; then allusion, stylization, and humour. All of week two is one skill: hearing — and saying — one thing while meaning another. You have the exact word; now you can turn it inside out on purpose."],"audio":"The rule for hearing irony: when the sunny words don't fit the grim moment, believe the moment. Answer the real meaning, not the flipped words — to a sarcastic 'Molodets' you don't say 'spasibo', you own the mess. Taking irony literally is the single most classic C2 stumble; a native never does. Using it needs a light hand and a shared frame. Irony works only if both of you see the gap between word and truth; aimed at the wrong person or moment it just reads as rude — or, worse, as sincere. And sarcasm cuts, so reach for it knowingly, not by reflex. The taste is the same dose you learned with particles. This is subtext at its sharpest. Next comes the pun and word-play, 'kalambur', where meaning splits inside a single word; then allusion, stylization, and humour. All of week two is one skill: hearing — and saying — one thing while meaning another. You have the exact word; now you can turn it inside out on purpose.","table":{"rows":[["— Молоде́ц. — Винова́т, сейча́с испра́влю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"виноват сейчас исправлю\">🔊</button>","answer the mockery, not the «praise»"],["Иро́ния тре́бует о́бщей ра́мки. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ирония требует общей рамки\">🔊</button>","it works only if both see the gap"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Believe the moment, not the sunny words.","en":"Верь моме́нту, а не со́лнечным слова́м.","say":"Верь моменту, а не солнечным словам."},{"ru":"Irony without a shared frame just sounds rude.","en":"Иро́ния без о́бщей ра́мки звучи́т про́сто гру́бо.","say":"Ирония без общей рамки звучит просто грубо."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"На сарка́зм «Ну ты ге́ний!» отвеча́ть и́скренним «Спаси́бо!».","right":"На сарка́зм «Ну ты ге́ний!» призна́ть про́мах: «Да, сплохова́л, сейча́с испра́влю».","why":"Answering sarcasm with a sincere «thank you» shows you missed the reversal. The native answers the real message — owns the blunder — and the sting dissolves."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The classic C2 mistake with irony is —","opts":["taking it literally, as sincere","using too many particles","speaking too formally"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sunny words, grim moment → it's irony. Reading it straight, as real praise, is the accent this week erases."},{"type":"choice","q":"Irony works only when —","opts":["it is written in bold","both speakers see the gap between word and truth","it is very long"],"answer":1,"explain":"Irony needs a <b>shared frame</b>. If the other person can't see the gap, it lands as rude or as sincere."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Irony sharpened to wound someone is called … (hint: сарк…). Type the Russian noun:","accept":["сарказм"],"placeholder":"сарк…","explain":"<b>сарка́зм</b> — the biting form of irony, aimed to hurt rather than to smile."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма knocks a cup off the table; Ива́н watches it shatter and delivers his verdict — perfectly flat. Эмма hears the irony and plays along. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну молоде́ц. Кра́сиво. Пря́мо ма́стер-класс.","ru":"Well done. Beautiful. A real masterclass."},{"who":"A","en":"Слы́шу сарка́зм. «Молоде́ц» тут зна́чит «растя́па».","ru":"I hear the sarcasm. “Well done” here means “butterfingers”."},{"who":"B","en":"То́же мне перево́дчик — ча́шку удержа́ть не мо́жет.","ru":"Some translator — can't even hold a cup."},{"who":"A","en":"Да-да, гига́нт то́чности. Вот тебе́ и самоиро́ния.","ru":"Yes, yes, a giant of precision. There's your self-irony for you."},{"who":"B","en":"О, ты уже́ отвеча́ешь мне мои́м же то́ном.","ru":"Oh, you're already answering me in my own tone."},{"who":"A","en":"А как же. Приняла́ бы за чи́стую моне́ту — оби́делась бы. А так — смешно́.","ru":"Naturally. Take it at face value and I'd be hurt. This way — it's funny."},{"who":"B","en":"Ра́ньше ты на «молоде́ц» говори́ла «спаси́бо».","ru":"You used to answer “well done” with “thank you”."},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше — да. Тепе́рь слы́шу, когда́ хва́лят наоборо́т.","ru":"Before — yes. Now I hear when the praise runs backwards."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for saying the opposite — the flip, the sneer, the biting thanks. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"иро́ния","ru":"irony (can be light, even fond)","ex":"Его́ иро́ния о́чень то́нкая."},{"en":"сарка́зм","ru":"sarcasm (biting, meant to wound)","ex":"В отве́те скво́зит сарка́зм."},{"en":"антифра́зис","ru":"antiphrasis (a word = its opposite)","ex":"Иро́ния стро́ится на антифра́зисе."},{"en":"насме́шка","ru":"a jibe, mockery","ex":"В её го́лосе — насме́шка."},{"en":"высме́ивать","ru":"to ridicule, to mock","ex":"Он высме́ивает чужи́е оши́бки."},{"en":"то́же мне","ru":"«some … you are!» (a sneer)","ex":"То́же мне знато́к!"},{"en":"как же","ru":"«yeah, right!» (dismissal)","ex":"Он придёт во́время? Как же!"},{"en":"удружи́ть","ru":"to do a «favour» (ironic)","ex":"Ну и удружи́л ты мне!"},{"en":"самоиро́ния","ru":"self-irony","ex":"Его́ спаса́ет то́лько самоиро́ния."},{"en":"кавы́чки","ru":"quotation marks","ex":"Ирони́ческие кавы́чки ме́няют смысл."},{"en":"наоборо́т","ru":"the other way round, opposite","ex":"Он име́л в виду́ наоборо́т."},{"en":"язви́ть","ru":"to be sarcastic, to needle","ex":"Переста́нь язви́ть, я всё по́нял."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each ironic phrase to what it really means.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each phrase on the left onto its real meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["То́же мне геро́й!","«some hero you are» — a sneer"],["Он изви́нится? Как же!","«yeah, right» — he certainly won't"],["Спаси́бо, удружи́л!","«thanks a lot» — you made it worse"],["Ну ты ге́ний, коне́чно.","flat = mockery, not a compliment"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Антифра́зис» means using a word to say —","opts":["exactly what it means","its own opposite","something more formal"],"answer":1},{"q":"иро́ния vs сарка́зм — the main difference is —","opts":["irony can be fond; sarcasm means to wound","sarcasm is gentler","there is none"],"answer":0},{"q":"«То́же мне специали́ст!» means —","opts":["a real expert","a licensed specialist","some expert you are (a sneer)"],"answer":2},{"q":"Ironic quotation marks around наш «геро́й» signal —","opts":["«so-called», a sneer","added respect","a real quotation"],"answer":0},{"q":"The classic C2 mistake with irony is —","opts":["overusing particles","taking it literally, as sincere","being too formal"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"The same words, said to mean their opposite — and the signals that tell you so. Read each line aloud and let the flip land.","title":"Похвала́, ска́занная наоборо́т","sentences":["«Ну ты молоде́ц!» — говоря́т над разби́той ча́шкой, и э́то во́все не похвала́.","Иро́ния — э́то похвала́, ска́занная наоборо́т, а сарка́зм — та же похвала́, но с жа́лом.","Ру́сский ме́тит иро́нию го́лосом и кавы́чками: наш «геро́й» зна́чит «так называ́емый».","Иностра́нец слы́шит сла́дкие слова́ и говори́т «спаси́бо» — и попада́ет впроса́к.","Услы́шать, когда́ хва́лят наоборо́т, — после́дний шаг к ру́сскому у́ху."],"translation":"“Well done, you!” — said over a shattered cup, and it's no praise at all. Irony is praise said backwards; sarcasm is the same praise, but with a sting. A Russian marks irony with voice and quotation marks: our “hero”, in quotes, means “so-called”. A foreigner hears the sweet words and says “thank you” — and walks right into it. To hear when praise runs backwards is the last step to a Russian ear."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to say the opposite.","prompt":"Write 4–5 sentences. First, describe a small mishap (a spilled coffee, a late reply, a clumsy plan). Then give an IRONIC line someone says about it — sunny words, opposite meaning — and mark the irony two ways: with a ready-made ironic phrase (то́же мне / как же / спаси́бо, удружи́л) OR ironic quotes, and by describing the flat tone. Finally, show the native reply that answers the real meaning, not the words.","hint":"Irony is antiphrasis — praise said backwards. Show HOW it's marked (tone, quotes, a set phrase), and don't answer it with a literal «спаси́бо» 🙂","example":"Анто́н опроки́нул на клавиату́ру ко́фе и зали́л её. «Ну, ты, коне́чно, ге́ний», — ро́вным то́ном сказа́ла О́льга, и в э́том «ге́ний» была́ одна́ насме́шка. То́же мне ма́стер: кавы́чки слы́шались да́же в го́лосе. Анто́н не стал говори́ть «спаси́бо» — он призна́л: «Да, растя́па, сейча́с всё вы́сушу»."}},"10":{"day":10,"week":"02","themeRu":"Playing with words","themeEn":"Языкова́я игра́ и каламбу́р","intro":"Yesterday a sentence meant the <b>opposite</b> of its words — that was irony. Today the words themselves start to play: two meanings held in one breath, sounds that rhyme into a joke, a dead idiom yanked suddenly literal. This is <b>языкова́я игра́</b> (wordplay) and its sharpest tool, the <b>каламбу́р</b> (pun). It sits at the very top of comprehension — a headline, a shop sign, an ad is funny only if you hear the seam split. Эмма spent a whole day this week on three words of wordplay and a page of notes, and never did translate them.","introAudio":"Yesterday a sentence meant the opposite of its words — that was irony. Today the words themselves start to play: two meanings held in one breath, sounds that rhyme into a joke, a dead idiom yanked suddenly literal. This is 'yazykovaya igra', wordplay, and its sharpest tool, the 'kalambur', the pun. It sits at the very top of comprehension — a headline, a shop sign, an ad is funny only if you hear the seam split. Emma spent a whole day this week on three words of wordplay and a page of notes, and never did translate them.","goals":["Hear a <b>каламбу́р</b> work: two meanings of one form (one word, one sound) held at the same instant","Catch <b>паро́нимы</b> pulled together — words that nearly sound alike, jammed side by side for a spark","Spot an idiom taken suddenly literally (<b>буквализа́ция</b>) and two words welded into one (<b>контамина́ция</b>)","Read the wordplay in a headline or ad the way a native does — and feel why it barely survives translation"],"learned":["Heard a каламбу́р work: two meanings of one form held at once","Caught paronyms pulled together for a spark (служи́ть / прислу́живаться)","Spotted an idiom taken literally and two words blended into one (контамина́ция)","Read the wordplay in a headline or ad — and felt why it barely translates"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday — irony, saying the opposite and meaning it. Hold that ear; today the words play in the open.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday — irony, saying the opposite and meaning it. Hold that ear; today the words play in the open.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's irony — an old friend.","q":"«Ну ты ге́ний, коне́чно!» — said while staring at a smashed cup. He means —","opts":["genuine admiration","the opposite — mockery","a real question"],"answer":1,"explain":"Praise words over a disaster → irony. He means the reverse: «ну ты ге́ний» here is a jab, not a compliment."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian term. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The device of saying the opposite of what you mean is called … . Type the Russian noun (hint: иро…):","accept":["ирония"],"placeholder":"иро…","explain":"<b>иро́ния</b> — irony. Yesterday's tool; today's wordplay often rides on top of it."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он оказа́л мне „услу́гу“» — why the quotes around «услу́гу»?","opts":["it is a book title","to mark irony — it was no favour at all","it is a direct quotation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ironic quotes flip the word: the «услу́га» was the opposite of a favour. You read the wink instantly now."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Two meanings, one breath","title":"The <em>каламбу́р</em>: one form that means two things at once","paras":["Wordplay — <b>языкова́я игра́</b> — is language catching sight of itself. Its sharpest form is the <b>каламбу́р</b> (the pun): one form — one sound, one word — carrying two meanings at the very same instant, so both fire and collide. English calls it a pun and mostly tolerates it; Russian adores it and builds shop signs, headlines and whole comic traditions on it.","The cleanest engine is <b>многозна́чность</b> (polysemy — one word, several senses) or outright homonymy. Take <b>язы́к</b>: a tongue and a language. Take <b>ключ</b>: a key and a spring of water. Say the word once and a native hears both doors swing open — that double swing is the joke.","The great master of the Russian pun, the poet Дми́трий Мина́ев, rhymed: «О́бласть рифм — моя́ стихи́я, / И легко́ пишу́ стихи́ я». <b>стихи́я</b> — my element — against <b>стихи́ я</b> — verses, I. One string of sounds split two ways; that split, caught in a blink, <i>is</i> the whole art."],"audio":"Wordplay — 'yazykovaya igra' — is language catching sight of itself. Its sharpest form is the 'kalambur', the pun: one form, one sound, one word, carrying two meanings at the very same instant, so both fire and collide. English calls it a pun and mostly tolerates it; Russian adores it and builds shop signs, headlines and whole comic traditions on it. The cleanest engine is 'mnogoznachnost', polysemy, one word with several senses, or outright homonymy. Take 'yazyk': a tongue and a language. Take 'klyuch': a key and a spring of water. Say the word once and a native hears both doors swing open — that double swing is the joke. The great master of the Russian pun, the poet Dmitry Minaev, rhymed: 'Oblast rifm — moya stikhiya, i legko pishu stikhi ya'. 'Stikhiya', my element, against 'stikhi ya', verses, I. One string of sounds split two ways; that split, caught in a blink, is the whole art.","table":{"rows":[["язы́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"язык\">🔊</button>","a tongue / a language"],["ключ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ключ\">🔊</button>","a key / a spring of water"],["коса́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"коса\">🔊</button>","a braid / a scythe / a sandbar"],["лук <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лук\">🔊</button>","an onion / a bow (for arrows)"],["мир <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мир\">🔊</button>","peace / the world"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In Minaev, «стихи́я» and «стихи́ я» are one sound, two meanings.","en":"У Мина́ева «стихи́я» и «стихи́ я» — одни́ зву́ки, два смы́сла.","say":"У Минаева стихия и стихи я — одни звуки, два смысла."},{"ru":"The one word «ключ» opens both a door and a spring — the pun writes itself.","en":"Одно́ сло́во «ключ» открыва́ет и дверь, и родни́к — каламбу́р сам гото́в.","say":"Одно слово ключ открывает и дверь, и родник — каламбур сам готов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Каламбу́р — э́то про́сто опи́ска, слу́чайно смешна́я.»","right":"«Каламбу́р — э́то наме́ренная игра́ двумя́ смы́слами.»","why":"A pun is not a slip that came out funny by accident — it is a <b>deliberate</b> holding of two meanings in one form. Reading it as an error (or missing it entirely) is the C2-level miss: the whole point is that the writer meant both senses."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"In a каламбу́р, one form —","opts":["means the same thing in two places","carries two different meanings at once","is simply misspelled"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pun holds <b>two meanings in one form</b>. Both senses are meant, and they collide — that collision is the joke."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word can anchor a pun because it carries two everyday senses?","opts":["ключ (a key / a spring)","стул (a chair)","окно́ (a window)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ключ</b> is one form with two live meanings — a key and a spring — so it can hold both at once. A pun needs exactly that double life."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Words that almost rhyme","title":"Paronyms pulled together — <em>служи́ть / прислу́живаться</em>","paras":["The second engine is sound. <b>Паро́нимы</b> (paronyms) are words that sound almost identical but mean different things: <b>наде́ть / оде́ть</b>, <b>эффе́ктный / эффекти́вный</b>, <b>чистота́ / чи́сто</b>. Set two of them shoulder to shoulder and the near-echo strikes a spark. The device has a grand name — пароними́ческая аттра́кция (paronymic attraction) — and a very plain feel: words that rhyme straight into a point.","Грибое́дов did it in one line every Russian carries by heart: «Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно». <b>служи́ть</b> — to serve honourably — beside <b>прислу́живаться</b> — to toady, to fawn. Same root, one syllable apart, worlds apart in worth. The paronym isn't decoration here; it <i>is</i> the argument.","Ads live on the same trick. The washing-powder line «Чистота́ — чи́сто „Тайд“» leans <b>чистота́</b> (cleanliness) on <b>чи́сто</b> (purely) until the brand rings like a promise. Hear the near-twins pull together and you've caught the joke before it's finished."],"audio":"The second engine is sound. 'Paronimy', paronyms, are words that sound almost identical but mean different things: 'nadet / odet', 'effektny / effektivny', 'chistota / chisto'. Set two of them shoulder to shoulder and the near-echo strikes a spark. The device has a grand name — paronimicheskaya attraktsiya, paronymic attraction — and a very plain feel: words that rhyme straight into a point. Griboedov did it in one line every Russian carries by heart: 'Sluzhit by rad — prisluzhivatsya toshno'. 'Sluzhit', to serve honourably, beside 'prisluzhivatsya', to toady, to fawn. Same root, one syllable apart, worlds apart in worth. The paronym isn't decoration here; it is the argument. Ads live on the same trick. The washing-powder line 'Chistota — chisto Tide' leans 'chistota', cleanliness, on 'chisto', purely, until the brand rings like a promise. Hear the near-twins pull together and you've caught the joke before it's finished.","table":{"rows":[["служи́ть / прислу́живаться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"служить, прислуживаться\">🔊</button>","to serve (honourably) / to toady, fawn"],["наде́ть / оде́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"надеть, одеть\">🔊</button>","put on (yourself) / dress (someone)"],["эффе́ктный / эффекти́вный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"эффектный, эффективный\">🔊</button>","striking to look at / effective, working"],["чистота́ / чи́сто <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чистота, чисто\">🔊</button>","cleanliness / purely, simply"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Griboedov: «I'd gladly serve — but toadying sickens me.»","en":"Грибое́дов: «Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно.»","say":"Грибоедов: Служить бы рад — прислуживаться тошно."},{"ru":"In the ad: «Cleanliness — purely Tide.»","en":"В рекла́ме: «Чистота́ — чи́сто „Тайд“.»","say":"В рекламе: Чистота — чисто Тайд."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Она́ наде́ла ребёнка потепле́е.»","right":"«Она́ оде́ла ребёнка потепле́е.»","why":"Paronyms are a joke on purpose but a trap by accident. <b>наде́ть</b> puts a thing onto yourself (наде́ть ша́пку); <b>оде́ть</b> dresses a person (оде́ть ребёнка). A poet pulls the twins together to make a point; a learner who confuses them just makes the classic slip."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build Griboedov's famous line.","q":"Build the line: “I'd gladly serve — but toadying sickens me.”","want":"Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно","answer":"Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно"},{"type":"choice","q":"Why is «служи́ть / прислу́живаться» a paronymic play?","opts":["near-identical sound, opposite worth","totally unrelated sounds","exactly the same meaning"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two words almost rhyme yet split honour from grovelling. That near-echo across a moral gap is paronymic attraction."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Taking it literally, blending it","title":"Waking a dead idiom — and welding two words into one","paras":["Two more moves. First, <b>буквализа́ция</b> (literalising): seize a frozen idiom and suddenly mean it literally. «Взять себя́ в ру́ки» means to pull yourself together — until a joke reclaims the hand image: «Он взял себя́ в ру́ки — и хороше́нько встряхну́л». The idiom and its buried picture flash at the same moment.","Second, <b>контамина́ция</b> (blending): fuse two words into one impossible word so both show through. The furniture-shop slogan «Ме́бель без пробле́бель» welds <b>ме́бель</b> into <b>без пробле́м</b> — no such word as «пробле́бель» exists, and that's exactly the joke: you hear «ме́бель» and «пробле́м» in a single breath.","These are the ad-writer's and headline-writer's daily tools. A serious paper will top a piece on a lost match with a bent proverb; the reader who hears the untouched original underneath gets a second, wry layer free of charge."],"audio":"Two more moves. First, 'bukvalizatsiya', literalising: seize a frozen idiom and suddenly mean it literally. 'Vzyat sebya v ruki' means to pull yourself together — until a joke reclaims the hand image: 'On vzyal sebya v ruki — i khoroshenko vstryakhnul', he took himself in hand and gave himself a good shake. The idiom and its buried picture flash at the same moment. Second, 'kontaminatsiya', blending: fuse two words into one impossible word so both show through. The furniture-shop slogan 'Mebel bez problebel' welds 'mebel', furniture, into 'bez problem', without problems — no such word as 'problebel' exists, and that's exactly the joke: you hear 'mebel' and 'problem' in a single breath. These are the ad-writer's and headline-writer's daily tools. A serious paper will top a piece on a lost match with a bent proverb; the reader who hears the untouched original underneath gets a second, wry layer free of charge.","table":{"rows":[["взять себя́ в ру́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"взять себя в руки\">🔊</button>","pull yourself together (lit. take yourself in hand)"],["ве́шать лапшу́ на у́ши <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вешать лапшу на уши\">🔊</button>","to fool sb (lit. hang noodles on the ears)"],["сесть в лу́жу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сесть в лужу\">🔊</button>","to end up looking foolish (lit. sit in a puddle)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"In a joke, «take yourself in hand» is taken literally.","en":"В шу́тке «взять себя́ в ру́ки» беру́т буква́льно.","say":"В шутке взять себя в руки берут буквально."},{"ru":"In the slogan: «Мебель без проблебель» — two words fused into one.","en":"В сло́гане: «Ме́бель без пробле́бель» — два сло́ва спла́влены в одно́.","say":"В слогане: Мебель без проблебель — два слова сплавлены в одно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я купи́л ме́бель без пробле́бель.» (as a plain statement)","right":"«Я купи́л ме́бель без пробле́м.»","why":"«Пробле́бель» lives only inside the joke-slogan — it is a one-off blend, not a real word. Wheeled out in ordinary speech it just sounds broken. The neutral, real phrase is <b>без пробле́м</b>. Enjoy the blend; don't bank it as vocabulary."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Taking «взять себя́ в ру́ки» literally for a joke is an example of —","opts":["буквализа́ция — waking the idiom's literal picture","a paronym pair","a plain synonym"],"answer":0,"explain":"The frozen idiom is suddenly meant by its picture — that reanimation is <b>буквализа́ция</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian term. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The move of fusing two words into one comic word («пробле́бель») is called … . Type it (hint: конт…):","accept":["контаминация"],"placeholder":"конт…","explain":"<b>контамина́ция</b> — blending. Two words melt into one impossible word, and you hear both at once."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Wordplay in the wild + the week","title":"The last thing you understand — and the first thing that won't translate","paras":["Put the moves together and the playful surface of Russian opens up: ad slogans, newspaper headlines, shop signs, the punning name of a café. Each is often a bent idiom, a pun on a brand, a pair of paronyms snapped into place. Miss the seam and the text is merely odd; hear it and you're inside the joke with everyone else.","This is also where a translator sweats. A каламбу́р lives in one language's exact sounds and double senses; carry the words across and the play falls out on the floor. Эмма's working rule: don't translate the pun — build a fresh one that does the same job. And when nothing works, the honest footnote is <b>«непереводи́мая игра́ слов»</b> — untranslatable wordplay.","Wordplay leans on what you already own — connotation and word-building from week one, plus the idioms you've banked. Tomorrow the play widens from single words to whole quoted phrases the culture keeps repeating — <b>аллю́зии и прецеде́нтные фено́мены</b>. Then borrowing a voice on purpose (стилиза́ция), then the joke itself (анекдо́т). The surface of Russian is about to get gloriously crowded — and you'll hear every layer."],"audio":"Put the moves together and the playful surface of Russian opens up: ad slogans, newspaper headlines, shop signs, the punning name of a cafe. Each is often a bent idiom, a pun on a brand, a pair of paronyms snapped into place. Miss the seam and the text is merely odd; hear it and you're inside the joke with everyone else. This is also where a translator sweats. A 'kalambur' lives in one language's exact sounds and double senses; carry the words across and the play falls out on the floor. Emma's working rule: don't translate the pun — build a fresh one that does the same job. And when nothing works, the honest footnote is 'neperevodimaya igra slov', untranslatable wordplay. Wordplay leans on what you already own — connotation and word-building from week one, plus the idioms you've banked. Tomorrow the play widens from single words to whole quoted phrases the culture keeps repeating — 'allyuzii i pretsedentnye fenomeny'. Then borrowing a voice on purpose, stilizatsiya, then the joke itself, anekdot. The surface of Russian is about to get gloriously crowded — and you'll hear every layer.","examples":[{"ru":"A headline bends a proverb — and the reader hears both layers.","en":"Заголо́вок гнёт посло́вицу — и чита́тель слы́шит о́ба сло́я.","say":"Заголовок гнёт пословицу — и читатель слышит оба слоя."},{"ru":"Often the most honest note is «untranslatable wordplay».","en":"Ча́сто честне́е всего́ поме́тка: «непереводи́мая игра́ слов».","say":"Часто честнее всего пометка: непереводимая игра слов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я перевёл каламбу́р сло́во в сло́во — и он переста́л быть смешны́м.»","right":"«Я не перевёл каламбу́р — я сочини́л но́вый на том же ме́сте.»","why":"Word-for-word is the one thing a pun cannot survive — its two meanings sit in <i>these</i> sounds. The master's move is re-creation: a new pun that does the same job, or an honest «непереводи́мая игра́ слов»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A pun rarely survives a word-for-word translation because it lives in —","opts":["universal logic, the same in every language","grammar rules alone","one language's exact sounds and double meanings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The two senses ride on <i>these</i> sounds. Swap the words and the collision — the whole joke — is gone."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it with a little grin — you're catching the joke, not reporting it.","target":"Непереводи́мая игра́ слов — но по-ру́сски она́ звучи́т.","sub":"let «звучи́т» land like a small punchline","want":"непереводимая игра слов но по русски она звучит"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The word for a pun — a play on two meanings of one form. Type it (hint: кала…):","accept":["каламбур"],"placeholder":"кала…","explain":"<b>каламбу́р</b> — a pun. One form, two meanings, held at once."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is at Ле́на's café when a new sign goes up across the street. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Смотри́, вы́веску напро́тив пове́сили: «Всё бу́дет в шокола́де».","ru":"Look, they've hung a sign across the way: «Everything'll be in chocolate»."},{"who":"A","en":"«В шокола́де» — и про конфе́ты, и «всё бу́дет хорошо́». Два смы́сла в трёх слова́х.","ru":"«В шоколаде» — both the actual chocolate and «everything'll be fine». Two meanings in three words."},{"who":"B","en":"А я про́сто поду́мала, что там сла́сти прода́ют.","ru":"And I just thought they were selling sweets."},{"who":"A","en":"В том и фо́кус: слы́шишь идио́му — и карти́нку под ней сра́зу.","ru":"That's the trick: you hear the idiom — and the picture right under it."},{"who":"B","en":"И на рабо́те тебе́ таки́е попада́ются?","ru":"And do you run into ones like that at work?"},{"who":"A","en":"Це́лый день. Вчера́ би́лась над одни́м каламбу́ром — исписа́ла це́лую страни́цу.","ru":"All day. Yesterday I fought one pun — filled a whole page with notes."},{"who":"B","en":"И перевела́ в конце́ концо́в?","ru":"And did you translate it in the end?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не перевела́ — сочини́ла но́вый. Каламбу́р не перево́дят, его́ пересоздаю́т.","ru":"I didn't translate it — I made a new one. You don't translate a pun, you rebuild it."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the play: the vocabulary of the pun. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"каламбу́р","ru":"a pun, wordplay","ex":"Э́тот заголо́вок — то́нкий каламбу́р."},{"en":"языкова́я игра́","ru":"wordplay (lit. language play)","ex":"Рекла́ма живёт языково́й игро́й."},{"en":"паро́ним","ru":"a paronym (a near-sounding word)","ex":"«Наде́ть» и «оде́ть» — паро́нимы."},{"en":"многозна́чность","ru":"polysemy (many meanings in one word)","ex":"Каламбу́р рождён из многозна́чности."},{"en":"омо́ним","ru":"a homonym","ex":"«Ключ» — класси́ческий омо́ним."},{"en":"буквализа́ция","ru":"literalising an idiom","ex":"Шу́тка на буквализа́ции идио́мы."},{"en":"контамина́ция","ru":"blending two words into one","ex":"«Пробле́бель» — э́то контамина́ция."},{"en":"обы́грывать","ru":"to play on, milk (a word)","ex":"А́втор обы́грывает два смы́сла сра́зу."},{"en":"остроу́мный","ru":"witty, sharp","ex":"О́чень остроу́мный заголо́вок."},{"en":"намёк","ru":"a hint, an allusion","ex":"В сло́ве спря́тан то́нкий намёк."},{"en":"вы́веска","ru":"a shop sign","ex":"На вы́веске — це́лый каламбу́р."},{"en":"заголо́вок","ru":"a headline","ex":"Заголо́вок гнёт изве́стную посло́вицу."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each device to the way it makes the play — the mechanism, not the example.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its mechanism — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["каламбу́р","two meanings of one form, held at once"],["паро́нимы","near-sounding words jammed together for a spark"],["буквализа́ция","a frozen idiom taken suddenly literally"],["контамина́ция","two words welded into one comic word"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A каламбу́р holds —","opts":["two meanings in one form","two forms of one meaning","a simple spelling error"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Служи́ть — прислу́живаться» is a play on —","opts":["homonyms (one form, two senses)","paronyms (near-sounding words)","exact synonyms"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ме́бель без пробле́бель» is —","opts":["an old proverb","a paronym pair","контамина́ция — a blend of two words"],"answer":2},{"q":"Taking «взять себя́ в ру́ки» literally for a joke is —","opts":["a plain synonym","буквализа́ция — the idiom taken literally","connotation"],"answer":1},{"q":"A pun resists word-for-word translation because —","opts":["it is grammatically wrong","it is always too long","it lives in one language's sounds and double senses"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short reflection on why Russians play with words everywhere — and what the play asks of your ear. Read each line aloud.","title":"Шов, где схо́дятся смы́слы","sentences":["Ру́сский лю́бит игра́ть слова́ми — и де́лает э́то повсю́ду: на вы́веске, в заголо́вке, в рекла́ме.","Одно́ сло́во ста́вят так, что́бы в нём зазвуча́ли сра́зу два смы́сла.","«Ключ» открыва́ет и дверь, и родни́к — и в э́том сты́ке уже́ спря́тана шу́тка.","Кто слы́шит шов, где смы́слы схо́дятся, тот смеётся вме́сте со все́ми.","А кто слы́шит то́лько пове́рхность — для того́ текст про́сто стра́нен.","Поэ́тому каламбу́р — не украше́ние, а ти́хая прове́рка слу́ха."],"translation":"Russians love to play with words — and they do it everywhere: on a shop sign, in a headline, in an ad. A single word is set so that two meanings ring out of it at once. «Ключ» opens both a door and a spring — and in that join a joke is already hidden. Whoever hears the seam where the meanings meet laughs along with everyone else. And whoever hears only the surface — for them the text is merely strange. That is why a pun is not decoration but a quiet test of the ear."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to catch — or make — a pun.","prompt":"Find or invent ONE piece of Russian wordplay: a pun on a two-meaning word (ключ, язы́к, коса́), a pair of paronyms pulled together, or an idiom taken literally. Write it out, then in 2–3 sentences explain exactly HOW it works — which two meanings (or which near-twin sounds) collide — and why a word-for-word English version would lose it. 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Ask the каламбу́р question of your example: what single form is doing double duty here — one sound, two senses? Name both senses out loud, then check whether English keeps them in one word 🙂","example":"Возьмём сло́во «ключ»: он и от две́ри, и бьёт из земли́ в лесу́. Заголо́вок «Ключ к ро́днику» игра́ет обо́ими смы́слами сра́зу — и «как найти́ исто́чник», и «клю́чик к та́йне». По-англи́йски «key» и «spring» — ра́зные слова́, и весь каламбу́р растворя́ется без оста́тка."}},"11":{"day":11,"week":"02","themeRu":"Everyone is quoting something","themeEn":"Аллю́зии и прецеде́нтные фено́мены","intro":"Yesterday single words played tricks. Today whole phrases do — phrases you never see marked as quotations. A Russian ends a story with «Рукопи́си не горя́т» and, with no footnote, drops all of Булга́ков into the room. Speech at native density is stitched through with these <b>прецеде́нтные фено́мены</b> (precedent phenomena — the quotes, names and scenes a whole culture shares). At C1 you learned to <b>recognise</b> a few. This month asks the native's other half: to catch every echo — and to <b>drop</b> one yourself, exactly to the point.","introAudio":"Yesterday single words played tricks. Today whole phrases do — phrases you never see marked as quotations. A Russian ends a story with 'Rukopisi ne goryat', manuscripts don't burn, and with no footnote drops all of Bulgakov into the room. Speech at native density is stitched through with these 'pretsedentnye fenomeny', precedent phenomena — the quotes, names and scenes a whole culture shares. At C1 you learned to recognise a few. This month asks the native's other half: to catch every echo — and to drop one yourself, exactly to the point.","goals":["Understand a <b>прецеде́нтный фено́мен</b>: a phrase whose real meaning is the source it points to, not its literal words","Tell apart the four shapes — <b>и́мя · выска́зывание · текст · ситуа́ция</b> (name / phrase / text / situation)","Recognise the biggest wells: Крыло́в, Грибое́дов, Пу́шкин, Толсто́й, Булга́ков, cinema, songs, fairy tales","Do the C2 half — <b>drop</b> a real quote to the point, lightly, without over-reaching or misquoting"],"learned":["Grasped that a precedent phrase means its source, not its literal words","Told apart name / phrase / text / situation (Плю́шкин · «Рукопи́си не горя́т» · «Го́ре от ума́» · «а коро́ль-то го́лый»)","Recognised the main wells — Крыло́в, Грибое́дов, Толсто́й, Булга́ков, film and fairy tale","Dropped a real quote to the point — and learned the traps of misquoting and over-reaching"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday — wordplay: the pun, paronyms, an idiom taken literally. Then whole phrases start quoting.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday — wordplay: the pun, paronyms, an idiom taken literally. Then whole phrases start quoting.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's wordplay.","q":"«Служи́ть бы рад — прислу́живаться то́шно» plays on —","opts":["paronyms — near-sounding words, opposite worth","homonyms — one form, two senses","exact synonyms"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two words almost rhyme yet split honour from grovelling — paronymic attraction, Грибое́дов's own line."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The word for a pun — one form, two meanings at once. Type it (hint: кала…):","accept":["каламбур"],"placeholder":"кала…","explain":"<b>каламбу́р</b> — a pun. Yesterday's tool; today the play jumps from a word to a whole phrase."},{"type":"choice","q":"Taking «взять себя́ в ру́ки» literally, for a joke, is —","opts":["буквализа́ция — the idiom taken literally","контамина́ция — a blend of two words","connotation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The frozen idiom is suddenly meant by its hand-picture — that reanimation is <b>буквализа́ция</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A phrase that carries its whole story","title":"<em>Прецеде́нтные фено́мены</em>: quoting without the quotation marks","paras":["Native speech is stitched through with quotations you never see marked. Someone says <b>«Рукопи́си не горя́т»</b> and, without a footnote, drops the whole weight of Булга́ков's «Ма́стер и Маргари́та» into the conversation — a novel's faith that truth cannot be destroyed, in four words. These are <b>прецеде́нтные фено́мены</b> (precedent phenomena): a phrase, a name, a scene the whole culture shares, so that naming it once summons all of it.","The catch is that the meaning is not in the words — it's in the source they point to. <b>«Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли»</b> (Антуа́н де Сент-Экзюпери́, «Ма́ленький принц», в перево́де Но́ры Галь) says far more than its literal line: it carries the fox, the rose, a whole ethic of responsibility for what you've tamed. Hear only the words and you've missed nine-tenths of it.","At C1 you learned to <b>recognise</b> a handful of winged words. C2 asks for the native's other half: to <b>drop</b> one yourself, exactly on target, at the right moment — and to catch the wink the instant someone drops one on you."],"audio":"Native speech is stitched through with quotations you never see marked. Someone says 'Rukopisi ne goryat', manuscripts don't burn, and without a footnote drops the whole weight of Bulgakov's Master and Margarita into the conversation — a novel's faith that truth cannot be destroyed, in four words. These are 'pretsedentnye fenomeny', precedent phenomena: a phrase, a name, a scene the whole culture shares, so that naming it once summons all of it. The catch is that the meaning is not in the words — it's in the source they point to. 'My v otvete za tekh, kogo priruchili', we are responsible for those we have tamed — Antoine de Saint-Exupery, The Little Prince, in Nora Gal's translation — says far more than its literal line: it carries the fox, the rose, a whole ethic of responsibility for what you've tamed. Hear only the words and you've missed nine-tenths of it. At C1 you learned to recognise a handful of winged words. C2 asks for the native's other half: to drop one yourself, exactly on target, at the right moment — and to catch the wink the instant someone drops one on you.","examples":[{"ru":"«Manuscripts don't burn» — and all of Bulgakov stands there in three words.","en":"«Рукопи́си не горя́т» — и в трёх слова́х весь Булга́ков.","say":"Рукописи не горят — и в трёх словах весь Булгаков."},{"ru":"«We are responsible for those we've tamed» — a whole ethic in one line (Saint-Exupéry).","en":"«Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли» — це́лая э́тика в одно́й стро́чке.","say":"Мы в ответе за тех, кого приручили — целая этика в одной строчке."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«„Рукопи́си не горя́т“ — зна́чит, бума́га не воспламеня́ется?»","right":"«„Рукопи́си не горя́т“ — зна́чит, настоя́щее нельзя́ уничто́жить.»","why":"Taken at face value it's a claim about paper. As a precedent phrase from Булга́ков it means truth and art survive their persecutors. Reading the allusion literally — hearing only the words — is the classic C2 miss."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"A прецеде́нтное выска́зывание carries its real meaning mainly in —","opts":["its literal, dictionary words","the source it points to","its grammatical structure"],"answer":1,"explain":"The words are just a pointer; the meaning is the whole story behind them. Miss the source and you've missed the phrase."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Рукопи́си не горя́т» comes from —","opts":["Булга́ков, «Ма́стер и Маргари́та»","an old folk proverb","a modern pop song"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's Вола́нд's line in Булга́ков's «Ма́стер и Маргари́та» — now a byword for the survival of truth and art."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Four shapes of a shared memory","title":"Name · phrase · text · situation — the four <em>прецеде́нтные фено́мены</em>","paras":["Precedent phenomena come in four shapes. A <b>прецеде́нтное и́мя</b> (a name) is a character turned common noun: call someone <b>Плю́шкин</b> and you've said «miser» with all of Го́голь's «Мёртвые ду́ши» behind it; <b>Обло́мов</b> is sofa-bound inertia (Гончаро́в); <b>Хлестако́в</b> a swaggering impostor (Го́голь, «Ревизо́р»).","A <b>прецеде́нтное выска́зывание</b> (a phrase) is a winged line: <b>«А Ва́ська слу́шает да ест»</b> (Крыло́в) — he ignores you and carries right on. A <b>прецеде́нтный текст</b> is the whole work standing behind the phrase — «Го́ре от ума́», «Ма́ленький принц». And a <b>прецеде́нтная ситуа́ция</b> (a situation) is a shared scene: <b>«А коро́ль-то го́лый!»</b> (Андерсен) — the moment someone finally says the obvious truth aloud.","You don't memorise a list; you soak them up the way a native child does — from books, films, cartoons, songs. What matters at C2 is hearing the pointer fire: the instant a plain word turns out to be a door into a story everyone but you just walked through."],"audio":"Precedent phenomena come in four shapes. A 'pretsedentnoe imya', a name, is a character turned common noun: call someone 'Plyushkin' and you've said miser with all of Gogol's Dead Souls behind it; 'Oblomov' is sofa-bound inertia, from Goncharov; 'Khlestakov' a swaggering impostor, from Gogol's Inspector General. A 'pretsedentnoe vyskazyvanie', a phrase, is a winged line: 'A Vaska slushaet da est', Krylov — he ignores you and carries right on. A 'pretsedentny tekst' is the whole work standing behind the phrase — Woe from Wit, The Little Prince. And a 'pretsedentnaya situatsiya', a situation, is a shared scene: 'A korol-to goly', the emperor has no clothes, from Andersen — the moment someone finally says the obvious truth aloud. You don't memorise a list; you soak them up the way a native child does — from books, films, cartoons, songs. What matters at C2 is hearing the pointer fire: the instant a plain word turns out to be a door into a story everyone but you just walked through.","table":{"rows":[["и́мя: Плю́шкин <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"имя, Плюшкин\">🔊</button>","a name turned byword — a miser (Го́голь)"],["выска́зывание: «А Ва́ська слу́шает да ест» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"высказывание, а Васька слушает да ест\">🔊</button>","a winged phrase — he ignores you and keeps on (Крыло́в)"],["текст: «Го́ре от ума́» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"текст, горе от ума\">🔊</button>","the whole work behind the phrases (Грибое́дов)"],["ситуа́ция: «А коро́ль-то го́лый» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ситуация, а король-то голый\">🔊</button>","a shared scene — naming the obvious truth (Андерсен)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"He's a total Обломов: there's a plan, but no motion.","en":"Он про́сто Обло́мов: план есть — движе́ния нет.","say":"Он просто Обломов: план есть — движения нет."},{"ru":"«And Vaska listens on, and eats» — he said, about the boss (Krylov).","en":"«А Ва́ська слу́шает да ест» — сказа́л он про нача́льника.","say":"А Васька слушает да ест — сказал он про начальника."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Обло́мов — э́то, ка́жется, како́й-то дре́вний царь?»","right":"«Обло́мов — геро́й Гончаро́ва, си́мвол ле́ни и засто́я.»","why":"Обло́мов isn't a historical figure but a <b>precedent name</b> from Гончаро́в's novel — the byword for inert, sofa-bound idleness. Not knowing the source flattens a rich allusion into a random name."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Call someone «Плю́шкин» and you mean —","opts":["a generous, open-handed host","a miser (from Го́голь's «Мёртвые ду́ши»)","a very fast runner"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Плю́шкин</b> is Го́голь's hoarder — a precedent name that says «miser» with a whole novel behind it."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the name. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The precedent name for sofa-bound inertia, from Гончаро́в's novel. Type it (hint: Обл…):","accept":["обломов"],"placeholder":"Обл…","explain":"<b>Обло́мов</b> — Гончаро́в's hero, now the byword for lying on the sofa while life waits."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The wells everyone drinks from","title":"Where the quotes come from — and how to recognise them","paras":["A handful of sources supply most of the phrases in the air. From <b>Крыло́в</b>'s fables: «А воз и ны́не там» (nothing has budged), «а Ва́ська слу́шает да ест». From <b>Грибое́дов</b>'s «Го́ре от ума́»: «Сча́стливые часо́в не наблюда́ют» (the happy don't watch the clock). From <b>Толсто́й</b>: «Все счастли́вые се́мьи похо́жи друг на дру́га…» opens «А́нну Каре́нину» and then gets bent onto anything at all.","The wells aren't only classic literature. <b>Cinema</b>, <b>songs</b>, <b>cartoons</b> and <b>fairy tales</b> feed the stream just as hard: <b>«Тепло́ ли тебе́, деви́ца?»</b> from the tale «Моро́зко» surfaces the instant someone's freezing or being tested. A line can be lofty or homely — both count, and both are dropped with the same easy wink.","You can't cram the canon in a month, and you don't need to. What you build is the reflex: a stock phrase suddenly rings with an echo, and you go looking for its home. Half of «getting» Russian journalism and humour is exactly this — catching which door a sentence quietly opened."],"audio":"A handful of sources supply most of the phrases in the air. From Krylov's fables: 'A voz i nyne tam', the cart is still where it was — nothing has budged; 'a Vaska slushaet da est'. From Griboedov's Woe from Wit: 'Schastlivye chasov ne nablyudayut', the happy don't watch the clock. From Tolstoy: 'Vse schastlivye semi pokhozhi drug na druga', all happy families are alike — the opening of Anna Karenina, then bent onto anything at all. The wells aren't only classic literature. Cinema, songs, cartoons and fairy tales feed the stream just as hard: 'Teplo li tebe, devitsa', are you warm, maiden — from the tale Morozko — surfaces the instant someone's freezing or being tested. A line can be lofty or homely; both count, and both are dropped with the same easy wink. You can't cram the canon in a month, and you don't need to. What you build is the reflex: a stock phrase suddenly rings with an echo, and you go looking for its home. Half of getting Russian journalism and humour is exactly this — catching which door a sentence quietly opened.","table":{"rows":[["Крыло́в → «А воз и ны́не там» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Крылов, а воз и ныне там\">🔊</button>","nothing has moved an inch"],["Грибое́дов → «Сча́стливые часо́в не наблюда́ют» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Грибоедов, счастливые часов не наблюдают\">🔊</button>","the happy don't watch the clock"],["Толсто́й → «Все счастли́вые се́мьи похо́жи…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Толстой, все счастливые семьи похожи\">🔊</button>","the opening line of «Анна Каренина»"],["«Моро́зко» → «Тепло́ ли тебе́, деви́ца?» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Морозко, тепло ли тебе, девица\">🔊</button>","from the fairy tale (and the beloved film)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"«And the cart is still there» — Krylov, yet it fits any deadlock.","en":"«А воз и ны́не там» — Крыло́в, а зву́чит про любо́й засто́й.","say":"А воз и ныне там — Крылов, а звучит про любой застой."},{"ru":"«Are you warm, maiden?» — and everyone hears «Морозко».","en":"«Тепло́ ли тебе́, деви́ца?» — и все слы́шат «Моро́зко».","say":"Тепло ли тебе, девица — и все слышат Морозко."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«„Все счастли́вые се́мьи похо́жи…“ — э́то, ка́жется, наро́дная посло́вица?»","right":"«„Все счастли́вые се́мьи похо́жи…“ — э́то нача́ло „А́нны Каре́ниной“ Толсто́го.»","why":"It feels folk-anonymous, but it's the exact opening line of Толсто́й's novel. Filing a signed literary line under «proverb» is a small but real allusion error — you've lost the author standing behind it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Сча́стливые часо́в не наблюда́ют» comes from —","opts":["Грибое́дов, «Го́ре от ума́»","a folk proverb of no fixed author","a recent film"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's Со́фья's line in Грибое́дов's «Го́ре от ума́» — one of the most-quoted comedies in the language."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Тепло́ ли тебе́, деви́ца?» will surface when someone is —","opts":["raising a wedding toast","freezing, or being put to the test","ordering a coffee"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's Моро́з's question in «Моро́зко» — dropped whenever someone's cold or being tried. Recognise the scene, get the wink."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Dropping it to the point","title":"The native's other half — placing a quote, and the two traps","paras":["Recognition is one half; placement is the C2 half. A quote dropped <b>to the point</b> does real work: cap a story about a stubborn boss with «а Ва́ська слу́шает да ест» and you've delivered a verdict and a wink in five words. The skill is fit and timing — the phrase must land on a moment that truly rhymes with its source, and land <i>lightly</i>. Over-quote and you're a bore; misfire and you're the one who got the reference wrong in public.","Two traps sit right here. The first is <b>misquoting</b> — a bent classic line said as if it were the original is worse than no quote at all, because everyone hears the crack. The second is over-reaching: «Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли» over a passed salt-cellar is comically too big. Match the weight of the source to the weight of the moment.","This is Эмма's daily tightrope as an editor: an author drops an allusion and she must judge — will the reader hear it, does the translation keep it, is it earned or just showing off. Tomorrow you go one step further: not quoting a voice but <b>wearing</b> one — writing deliberately «под» a chronicle, a fairy tale, a report (стилиза́ция). Then the joke itself (анекдо́т), and Review 2."],"audio":"Recognition is one half; placement is the C2 half. A quote dropped to the point does real work: cap a story about a stubborn boss with 'a Vaska slushaet da est' and you've delivered a verdict and a wink in five words. The skill is fit and timing — the phrase must land on a moment that truly rhymes with its source, and land lightly. Over-quote and you're a bore; misfire and you're the one who got the reference wrong in public. Two traps sit right here. The first is misquoting — a bent classic line said as if it were the original is worse than no quote at all, because everyone hears the crack. The second is over-reaching: 'My v otvete za tekh, kogo priruchili' over a passed salt-cellar is comically too big. Match the weight of the source to the weight of the moment. This is Emma's daily tightrope as an editor: an author drops an allusion and she must judge — will the reader hear it, does the translation keep it, is it earned or just showing off. Tomorrow you go one step further: not quoting a voice but wearing one — writing deliberately in the manner of a chronicle, a fairy tale, a report, stilizatsiya. Then the joke itself, anekdot, and Review 2.","examples":[{"ru":"A well-placed quote is a verdict and a wink in five words.","en":"Уме́стная цита́та — э́то верди́кт и подмигива́ние в пяти́ слова́х.","say":"Уместная цитата — это вердикт и подмигивание в пяти словах."},{"ru":"Misquote a classic — and everyone heard the crack.","en":"Перевра́л кла́ссика — и все услы́шали тре́щину.","say":"Переврал классика — и все услышали трещину."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он переда́л мне соль — „Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли“.»","right":"«Он переда́л мне соль — я сказа́л „спаси́бо“.»","why":"The Экзюпери́ line carries a whole ethic of responsibility; hung on a passed salt-cellar it's comically oversized. The trap isn't grammar — it's scale. Match the source's weight to the moment's, or the quote reads as showing off."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A quote works best when it's dropped —","opts":["as often as possible, to show your reading","lightly, onto a moment that truly rhymes with its source","only in formal, written texts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fit and lightness. The phrase must rhyme with its source's situation — and never strain for weight the moment can't carry."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and land the quote lightly, like a verdict you're enjoying.","target":"«А Ва́ська слу́шает да ест» — вот и весь наш нача́льник.","sub":"drop the Крыло́в line, then let «нача́льник» close it off","want":"а Васька слушает да ест вот и весь наш начальник"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two-word ending. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Complete Булга́ков's four-word line, meaning truth can't be destroyed: «Рукопи́си …». Type the two words (hint: не го…):","accept":["не горят","рукописи не горят"],"placeholder":"не го…","explain":"<b>«Рукопи́си не горя́т»</b> — Вола́нд, in «Ма́стер и Маргари́та». Four words, a whole faith that the real survives."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма and Ива́н over coffee. He vents about his boss; she can't help hearing Крыло́в. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Расска́зываю ше́фу про сро́ки — а он бу́дто и не слы́шит.","ru":"I keep telling the boss about the deadlines — and it's as if he doesn't hear."},{"who":"A","en":"А Ва́ська слу́шает да ест?","ru":"«And Vaska listens on, and eats»?"},{"who":"B","en":"Вот и́менно он! А отку́да ты так то́чно?","ru":"That's him exactly! And how do you hit it so precisely?"},{"who":"A","en":"Крыло́в, ба́сня. Тепе́рь я не про́сто узнаю́ таки́е стро́чки — я их вставля́ю.","ru":"Krylov, a fable. Now I don't just recognise such lines — I drop them in."},{"who":"B","en":"Ра́ньше ты их про́сто пропуска́ла ми́мо уше́й.","ru":"You used to let them go right past you."},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше — да. Це́лый слой ру́сского шёл ми́мо меня́.","ru":"Before — yes. A whole layer of Russian went past me."},{"who":"B","en":"Гла́вное — не перебо́рщить. А то бу́дет как тост на ка́ждый чих.","ru":"The main thing is not to overdo it. Otherwise it's like a toast for every sneeze."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Цита́та к ме́сту — по́дарок; не к ме́сту — хвастовство́.","ru":"Exactly. A quote to the point is a gift; out of place, it's showing off."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the layer under the words. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"аллю́зия","ru":"an allusion","ex":"В статье́ то́нкая аллю́зия на Го́голя."},{"en":"прецеде́нтный","ru":"precedent, culturally shared","ex":"Э́то прецеде́нтное выска́зывание."},{"en":"цита́та","ru":"a quotation","ex":"Он вста́вил цита́ту к ме́сту."},{"en":"крыла́тое сло́во","ru":"a winged phrase, a stock quote","ex":"«Рукопи́си не горя́т» — крыла́тое сло́во."},{"en":"отсы́лка","ru":"a reference, a nod","ex":"Я по́нял отсы́лку к фи́льму."},{"en":"узнава́ть","ru":"to recognise (a quote)","ex":"Носи́тель сра́зу узнаёт стро́чку."},{"en":"исто́чник","ru":"a source","ex":"У ка́ждой цита́ты есть исто́чник."},{"en":"ба́сня","ru":"a fable (Крыло́в's genre)","ex":"Э́то стро́чка из ба́сни Крыло́ва."},{"en":"посло́вица","ru":"a proverb","ex":"Он спу́тал цита́ту с посло́вицей."},{"en":"геро́й","ru":"a character (behind a name)","ex":"Плю́шкин — геро́й Го́голя."},{"en":"уме́стный","ru":"apt, to the point","ex":"Уме́стная цита́та — по́дарок."},{"en":"перевра́ть","ru":"to misquote, to garble","ex":"Перевра́ть кла́ссику — ху́же молча́ния."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each kind of precedent phenomenon to what it is — the shape, not the example.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its description — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["прецеде́нтное и́мя","a character turned byword (Плюшкин = a miser)"],["крыла́тое сло́во","a winged phrase the whole culture quotes"],["прецеде́нтная ситуа́ция","a shared scene ('the emperor has no clothes')"],["отсы́лка","a light nod pointing to a source"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A прецеде́нтное выска́зывание carries its meaning mainly in —","opts":["its literal words","the source it points to","its grammar"],"answer":1},{"q":"Call someone «Плю́шкин» and you mean —","opts":["a miser (from Го́голь)","a generous host","a fast runner"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Сча́стливые часо́в не наблюда́ют» is from —","opts":["an anonymous proverb","Грибое́дов, «Го́ре от ума́»","a pop song"],"answer":1},{"q":"«А коро́ль-то го́лый!» names the moment when —","opts":["everyone finally says the obvious truth aloud","a king finishes dressing","a play comes to an end"],"answer":0},{"q":"Beyond recognising a quote, the C2 skill is —","opts":["quoting as often as possible","dropping one lightly, to the point","avoiding all quotation"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short reflection on the layer that runs under native speech — and what it asks of your ear. Read each line aloud.","title":"Речь, проши́тая цита́тами","sentences":["Речь носи́теля проши́та цита́тами, кото́рых никто́ не берёт в кавы́чки.","Одна́ стро́чка — и за ней встаёт це́лая кни́га, фильм и́ли ста́рая ска́зка.","«Рукопи́си не горя́т» — и вот уже́ ря́дом стои́т весь Булга́ков.","Кто слы́шит исто́чник, тот чита́ет второ́й слой; кто не слы́шит — то́лько пе́рвый.","На э́том у́ровне цита́ту не про́сто узнаю́т — её уме́стно роня́ют в разгово́р.","Но чуть перевра́л и́ли переборщи́л — и всё волшебство́ рассыпа́ется."],"translation":"A native's speech is stitched through with quotations no one ever puts in quotation marks. A single line — and behind it stands a whole book, a film, or an old fairy tale. «Manuscripts don't burn» — and there beside you stands all of Bulgakov. Whoever hears the source reads the second layer; whoever doesn't, only the first. At this level a quote isn't merely recognised — it's dropped into the conversation exactly to the point. But misquote it just a little, or overdo it, and the whole magic falls apart."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to place a real quote.","prompt":"Take ONE real Russian precedent phrase or name you genuinely know — «Рукопи́си не горя́т», «А воз и ны́не там», Обло́мов, Плю́шкин — and use it correctly in a short paragraph about a real situation (a stalled project, a stubborn colleague, a hoarder's flat). Then, in one sentence, name its source and say what it carries beyond its literal words. Attribute it accurately. 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Ask two questions of your quote: what STORY stands behind it, and does my situation truly rhyme with that story? If yes, drop it lightly — a verdict and a wink, not a lecture 🙂","example":"Прое́кт обсужда́ем ме́сяц, а воз и ны́не там: реши́ли на́чинать в понеде́льник — и сно́ва ничего́. Э́ту стро́чку по́дарил Крыло́в в ба́сне «Ле́бедь, Щу́ка и Рак»: она́ зна́чит не про́сто «нет движе́ния», а «ка́ждый тя́нет в свою́ сто́рону — и де́ло стои́т». Одна́ отсы́лка — и весь наш засто́й ска́зан то́чно и с усме́шкой."}},"12":{"day":12,"week":"02","themeRu":"Borrowing a voice","themeEn":"Стилиза́ция и интерте́кст","intro":"Yesterday you quoted other voices. Today you <b>wear</b> one. A master doesn't only have a style — they have a wardrobe: they can write «под» a fairy tale, «под» a medieval chronicle, «под» a police report, and switch costumes mid-page. This is <b>стилиза́ция</b> (stylization — putting on a voice on purpose). Say «Жил-был…» and a fairy tale opens; say «В ле́то…» and a chronicle does. Эмма once retold a start-up's origin story «под ле́топись» and it was grander and funnier than the plain version ever could be.","introAudio":"Yesterday you quoted other voices. Today you wear one. A master doesn't only have a style — they have a wardrobe: they can write in the manner of a fairy tale, of a medieval chronicle, of a police report, and switch costumes mid-page. This is 'stilizatsiya', stylization — putting on a voice on purpose. Say 'Zhil-byl', once there was, and a fairy tale opens; say 'V leto', in the year, and a chronicle does. Emma once retold a start-up's origin story in the manner of a chronicle and it was grander and funnier than the plain version ever could be.","goals":["Understand <b>стилиза́ция</b>: writing deliberately «под» another genre or voice — a costume, put on knowingly","Open a genre with its signature kit: <b>«Жил-был…»</b> (a fairy tale), <b>«В ле́то…»</b> (a chronicle)","Meet parody, pastiche and deadpan officialese — and <b>Козьма́ Прутко́в</b>, a mask who never existed","Guard the one line: стилиза́ция (deliberate, held whole) versus <b>срыв сти́ля</b> (an accidental slip of register)"],"learned":["Grasped стилиза́ция — putting on another voice on purpose","Opened genres with their kits («Жил-был…», «В ле́то…, и бысть»)","Met parody, pastiche, канцеляри́т and the invented mask Козьма́ Прутко́в","Held the line between deliberate stylization and an accidental срыв сти́ля"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday — allusion: quoting whole phrases without the quotation marks, and dropping them to the point. Then we stop quoting a voice and start wearing one.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday — allusion: quoting whole phrases without the quotation marks, and dropping them to the point. Then we stop quoting a voice and start wearing one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's allusion.","q":"A прецеде́нтное выска́зывание like «Рукопи́си не горя́т» carries its meaning mainly in —","opts":["its literal, dictionary words","the source it points to","its grammatical form"],"answer":1,"explain":"The words are a pointer; the meaning is all of Булга́ков behind them. Miss the source, miss the phrase."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the name. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The precedent name for sofa-bound inertia, from Гончаро́в's novel. Type it (hint: Обл…):","accept":["обломов"],"placeholder":"Обл…","explain":"<b>Обло́мов</b> — the byword for lying on the sofa while life waits. A name that carries a whole novel."},{"type":"choice","q":"The C2 skill beyond recognising a quote is —","opts":["dropping one lightly, exactly to the point","quoting as often as you can","avoiding quotation entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Placement is the native's other half: a quote lands on a moment that rhymes with its source — and lands lightly."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Style as a costume","title":"<em>Стилиза́ция</em>: putting on a voice on purpose","paras":["Yesterday you quoted other voices. Today you <b>wear</b> one. <b>Стилиза́ция</b> (stylization) is writing deliberately «под» something — under the manner of a fairy tale, a chronicle, a police report, a particular author — so the reader hears the borrowed voice, not yours. It's a costume, put on knowingly and taken off at will.","The trick is that every genre carries a small kit of <b>signature markers</b>: opening formulae, favourite words, a typical word order, a rhythm. Hit two or three and the reader is instantly «inside» the genre. Say «Жил-был…» and you've promised a fairy tale before the sentence ends; say «В ле́то…» and you've opened a chronicle.","This is a mastery move: your own voice stays under control while you speak in another. The line to guard is <b>стилиза́ция</b> (deliberate, held whole) versus <b>срыв сти́ля</b> (an accidental slip out of your register). One is art; the other is exactly the mistake C1 taught you to avoid."],"audio":"Yesterday you quoted other voices. Today you wear one. 'Stilizatsiya', stylization, is writing deliberately in the manner of something — a fairy tale, a chronicle, a police report, a particular author — so the reader hears the borrowed voice, not yours. It's a costume, put on knowingly and taken off at will. The trick is that every genre carries a small kit of signature markers: opening formulae, favourite words, a typical word order, a rhythm. Hit two or three and the reader is instantly inside the genre. Say 'Zhil-byl', once there was, and you've promised a fairy tale before the sentence ends; say 'V leto', in the year, and you've opened a chronicle. This is a mastery move: your own voice stays under control while you speak in another. The line to guard is 'stilizatsiya', deliberate and held whole, versus 'sryv stilya', an accidental slip out of your register. One is art; the other is exactly the mistake C1 taught you to avoid.","table":{"rows":[["ска́зка → «Жил-был…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сказка, жил-был\">🔊</button>","a fairy tale opens"],["ле́топись → «В ле́то…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"летопись, в лето\">🔊</button>","a chronicle opens (in the year…)"],["докуме́нт → «Настоя́щим уведомля́ю…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"документ, настоящим уведомляю\">🔊</button>","an official notice opens"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Say «Once there was» — and the reader is already in a fairy tale.","en":"Скажи́ «Жил-был» — и чита́тель уже́ в ска́зке.","say":"Скажи жил-был — и читатель уже в сказке."},{"ru":"Stylization is a set of clothes you put on deliberately.","en":"Стилиза́ция — оде́жда, кото́рую надева́ют наро́чно.","say":"Стилизация — одежда, которую надевают нарочно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В нау́чной статье́ вдруг: „и жи́ли они́ до́лго и сча́стливо“.»","right":"«В нау́чной статье́ — нейтра́льный вы́вод, без ска́зочных фо́рмул.»","why":"Dropping a fairy-tale ending into a scientific article isn't stylization — it's <b>срыв сти́ля</b>, an accidental fall out of register. Stylization is deliberate and consistent; this is just a leak the reader hears as a mistake."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Стилиза́ция means writing —","opts":["deliberately «под» another genre or voice","in your own natural, unmarked voice","with as many errors as possible"],"answer":0,"explain":"A costume put on knowingly: you borrow a genre's voice on purpose, and the reader hears it as borrowed."},{"type":"choice","q":"Writing «под» a genre on purpose is stylization; slipping out of register by accident is —","opts":["коннота́ция","срыв сти́ля","каламбу́р"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>срыв сти́ля</b> — a slip out of style. Same archaic word: deliberate and whole, it's art; leaked by accident, it's the error."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Under the fairy tale and the chronicle","title":"The marker-kits: <em>«Жил-был»</em> and <em>«В ле́то»</em>","paras":["Each genre hands you a starter kit. The <b>ска́зка</b> (fairy tale): open with «Жил-был…», set it «в не́котором ца́рстве, в тридевя́том госуда́рстве», stretch time with «до́лго ли, ко́ротко ли», close with «ста́ли жить-пожива́ть да добра́ нажива́ть». Three of these and any modern story turns folk.","The <b>ле́топись</b> (chronicle): begin each entry «В ле́то…» (in the year…), reach for archaic grammar and words — «и бысть» (and it came to pass), «рече́» (spake) — and keep the tone grave and impersonal. Even a joke about your office reads as ancient history the moment you frame it this way.","You already recognise these formulae passively. Stylization is the active move: pick the kit on purpose and dress brand-new content in it. Эмма once rendered a start-up's origin story «под ле́топись» — and it landed grander and funnier than any straight retelling could."],"audio":"Each genre hands you a starter kit. The 'skazka', fairy tale: open with 'Zhil-byl', once there was; set it 'v nekotorom tsarstve, v tridevyatom gosudarstve', in a certain kingdom far away; stretch time with 'dolgo li, korotko li', however long it was; close with 'stali zhit-pozhivat da dobra nazhivat', and they lived happily ever after. Three of these and any modern story turns folk. The 'letopis', chronicle: begin each entry 'V leto', in the year; reach for archaic grammar and words — 'i byst', and it came to pass, 'reche', spake — and keep the tone grave and impersonal. Even a joke about your office reads as ancient history the moment you frame it this way. You already recognise these formulae passively. Stylization is the active move: pick the kit on purpose and dress brand-new content in it. Emma once rendered a start-up's origin story in the manner of a chronicle — and it landed grander and funnier than any straight retelling could.","table":{"rows":[["Жил-был… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жил-был\">🔊</button>","fairy-tale opening: «Once there was…»"],["в тридевя́том ца́рстве <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в тридевятом царстве\">🔊</button>","«in a faraway kingdom» — the fairy-tale setting"],["до́лго ли, ко́ротко ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"долго ли, коротко ли\">🔊</button>","«however long it lasted» — a folk time-skip"],["В ле́то… · и бысть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в лето, и бысть\">🔊</button>","chronicle: «In the year…», «and it came to pass»"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«In a certain kingdom there lived a start-up…» — and everyone smiles.","en":"«В не́котором ца́рстве жил-был старта́п…» — и все улыба́ются.","say":"В некотором царстве жил-был стартап — и все улыбаются."},{"ru":"«And there came a meeting, and the boss spake…» — the office as a chronicle.","en":"«И бысть совеща́ние, и рече́ нача́льник…» — о́фис как ле́топись.","say":"И бысть совещание, и рече начальник — офис как летопись."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Стилиза́ция „под ска́зку“ — э́то ста́рые слова́ нау́гад.»","right":"«Стилиза́ция „под ска́зку“ — э́то её узнава́емые фо́рмулы к ме́сту.»","why":"Stylization isn't scattering random archaic words; it's placing a genre's <b>recognisable formulae</b> so the reader names the genre at once. Precision of the kit, not a fog of old-sounding vocabulary."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build a fairy-tale opening.","q":"Build a fairy-tale opening: “Once there lived a king.”","want":"Жил-был на све́те царь","answer":"Жил-был на све́те царь"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two-word formula. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The chronicle's opening formula, meaning «In the year…». Type it (hint: В ле…):","accept":["в лето"],"placeholder":"В ле…","explain":"<b>«В ле́то…»</b> — the chronicle's opening. Frame any tale with it and it turns solemn and ancient."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Parody, pastiche, officialese","title":"Wearing an author — and the deadpan of <em>канцеляри́т</em>","paras":["Stylization scales up from genres to whole voices. A <b>паро́дия</b> (parody) exaggerates a manner for laughs; a <b>пасти́ш</b> (pastiche) reproduces it in homage; a <b>парафра́з</b> (paraphrase) recasts a known text in a new key. The most beloved Russian example is a man who never existed: <b>Козьма́ Прутко́в</b>, a pompous official-poet invented by А. К. Толсто́й and the Жемчу́жниковы brothers, whose solemn nonsense — «Зри в ко́рень», «Никто́ не обни́мет необъя́тного» — parodies the self-important sage.","The sharpest everyday costume is <b>канцеляри́т</b> — bureaucratic officialese — worn on purpose for deadpan comedy. Retell a kitchen quarrel as a police report — «гр. Ивано́в, находя́сь на ку́хне, допусти́л…» — and the gap between the grand form and the tiny content <i>is</i> the joke.","Notice how all of this leans on last month's five styles: you can only put a style on if you know it cold. Stylization is C1's register knowledge turned into a game — you don't merely fit the room now, you can dress up as it."],"audio":"Stylization scales up from genres to whole voices. A 'parodiya', parody, exaggerates a manner for laughs; a 'pastish', pastiche, reproduces it in homage; a 'parafraz', paraphrase, recasts a known text in a new key. The most beloved Russian example is a man who never existed: Kozma Prutkov, a pompous official-poet invented by A. K. Tolstoy and the Zhemchuzhnikov brothers, whose solemn nonsense — 'Zri v koren', look to the root, 'Nikto ne obnimet neobyatnogo', no one can embrace the unembraceable — parodies the self-important sage. The sharpest everyday costume is 'kantselyarit', bureaucratic officialese, worn on purpose for deadpan comedy. Retell a kitchen quarrel as a police report — 'gr. Ivanov, nakhodyas na kukhne, dopustil', citizen Ivanov, being in the kitchen, did commit — and the gap between the grand form and the tiny content is the joke. Notice how all of this leans on last month's five styles: you can only put a style on if you know it cold. Stylization is C1's register knowledge turned into a game — you don't merely fit the room now, you can dress up as it.","table":{"rows":[["паро́дия <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пародия\">🔊</button>","exaggerates a manner for laughs"],["пасти́ш <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пастиш\">🔊</button>","reproduces a manner in homage"],["парафра́з <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"парафраз\">🔊</button>","recasts a known text in a new key"],["канцеляри́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"канцелярит\">🔊</button>","bureaucratese, worn for deadpan comedy"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Kozma Prutkov: «Look to the root» — a parody of grand wisdom.","en":"Козьма́ Прутко́в: «Зри в ко́рень» — паро́дия на му́дрость.","say":"Козьма Прутков: зри в корень — пародия на мудрость."},{"ru":"A kitchen quarrel «as a police report» — and it's already funny.","en":"Ссо́ру на ку́хне — «под протоко́л», и уже́ смешно́.","say":"Ссору на кухне — под протокол, и уже смешно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Козьма́ Прутко́в — ре́дкий ру́сский поэ́т девятна́дцатого ве́ка.»","right":"«Козьма́ Прутко́в — вы́мышленная ма́ска, паро́дия на ва́жного мудреца́.»","why":"Козьма́ Прутко́в is not a real poet but a collective <b>mask</b> — a stylization-persona built by А. К. Толсто́й and the Жемчу́жниковы to parody solemn wisdom. Taking the costume for a real body is the miss."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Козьма́ Прутко́в is —","opts":["a real 19th-century poet","a fictional mask parodying pompous wisdom","a present-day blogger"],"answer":1,"explain":"An invented author — a collective mask by А. К. Толсто́й and the Жемчу́жниковы — whose solemn nonsense mocks the self-important sage."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Bureaucratic officialese, worn on purpose for deadpan comedy, is called … . Type it (hint: канц…):","accept":["канцелярит"],"placeholder":"канц…","explain":"<b>канцеляри́т</b> — the grey language of forms and reports. Put on knowingly, it's comedy; leaked into normal prose, it's a fault."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The costume and its risk","title":"Why a master dresses up — and where the costume tears","paras":["Why bother? Because a borrowed voice says what your own can't. «Под ле́топись» lends grandeur; «под ска́зку» lends warmth and irony; «под протоко́л» lends deadpan. A master keeps a wardrobe and reaches for the costume that carries the feeling — a toast «под ба́бушкино письмо́», an app's terms «под инстру́кцию», a tribute «под о́ду».","The risk is the same line as Rule 1: a costume worn <b>on purpose and consistently</b> is стилиза́ция; the same archaic word dropped by accident into a modern email is <b>срыв сти́ля</b>. The test is control and consistency — put the voice on fully, hold it whole, take it off cleanly. Half a costume just looks like a mistake.","This is Эмма's craft at its most playful: she can write «под ба́бушкино письмо́», «под кла́ссика», «под казённую спра́вку», and switch between them on the same page. Tomorrow the play turns to laughter itself — the anatomy of the Russian <b>анекдо́т</b> and what exactly makes it funny. Then Review 2 gathers the whole week: subtext, irony, wordplay, allusion, stylization, humour."],"audio":"Why bother? Because a borrowed voice says what your own can't. In the manner of a chronicle it lends grandeur; in the manner of a fairy tale, warmth and irony; in the manner of a police report, deadpan. A master keeps a wardrobe and reaches for the costume that carries the feeling — a toast written like grandmother's letter, an app's terms written like an instruction manual, a tribute written like an ode. The risk is the same line as Rule 1: a costume worn on purpose and consistently is 'stilizatsiya'; the same archaic word dropped by accident into a modern email is 'sryv stilya'. The test is control and consistency — put the voice on fully, hold it whole, take it off cleanly. Half a costume just looks like a mistake. This is Emma's craft at its most playful: she can write like grandmother's letter, like a classic author, like an official certificate, and switch between them on the same page. Tomorrow the play turns to laughter itself — the anatomy of the Russian 'anekdot' and what exactly makes it funny. Then Review 2 gathers the whole week: subtext, irony, wordplay, allusion, stylization, humour.","examples":[{"ru":"A costume worn fully is stylization; worn half, a mistake.","en":"Ко́стюм наде́т по́лностью — э́то стилиза́ция; наполови́ну — оши́бка.","say":"Костюм надет полностью — это стилизация; наполовину — ошибка."},{"ru":"A master changes voice like clothes — and always on purpose.","en":"Ма́стер меня́ет го́лос, как оде́жду, — и всегда́ наро́чно.","say":"Мастер меняет голос, как одежду, — и всегда нарочно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«На́чал письмо́ „под ле́топись“, а зако́нчил дежу́рным „ОК, до свя́зи“.»","right":"«„Под ле́топись“ — вы́держал го́лос до после́дней стро́чки.»","why":"Half a costume tears. Stylization demands consistency: hold the borrowed voice to the very end, or the switch back reads as a slip, not a game. Put it on fully, or don't put it on at all."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The line between стилиза́ция and срыв сти́ля is —","opts":["how old the words are","control and consistency — on purpose, and held whole","the length of the text"],"answer":1,"explain":"A voice worn deliberately and all the way through is stylization; the same word leaking in by accident is a срыв сти́ля."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and open in full, unhurried fairy-tale voice.","target":"Жил-был на све́те оди́н о́чень упря́мый прое́кт…","sub":"let «жил-был» set the folk rhythm before the modern word «прое́кт» lands","want":"жил-был на свете один очень упрямый проект"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Writing deliberately «под» a genre or voice — style worn as a costume — is called … . Type it (hint: стил…):","accept":["стилизация"],"placeholder":"стил…","explain":"<b>стилиза́ция</b> — stylization. Not your voice by accident, but another's on purpose."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is grinning at her laptop when Макси́м, her brother-in-law the engineer, looks over. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты чего́ ухмыля́ешься в экра́н?","ru":"What are you smirking at on the screen?"},{"who":"A","en":"Переписа́ла исто́рию на́шего прое́кта «под ле́топись». Прочита́ть нача́ло?","ru":"I've rewritten our project's story «as a chronicle». Want me to read the opening?"},{"who":"B","en":"Дава́й, интере́сно.","ru":"Go on, I'm curious."},{"who":"A","en":"«В ле́то две ты́сячи два́дцать пе́рвое собра́лись три инжене́ра, и бысть совеща́ние…»","ru":"«In the year two thousand twenty-one three engineers gathered, and there was a meeting…»"},{"who":"B","en":"Ха! Зву́чит как ста́рая ру́копись, а речь про наш рабо́чий чат.","ru":"Ha! It sounds like an old manuscript, but it's about our work chat."},{"who":"A","en":"В том и шу́тка: фо́рма ва́жная, а внутри́ — обы́чная планёрка.","ru":"That's the joke: a grand form, and inside — an ordinary stand-up meeting."},{"who":"B","en":"А то же са́мое «под ска́зку» смо́жешь?","ru":"And could you do the same thing «as a fairy tale»?"},{"who":"A","en":"Легко́: «Жил-был старта́п в не́котором ца́рстве…» Го́лос — как костю́м: надева́ю тот, что ну́жен.","ru":"Easily: «Once there lived a start-up in a certain kingdom…» A voice is like a costume: I put on whichever one I need."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the wardrobe of voices. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"стилиза́ция","ru":"stylization (writing «под» a voice)","ex":"Он ма́стер стилиза́ции «под кла́ссика»."},{"en":"интерте́кст","ru":"intertext (a text woven from others)","ex":"Весь расска́з — то́нкий интерте́кст."},{"en":"паро́дия","ru":"a parody","ex":"Э́то паро́дия на ва́жный тон."},{"en":"пасти́ш","ru":"a pastiche (imitation in homage)","ex":"Пасти́ш — почти́тельное подража́ние."},{"en":"парафра́з","ru":"a paraphrase (a known text recast)","ex":"Он дал парафра́з изве́стной строки́."},{"en":"ска́зка","ru":"a fairy tale","ex":"Ска́зка начина́ется с «Жил-был»."},{"en":"ле́топись","ru":"a chronicle","ex":"Ле́топись начина́ется с «В ле́то»."},{"en":"канцеляри́т","ru":"bureaucratese, officialese","ex":"Канцеляри́т — язы́к спра́вок и протоко́лов."},{"en":"го́лос","ru":"a voice (a manner)","ex":"Ма́стер меня́ет го́лос по жела́нию."},{"en":"подража́ть","ru":"to imitate (a manner)","ex":"Он уме́ет подража́ть любо́му сти́лю."},{"en":"фо́рмула","ru":"a set formula (an opening line)","ex":"«Жил-был» — фо́рмула ру́сской ска́зки."},{"en":"срыв сти́ля","ru":"a slip out of register","ex":"Ста́рое сло́во не к ме́сту — срыв сти́ля."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each term to what it is — the move, not an example.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["стилиза́ция","writing deliberately in another's voice"],["паро́дия","exaggerating a manner for laughs"],["канцеляри́т","bureaucratese, worn for deadpan comedy"],["срыв сти́ля","an accidental slip out of register"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Стилиза́ция means writing —","opts":["deliberately «под» another genre or voice","in your own natural voice","with random mistakes"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Жил-был…» instantly signals —","opts":["a chronicle","a fairy tale","a police report"],"answer":1},{"q":"«В ле́то…» and «и бысть» belong to —","opts":["an advertisement","a fairy tale","a chronicle (ле́топись)"],"answer":2},{"q":"Козьма́ Прутко́в is —","opts":["a fictional mask parodying pompous wisdom","a real 19th-century poet","a folk hero"],"answer":0},{"q":"The line between стилиза́ция and срыв сти́ля is —","opts":["how old the words sound","control and consistency — deliberate, held whole","the length of the text"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short reflection on style worn as a costume — and the one rule that keeps it from tearing. Read each line aloud.","title":"Го́лос, кото́рый надева́ют","sentences":["Ма́стер ру́сского сло́ва уме́ет писа́ть не то́лько свои́м го́лосом.","Захо́чет — и расска́жет о́фисный день «под ле́топись», ва́жно и стро́го.","Захо́чет — преврати́т ту же исто́рию в ска́зку: «Жил-был прое́кт…».","Секре́т — в узнава́емых фо́рмулах: две-три, и жанр уже́ слы́шен.","Но ко́стюм на́до носи́ть це́ликом: полусти́ль чита́ется как оши́бка.","Стилиза́ция — э́то реги́стр, кото́рым игра́ют наро́чно, а не сбива́ются неча́янно."],"translation":"A master of Russian can write in more than just their own voice. If they like, they'll tell the story of an office day «as a chronicle», grave and solemn. If they like, they'll turn the same story into a fairy tale: «Once there was a project…». The secret is in the recognisable formulae: two or three, and the genre can already be heard. But the costume must be worn all the way: a half-style reads as a mistake. Stylization is register that you play with on purpose — not one you slip out of by accident."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to wear a voice.","prompt":"Take one ordinary, modern scene — a work meeting, a grocery run, a family squabble — and rewrite it in 3–4 sentences «под» ONE borrowed voice: a fairy tale («Жил-был…»), a chronicle («В ле́то…, и бысть»), or officialese (канцеляри́т, «под протоко́л»). Hold the costume to the last word. Then, in one sentence, name the voice you wore and the two or three markers you used.","hint":"Grab the genre's starter kit first — its opening formula and one or two signature words — and keep the voice whole to the end. Half a costume just looks like a slip 🙂","example":"В ле́то две ты́сячи два́дцать пя́тое собрали́сь мы у холоди́льника, и бысть вели́кий спор о после́дней котле́те. И рече́ брат мой Макси́м: «Она́ моя́!» — и не́ было ми́ра ме́жду на́ми. Я писа́ла «под ле́топись»: взяла́ фо́рмулу «В ле́то…», ста́рое «и бысть» и «рече́» — и ку́хонная ссо́ра ста́ла торже́ственной хро́никой."}},"13":{"day":13,"week":"02","themeRu":"What makes it funny","themeEn":"Ю́мор, анекдо́т и остроу́мие","intro":"All week the surface of Russian got more crowded — subtext, irony, puns, quotes, borrowed voices. Today they all meet in the place they were heading: the joke. The Russian <b>анекдо́т</b> (a joke told aloud) has a tight shape and a merciless rule — the funny word comes <b>last</b>. And it so often «doesn't translate» because the laugh is welded to <i>these</i> sounds, this case ending, this shared detail. When Эмма first laughed at a Шти́рлиц pun before anyone explained it, she knew a wall had come down. Getting the joke in real time is the last exam of the language.","introAudio":"All week the surface of Russian got more crowded — subtext, irony, puns, quotes, borrowed voices. Today they all meet in the place they were heading: the joke. The Russian 'anekdot', a joke told aloud, has a tight shape and a merciless rule — the funny word comes last. And it so often doesn't translate because the laugh is welded to these sounds, this case ending, this shared detail. When Emma first laughed at a Stirlitz pun before anyone explained it, she knew a wall had come down. Getting the joke in real time is the last exam of the language.","goals":["Know the anatomy of an <b>анекдо́т</b>: <b>зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн</b> (setup, turn, punchline), and why timing is the mechanism","Hear the engine under the laugh — a <b>каламбу́р</b>, a grammar twist, absurd, a cultural detail","Meet the stock cast and formats: <b>Шти́рлиц</b>, <b>Во́вочка</b>, <b>чу́кча</b>, «Армя́нское ра́дио», and <b>самоиро́ния</b>","Understand why a Russian joke often won't translate — and why «getting it» in real time is the final test"],"learned":["Learned the shape of an анекдо́т: зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн, funny word last","Heard the engines under the laugh — pun, grammar, absurd, cultural detail","Met the stock cast and formats — Шти́рлиц, Во́вочка, чу́кча, Армя́нское ра́дио, самоиро́ния","Understood why jokes resist translation — and why laughing on the beat is the final test"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday — stylization: wearing a voice on purpose, «под» a fairy tale or a chronicle, held whole. Then the whole week meets in the joke.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday — stylization: wearing a voice on purpose, in the manner of a fairy tale or a chronicle, held whole. Then the whole week meets in the joke.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's stylization.","q":"Writing an office memo «под ле́топись» — «В ле́то…, и бысть совеща́ние» — is —","opts":["стилиза́ция — a voice worn on purpose","срыв сти́ля — an accidental slip","a grammar mistake"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deliberate and consistent — a chronicle's kit put on knowingly. That's стилиза́ция, not a leak."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Bureaucratic officialese, worn on purpose for deadpan comedy, is … . Type it (hint: канц…):","accept":["канцелярит"],"placeholder":"канц…","explain":"<b>канцеляри́т</b> — the grey language of forms. As a costume, deadpan; leaked in by accident, a fault."},{"type":"choice","q":"The line between стилиза́ция and срыв сти́ля is —","opts":["the age of the words","control and consistency — deliberate and held whole","the length of the text"],"answer":1,"explain":"A voice worn all the way through is stylization; the same word leaking in by accident is a срыв сти́ля."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The shape of a joke","title":"Anatomy of the <em>анекдо́т</em>: setup, turn, punchline","paras":["The Russian <b>анекдо́т</b> (a joke, told aloud — not the English «anecdote») has a tight shape. First the <b>зачи́н</b> (the setup): a bare scene, almost always in the present tense — «Захо́дит мужи́к в бар…», «Учи́тельница спра́шивает…». Then the <b>разворо́т</b> (the turn): the situation develops, a line or two. Then the <b>панчла́йн</b> — the punchline, the last words, where the whole thing snaps shut.","The iron rule is placement: the funny word comes <b>last</b>. Move it earlier and the joke dies; a Russian teller guards the final word and the tiny <b>па́уза</b> before it the way a musician holds a rest. Timing isn't decoration here — it's the mechanism itself.","Take a classic school joke. Setup: the teacher asks Во́вочка for two pronouns. Turn: Во́вочка, baffled, blurts «Кто? Я?». Punchline: «Пра́вильно!» — because «кто» and «я» <i>are</i> two pronouns. The snap is in the very last word, and not a syllable sooner."],"audio":"The Russian 'anekdot', a joke told aloud, not the English anecdote, has a tight shape. First the 'zachin', the setup: a bare scene, almost always in the present tense — 'Zakhodit muzhik v bar', a man walks into a bar; 'Uchitelnitsa sprashivaet', the teacher asks. Then the 'razvorot', the turn: the situation develops, a line or two. Then the 'panchlayn', the punchline, the last words, where the whole thing snaps shut. The iron rule is placement: the funny word comes last. Move it earlier and the joke dies; a Russian teller guards the final word and the tiny 'pauza' before it the way a musician holds a rest. Timing isn't decoration here — it's the mechanism itself. Take a classic school joke. Setup: the teacher asks Vovochka for two pronouns. Turn: Vovochka, baffled, blurts 'Kto? Ya?', who, me? Punchline: 'Pravilno!', correct! — because 'kto' and 'ya' are two pronouns. The snap is in the very last word, and not a syllable sooner.","table":{"rows":[["зачи́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зачин\">🔊</button>","the setup — a bare scene, usually present tense"],["разворо́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"разворот\">🔊</button>","the turn — a line or two of development"],["панчла́йн <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"панчлайн\">🔊</button>","the punchline — the last words, where it snaps"],["па́уза <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пауза\">🔊</button>","the beat of silence just before the punchline"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Setup, turn, punchline — and it's funny only in that order.","en":"Зачи́н, разворо́т, панчла́йн — и сме́шно то́лько в э́том поря́дке.","say":"Зачин, разворот, панчлайн — и смешно только в этом порядке."},{"ru":"Teacher: «Name two pronouns.» — «Who? Me?» — «Correct!»","en":"Учи́тельница: «Назови́ два местоиме́ния.» — «Кто? Я?» — «Пра́вильно!»","say":"Учительница: назови два местоимения. — Кто? Я? — Правильно!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Сра́зу вы́дал развя́зку, а пото́м — зачи́н и разворо́т.»","right":"«Снача́ла зачи́н и разворо́т — и то́лько в конце́ панчла́йн.»","why":"Give away the punchline first and the joke is dead on arrival. The анекдо́т is built so the funny word lands <b>last</b>; reorder the beats and you've broken the machine."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"In an анекдо́т, the funny word comes —","opts":["first, to grab attention","somewhere in the middle","last — it's the punchline"],"answer":2,"explain":"The whole machine is built to snap on the <b>last</b> word. Move it earlier and the joke dies."},{"type":"choice","q":"The three beats of a joke, in order, are —","opts":["зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн","панчла́йн → зачи́н → разворо́т","разворо́т → панчла́йн → зачи́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Setup, then turn, then punchline. The order is the joke — the snap has to come at the end."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · What powers the laugh","title":"Puns, grammar, and the joke that won't translate","paras":["Under the shape sits an engine, and often it's <b>language itself</b>. A whole genre of jokes about the spy <b>Шти́рлиц</b> runs on the pun: «Шти́рлиц вы́стрелил вслепу́ю. Слепа́я упа́ла.» — «вслепу́ю» (blindly) is heard as «в слепу́ю» (at the blind woman). One phrase, two parsings; that's yesterday-of-yesterday's каламбу́р turned into a punchline.","Just as often the engine is <b>grammar</b>. The pronoun joke — «Кто? Я?» being literally two pronouns — works only in a language where «кто» and «я» are pronouns and the startled reply happens to use exactly two. Translate the words and the joke evaporates, because it was never in the words — it was in the grammar and the double sense.","This is why a Russian joke so often «doesn't translate»: the laugh is welded to <i>these</i> sounds, <i>this</i> ending, <i>this</i> cultural detail. Getting it in real time — with no pause to work it out — is one of the last things to click, and one of the surest signs the language has gone from studied to owned."],"audio":"Under the shape sits an engine, and often it's language itself. A whole genre of jokes about the spy Stirlitz runs on the pun: 'Stirlits vystrelil vslepuyu. Slepaya upala.' — 'vslepuyu', blindly, is heard as 'v slepuyu', at the blind woman. One phrase, two parsings; that's the pun, the kalambur, turned into a punchline. Just as often the engine is grammar. The pronoun joke — 'Kto? Ya?', who, me, being literally two pronouns — works only in a language where 'kto' and 'ya' are pronouns and the startled reply happens to use exactly two. Translate the words and the joke evaporates, because it was never in the words — it was in the grammar and the double sense. This is why a Russian joke so often doesn't translate: the laugh is welded to these sounds, this ending, this cultural detail. Getting it in real time — with no pause to work it out — is one of the last things to click, and one of the surest signs the language has gone from studied to owned.","table":{"rows":[["игра́ слов <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"игра слов\">🔊</button>","a pun — «вслепу́ю» / «в слепу́ю»"],["грамма́тика <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"грамматика\">🔊</button>","a grammar twist — «Кто? Я?» = two pronouns"],["абсу́рд <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"абсурд\">🔊</button>","logic pushed until it breaks"],["культу́рная дета́ль <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"культурная деталь\">🔊</button>","a shared reference the joke rests on"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«Вслепую» and «в слепую» — one sound, two meanings: there's the joke.","en":"«Вслепу́ю» и «в слепу́ю» — оди́н звук, два смы́сла: вот и шу́тка.","say":"Вслепую и в слепую — один звук, два смысла: вот и шутка."},{"ru":"A Russian joke often can't be translated — it lives in the words themselves.","en":"Ру́сскую шу́тку ча́сто нельзя́ перевести́ — она́ в са́мих слова́х.","say":"Русскую шутку часто нельзя перевести — она в самих словах."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Переда́л анекдо́т досло́вно — и он переста́л быть смешны́м.»","right":"«Переда́л анекдо́т — нашёл шу́тку, кото́рая рабо́тает в друго́м языке́.»","why":"Like a pun, a grammar-joke can't survive word-for-word — the laugh lives in the Russian forms. The move (the same one you'll meet in translation, day 26) is to <b>re-create</b> the joke, not carry the words across."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Шти́рлиц вы́стрелил вслепу́ю. Слепа́я упа́ла.» is powered by —","opts":["a pun («вслепу́ю» / «в слепу́ю»)","pure absurd, no wordplay","a historical fact"],"answer":0,"explain":"One phrase heard two ways — «вслепу́ю» / «в слепу́ю». A каламбу́р, delivered as a punchline."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"A joke built on grammar or a pun can't be carried word-for-word; we say it doesn't … . Type the verb (hint: перев…):","accept":["переводится","переводятся","перевести"],"placeholder":"перев…","explain":"It doesn't <b>перево́дится</b> — the laugh is welded to the Russian words, so you re-create the joke instead."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The regular cast","title":"The stock heroes, the formats — and <em>самоиро́ния</em>","paras":["Russian jokes arrive in familiar packaging. There's a stock cast: <b>Шти́рлиц</b> (the pun-machine), <b>Во́вочка</b> (the too-clever schoolboy), the <b>чу́кча</b> (the naïve outsider, always in on his own punchline — «Чу́кча не чита́тель, чу́кча — писа́тель!»), <b>пору́чик Рже́вский</b> (the blunt officer). Name the hero and a native already knows the rules of that little world.","There are fixed <b>formats</b> too. «Армя́нское ра́дио» (Armenian Radio) answers absurd listener questions with a deadpan «В при́нципе да, но…» — «In principle yes, but…». Much of the rest is <b>бытово́й ю́мор</b> — jokes about the kitchen, the queue, the mother-in-law, the boss: the comedy of ordinary life.","And threading through all of it is <b>самоиро́ния</b> (self-irony). Russians laugh hardest at themselves — at hardship, at their own circumstances. «Как дела́?» — «Лу́чше всех, но никто́ не зави́дует.» Learning to laugh <i>with</i> that, instead of hearing it as a complaint, is part of the ear."],"audio":"Russian jokes arrive in familiar packaging. There's a stock cast: Stirlitz, the pun-machine; Vovochka, the too-clever schoolboy; the 'chukcha', the naive outsider, always in on his own punchline — 'Chukcha ne chitatel, chukcha — pisatel', the chukcha's no reader, the chukcha's a writer; and 'poruchik Rzhevsky', the blunt officer. Name the hero and a native already knows the rules of that little world. There are fixed formats too. 'Armyanskoe radio', Armenian Radio, answers absurd listener questions with a deadpan 'V printsipe da, no', in principle yes, but. Much of the rest is 'bytovoy yumor' — jokes about the kitchen, the queue, the mother-in-law, the boss: the comedy of ordinary life. And threading through all of it is 'samoironiya', self-irony. Russians laugh hardest at themselves — at hardship, at their own circumstances. 'Kak dela?', how are you — 'Luchshe vsekh, no nikto ne zaviduet', better than anyone, but nobody envies me. Learning to laugh with that, instead of hearing it as a complaint, is part of the ear.","table":{"rows":[["Шти́рлиц <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Штирлиц\">🔊</button>","the spy — jokes built on puns"],["чу́кча <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чукча\">🔊</button>","the naïve outsider, in on his own joke"],["Армя́нское ра́дио <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"армянское радио\">🔊</button>","«In principle yes, but…» — deadpan Q&A"],["самоиро́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самоирония\">🔊</button>","laughing at oneself — the Russian core"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"«The chukcha's no reader, the chukcha's a writer!» — the hero laughs at himself.","en":"«Чу́кча не чита́тель, чу́кча — писа́тель!» — геро́й сам смеётся над собо́й.","say":"Чукча не читатель, чукча — писатель! — герой сам смеётся над собой."},{"ru":"Self-irony: «How are you?» — «Better than anyone, but nobody envies me.»","en":"Самоиро́ния: «Как дела́?» — «Лу́чше всех, но никто́ не зави́дует.»","say":"Самоирония: как дела? — Лучше всех, но никто не завидует."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«„Лу́чше всех, но никто́ не зави́дует“ — челове́к и́скренне жа́луется.»","right":"«„Лу́чше всех, но никто́ не зави́дует“ — э́то самоиро́ния, а не жа́лоба.»","why":"Read as a straight complaint it sounds bleak; heard as <b>самоиро́ния</b> it's warm and funny. Taking Russian self-mockery literally — as grievance — is a classic outsider's miss."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Чу́кча не чита́тель, чу́кча — писа́тель!» — in the joke the hero is —","opts":["quietly mocked by everyone else","delivering his own punchline, in on the joke","the villain of the story"],"answer":1,"explain":"The чу́кча snaps out the punchline himself — the humour runs through him, not just at him."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The Russian habit of laughing at oneself is called … . Type it (hint: само…):","accept":["самоирония"],"placeholder":"само…","explain":"<b>самоиро́ния</b> — self-irony. Heard as complaint it's bleak; heard as self-mockery it's the warm core of Russian humour."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Getting it in real time","title":"Why the joke is the final exam — and the week that laughed","paras":["Humour is the last thing to click. To get an анекдо́т at speed you need everything at once: the vocabulary, the grammar the pun bends, the cultural detail it rests on, and the timing that makes the last word land. There's no pause to work it out — you laugh on the beat or you don't. That instant, unforced laugh is one of the truest signs the language has stopped being a subject and become a home.","It's also, quietly, the sum of this whole week. A joke can carry <b>подте́кст</b> (day 8), turn on <b>иро́ния</b> (day 9), run on a <b>каламбу́р</b> (day 10), nod to a <b>прецеде́нтный</b> hero (day 11), or dress up «под» a genre (day 12). When Эмма first laughed at a Шти́рлиц pun before anyone explained it, she knew a wall had come down.","Tomorrow is <b>Review 2</b>: we gather the week — subtext, irony, wordplay, allusion, stylization, humour — and check that you hear all six layers at once. After that, week three turns to rhetoric and the art of argument. But today, just enjoy the click: the joke that needs no footnote."],"audio":"Humour is the last thing to click. To get an anekdot at speed you need everything at once: the vocabulary, the grammar the pun bends, the cultural detail it rests on, and the timing that makes the last word land. There's no pause to work it out — you laugh on the beat or you don't. That instant, unforced laugh is one of the truest signs the language has stopped being a subject and become a home. It's also, quietly, the sum of this whole week. A joke can carry 'podtekst', subtext, from day eight; turn on 'ironiya', irony, day nine; run on a 'kalambur', a pun, day ten; nod to a 'pretsedentny' hero, day eleven; or dress up in the manner of a genre, day twelve. When Emma first laughed at a Stirlitz pun before anyone explained it, she knew a wall had come down. Tomorrow is Review 2: we gather the week — subtext, irony, wordplay, allusion, stylization, humour — and check that you hear all six layers at once. After that, week three turns to rhetoric and the art of argument. But today, just enjoy the click: the joke that needs no footnote.","examples":[{"ru":"Laughed on time, with no explanation — that means you heard it all at once.","en":"Засмея́лся во́время, без объясне́ний — зна́чит, услы́шал всё сра́зу.","say":"Засмеялся вовремя, без объяснений — значит, услышал всё сразу."},{"ru":"Getting a joke in real time is the language's final exam.","en":"Понима́ть шу́тку в реа́льном вре́мени — после́дний экза́мен языка́.","say":"Понимать шутку в реальном времени — последний экзамен языка."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Шу́тку тебе́ объясни́ли — и ты по́нял. Зна́чит, ты её услы́шал?»","right":"«Шу́тку понима́ешь сам и во́время — вот тогда́ ты её услы́шал.»","why":"A joke you needed explained is a joke you missed. The test is the unforced laugh <b>on the beat</b> — comprehension after the fact isn't the same as getting it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The surest sign you've «got» a Russian joke is —","opts":["you can explain it afterwards","you laugh on the beat, with no explanation","you can translate every word"],"answer":1,"explain":"Not analysis after the fact — the instant, unforced laugh, right on the punchline. That's the language owned."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and land «пра́вильно» like a punchline — after a tiny pause.","target":"«Кто? Я?» — «Пра́вильно!» — и вся шу́тка в после́днем сло́ве.","sub":"hold the beat before «пра́вильно» — the pause is the joke","want":"кто я правильно и вся шутка в последнем слове"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"A joke told aloud, with a setup and a punchline, is an … . Type it (hint: анек…):","accept":["анекдот"],"placeholder":"анек…","explain":"<b>анекдо́т</b> — a joke told aloud. Note: not the English «anecdote» (that's «слу́чай» / «исто́рия»)."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя, Ива́н's friend, tries a joke on Эмма — and this time it lands without a beat of delay. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Слу́шай анекдо́т: Шти́рлиц вы́стрелил вслепу́ю. Слепа́я упа́ла.","ru":"Listen to this joke: Stirlitz fired blindly. The blind woman fell."},{"who":"A","en":"«Вслепу́ю» — «в слепу́ю»! Дошло́ сра́зу, без пере́вода в голове́.","ru":"«Blindly» — «at the blind woman»! I got it instantly, no translating in my head."},{"who":"B","en":"А ра́ньше ты бы переспроси́ла и попроси́ла объясни́ть.","ru":"You'd have asked me to repeat it and explain, before."},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше — да. Пока́ разберёшь каламбу́р, уже́ не смешно́.","ru":"Before — yes. By the time you unpick the pun, it's not funny anymore."},{"who":"B","en":"Вот и́менно! Шу́тку на́до пойма́ть на после́днем сло́ве.","ru":"Exactly! You have to catch a joke on its last word."},{"who":"A","en":"И са́мое гла́вное — я засмея́лась сама́, без подска́зки.","ru":"And the main thing — I laughed on my own, without a nudge."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, ру́сский тебе́ уже́ почти́ родно́й.","ru":"Then Russian is almost native to you now."},{"who":"A","en":"Почти́. Оста́лось научи́ться так же бы́стро остри́ть в отве́т.","ru":"Almost. I just have to learn to quip back just as fast."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the joke and its wit. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"анекдо́т","ru":"a joke (told aloud)","ex":"Он рассказа́л ста́рый анекдо́т."},{"en":"ю́мор","ru":"humour","ex":"У неё то́нкий ю́мор."},{"en":"остроу́мие","ru":"wit, quickness of mind","ex":"В нём мно́го остроу́мия."},{"en":"зачи́н","ru":"the setup (of a joke)","ex":"Зачи́н — всегда́ в настоя́щем вре́мени."},{"en":"панчла́йн","ru":"the punchline","ex":"Весь смысл — в панчла́йне."},{"en":"развя́зка","ru":"the resolution, the payoff","ex":"Развя́зка прихо́дит в после́днем сло́ве."},{"en":"шу́тка","ru":"a joke, a jest","ex":"Э́то ста́рая до́брая шу́тка."},{"en":"самоиро́ния","ru":"self-irony","ex":"Ру́сский ю́мор по́лон самоиро́нии."},{"en":"остри́ть","ru":"to quip, to crack jokes","ex":"Он уме́ет остри́ть в отве́т."},{"en":"смешно́й","ru":"funny","ex":"Э́то был о́чень смешно́й слу́чай."},{"en":"па́уза","ru":"a pause (the beat)","ex":"Па́уза пе́ред панчла́йном реша́ет всё."},{"en":"бытово́й","ru":"everyday (of daily life)","ex":"Бытово́й ю́мор — про ку́хню и о́чередь."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each term to what it is — the part of the joke, not an example.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["зачи́н","the setup — a bare scene, present tense"],["панчла́йн","the punchline — the last words, the snap"],["самоиро́ния","laughing at oneself"],["бытово́й ю́мор","the comedy of ordinary daily life"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"In an анекдо́т, the funny word comes —","opts":["first, to grab attention","somewhere in the middle","last — it's the punchline"],"answer":2},{"q":"The three beats of a joke, in order, are —","opts":["зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн","панчла́йн → зачи́н → разворо́т","разворо́т → зачи́н → панчла́йн"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Шти́рлиц вы́стрелил вслепу́ю…» is powered by —","opts":["a pun («вслепу́ю» / «в слепу́ю»)","a shared cultural detail","pure chance"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Лу́чше всех, но никто́ не зави́дует» is —","opts":["a genuine complaint","самоиро́ния — self-mockery","a threat"],"answer":1},{"q":"The surest sign you've «got» a Russian joke is —","opts":["you can explain it later","you can translate every word","you laugh on the beat, no explanation"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short reflection on why the Russian joke lands on its last word — and why so much of it stays in Russian. Read each line aloud.","title":"После́днее сло́во реша́ет всё","sentences":["Ру́сский анекдо́т — э́то маши́на, у кото́рой всё де́ржится на после́днем сло́ве.","Снача́ла зачи́н, пото́м разворо́т, и то́лько в конце́ — панчла́йн.","Ча́сто шу́тка сиди́т в са́мом языке́: в каламбу́ре, в падеже́, в двойно́м смы́сле.","Поэ́тому мно́гие анекдо́ты нельзя́ перевести́ — сме́шно то́лько по-ру́сски.","А ру́сский ю́мор ча́ще всего́ смеётся над сами́м собо́й.","Кто смеётся во́время, без объясне́ний, — тот услы́шал язы́к до конца́."],"translation":"The Russian joke is a machine, and everything in it hangs on the last word. First the setup, then the turn, and only at the end — the punchline. Often the joke sits in the language itself: in a pun, in a case ending, in a double meaning. That's why many jokes can't be translated — they're funny only in Russian. And Russian humour, more often than not, laughs at itself. Whoever laughs on time, without an explanation, has heard the language all the way to the end."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to build a joke — and take it apart.","prompt":"Find or build ONE short, clean Russian анекдо́т (2–4 lines) with a clear setup and a punchline at the very end — or take a joke you know and lay out its three beats: зачи́н, разворо́т, панчла́йн. Then, in one or two sentences, say what powers it — a pun, a grammar twist, absurd, self-irony — and whether it would survive a word-for-word English version. Keep it kind. 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Guard the last word: the funny bit must land at the very end, after a tiny pause. Then ask WHY it's funny — and whether English keeps the same double sense 🙂","example":"Учи́тельница про́сит Во́вочку назва́ть два местоиме́ния. Во́вочка удивлённо: «Кто? Я?» — «Пра́вильно!» Шу́тка де́ржится на грамма́тике: «кто» и «я» — э́то и есть два местоиме́ния, а панчла́йн стои́т в са́мом после́днем сло́ве. По-англи́йски «Who? Me?» то́же местоиме́ния, но по-ру́сски э́то слы́шно сра́зу, на одно́м дыха́нии."}},"14":{"day":14,"week":"02","themeRu":"Review 2","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 2","intro":"Six days ago a sentence meant only what it said. Now you hear the second layer under «Я про́сто спра́шиваю», you catch the wink in «Ну ты ге́ний, коне́чно», you see the seam a pun splits open, and you clock the quotation Эмма drops without a footnote. That's a whole week of hearing what <i>isn't</i> on the page — подте́кст, иро́ния, языкова́я игра́, аллю́зия, стилиза́ция, and the anatomy of a joke. Today we hold it still and check that the second layer is really yours.","introAudio":"Six days ago a sentence meant only what it said. Now you hear the second layer under 'Ya prosto sprashivayu', you catch the wink in 'Nu ty geniy, konechno', you see the seam a pun splits open, and you clock the quotation Emma drops without a footnote. That's a whole week of hearing what isn't on the page — podtekst, ironiya, yazykovaya igra, allyuziya, stilizatsiya, and the anatomy of a joke. Today we hold it still and check that the second layer is really yours.","goals":["Pin down the four moves of week two: <b>подте́кст</b> (subtext), <b>иро́ния</b> (irony), <b>языкова́я игра́</b> (wordplay), <b>аллю́зия</b> (allusion)","Read what <i>isn't</i> said — the reproach under «Я про́сто спра́шиваю», the opposite under «Молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь»","Split a pun at its seam, and place a precedent phrase (<b>прецеде́нтная фра́за</b>) to the source it winks at","Tell a deliberate borrowed voice (<b>стилиза́ция</b>) from an accident, and feel why a Russian анекдо́т lands"],"learned":["Pinned down subtext, irony, wordplay and allusion — the four ways meaning hides","Read the reproach and the opposite under an innocent-looking line","Split a pun at its seam and traced a precedent phrase to its source","Told a chosen voice from an accident, and named why a joke lands"],"review":{"intro":"Backward first — yesterday's анекдо́т. A Russian joke is wound like a spring: <b>зачи́н</b> (set-up) → <b>разворо́т</b> (turn) → <b>панчла́йн</b> (the release). One minute on how it's wound, then we gather the whole week.","introAudio":"Backward first — yesterday's anekdot. A Russian joke is wound like a spring: zachin, the set-up, razvorot, the turn, panchlayn, the release. One minute on how it's wound, then we gather the whole week.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's joke-shape.","q":"A Russian анекдо́т is usually built in three beats. In order, they are —","opts":["зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн","панчла́йн → зачи́н → разворо́т","разворо́т → панчла́йн → зачи́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Set-up, turn, then the release: <b>зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн</b>. The last beat springs the trap the first two quietly set."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the self-ironic line.","q":"Самоиро́ния (self-irony) in one breath: “Well, I'm a genius, of course.” Build it:","want":"Ну я, коне́чно, ге́ний","answer":"Ну я, коне́чно, ге́ний"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Saying «Молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь» flatly after a blunder means the opposite of the words. That reversal is called — type it (hint: иро́…):","accept":["ирония"],"placeholder":"иро́…","explain":"<b>иро́ния</b> — saying the opposite of what you mean and trusting the hearer to flip it."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The layer under the words","title":"<em>Подте́кст</em> and <em>иро́ния</em>: meaning that hides","paras":["Two of this week's moves live <i>below</i> the sentence. <b>Подте́кст</b> (subtext) is meaning the words don't state but plainly carry: «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» — “I'm just asking” — over the right situation isn't a question at all, it's a reproach. Nothing on the surface says so; a native hears it anyway.","<b>Иро́ния</b> is the sharper cousin: you say one thing and mean its opposite, trusting the hearer to flip it. «Молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь» after someone spills the coffee praises nothing. The flip is carried by tone, by the situation, and sometimes by ironic quotes: он «отремонти́ровал» кран — and now it leaks worse.","The English-speaker's trap all week: reading these flat. Take «Я про́сто спра́шиваю» at face value and you miss the reproach; take «Ну ты ге́ний» literally and you thank someone who's mocking you. The words are the smaller half of the message."],"audio":"Two of this week's moves live below the sentence. Podtekst, subtext, is meaning the words don't state but plainly carry: 'Ya prosto sprashivayu', I'm just asking, over the right situation isn't a question at all, it's a reproach. Nothing on the surface says so; a native hears it anyway. Ironiya is the sharper cousin: you say one thing and mean its opposite, trusting the hearer to flip it. 'Molodets, nichego ne skazhesh' after someone spills the coffee praises nothing. The flip is carried by tone, by the situation, and sometimes by ironic quotes: on 'otremontiroval' kran — and now it leaks worse. The English-speaker's trap all week: reading these flat. Take 'Ya prosto sprashivayu' at face value and you miss the reproach; take 'Nu ty geniy' literally and you thank someone who's mocking you. The words are the smaller half of the message.","table":{"rows":[["«Я про́сто спра́шиваю» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я просто спрашиваю\">🔊</button>","surface: a question · subtext: a reproach"],["«Молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Молодец, ничего не скажешь\">🔊</button>","surface: praise · irony: the opposite"],["он «почини́л» кран <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он починил кран\">🔊</button>","ironic quotes: he 'fixed' it — now it's worse"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"“I'm just asking” — but everyone at the table heard the reproach.","en":"«Я про́сто спра́шиваю» — но за столо́м все услы́шали упрёк.","say":"Я просто спрашиваю — но за столом все услышали упрёк."},{"ru":"“Nice work, I must say,” she said, looking at the shattered cup.","en":"«Молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь», — сказа́ла она́, гля́дя на разби́тую ча́шку.","say":"Молодец, ничего не скажешь, — сказала она, глядя на разбитую чашку."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он наконе́ц «почини́л» кран, и тепе́рь всё отли́чно.»","right":"«Он наконе́ц почини́л кран, и тепе́рь всё отли́чно.»","why":"The ironic quotes around «почини́л» flip the sentence into sarcasm — “he ‘fixed’ it”, i.e. he didn't. If you really mean he fixed it and all is well, drop the quotes. The punctuation alone carries the irony."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"At the office «Я про́сто спра́шиваю», said after a colleague's third late report, most likely carries —","opts":["a neutral request for information","a reproach dressed as a question","an apology"],"answer":1,"explain":"The words say “just asking”; the situation makes it a <b>reproach</b>. That gap between said and meant is подте́кст."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ну ты ге́ний, коне́чно!» after a friend deletes the wrong file is —","opts":["sincere praise","иро́ния — the opposite is meant","a factual claim about intelligence"],"answer":1,"explain":"Said over a fresh disaster, it flips: <b>иро́ния</b>. The hearer is trusted to reverse it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The seam and the wink","title":"<em>Языкова́я игра́</em> and <em>аллю́зия</em>: meaning that plays","paras":["<b>Языкова́я игра́</b> (wordplay) makes a word do two jobs at once. A каламбу́р (pun) rides on words that sound alike, or on one word with two senses; a headline like «Ме́бель без пробле́бель» is funny only if you hear «пробле́м» split and re-glued. The joke lives in the seam — miss it and you see a typo.","<b>Аллю́зия</b> (allusion) points outward. A прецеде́нтная фра́за — a precedent phrase — is a line so well known it drags its whole source along: «Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли» (from “The Little Prince”) lands a note of tender duty in one breath. Native speech is stitched with these; drop «Тепло́ ли тебе́, деви́ца?» and a whole fairy tale colours the moment.","Both slip past a literal reader. Miss the seam and a pun is just a typo; miss the source and an allusion is just an odd sentence. This week you learned to hear both — and, at C2, to <i>place</i> them, not only smile at them."],"audio":"Yazykovaya igra, wordplay, makes a word do two jobs at once. A kalambur, a pun, rides on words that sound alike, or on one word with two senses; a headline like 'Mebel bez problebel' is funny only if you hear 'problem' split and re-glued. The joke lives in the seam — miss it and you see a typo. Allyuziya, allusion, points outward. A pretsedentnaya fraza, a precedent phrase, is a line so well known it drags its whole source along: 'My v otvete za tekh, kogo priruchili', from The Little Prince, lands a note of tender duty in one breath. Native speech is stitched with these; drop 'Teplo li tebe, devitsa?' and a whole fairy tale colours the moment. Both slip past a literal reader. Miss the seam and a pun is just a typo; miss the source and an allusion is just an odd sentence. This week you learned to hear both — and, at C2, to place them, not only smile at them.","examples":[{"ru":"The pun only works if you hear «пробле́м» inside «пробле́бель».","en":"Каламбу́р рабо́тает, то́лько е́сли слы́шишь «пробле́м» в «пробле́бель».","say":"Каламбур работает, только если слышишь проблем в проблебель."},{"ru":"“We are responsible for those we've tamed” — and everyone hears the Little Prince.","en":"«Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли» — и все слы́шат Ма́ленького при́нца.","say":"Мы в ответе за тех, кого приручили — и все слышат Маленького принца."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Прочита́ть „Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли“ как сове́т по ухо́ду за живо́тными.»","right":"«Услы́шать в „Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли“ отсы́лку к „Ма́ленькому при́нцу“.»","why":"Read literally, it's advice about pets; heard as a прецеде́нтная фра́за, it's Saint-Exupéry, and it lends the moment a note of tender responsibility. The precedent phrase carries its whole source with it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ме́бель без пробле́бель» is funny because —","opts":["it's a spelling error","«пробле́м» is split and re-glued into a new word — a каламбу́р","it quotes a famous poem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The seam where «пробле́м» breaks and rejoins is the whole joke — <b>языкова́я игра́</b>, not a typo."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · A voice on purpose, and why it's funny","title":"<em>Стилиза́ция</em> and the <em>анекдо́т</em>: meaning you stage","paras":["<b>Стилиза́ция</b> (stylization) is wearing another voice on purpose. A master can write «под ле́топись» (like an old chronicle), «под ска́зку», «под протоко́л», or «под Го́голя» — the borrowed style is a costume, chosen, not slipped into by accident. The tell is control: every marker points the same way, and the writer could drop the mask at will.","The week ends on laughter. A Russian <b>анекдо́т</b> winds like a spring — <b>зачи́н</b> (set-up), <b>разворо́т</b> (turn), <b>панчла́йн</b> (release) — and often springs on the very things you drilled this week: a double meaning, a flipped word, a precedent phrase turned sideways. Самоиро́ния — laughing at yourself first — is the most native move of all.","Put the week together: подте́кст, иро́ния, языкова́я игра́, аллю́зия, стилиза́ция, ю́мор. Six ways a sentence can mean more, less, or the opposite of what it says — and by now you hear all six."],"audio":"Stilizatsiya, stylization, is wearing another voice on purpose. A master can write 'pod letopis', like an old chronicle, 'pod skazku', 'pod protokol', or 'pod Gogolya' — the borrowed style is a costume, chosen, not slipped into by accident. The tell is control: every marker points the same way, and the writer could drop the mask at will. The week ends on laughter. A Russian anekdot winds like a spring — zachin, the set-up, razvorot, the turn, panchlayn, the release — and often springs on the very things you drilled this week: a double meaning, a flipped word, a precedent phrase turned sideways. Samoironiya, laughing at yourself first, is the most native move of all. Put the week together: podtekst, ironiya, yazykovaya igra, allyuziya, stilizatsiya, yumor. Six ways a sentence can mean more, less, or the opposite of what it says — and by now you hear all six.","examples":[{"ru":"As a joke she wrote it “in protocol style”: “I hereby inform you…”.","en":"Она́ шутя́ написа́ла «под протоко́л»: «Настоя́щим уведомля́ю…».","say":"Она шутя написала под протокол: Настоящим уведомляю…"},{"ru":"A good joke sets a trap in the first line and springs it in the last.","en":"Хоро́ший анекдо́т ста́вит лову́шку в пе́рвой стро́ке и захло́пывает в после́дней.","say":"Хороший анекдот ставит ловушку в первой строке и захлопывает в последней."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он неча́янно написа́л под старину́.»","right":"«Он наро́чно написа́л под старину́ — э́то стилиза́ция.»","why":"Стилиза́ция is deliberate — a chosen costume, every marker controlled. If it just happened by accident, it's an uneven style, not стилиза́ция. The whole point is the choice."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Writing a shopping list «под ле́топись» — in the voice of an old chronicle — for fun is an example of —","opts":["стилиза́ция — a borrowed voice worn on purpose","подте́кст — an unspoken reproach","a translation error"],"answer":0,"explain":"A chosen costume of style, controlled from outside — that's <b>стилиза́ция</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма shows Ива́н a newspaper she's marking up for a lesson on wordplay. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Что смешно́го в газе́те? Ты уже́ пять мину́т улыба́ешься.","ru":"What's so funny in the paper? You've been smiling for five minutes."},{"who":"A","en":"Заголо́вок: «Ме́бель без пробле́бель». Слы́шишь, как «пробле́м» слома́лось?","ru":"The headline: “Furniture without probl-blems.” Hear how «пробле́м» breaks?"},{"who":"B","en":"А, каламбу́р. Я снача́ла при́нял его́ за опеча́тку.","ru":"Ah, a pun. At first I took it for a typo."},{"who":"A","en":"На то и расчёт. А ни́же — «Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли», про прию́т для соба́к.","ru":"That's the whole trick. And below — “We are responsible for those we've tamed,” about a dog shelter."},{"who":"B","en":"Ма́ленький принц в объявле́нии про соба́к. Наха́льно и то́чно.","ru":"The Little Prince in a dog ad. Cheeky and exact."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот э́то и есть язы́к носи́теля: намёк, отсы́лка, второ́й слой.","ru":"That right there is the native's language: a hint, a reference, a second layer."},{"who":"B","en":"А по́мнишь, ты э́того всего́ не слы́шала?","ru":"And remember when you didn't hear any of this?"},{"who":"A","en":"По́мню. Тепе́рь не слы́шать — не получа́ется.","ru":"I remember. Now not hearing it — that's the part I can't do."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the week's second layer. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"подте́кст","ru":"subtext, the unspoken layer","ex":"В её вопро́се был я́вный подте́кст."},{"en":"намёк","ru":"a hint, an insinuation","ex":"Он по́нял намёк без слов."},{"en":"иро́ния","ru":"irony (saying the opposite)","ex":"В его́ го́лосе слы́шалась иро́ния."},{"en":"сарка́зм","ru":"sarcasm (sharp, mocking irony)","ex":"Э́то был не ю́мор, а сарка́зм."},{"en":"языкова́я игра́","ru":"wordplay, language play","ex":"Рекла́ма постро́ена на языково́й игре́."},{"en":"каламбу́р","ru":"a pun","ex":"Уда́чный каламбу́р запомина́ется."},{"en":"аллю́зия","ru":"an allusion","ex":"Заголо́вок — аллю́зия на изве́стный фильм."},{"en":"прецеде́нтная фра́за","ru":"a precedent phrase (a widely known line)","ex":"«Быть и́ли не быть» — прецеде́нтная фра́за."},{"en":"отсы́лка","ru":"a reference (to a source)","ex":"Все по́няли отсы́лку к ска́зке."},{"en":"стилиза́ция","ru":"stylization (a deliberate borrowed voice)","ex":"Э́то стилиза́ция под стари́нный слог."},{"en":"анекдо́т","ru":"a joke (with a punchline)","ex":"Он рассказа́л ста́рый анекдо́т."},{"en":"самоиро́ния","ru":"self-irony","ex":"Самоиро́ния — при́знак ума́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each week-two move to what it does — the mechanism, not just the label.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto what it does — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["подте́кст","subtext — meaning the words carry but don't state"],["иро́ния","irony — saying one thing, meaning the opposite"],["каламбу́р","a pun — one word doing two jobs at a seam"],["аллю́зия","an allusion — a wink at a known source"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions across the whole week. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Я про́сто спра́шиваю», said pointedly, is an example of —","opts":["подте́кст — a reproach under a question","a real request for facts","a каламбу́р"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь» over a fresh mess means —","opts":["genuine praise","the opposite — иро́ния","plain confusion"],"answer":1},{"q":"The headline «Ме́бель без пробле́бель» works by —","opts":["quoting a poem","a spelling mistake","splitting «пробле́м» — a каламбу́р"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли» dropped into a dog-shelter ad is —","opts":["an аллю́зия to “The Little Prince”","a legal term","an accident of wording"],"answer":0},{"q":"Writing a grocery list in the voice of an old chronicle, on purpose, is —","opts":["подте́кст","стилиза́ция","сарка́зм"],"answer":1},{"q":"The three beats of a Russian анекдо́т, in order, are —","opts":["зачи́н → разворо́т → панчла́йн","панчла́йн → зачи́н → разворо́т","разворо́т → панчла́йн → зачи́н"],"answer":0},{"q":"Ironic quotes in «он „почини́л“ кран» tell you the sentence is —","opts":["sincere","sarcastic — he didn't really fix it","a quotation from a book"],"answer":1},{"q":"Laughing at yourself before anyone else can is —","opts":["сарка́зм","самоиро́ния","стилиза́ция"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A tiny scene where nothing is said straight. Read each line aloud and name the layer under it — subtext, irony, or a wink.","title":"Второ́й слой","sentences":["«Ты, коне́чно, лу́чше зна́ешь», — сказа́ла Ле́на, и в её го́лосе не́ было тепла́.","Он «внима́тельно вы́слушал» — то есть смотре́л в телефо́н.","«Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли», — вздохну́л он, гля́дя на щенка́.","«Ну ты, коне́чно, ге́ний», — и все по́няли, что э́то наоборо́т.","Ни одного́ прямо́го сло́ва — а всё по́нято до конца́."],"translation":"“You know better, of course,” said Лена — and there was no warmth in her voice. He “listened attentively” — that is, he stared at his phone. “We are responsible for those we've tamed,” he sighed, looking at the puppy. “Well, you're a genius, of course,” — and everyone understood it meant the opposite. Not a single direct word — and everything understood, completely."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to write in two layers.","prompt":"Write a short exchange (4–6 lines) between two people where <b>nothing is said straight</b>. Use at least three of the week's moves — подте́кст (subtext), иро́ния (irony), and a каламбу́р (pun) or an аллю́зия (allusion) — and after each line, in a few English words, name the layer under it. Keep it natural, the way friends actually needle each other.","hint":"Ask of every line: could a literal reader take this the wrong way? If yes, you've got a second layer. Irony flips the words; subtext hides under them; an allusion points past them 🙂","example":"— Ты, как всегда́, во́время. (иро́ния: он опозда́л на час.) — Я про́сто уточня́ю, кто гото́вит у́жин. (подте́кст: гото́вить — твоя́ о́чередь.) — Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ пригласи́ли. (аллю́зия на «Ма́ленького при́нца» — шу́тка про госте́й.) Три ко́ротких ре́плики — и ни одно́й пря́мо."}},"15":{"day":15,"week":"03","themeRu":"Figures of speech as tools","themeEn":"Ритори́ческие фигу́ры как инструме́нт","intro":"«Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л» — three words everyone can name as a figure. But naming it is where C1 stopped. This week you cross to the other side of the desk: you don't spot the anaphora in someone's essay, you <b>build</b> one into yours. Эмма is polishing a friend's speech today, and the difference between a flat list and a line that lands is exactly a rhetorical figure, placed on purpose. Let's pick up the tools — and learn the one rule that keeps them from turning purple.","introAudio":"'Prishyol, uvidel, pobedil' — three words everyone can name as a figure. But naming it is where C1 stopped. This week you cross to the other side of the desk: you don't spot the anaphora in someone's essay, you build one into yours. Emma is polishing a friend's speech today, and the difference between a flat list and a line that lands is exactly a rhetorical figure, placed on purpose. Let's pick up the tools — and learn the one rule that keeps them from turning purple.","goals":["Cross from <i>naming</i> a figure to <b>building</b> one — the C2 turn","Build the two engines of repetition: <b>ана́фора</b> (same opening) and <b>града́ция</b> (each step stronger)","Pin opposites with <b>антите́за</b> and cross the order with <b>хиа́зм</b> (A-B → B-A)","Use the quieter figures — <b>ритори́ческий вопро́с</b>, <b>умолча́ние</b> — and obey the dose"],"learned":["Crossed from naming figures to building them","Built anaphora (a repeated opening) and gradation (a climb to the heaviest word)","Pinned opposites with antithesis and crossed the order with chiasmus","Used the rhetorical question and aposiopesis — and learned the dose that keeps them from curdling"],"review":{"intro":"Backward first: yesterday you gathered the second layer — subtext, irony, the wink of a quotation. Hold one thread of it, then we pick up a new tool: figures you don't just spot but build.","introAudio":"Backward first: yesterday you gathered the second layer — subtext, irony, the wink of a quotation. Hold one thread of it, then we pick up a new tool: figures you don't just spot but build.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's second layer.","q":"«Ну ты ге́ний, коне́чно», said over a fresh mistake, is —","opts":["sincere praise","иро́ния — the opposite is meant","a rhetorical figure"],"answer":1,"explain":"Said over a disaster, it flips to its opposite: <b>иро́ния</b>. You learned to hear that reversal all last week."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Мы в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли» dropped into an ad is a(n) —","opts":["аллю́зия to a known source","spelling error","official term"],"answer":0,"explain":"A line famous enough to drag its whole source along — an <b>аллю́зия</b> (here, “The Little Prince”)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to rebuild the innocent line.","q":"Rebuild yesterday's innocent line that hides a reproach: “I'm just asking.”","want":"Я про́сто спра́шиваю","answer":"Я про́сто спра́шиваю"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · From naming to building","title":"A figure is a <em>tool</em>, not a label — and <em>параллели́зм</em> is the frame","paras":["You can already name the figures — that was C1: recognising тропы (tropes) and expressive syntax in someone else's text. C2 turns it the other way round. You <b>build</b> with them. A ритори́ческая фигу́ра (rhetorical figure) is a way of arranging words so the shape itself carries force — repetition, contrast, a mounting rhythm — beyond what the words alone say.","Under most of them sits one frame: <b>параллели́зм</b> (parallelism) — matching parts built to the same pattern. «Го́род спал, спал вокза́л, спа́ли фонари́» — the same shape three times, and the sentence starts to march. Set the frame first; the sharper figures are all variations on it.","The English-speaker's trap is treating a figure as decoration bolted on afterward. A native builds the thought <i>into</i> the shape: the parallel isn't ornament, it's the load-bearing wall. Get the frame right and the force comes almost for free."],"audio":"You can already name the figures — that was C1: recognising tropy, tropes, and expressive syntax in someone else's text. C2 turns it the other way round. You build with them. A ritoricheskaya figura, a rhetorical figure, is a way of arranging words so the shape itself carries force — repetition, contrast, a mounting rhythm — beyond what the words alone say. Under most of them sits one frame: parallelizm, parallelism, matching parts built to the same pattern. 'Gorod spal, spal vokzal, spali fonari' — the same shape three times, and the sentence starts to march. Set the frame first; the sharper figures are all variations on it. The English-speaker's trap is treating a figure as decoration bolted on afterward. A native builds the thought into the shape: the parallel isn't ornament, it's the load-bearing wall. Get the frame right and the force comes almost for free.","table":{"rows":[["параллели́зм <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"параллелизм\">🔊</button>","matching parts on one pattern — the frame"],["ана́фора <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"анафора\">🔊</button>","the same opening, part after part"],["града́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"градация\">🔊</button>","each step stronger — a climb to a peak"],["антите́за <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"антитеза\">🔊</button>","two opposites pinned side by side"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The same shape three times, and the line starts to sound like a march.","en":"Оди́н рису́нок фра́зы три ра́за — и стро́ка звучи́т как марш.","say":"Один рисунок фразы три раза — и строка звучит как марш."},{"ru":"A figure isn't decoration you glue on — it's the shape of the thought itself.","en":"Фигу́ра — не украше́ние, кото́рое кле́ят све́рху, а фо́рма са́мой мы́сли.","say":"Фигура — не украшение, которое клеят сверху, а форма самой мысли."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я хочу́ сказа́ть, что жизнь — борьба́, и, в о́бщем, на́до боро́ться.»","right":"«Жизнь — борьба́. И тот, кто не бо́рется, уже́ проигра́л.»","why":"The first states the thought flat, then pads it. The second gives it a shape — a short claim, then a parallel that turns the screw. Same idea; the figure is what makes it land."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"At C2, the point of a rhetorical figure is to —","opts":["name it correctly in someone else's text","build the thought into a shape that carries force","make the sentence longer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Recognising figures was C1. C2 <b>builds</b> with them — the shape does work the bare words can't."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Го́род спал, спал вокза́л, спа́ли фонари́» is held together by —","opts":["параллели́зм — one pattern, repeated","an allusion","a spelling pun"],"answer":0,"explain":"Three clauses on one matched pattern — <b>параллели́зм</b>, the frame under most figures."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Repeat, and climb","title":"<em>Ана́фора</em> repeats the opening; <em>града́ция</em> makes each step stronger","paras":["<b>Ана́фора</b> (anaphora) begins successive parts with the same word, and the repetition itself builds pressure: «Жду тебя́ у́тром. Жду тебя́ днём. Жду тебя́ но́чью». The word «жду» hammers; the sentence gains weight with every return.","<b>Града́ция</b> (gradation) arranges the parts so each is stronger than the last, climbing to a peak: «Не проси́, не тре́буй, не умоля́й» — the verbs mount. Its most famous shape is «Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л». Build it so the heaviest word lands last; a gradation that sags at the end fails.","Stack them and the effect compounds: anaphora gives the repeated launch, gradation the rising force. But dose them. One built figure per paragraph lands like a blow; three in a row read as a schoolboy exercise. The native reaches for the figure at the moment that matters, not on every line."],"audio":"Anafora, anaphora, begins successive parts with the same word, and the repetition itself builds pressure: 'Zhdu tebya utrom. Zhdu tebya dnyom. Zhdu tebya nochyu.' The word 'zhdu' hammers; the sentence gains weight with every return. Gradatsiya, gradation, arranges the parts so each is stronger than the last, climbing to a peak: 'Ne prosi, ne trebuy, ne umolyay' — the verbs mount. Its most famous shape is 'Prishyol, uvidel, pobedil.' Build it so the heaviest word lands last; a gradation that sags at the end fails. Stack them and the effect compounds: anaphora gives the repeated launch, gradation the rising force. But dose them. One built figure per paragraph lands like a blow; three in a row read as a schoolboy exercise. The native reaches for the figure at the moment that matters, not on every line.","table":{"rows":[["Жду у́тром, жду днём, жду но́чью <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Жду утром, жду днём, жду ночью\">🔊</button>","ана́фора — the same opening each time"],["Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Пришёл, увидел, победил\">🔊</button>","града́ция — each step a bigger claim"],["Не проси́, не тре́буй, не умоля́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не проси, не требуй, не умоляй\">🔊</button>","gradation on «не» — mounting refusal"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"“I wait for you in the morning, I wait at noon, I wait at night.”","en":"«Жду тебя́ у́тром, жду тебя́ днём, жду тебя́ но́чью».","say":"Жду тебя утром, жду тебя днём, жду тебя ночью."},{"ru":"Build a gradation so the last word is the heaviest.","en":"Стро́й града́цию так, что́бы после́днее сло́во бы́ло са́мым тяжёлым.","say":"Строй градацию так, чтобы последнее слово было самым тяжёлым."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он был вели́к, зна́чим и про́сто хоро́ший челове́к.»","right":"«Он был хоро́ший челове́к, зна́чимый — и по-настоя́щему вели́кий.»","why":"A gradation must climb. The first starts at «вели́к» (great) and drops to «хоро́ший» — the energy leaks out. Reorder so the heaviest word lands last: that's what makes града́ция pull."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build a mounting gradation.","q":"Build a three-step града́ция that climbs: “don't beg, don't demand, don't implore.”","want":"Не проси́, не тре́буй, не умоля́й","answer":"Не проси́, не тре́буй, не умоля́й"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Жду у́тром. Жду днём. Жду но́чью.» — the repeated opening «жду» is —","opts":["града́ция","ана́фора — the same opening repeated","антите́за"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each part launches from the same word «жду» — that's <b>ана́фора</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Set against, and cross over","title":"<em>Антите́за</em> pins opposites; <em>хиа́зм</em> crosses the order","paras":["<b>Антите́за</b> (antithesis) sets two opposites in one frame so each sharpens the other: «Они́ говоря́т — мы де́лаем», «Ум с се́рдцем не в ладу́». The parallel shape holds the two halves level while the meanings fight — the contrast is the point.","<b>Хиа́зм</b> (chiasmus) takes a phrase and repeats it with the order reversed — A-B, then B-A: «Еди́м, что́бы жить, а не живём, что́бы есть», «Не спра́шивай, что страна́ сде́лала для тебя́, — спроси́, что ты сде́лал для страны́». The cross makes the sentence feel closed, inevitable, quotable.","Both need a clean frame: matched grammar on each side, so the ear hears the pivot. Sloppy halves and the figure collapses into an ordinary sentence. And both are strong medicine — a chiasmus is memorable precisely because it's rare, so spend it on the one line you want quoted back."],"audio":"Antiteza, antithesis, sets two opposites in one frame so each sharpens the other: 'Oni govoryat — my delaem', 'Um s serdtsem ne v ladu.' The parallel shape holds the two halves level while the meanings fight — the contrast is the point. Khiazm, chiasmus, takes a phrase and repeats it with the order reversed — A-B, then B-A: 'Edim, chtoby zhit, a ne zhivyom, chtoby est', 'Ne sprashivay, chto strana sdelala dlya tebya, sprosi, chto ty sdelal dlya strany.' The cross makes the sentence feel closed, inevitable, quotable. Both need a clean frame: matched grammar on each side, so the ear hears the pivot. Sloppy halves and the figure collapses into an ordinary sentence. And both are strong medicine — a chiasmus is memorable precisely because it's rare, so spend it on the one line you want quoted back.","table":{"rows":[["Они́ говоря́т — мы де́лаем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Они говорят — мы делаем\">🔊</button>","антите́за — opposites in one frame"],["Ум с се́рдцем не в ладу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ум с сердцем не в ладу\">🔊</button>","антите́за — head against heart"],["Еди́м, что́бы жить, а не живём, что́бы есть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Едим, чтобы жить, а не живём, чтобы есть\">🔊</button>","хиа́зм — the order crosses over"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"“They talk — we act”: two opposites in one level frame.","en":"«Они́ говоря́т — мы де́лаем»: два противополо́жных в одно́й ра́вной ра́мке.","say":"Они говорят — мы делаем: два противоположных в одной равной рамке."},{"ru":"The cross makes the line feel closed and quotable.","en":"Перекрёстие де́лает фра́зу закры́той и цита́тной.","say":"Перекрёстие делает фразу закрытой и цитатной."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он лю́бит чита́ть, а его́ брат уме́ет пла́вать.»","right":"«Он лю́бит слова́, а брат — молча́ние.»","why":"The first only lines up two facts — no opposition, so no антите́за. The second pins genuine opposites (words ↔ silence) in a matched frame, and the contrast does the work. Antithesis needs a real clash, not just two clauses."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Не спра́шивай, что страна́ сде́лала для тебя́, — спроси́, что ты сде́лал для страны́» is —","opts":["ана́фора","хиа́зм — the order crosses (A-B → B-A)","града́ция"],"answer":1,"explain":"«страна́ — для тебя́» flips to «ты — для страны́»: the order crosses, so it's <b>хиа́зм</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the figure's name. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Two opposites pinned in one matched frame («бога́т душо́й, но бе́ден слова́ми») — name the figure. Type it (hint: анти…):","accept":["антитеза"],"placeholder":"анти…","explain":"<b>антите́за</b> — a contrast set in a level frame so each half sharpens the other."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Ask without asking, stop short — and the dose","title":"<em>Ритори́ческий вопро́с</em>, <em>умолча́ние</em> — and the rule that governs them all","paras":["Two quieter figures. A <b>ритори́ческий вопро́с</b> (rhetorical question) asks what needs no answer, because the answer is obvious and the asking is itself the argument: «И кто с э́тим согласи́тся?» — nobody, and everyone knows it. It pulls the listener onto your side without your stating the claim.","<b>Умолча́ние</b> (aposiopesis) is the opposite move — you break off and leave the rest unsaid, letting the listener finish it: «Е́сли он опя́ть опозда́ет, то…». The dash does the threatening. What you don't say can hit harder than what you do — last week's подте́кст, now turned into a deliberate figure.","And the one rule that governs all of them: <b>the dose</b>. A built figure is a spice, not the meal. One anaphora, one antithesis, one rhetorical question at the turning point, and the paragraph lifts. Three per line and it curdles into purple — the surest mark of someone <i>performing</i> eloquence rather than having it. Restraint is the C2 signature.","Tomorrow these tools go to work in real time: <b>ора́торское иску́сство</b> (the art of the speech) — how a whole talk is built to move a room, and where in its arc each figure earns its place."],"audio":"Two quieter figures. A ritoricheskiy vopros, a rhetorical question, asks what needs no answer, because the answer is obvious and the asking is itself the argument: 'I kto s etim soglasitsya?' — nobody, and everyone knows it. It pulls the listener onto your side without your stating the claim. Umolchanie, aposiopesis, is the opposite move — you break off and leave the rest unsaid, letting the listener finish it: 'Yesli on opyat opozdayet, to…'. The dash does the threatening. What you don't say can hit harder than what you do — last week's podtekst, now turned into a deliberate figure. And the one rule that governs all of them: the dose. A built figure is a spice, not the meal. One anaphora, one antithesis, one rhetorical question at the turning point, and the paragraph lifts. Three per line and it curdles into purple — the surest mark of someone performing eloquence rather than having it. Restraint is the C2 signature. Tomorrow these tools go to work in real time: oratorskoye iskusstvo, the art of the speech — how a whole talk is built to move a room, and where in its arc each figure earns its place.","table":{"rows":[["И кто с э́тим согласи́тся? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"И кто с этим согласится?\">🔊</button>","ритори́ческий вопро́с — the answer is obvious"],["Е́сли он опя́ть опозда́ет, то… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Если он опять опоздает, то…\">🔊</button>","умолча́ние — break off, let them finish it"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“And who would agree to that?” — a question that is really a claim.","en":"«И кто с э́тим согласи́тся?» — вопро́с, кото́рый на де́ле утвержде́ние.","say":"И кто с этим согласится? — вопрос, который на деле утверждение."},{"ru":"One figure at the turn lifts a paragraph; three curdle it.","en":"Одна́ фигу́ра на поворо́те поднима́ет абза́ц; три — по́ртят.","say":"Одна фигура на повороте поднимает абзац; три — портят."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́то был вели́кий, огро́мный, грандио́зный, невероя́тный, потряса́ющий день.»","right":"«Э́то был день, кото́рый всё измени́л.»","why":"Piling five heightened adjectives isn't a figure — it's noise, and it reads as someone performing emotion. One clean line with a real turn carries more. The dose, not the pile, is the C2 move."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«И кто в здра́вом уме́ на э́то пойдёт?», expecting no answer, is a —","opts":["ритори́ческий вопро́с — the asking is the argument","умолча́ние","an accident of grammar"],"answer":0,"explain":"It asks what needs no answer — everyone knows it's “nobody”. The question <i>is</i> the claim: <b>ритори́ческий вопро́с</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it so the antithesis lands — one side up, one side down.","target":"Оди́н то́чный о́браз — и абза́ц ожива́ет; три подря́д — и он умира́ет.","sub":"let «ожива́ет» rise and «умира́ет» fall — the contrast is the whole line","want":"один точный образ — и абзац оживает; три подряд — и он умирает"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м has to give a talk at work and asks Эмма, the editor, to make his opening «звуча́ть». Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Вот моё нача́ло: «Наш прое́кт ва́жный, поле́зный и, в о́бщем, ну́жный». Ску́чно?","ru":"Here's my opening: “Our project is important, useful and, well, needed.” Boring?"},{"who":"A","en":"Че́стно — да. Ты перечи́слил, но не постро́ил. Дава́й сде́лаем града́цию.","ru":"Honestly — yes. You listed, but you didn't build. Let's make a gradation."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то как?","ru":"How's that?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ка́ждое сло́во сильне́е предыду́щего, и са́мое ва́жное — в конце́: «поле́зный, необходи́мый, незамени́мый».","ru":"Each word stronger than the last, the most important one at the end: “useful, indispensable, irreplaceable.”"},{"who":"B","en":"О, слы́шу подъём. А е́сли ещё повтори́ть нача́ло ка́ждой фра́зы?","ru":"Oh, I hear the rise. And what if I also repeat the start of each phrase?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то ана́фора. Рабо́тает — но не увлека́йся: одна́ фигу́ра на абза́ц, не три.","ru":"That's anaphora. It works — but don't overdo it: one figure per paragraph, not three."},{"who":"B","en":"То есть си́ла — в ме́ре?","ru":"So the power is in the dose?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Фигу́ра — припра́ва, а не всё блю́до.","ru":"Exactly. A figure is a spice, not the whole dish."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the toolbox of figures. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ритори́ческая фигу́ра","ru":"a rhetorical figure","ex":"Ана́фора — ча́стая ритори́ческая фигу́ра."},{"en":"ана́фора","ru":"anaphora (a repeated opening)","ex":"Ана́фора повторя́ет нача́ло фраз."},{"en":"града́ция","ru":"gradation (a mounting climax)","ex":"Града́ция ведёт к са́мому си́льному сло́ву."},{"en":"антите́за","ru":"antithesis (opposites set together)","ex":"На антите́зе постро́ен весь ло́зунг."},{"en":"хиа́зм","ru":"chiasmus (the order crosses over)","ex":"Хиа́зм де́лает фра́зу цита́тной."},{"en":"параллели́зм","ru":"parallelism (matching parts)","ex":"Параллели́зм — карка́с мно́гих фигу́р."},{"en":"ритори́ческий вопро́с","ru":"a rhetorical question","ex":"Ритори́ческий вопро́с не ждёт отве́та."},{"en":"умолча́ние","ru":"aposiopesis (breaking off)","ex":"По́сле «е́сли…» — вырази́тельное умолча́ние."},{"en":"повто́р","ru":"a repetition (as a device)","ex":"Повто́р уси́ливает гла́вную мысль."},{"en":"противопоставле́ние","ru":"a contrast, an opposition","ex":"В осно́ве антите́зы — противопоставле́ние."},{"en":"нагнета́ние","ru":"a build-up, mounting tension","ex":"Нагнета́ние гото́вит кульмина́цию."},{"en":"кульмина́ция","ru":"a climax, the peak","ex":"Речь вы́шла на кульмина́цию."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each figure to what it actually does — the mechanism, not just the name.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each figure on the left onto what it does — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["ана́фора","anaphora — begin each part with the same words"],["града́ция","gradation — mount step by step to a peak"],["антите́за","antithesis — set two opposites side by side"],["хиа́зм","chiasmus — cross the order: A-B, then B-A"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"At C2, a rhetorical figure is something you —","opts":["only recognise in others' writing","build to give a thought force","avoid entirely"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л» is a classic —","opts":["ана́фора","града́ция — each step a bigger claim","умолча́ние"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Жду у́тром, жду днём, жду но́чью» — the repeated opening «жду» is —","opts":["ана́фора","хиа́зм","антите́за"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Еди́м, что́бы жить, а не живём, что́бы есть» crosses its own order. That's —","opts":["антите́за","хиа́зм","ритори́ческий вопро́с"],"answer":1},{"q":"The rule that keeps figures from curdling into purple prose is —","opts":["use as many as you can","the dose — one at the turn, not three per line","always end with a question"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short passage that doesn't just describe figures — it uses them. Read it aloud and feel each one do its work; the labels are in the translation.","title":"Постро́енная фра́за","sentences":["Мы чита́ли, мы спо́рили, мы иска́ли — и не сдава́лись.","Они́ обеща́ли — мы де́лали.","Кни́га не развлека́ет — она́ бу́дит.","И кто ска́жет, что э́то не сто́ило труда́?","Сло́во за сло́вом, страни́ца за страни́цей — так и растёт ма́стер."],"translation":"We read, we argued, we searched — and we didn't give up (anaphora + gradation). They promised — we did (antithesis). A book doesn't entertain — it wakes you (antithesis). And who would say it wasn't worth the effort (rhetorical question)? Word by word, page by page — that's how a master grows (parallelism)."},"essay":{"intro":"Now you build the figure yourself.","prompt":"Take a claim you actually believe — about reading, work, your city, anything — and write it up in 3–4 sentences using <b>at least two</b> built figures from today (ана́фора, града́ция, антите́за, хиа́зм, or a ритори́ческий вопро́с). Then, in a few English words, label which figure you used where. Remember the dose: two figures, placed — not five, piled.","hint":"Draft the plain thought first, then reshape it: repeat an opening, order three things so the heaviest lands last, or pin two opposites in one frame. If it starts to feel purple, cut a figure 🙂","example":"Го́род не спит — го́род ды́шит. У́тром он спеши́т, днём он рабо́тает, но́чью он мечта́ет. И кто ска́жет, что у него́ нет души́? (ана́фора «го́род» и «он»; града́ция спеши́т → рабо́тает → мечта́ет; ритори́ческий вопро́с в конце́.)"}},"16":{"day":16,"week":"03","themeRu":"The art of the speech","themeEn":"Ора́торское иску́сство","intro":"Yesterday you forged the figures; today you learn where to hang them. A speech isn't a bag of clever lines — it's an arc, built to carry a room from «Друзья́…» to the moment everyone leans in. Эмма has been asked to speak at a translators' evening — ten minutes on why a good translation is an act of love — and she's terrified. We'll build her talk the way Russians build one: on ло́гика AND on па́фос, the high, honest feeling an Anglo pitch is too shy to reach for.","introAudio":"Yesterday you forged the figures; today you learn where to hang them. A speech isn't a bag of clever lines — it's an arc, built to carry a room from 'Druzya…' to the moment everyone leans in. Emma has been asked to speak at a translators' evening — ten minutes on why a good translation is an act of love — and she's terrified. We'll build her talk the way Russians build one: on logika AND on pafos, the high, honest feeling an Anglo pitch is too shy to reach for.","goals":["Lay out the arc of a speech: <b>зачи́н → те́зис → аргуме́нты → кульмина́ция → концо́вка</b>","Balance the three ancient pillars in Russian: <b>э́тос</b> (who's speaking), <b>ло́гос</b> (the argument), <b>па́фос</b> (elevated feeling — not pity)","Speak <i>to</i> the room: <b>обраще́ние к за́лу</b>, the inclusive «мы», the live rhetorical question","Raise the register toward the <b>кульмина́ция</b> and land a clean <b>концо́вка</b>"],"learned":["Laid out the five-part arc of a speech, from opening hook to close","Balanced этос, логос and пафос — and learned пафос isn't pity but elevated conviction","Spoke to the room: direct address, the inclusive «мы», the live question","Raised the register toward the climax and landed a clean ending"],"review":{"intro":"Backward one step: yesterday you built the figures — anaphora, gradation, antithesis, chiasmus. Today they get a home. One minute to make sure they're loaded, then we build the whole speech around them.","introAudio":"Backward one step: yesterday you built the figures — anaphora, gradation, antithesis, chiasmus. Today they get a home. One minute to make sure they're loaded, then we build the whole speech around them.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's figures.","q":"«Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л» is —","opts":["ана́фора","града́ция — a mounting climb","умолча́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three verbs, each a bigger claim, climbing to the peak — <b>града́ция</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"The rule that keeps figures from turning purple is —","opts":["use as many as possible","the dose — one at the turn, not three per line","end every line with a question"],"answer":1,"explain":"A figure is a spice, not the meal. The <b>dose</b> is the C2 signature."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to rebuild the chiasmus.","q":"Rebuild yesterday's chiasmus: “we eat to live, and not live to eat.”","want":"Еди́м, что́бы жить, а не живём, что́бы есть","answer":"Еди́м, что́бы жить, а не живём, что́бы есть"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The arc of a speech","title":"Five parts, one climb: <em>зачи́н → те́зис → аргуме́нты → кульмина́ция → концо́вка</em>","paras":["A speech is an arc, not a list. It opens with a <b>зачи́н</b> — a hook that earns attention (a question, a small story, a sharp image), never «Здра́вствуйте, сего́дня я хочу́ рассказа́ть…», which surrenders the room in the first breath. Then the <b>те́зис</b>: one clear claim the whole talk will defend.","The body is the <b>аргуме́нты</b> — three is the native rhythm, ordered weakest-to-strongest so the ground keeps rising under the listener. That rise crests at the <b>кульмина́ция</b>: the emotional and logical peak, the one sentence you want them to carry out the door. Then a short <b>концо́вка</b> — a close that rhymes with the opening and releases the tension, not a limp «ну вот, в о́бщем, спаси́бо».","The English-speaker's instinct is to front-load: thesis first, then evidence, then stop. It's clear — and flat. A Russian talk is built to <i>climb</i>; if the last minute isn't the strongest, the architecture has failed, however sound the content."],"audio":"A speech is an arc, not a list. It opens with a zachin — a hook that earns attention (a question, a small story, a sharp image), never 'Zdravstvuyte, segodnya ya khochu rasskazat…', which surrenders the room in the first breath. Then the tezis: one clear claim the whole talk will defend. The body is the argumenty — three is the native rhythm, ordered weakest-to-strongest so the ground keeps rising under the listener. That rise crests at the kulminatsiya: the emotional and logical peak, the one sentence you want them to carry out the door. Then a short kontsovka — a close that rhymes with the opening and releases the tension, not a limp 'nu vot, v obshchem, spasibo'. The English-speaker's instinct is to front-load: thesis first, then evidence, then stop. It's clear — and flat. A Russian talk is built to climb; if the last minute isn't the strongest, the architecture has failed, however sound the content.","table":{"rows":[["зачи́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зачин\">🔊</button>","the hook — earn attention (a question, an image)"],["те́зис <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тезис\">🔊</button>","the one claim the talk will defend"],["аргуме́нты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"аргументы\">🔊</button>","three reasons, weakest to strongest"],["кульмина́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кульминация\">🔊</button>","the peak — the line they carry out"],["концо́вка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"концовка\">🔊</button>","the close that echoes the opening"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Don't open with “Hello, today I want to tell you…” — you lose the room at once.","en":"Не начина́й с «Здра́вствуйте, сего́дня я хочу́ рассказа́ть…» — ты сра́зу теря́ешь зал.","say":"Не начинай с Здравствуйте, сегодня я хочу рассказать… — ты сразу теряешь зал."},{"ru":"A speech is built to climb — the last minute must be the strongest.","en":"Речь стро́ится по восходя́щей — после́дняя мину́та должна́ быть са́мой си́льной.","say":"Речь строится по восходящей — последняя минута должна быть самой сильной."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Здра́вствуйте. Меня́ зову́т А́нна. Сего́дня я расскажу́ о перево́де.»","right":"«Что мы теря́ем, когда́ чита́ем кни́гу в перево́де? Сего́дня — об э́том.»","why":"The first opening spends the room's attention on a formality. The second is a зачи́н: a question that makes the hall lean in before you've stated a single thesis. Open by earning attention, not by announcing yourself."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The opening move of a Russian speech, the зачи́н, should —","opts":["announce your name and topic","hook the room — a question, an image, a small story","summarise the conclusion first"],"answer":1,"explain":"The зачи́н earns attention. Announcing yourself spends it; a hook wins it."},{"type":"choice","q":"In the arc «зачи́н → те́зис → … → концо́вка», the emotional and logical peak is the —","opts":["кульмина́ция","те́зис","зачи́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"The high point the whole talk climbs toward is the <b>кульмина́ция</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The three pillars","title":"<em>Э́тос</em>, <em>ло́гос</em>, <em>па́фос</em> — and why па́фос isn't pity","paras":["Since antiquity a speech stands on three pillars. <b>Ло́гос</b> is the argument — facts, reasons, structure. <b>Э́тос</b> is you — why the room should trust the person speaking (your standing, your honesty, your right to say this). <b>Па́фос</b> is feeling — the emotion the speech raises in the hall.","Here is the trap, and it's a big one. English «pathos» has drifted toward <i>pity</i> — something sad and small. Russian <b>па́фос</b> means the opposite pole: high, elevated, honest feeling — conviction, inspiration, the lump in the throat at something that matters. «Говори́ть с па́фосом» is to speak with lifted, heartfelt force. Only «дешёвый па́фос» — cheap, hollow grandstanding — carries the sneer; earned па́фос is a virtue.","A Russian audience wants all three and is unmoved by ло́гос alone. The dry, data-only corporate pitch — impeccable logic, zero па́фос — reads as cold and, oddly, less trustworthy. Give them the argument, earn their trust, and at the peak let the feeling rise. Sincerity is the license; па́фос with no ло́гос behind it is exactly the “cheap” kind."],"audio":"Since antiquity a speech stands on three pillars. Logos is the argument — facts, reasons, structure. Etos is you — why the room should trust the person speaking: your standing, your honesty, your right to say this. Pafos is feeling — the emotion the speech raises in the hall. Here is the trap, and it's a big one. English 'pathos' has drifted toward pity — something sad and small. Russian pafos means the opposite pole: high, elevated, honest feeling — conviction, inspiration, the lump in the throat at something that matters. 'Govorit s pafosom' is to speak with lifted, heartfelt force. Only 'deshyovy pafos' — cheap, hollow grandstanding — carries the sneer; earned pafos is a virtue. A Russian audience wants all three and is unmoved by logos alone. The dry, data-only corporate pitch — impeccable logic, zero pafos — reads as cold and, oddly, less trustworthy. Give them the argument, earn their trust, and at the peak let the feeling rise. Sincerity is the license; pafos with no logos behind it is exactly the 'cheap' kind.","table":{"rows":[["ло́гос <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"логос\">🔊</button>","the argument — facts, reasons, structure"],["э́тос <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"этос\">🔊</button>","the speaker — trust, standing, honesty"],["па́фос <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пафос\">🔊</button>","elevated feeling — conviction, NOT pity"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"“To speak with pafos” means with lifted, heartfelt force — not with pity.","en":"«Говори́ть с па́фосом» зна́чит с высо́ким, и́скренним чу́вством — не с жа́лостью.","say":"Говорить с пафосом значит с высоким, искренним чувством — не с жалостью."},{"ru":"Logic alone leaves a Russian hall cold; honest feeling is what moves it.","en":"Одна́ ло́гика оставля́ет ру́сский зал холо́дным; тро́гает и́скреннее чу́вство.","say":"Одна логика оставляет русский зал холодным; трогает искреннее чувство."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Дава́йте я сухо́ приведу́ фа́кты — без вся́ких чувств.»","right":"«Фа́кты — вот они́. А тепе́рь — почему́ э́то ва́жно для ка́ждого из нас.»","why":"Facts with the feeling deliberately stripped out read as cold to a Russian hall — pure ло́гос, no па́фос. The fix isn't to drop the facts but to add the honest “why it matters”. Earned па́фос is trust, not decoration."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Russian <b>па́фос</b> in a speech means —","opts":["pity, something sad and small","elevated, sincere feeling — conviction","boring statistics"],"answer":1,"explain":"The false friend bites here: Russian <b>па́фос</b> is high, honest feeling, not English “pathos” as pity."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The pillar that is the argument itself — facts, reasons, structure (from Greek). Type it (hint: ло́…):","accept":["логос"],"placeholder":"ло́…","explain":"<b>ло́гос</b> — the logical spine of the speech; on its own, though, it leaves a Russian hall cold."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Speak to the room, not at it","title":"<em>Обраще́ние к за́лу</em>: pull the hall into the speech","paras":["A speech is not read <i>at</i> a room; it's built <i>with</i> it. The first tool is <b>обраще́ние</b> — direct address: «Друзья́», «Колле́ги», «Дороги́е го́сти», or a mid-speech «Согласи́тесь…», «Предста́вьте себе́…». It turns a monologue into something the hall is inside.","The inclusive <b>мы</b> does the same quietly: «Мы все зна́ем э́то чу́вство» folds speaker and listeners onto one side. And the live <b>ритори́ческий вопро́с</b> from yesterday earns its keep here — «А ра́зве не э́того мы хоте́ли?» pulls a silent «да» out of the room without your asking for it.","The trap is the wall of «я»: «я счита́ю», «я хочу́ сказа́ть», «по-мо́ему» on repeat builds a barrier between you and the hall. Trade some of those «я» for «мы» and a question, and the same content suddenly includes the listener instead of lecturing them."],"audio":"A speech is not read at a room; it's built with it. The first tool is obrashchenie — direct address: 'Druzya', 'Kollegi', 'Dorogie gosti', or a mid-speech 'Soglasites…', 'Predstavte sebe…'. It turns a monologue into something the hall is inside. The inclusive 'my' does the same quietly: 'My vse znaem eto chuvstvo' folds speaker and listeners onto one side. And the live ritoricheskiy vopros from yesterday earns its keep here — 'A razve ne etogo my khoteli?' pulls a silent 'da' out of the room without your asking for it. The trap is the wall of 'ya': 'ya schitayu', 'ya khochu skazat', 'po-moyemu' on repeat builds a barrier between you and the hall. Trade some of those 'ya' for 'my' and a question, and the same content suddenly includes the listener instead of lecturing them.","table":{"rows":[["Друзья́, согласи́тесь… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Друзья, согласитесь…\">🔊</button>","обраще́ние — speak straight to the hall"],["Мы все зна́ем э́то чу́вство <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мы все знаем это чувство\">🔊</button>","inclusive «мы» — one side, together"],["А ра́зве не э́того мы хоте́ли? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"А разве не этого мы хотели?\">🔊</button>","live question — pulls a silent «да»"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"“Picture it for a moment” pulls the whole hall into your example.","en":"«Предста́вьте себе́ на мину́ту» втя́гивает весь зал в твой приме́р.","say":"Представьте себе на минуту втягивает весь зал в твой пример."},{"ru":"Trade a few of your “I think”s for “we” and a question.","en":"Замени́ не́сколько свои́х «я счита́ю» на «мы» и вопро́с.","say":"Замени несколько своих я считаю на мы и вопрос."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я счита́ю, я ду́маю, я хочу́ сказа́ть, что э́то ва́жно.»","right":"«Согласи́тесь: э́то ва́жно для ка́ждого из нас.»","why":"A wall of «я» keeps the hall at arm's length. Swapping it for обраще́ние and an inclusive «мы»/«нас» folds the listeners into the claim — the same point, now shared instead of asserted at them."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Друзья́, предста́вьте себе́…» in the middle of a talk is an example of —","opts":["обраще́ние к за́лу — direct address","умолча́ние","a spelling pun"],"answer":0,"explain":"Speaking straight to the hall and inviting it into your example — <b>обраще́ние</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which line pulls the hall onto the speaker's side most?","opts":["«По-мо́ему, э́то так.»","«Мы все зна́ем э́то чу́вство, ра́зве нет?»","«Я хочу́ изложи́ть фа́кты.»"],"answer":1,"explain":"The inclusive «мы» plus a live question folds the room in; the «я»-lines keep it outside."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Lift to the peak, then land","title":"Raise the register toward the <em>кульмина́ция</em> — then a clean <em>концо́вка</em>","paras":["A speech that stays at one pitch never peaks. As you climb toward the кульмина́ция, the Russian orator <b>lifts the register</b>: the word choice turns a little more bookish and weighty, the sentences lengthen and gather, and a single built figure — an anaphora, an antithesis — lands at the top. «Мы не про́сто чита́ем — мы храни́м, передаём, продолжа́ем»: the gradation crests exactly where the feeling does.","Then the <b>концо́вка</b> releases it. The best endings echo the зачи́н — answer the opening question, close the opening image — so the whole arc clicks shut. Short, and on the strongest word. Never trail off into «ну, в о́бщем, э́то всё» — that unbuilds everything you climbed.","So the dose still rules: you lift <i>once</i>, at the peak, and the rest of the speech stays plain enough to make that lift feel earned. A talk that shouts throughout has no climax; a talk that never lifts has no heart.","Tomorrow the register turns combative but courteous: <b>поле́мика и усту́пка</b> (debate and concession) — how to disagree firmly and still shake hands: «позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»."],"audio":"A speech that stays at one pitch never peaks. As you climb toward the kulminatsiya, the Russian orator lifts the register: the word choice turns a little more bookish and weighty, the sentences lengthen and gather, and a single built figure — an anaphora, an antithesis — lands at the top. 'My ne prosto chitaem — my khranim, peredayom, prodolzhaem': the gradation crests exactly where the feeling does. Then the kontsovka releases it. The best endings echo the zachin — answer the opening question, close the opening image — so the whole arc clicks shut. Short, and on the strongest word. Never trail off into 'nu, v obshchem, eto vsyo' — that unbuilds everything you climbed. So the dose still rules: you lift once, at the peak, and the rest of the speech stays plain enough to make that lift feel earned. A talk that shouts throughout has no climax; a talk that never lifts has no heart. Tomorrow the register turns combative but courteous: polemika i ustupka, debate and concession — how to disagree firmly and still shake hands: 'pozvolyu sebe ne soglasitsya'.","table":{"rows":[["Мы не про́сто чита́ем — мы храни́м, передаём, продолжа́ем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мы не просто читаем — мы храним, передаём, продолжаем\">🔊</button>","the register lifts as the figure crests"],["…и вот тогда́ мы поймём, заче́м всё э́то бы́ло. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"и вот тогда мы поймём, зачем всё это было\">🔊</button>","концо́вка — short, echoes the opening"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Lift the register once — at the peak — so the rise feels earned.","en":"Поднима́й ре́гистр оди́н раз — на верши́не — что́бы подъём был опра́вдан.","say":"Поднимай регистр один раз — на вершине — чтобы подъём был оправдан."},{"ru":"The best ending answers the very question the opening asked.","en":"Лу́чшая концо́вка отвеча́ет на вопро́с, кото́рый за́дал зачи́н.","say":"Лучшая концовка отвечает на вопрос, который задал зачин."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«…и поэ́тому чте́ние — э́то ва́жно. Ну, в о́бщем, э́то всё, спаси́бо.»","right":"«…и поэ́тому вопро́с не в том, есть ли у нас вре́мя чита́ть, а в том, кто мы без э́того.»","why":"The first climbs to a point and then unbuilds it with a mumbled sign-off. The second is a концо́вка that lifts on an antithesis and lands on the strongest word, echoing the opening question. End on the peak, not after it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Toward the кульмина́ция, a Russian orator typically —","opts":["drops to a flat monotone","lifts the register and lands one built figure","reads faster to finish"],"answer":1,"explain":"The register climbs with the feeling, and a single figure crests at the top. Lift once, and land it."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it so it climbs — each verb a little heavier than the last.","target":"Мы не про́сто чита́ем — мы храни́м, передаём и продолжа́ем.","sub":"let «продолжа́ем» be the heaviest word — the gradation crests on it","want":"мы не просто читаем — мы храним, передаём и продолжаем"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма rehearses her ten-minute talk for a translators' evening; Ка́тя plays the tough audience. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты начина́ешь с «Здра́вствуйте, меня́ зову́т Э́мма». Я уже́ засыпа́ю.","ru":"You open with “Hello, my name is Emma.” I'm already falling asleep."},{"who":"A","en":"Ла́дно. Тогда́ так: «Что мы теря́ем, когда́ чита́ем кни́гу в перево́де?»","ru":"Fine. Then like this: “What do we lose when we read a book in translation?”"},{"who":"B","en":"Вот э́то заце́пка. Сра́зу хо́чется отве́тить.","ru":"Now that's a hook. You immediately want to answer."},{"who":"A","en":"А в конце́ я подниму́ ре́гистр: «Мы не про́сто чита́ем — мы храни́м и передаём».","ru":"And at the end I'll lift the register: “We don't just read — we keep and pass on.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Не сли́шком ли па́фосно?","ru":"Isn't that a bit too much pathos?"},{"who":"A","en":"Па́фос — э́то не сты́дно, е́сли и́скренне. Гла́вное — оди́н раз, на верши́не.","ru":"Pathos isn't shameful if it's sincere. The main thing — once, at the peak."},{"who":"B","en":"А сухи́е ци́фры куда́?","ru":"And where do the dry numbers go?"},{"who":"A","en":"В середи́ну, как фунда́мент. Ло́гос де́ржит, па́фос поднима́ет.","ru":"In the middle, as the foundation. Logos holds it up, pathos lifts it."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the podium. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ора́торское иску́сство","ru":"the art of public speaking, oratory","ex":"Ора́торское иску́сство мо́жно развива́ть."},{"en":"речь","ru":"a speech","ex":"Его́ речь дли́лась де́сять мину́т."},{"en":"зачи́н","ru":"the opening hook","ex":"Уда́чный зачи́н де́ржит внима́ние."},{"en":"те́зис","ru":"the thesis, the main claim","ex":"Весь докла́д защища́ет оди́н те́зис."},{"en":"аргуме́нт","ru":"an argument, a reason","ex":"Она́ привела́ три аргуме́нта."},{"en":"кульмина́ция","ru":"the climax, the peak","ex":"Речь дости́гла кульмина́ции."},{"en":"концо́вка","ru":"the ending, the close","ex":"Си́льная концо́вка запомина́ется."},{"en":"па́фос","ru":"elevated feeling, high emotion (not pity)","ex":"Он говори́л с и́скренним па́фосом."},{"en":"э́тос","ru":"ethos, the speaker's standing","ex":"Дове́рие к ора́тору — э́то э́тос."},{"en":"ло́гос","ru":"logos, the logic of the argument","ex":"Ло́гос — э́то стро́йность до́водов."},{"en":"обраще́ние","ru":"an address (to the audience)","ex":"«Друзья́!» — прямо́е обраще́ние."},{"en":"вы́ступить","ru":"to give a talk, to speak (perfective)","ex":"Ей предложи́ли вы́ступить на ве́чере."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each part of the orator's craft to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto what it does — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["зачи́н","the opening hook — earns the room's attention"],["кульмина́ция","the climax — the peak they carry out the door"],["па́фос","elevated feeling — conviction, not pity"],["обраще́ние","direct address — pull the hall into the speech"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The correct order of a speech's arc is —","opts":["зачи́н → те́зис → аргуме́нты → кульмина́ция → концо́вка","те́зис → зачи́н → концо́вка → аргуме́нты → кульмина́ция","концо́вка → кульмина́ция → аргуме́нты → те́зис → зачи́н"],"answer":0},{"q":"Russian <b>па́фос</b> is —","opts":["pity, something small and sad","elevated, sincere feeling","a logical fallacy"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Друзья́, согласи́тесь…» mid-speech is —","opts":["обраще́ние к за́лу — direct address","a logical fallacy","умолча́ние"],"answer":0},{"q":"A Russian hall is left cold by a talk that relies on —","opts":["ло́гос alone, with the feeling stripped out","too much direct address","a strong ending"],"answer":0},{"q":"The best концо́вка —","opts":["trails off with «ну, в о́бщем, спаси́бо»","echoes the opening and lands on the strongest word","introduces a brand-new argument"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A thirty-second speech in five lines — hook, claim, reason, peak, close. Read it aloud and feel the register lift toward the end.","title":"Ма́ленькая речь","sentences":["Что остаётся от кни́ги, когда́ мы забыва́ем сюже́т?","Остаётся го́лос — тот, что говори́л с на́ми.","Мы забыва́ем имена́ геро́ев, но по́мним, как нам бы́ло с ни́ми.","И вот тогда́ поня́тно: чита́ть — зна́чит не проводи́ть вре́мя, а обрета́ть его́.","Кни́га конча́ется. Го́лос — нет."],"translation":"What's left of a book once we forget the plot? What's left is a voice — the one that spoke to us. We forget the heroes' names, but we remember how it felt to be with them. And then it's clear: to read is not to pass the time, but to gain it. The book ends. The voice does not."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn at the podium.","prompt":"Draft a very short speech — 4–6 sentences — on something you'd actually defend (a book, a habit, a place). Build the full arc: a <b>зачи́н</b> that hooks (a question or image), a clear <b>те́зис</b>, one or two <b>аргуме́нты</b>, a <b>кульмина́ция</b> where you lift the register and land one figure, and a short <b>концо́вка</b> that echoes your opening. Keep the пафос sincere and used once.","hint":"Write the thesis first, then wrap the arc around it. The opening and the closing should feel like two halves of one frame. Lift the register only at the peak — one honest rise, not five 🙂","example":"С чего́ начина́ется у́тро? Для меня́ — с ти́хой ча́шки ко́фе до того́, как проснётся мир. Э́то не привы́чка — э́то грани́ца ме́жду мои́м вре́менем и чужи́м. По́ка го́род спит, день ещё принадлежи́т мне. И́менно поэ́тому я встаю́ ра́ньше всех: не что́бы успе́ть бо́льше, а что́бы успе́ть побы́ть собо́й."}},"17":{"day":17,"week":"03","themeRu":"Winning gracefully","themeEn":"Поле́мика и усту́пка","intro":"A speech moves a room that already leans your way. A debate is harder: someone across the table thinks you're wrong, and says so. Yesterday Эмма learned to lift a hall; today she learns to disagree with one person and keep them as a friend. Russian argument has a paradox at its heart — the firmer your «нет», the more courteous its form. Not the English softening into «ну, мо́жет быть, вы и пра́вы», not the blunt chop — but «Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться», steel wrapped in silk.","introAudio":"A speech moves a room that already leans your way. A debate is harder: someone across the table thinks you're wrong, and says so. Yesterday Emma learned to lift a hall; today she learns to disagree with one person and keep them as a friend. Russian argument has a paradox at its heart — the firmer your 'net', the more courteous its form. Not the English softening into 'nu, mozhet byt, vy i pravy', not the blunt chop — but 'Pozvolyu sebe ne soglasitsya', steel wrapped in silk.","goals":["Hold the paradox of Russian debate: <b>firmness in the form of courtesy</b> — «Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»","Use <b>усту́пка</b> (concession) as a weapon: grant the strong side first — «Допу́стим…», «Не спо́рю, но…» — then counter","Refute the <i>thesis</i>, not the person: argue «по существу́», never «переходи́ть на ли́чности»","Spot the <b>эристи́ческие уло́вки</b> (debate tricks) — straw man, «сам дура́к» — name them, don't use them"],"learned":["Held the paradox: the firmer the disagreement, the more courteous its wrapping","Used concession as a weapon — grant the strong point, then turn it","Kept the thesis and the person apart — argued on the merits, not the man","Recognised the common eristic tricks and refused to use them"],"review":{"intro":"Backward once more: yesterday's speech — its arc, its three pillars, its climb to the peak. One minute to hold it, then we turn the register combative: how to say «нет» and still shake hands.","introAudio":"Backward once more: yesterday's speech — its arc, its three pillars, its climb to the peak. One minute to hold it, then we turn the register combative: how to say no and still shake hands.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's podium.","q":"Russian <b>па́фос</b> in a speech is —","opts":["pity, something sad and small","elevated, sincere feeling","a logical fallacy"],"answer":1,"explain":"The false friend again: Russian <b>па́фос</b> is high, honest conviction, not English “pathos” as pity."},{"type":"choice","q":"The peak a speech climbs toward is the —","opts":["зачи́н","кульмина́ция","концо́вка"],"answer":1,"explain":"The high point of the whole arc — the <b>кульмина́ция</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to rebuild the inclusive opener.","q":"Rebuild yesterday's address to a hall: “Friends, admit it…”","want":"Друзья́, согласи́тесь","answer":"Друзья́, согласи́тесь"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Steel in silk","title":"The firmer the «нет», the more courteous its form — <em>«позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»</em>","paras":["Russian argument runs on a paradox that catches every English speaker. You are <i>expected</i> to disagree firmly — a limp «ну, мо́жет быть, вы и пра́вы» reads as having no position — but the firmness must be wrapped in courtesy. The stronger your objection, the more polished its frame.","The master formula is <b>«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»</b> — “allow me to disagree.” It is dead firm (you <i>are</i> disagreeing, fully) and perfectly courteous (you ask leave to). Its cousins: «Бою́сь, здесь я не могу́ согласи́ться», «У меня́ друго́й взгляд», «Не могу́ раздели́ть э́ту то́чку зре́ния». All say a hard «нет» in a soft voice.","The two English defaults both misfire. Over-softening — «ну, в чём-то вы, наве́рное, пра́вы, я не зна́ю» — sounds spineless to a Russian ear and loses the room. The blunt chop — «Э́то непра́вда», «Вы ошиба́етесь» — sounds rude and makes an enemy. C2 is the third road: unmistakably firm, unfailingly polite."],"audio":"Russian argument runs on a paradox that catches every English speaker. You are expected to disagree firmly — a limp 'nu, mozhet byt, vy i pravy' reads as having no position — but the firmness must be wrapped in courtesy. The stronger your objection, the more polished its frame. The master formula is 'Pozvolyu sebe ne soglasitsya' — allow me to disagree. It is dead firm — you are disagreeing, fully — and perfectly courteous, you ask leave to. Its cousins: 'Boyus, zdes ya ne mogu soglasitsya', 'U menya drugoy vzglyad', 'Ne mogu razdelit etu tochku zreniya'. All say a hard 'net' in a soft voice. The two English defaults both misfire. Over-softening — 'nu, v chyom-to vy, navernoe, pravy, ya ne znayu' — sounds spineless to a Russian ear and loses the room. The blunt chop — 'Eto nepravda', 'Vy oshibaetes' — sounds rude and makes an enemy. C2 is the third road: unmistakably firm, unfailingly polite.","table":{"rows":[["Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Позволю себе не согласиться\">🔊</button>","firm + courteous — the master formula"],["Бою́сь, здесь я не могу́ согласи́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Боюсь, здесь я не могу согласиться\">🔊</button>","a hard «нет» in a soft voice"],["У меня́ друго́й взгляд <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня другой взгляд\">🔊</button>","firm dissent, no aggression"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“Allow me to disagree” — fully firm, and perfectly courteous.","en":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться» — вполне́ твёрдо и соверше́нно ве́жливо.","say":"Позволю себе не согласиться — вполне твёрдо и совершенно вежливо."},{"ru":"Not “you're wrong” — that makes an enemy; wrap the steel in silk.","en":"Не «вы ошиба́етесь» — так наживёшь врага́; оберни́ сталь в шёлк.","say":"Не вы ошибаетесь — так наживёшь врага; оберни сталь в шёлк."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́то по́лная чушь, вы соверше́нно непра́вы.»","right":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться — вот в чём, по-мо́ему, сла́бое ме́сто.»","why":"The blunt chop wins the point and loses the person. The formula keeps the disagreement whole but hands the opponent a courteous frame to answer within."},{"wrong":"«Ну, наве́рное, вы в чём-то пра́вы, не зна́ю…»","right":"«В одно́м вы пра́вы, и всё же гла́вный вы́вод я не разделя́ю.»","why":"Over-softening reads as having no spine. Grant what's true, then state your firm dissent plainly — concession is not surrender."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться» is prized because it is —","opts":["vague and non-committal","firm in substance, courteous in form","openly aggressive"],"answer":1,"explain":"It disagrees fully yet asks leave to — steel wrapped in silk, the C2 third road between mush and rudeness."},{"type":"choice","q":"To a Russian ear, «Ну, мо́жет быть, вы и пра́вы, я не зна́ю» sounds —","opts":["admirably polite","spineless — like having no position","rude and aggressive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Over-softening isn't courtesy here — it reads as having no stance at all. Firmness is expected; only its <i>form</i> is soft."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Concede to conquer","title":"<em>Усту́пка</em> is a weapon: grant the strong point, then turn it","paras":["The counter-intuitive move: <b>усту́пка</b> (concession) makes your argument <i>stronger</i>, not weaker. Before you strike, you grant your opponent their best point — «Допу́стим», «Не спо́рю», «Вы пра́вы в том, что…» — and only then bring the «но». Conceding what's true buys you the authority to reject what isn't.","The shape is always concession + pivot: «Согла́сен, что зада́ча сло́жная, — <b>но</b> из э́того не сле́дует, что она́ невыполни́ма». «Допу́стим, э́то до́рого; <b>одна́ко</b> оши́бка обойдётся доро́же». The pivot word — но, одна́ко, тем не ме́нее, и всё же — is the hinge the whole move turns on.","The English speaker often skips the concession and leads with the «но», which reads as not having listened. Granting first proves you heard, disarms the defence, and makes the counter land as reason rather than reflex. You give an inch to take the mile."],"audio":"The counter-intuitive move: ustupka, concession, makes your argument stronger, not weaker. Before you strike, you grant your opponent their best point — 'Dopustim', 'Ne sporyu', 'Vy pravy v tom, chto…' — and only then bring the 'no'. Conceding what's true buys you the authority to reject what isn't. The shape is always concession plus pivot: 'Soglasen, chto zadacha slozhnaya, no iz etogo ne sleduet, chto ona nevypolnima'. 'Dopustim, eto dorogo; odnako oshibka oboydyotsya dorozhe'. The pivot word — no, odnako, tem ne menee, i vsyo zhe — is the hinge the whole move turns on. The English speaker often skips the concession and leads with the 'no', which reads as not having listened. Granting first proves you heard, disarms the defence, and makes the counter land as reason rather than reflex. You give an inch to take the mile.","table":{"rows":[["Допу́стим, но… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Допустим, но…\">🔊</button>","grant it — then pivot"],["Не спо́рю, одна́ко… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не спорю, однако…\">🔊</button>","concede, then turn"],["Вы пра́вы в том, что… тем не ме́нее… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Вы правы в том, что… тем не менее…\">🔊</button>","concession + the hinge word"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"“Granted, it's expensive — but the mistake will cost more.”","en":"«Допу́стим, э́то до́рого, — но оши́бка обойдётся доро́же».","say":"Допустим, это дорого, — но ошибка обойдётся дороже."},{"ru":"Grant the true point first — it earns you the right to the «но».","en":"Уступи́ ве́рное — э́то даёт пра́во на «но».","say":"Уступи верное — это даёт право на но."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Нет, вы не пра́вы, зада́ча вполне́ выполни́ма.»","right":"«Согла́сен, зада́ча сло́жная, — но из э́того не сле́дует, что она́ невыполни́ма.»","why":"Leading with «нет» reads as not having listened. The version that concedes the hard part first («сло́жная») proves you heard, then turns on the pivot «но». Same conclusion; the concession is what makes it persuasive."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build a concession that pivots.","q":"Build concession + pivot: “Granted, it's expensive, but a mistake will cost more.”","want":"Допу́стим, э́то до́рого, но оши́бка обойдётся доро́же","answer":"Допу́стим, э́то до́рого, но оши́бка обойдётся доро́же"},{"type":"choice","q":"Why grant the opponent's strong point before countering?","opts":["to surrender the argument","it proves you listened and earns the counter its force","to fill time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Concession isn't surrender — it disarms the defence and makes your «но» land as reason, not reflex."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Hit the claim, not the man","title":"Argue <em>по существу́</em> — split the <em>те́зис</em> from the person","paras":["The cleanest fighter attacks the <b>те́зис</b>, never the person holding it. Argue <b>по существу́</b> — on the merits: «В э́том до́воде сла́бое ме́сто вот здесь…». The moment you switch to the human — «вы про́сто не разбира́етесь», «что вы вообще́ понима́ете» — you've committed <b>перехо́д на ли́чности</b> (going personal), and in Russian debate culture that means you've already lost, however right your point.","Keep the two apart out loud: «Я уважа́ю ва́шу пози́цию, но с те́зисом не согла́сен». Naming that split — respect for the person, disagreement with the claim — is itself a courtesy move, and it lets the opponent lose the point without losing face.","The English speaker rarely goes openly ad hominem, but slips sideways into it under pressure — a sarcastic aside, an «е́сли бы вы то́лько прочита́ли…». A Russian audience hears that instantly and marks it against you. Stay on the те́зис and you keep the high ground."],"audio":"The cleanest fighter attacks the tezis, never the person holding it. Argue po sushchestvu — on the merits: 'V etom dovode slaboe mesto vot zdes…'. The moment you switch to the human — 'vy prosto ne razbiraetes', 'chto vy voobshche ponimaete' — you've committed perekhod na lichnosti, going personal, and in Russian debate culture that means you've already lost, however right your point. Keep the two apart out loud: 'Ya uvazhayu vashu pozitsiyu, no s tezisom ne soglasen'. Naming that split — respect for the person, disagreement with the claim — is itself a courtesy move, and it lets the opponent lose the point without losing face. The English speaker rarely goes openly ad hominem, but slips sideways into it under pressure — a sarcastic aside, an 'esli by vy tolko prochitali…'. A Russian audience hears that instantly and marks it against you. Stay on the tezis and you keep the high ground.","table":{"rows":[["спо́рить по существу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спорить по существу\">🔊</button>","argue on the merits — attack the claim"],["перехо́д на ли́чности <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"переход на личности\">🔊</button>","going personal — you've already lost"],["Уважа́ю пози́цию, но не те́зис <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Уважаю позицию, но не тезис\">🔊</button>","split the person from the claim, aloud"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Not “you don't understand” — say where the argument is weak.","en":"Не «вы не понима́ете» — скажи́, где до́вод сла́бый.","say":"Не вы не понимаете — скажи, где довод слабый."},{"ru":"“I respect your position, but I don't agree with the thesis.”","en":"«Уважа́ю ва́шу пози́цию, но с те́зисом не согла́сен».","say":"Уважаю вашу позицию, но с тезисом не согласен."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Е́сли бы вы хоть немно́го разбира́лись, вы бы так не говори́ли.»","right":"«Здесь, по-мо́ему, до́вод не рабо́тает — и вот почему́.»","why":"The first hits the person — <b>перехо́д на ли́чности</b> — and in a Russian debate that loses you the exchange on the spot. The second stays «по существу́», on the argument. Beat the claim, and you never need to belittle the челове́к."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In Russian debate, «перехо́д на ли́чности» (going personal) means you've —","opts":["clinched the argument","effectively lost, however right your point","made a fair concession"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once you attack the person instead of the claim, the culture counts the exchange lost — even if your point was sound."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"To argue on the merits — against the claim itself, not the person — is to argue «по …». Type the noun (hint: суще…):","accept":["существу"],"placeholder":"суще…","explain":"«по существу́» — on the merits. Attack the те́зис; leave the челове́к alone."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Know the dirty tricks — and refuse them","title":"<em>Эристи́ческие уло́вки</em>: name the trick, don't throw it","paras":["Not every opponent fights clean, and a master spots the <b>эристи́ческие уло́вки</b> (eristic tricks — the sophist's dodges) at once. <b>Подме́на те́зиса</b> (the straw man): they answer a claim you never made — «Так вы про́тив прогре́сса?» when you said nothing of the sort. <b>«Сам дура́к»</b> (tu quoque): dodging your point by throwing it back — «А вы са́ми-то?». The <b>ло́жная диле́мма</b> (false dichotomy): «и́ли с на́ми, и́ли про́тив нас», as if there were only two doors.","The counter is calm and named: point at the trick without heat. «Э́то подме́на те́зиса — я говори́л о друго́м». «Вы предлага́ете ло́жный вы́бор». Naming it defuses it and keeps you «по существу́»; taking the bait drags you off your ground.","And the rule that outranks all the tricks: don't use them. Winning by подме́на те́зиса is losing at C2 — a native master hears the sleight and trusts you less. The whole art is to disagree so cleanly that even the person you defeat respects how you did it.","Tomorrow the register softens by design: <b>мода́льность и хеджи́рование</b> (modality and hedging) — «я скло́нен полага́ть», «не исключено́» — how to state a view without overclaiming, the exact opposite pole from today's firmness, and just as precise."],"audio":"Not every opponent fights clean, and a master spots the eristicheskie ulovki — eristic tricks, the sophist's dodges — at once. Podmena tezisa, the straw man: they answer a claim you never made — 'Tak vy protiv progressa?' when you said nothing of the sort. 'Sam durak', tu quoque: dodging your point by throwing it back — 'A vy sami-to?'. The lozhnaya dilemma, false dichotomy: 'ili s nami, ili protiv nas', as if there were only two doors. The counter is calm and named: point at the trick without heat. 'Eto podmena tezisa — ya govoril o drugom'. 'Vy predlagaete lozhny vybor'. Naming it defuses it and keeps you po sushchestvu; taking the bait drags you off your ground. And the rule that outranks all the tricks: don't use them. Winning by podmena tezisa is losing at C2 — a native master hears the sleight and trusts you less. The whole art is to disagree so cleanly that even the person you defeat respects how you did it. Tomorrow the register softens by design: modalnost i khedzhirovanie, modality and hedging — 'ya sklonen polagat', 'ne isklyucheno' — how to state a view without overclaiming, the exact opposite pole from today's firmness, and just as precise.","table":{"rows":[["подме́на те́зиса <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"подмена тезиса\">🔊</button>","straw man — answering a claim you didn't make"],["«сам дура́к» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сам дурак\">🔊</button>","tu quoque — throwing your point back"],["ло́жная диле́мма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ложная дилемма\">🔊</button>","false choice — «или с нами, или против»"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“That's a straw man — I was talking about something else.”","en":"«Э́то подме́на те́зиса — я говори́л о друго́м».","say":"Это подмена тезиса — я говорил о другом."},{"ru":"Winning by a trick is losing at C2 — the master hears it.","en":"Вы́играть уло́вкой — зна́чит проигра́ть на C2: ма́стер её слы́шит.","say":"Выиграть уловкой — значит проиграть на C2: мастер её слышит."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Зна́чит, вы вообще́ про́тив нау́ки?» (в отве́т на у́зкое возраже́ние)","right":"«Вы приписа́ли мне то, чего́ я не говори́л, — э́то подме́на те́зиса.»","why":"Blowing a narrow objection up into “you're against science” is <b>подме́на те́зиса</b> — a straw man. Naming it calmly, without heat, defuses the trick and drags the argument back to what was actually said."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Answering «Так вы про́тив прогре́сса?» to a claim that said no such thing is —","opts":["подме́на те́зиса — a straw man","a fair concession","arguing по существу́"],"answer":0,"explain":"It swaps your real claim for one you never made — <b>подме́на те́зиса</b>, the straw man. Name it and step back onto your ground."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it — firm on the «нет», warm on the concession.","target":"Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться, но ва́ша мысль о гла́вном — ве́рная.","sub":"steel in silk — the «нет» is firm, the concession is genuine","want":"позволю себе не согласиться, но ваша мысль о главном — верная"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At Ле́на's café, Ива́н teases Эмма that every translation “betrays” the original. She disagrees — the C2 way. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Любо́й перево́д — преда́тельство. Ты же са́ма э́то зна́ешь.","ru":"Every translation is a betrayal. You know that yourself."},{"who":"A","en":"Допу́стим. В чём-то ты прав: что-то всегда́ теря́ется.","ru":"Granted. You're right about something: something is always lost."},{"who":"B","en":"Вот ви́дишь!","ru":"See!"},{"who":"A","en":"И всё же позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться. Теря́ть — не зна́чит предава́ть.","ru":"And yet, allow me to disagree. To lose isn't to betray."},{"who":"B","en":"Кра́сиво уверну́лась. Но по существу́?","ru":"Nicely dodged. But on the merits?"},{"who":"A","en":"По существу́: перево́дчик не теря́ет — он выбира́ет, что сохрани́ть. Э́то не изме́на, а вы́бор.","ru":"On the merits: a translator doesn't lose — he chooses what to keep. That's not betrayal, it's a choice."},{"who":"B","en":"Ла́дно, тут ты сильне́е. Сдаю́сь по-настоя́щему.","ru":"Fine, you're stronger here. I genuinely give up."},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́дишь: я не сказа́ла «ты не прав». Я сказа́ла «позво́лю не согласи́ться». Ты до сих пор мой друг.","ru":"See: I didn't say “you're wrong.” I said “allow me to disagree.” You're still my friend."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for arguing well — firm, courteous, clean. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"поле́мика","ru":"polemic, a debate","ex":"Поле́мика была́ о́строй, но ве́жливой."},{"en":"усту́пка","ru":"a concession","ex":"Усту́пка де́лает до́вод сильне́е."},{"en":"возраже́ние","ru":"an objection","ex":"У меня́ есть одно́ возраже́ние."},{"en":"до́вод","ru":"an argument, a point","ex":"Э́то сла́бый до́вод."},{"en":"опроверже́ние","ru":"a refutation","ex":"Он привёл чёткое опроверже́ние."},{"en":"согласи́ться","ru":"to agree","ex":"Я гото́в согласи́ться с э́тим."},{"en":"по существу́","ru":"on the merits, to the point","ex":"Дава́йте спо́рить по существу́."},{"en":"перехо́д на ли́чности","ru":"going personal (ad hominem)","ex":"Перехо́д на ли́чности — при́знак сла́бости."},{"en":"уло́вка","ru":"a trick, a ploy","ex":"Э́то ста́рая эристи́ческая уло́вка."},{"en":"подме́на те́зиса","ru":"a straw man (substituting the claim)","ex":"Он заме́тил подме́ну те́зиса."},{"en":"твёрдо","ru":"firmly","ex":"Она́ твёрдо, но ве́жливо возрази́ла."},{"en":"взгляд","ru":"a view, a viewpoint","ex":"У меня́ ины́й взгляд на э́то."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each move of the debate to what it actually is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto what it is — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["усту́пка","concession — grant the strong point, then pivot"],["перехо́д на ли́чности","going personal — the mark of a lost argument"],["подме́на те́зиса","straw man — answering a claim not made"],["по существу́","on the merits — argue the claim, not the person"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The master formula for firm-but-courteous disagreement is —","opts":["«Э́то непра́вда, вы ошиба́етесь»","«Ну, мо́жет быть, вы и пра́вы»","«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»"],"answer":2},{"q":"In Russian debate, <b>усту́пка</b> (conceding the opponent's strong point) —","opts":["weakens your argument","strengthens it — it earns the counter its force","is considered rude"],"answer":1},{"q":"Switching from the argument to the person — «вы про́сто не разбира́етесь» — is —","opts":["перехо́д на ли́чности — you've lost the high ground","arguing по существу́","a fair concession"],"answer":0},{"q":"«и́ли с на́ми, и́ли про́тив нас» is which eristic trick?","opts":["подме́на те́зиса","ло́жная диле́мма — a false choice","усту́пка"],"answer":1},{"q":"At C2, winning an argument by a dirty trick (подме́на те́зиса) is —","opts":["a clever victory","actually a loss — the master hears the sleight","the polite thing to do"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A model disagreement in five lines: concede, pivot, stay on the merits, refuse the trick. Read it aloud and hear the steel stay wrapped in silk.","title":"Твёрдо и ве́жливо","sentences":["Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться, хотя́ ваш до́вод я по́нял.","Допу́стим, вы пра́вы в ма́лом, — но не в гла́вном.","Дава́йте спо́рить по существу́, а не о ли́чностях.","Вы приписа́ли мне то, чего́ я не говори́л, — э́то подме́на те́зиса.","И всё же спаси́бо за спор: он был че́стным."],"translation":"Allow me to disagree, though I did understand your point. Granted, you're right in a small thing — but not in the main one. Let's argue on the merits, not about personalities. You've ascribed to me something I never said — that's a straw man. And still, thank you for the argument: it was an honest one."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to disagree — gracefully.","prompt":"Write a short, courteous rebuttal — 4–5 sentences — to a claim you actually reject (a common opinion, a friend's take). Use all three moves from today: a <b>усту́пка</b> that grants the opponent's strongest point, a firm but polite «нет» («Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться…»), and one argument «по существу́». Stay off the person entirely — no перехо́д на ли́чности.","hint":"Start by naming, honestly, the best thing about the view you're rejecting — that's your concession. Then pivot on «но / одна́ко» and give one clean reason. If you feel the urge to belittle the person, that's the line not to cross 🙂","example":"Допу́стим, вы пра́вы: бума́жные кни́ги не всегда́ удо́бны, и цифрово́е чте́ние вы́играло в удо́бстве. И всё же позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться с те́зисом, что бума́га умерла́. По существу́: кни́га — не то́лько текст, но и вре́мя, проведённое с ней. Мы не спо́рим о вку́сах — мы говори́м о ра́зном о́пыте. И э́то не преда́тельство экра́на, а про́сто друго́й спо́соб чита́ть."}},"18":{"day":18,"week":"03","themeRu":"Saying it with care","themeEn":"Мода́льность и хеджи́рование","intro":"An author hands Эмма a line: «Э́то доказыва́ет, что рефо́рма прова́лится» — <i>this proves the reform will fail</i>. Correct Russian. But in a serious article it lands like a fist on the table: too sure, too loud. A native scholar wouldn't claim less — they'd claim it more carefully: «Э́то, по всей ви́димости, ста́вит успе́х рефо́рмы под вопро́с». Same thought, measured. Today is <b>хеджи́рование</b> (hedging — softening a claim) — the calibrated formulas Russian uses to soften a statement exactly as much as it needs, and no more. Not the timid «мо́жет быть» five times over, not the blunt «доказано» — the one measured phrase.","introAudio":"An author hands Emma a line: 'Eto dokazyvayet, chto reforma provalitsya' — this proves the reform will fail. Correct Russian. But in a serious article it lands like a fist on the table: too sure, too loud. A native scholar wouldn't claim less — they'd claim it more carefully: 'Eto, po vsey vidimosti, stavit uspekh reformy pod vopros'. Same thought, measured. Today is 'khedzhirovaniye', hedging — the calibrated formulas Russian uses to soften a statement exactly as much as it needs, and no more. Not the timid 'mozhet byt' five times over, not the blunt 'dokazano' — the one measured phrase.","goals":["Feel the C2 balance: not the blunt «доказано», not five «мо́жет быть» — the <b>one calibrated hedge</b>","Place the formulas on a certainty scale: <b>вряд ли · не исключено́ · по всей ви́димости · скоре́е всего́</b>","Distance your own view without going soft: <b>я скло́нен полага́ть · рискну́ предположи́ть · мне представля́ется</b>","Hear what a hedge <b>carries</b>: «я́кобы» isn't neutral — it says you don't believe it"],"learned":["Felt the balance between the blunt claim and the fog of «мо́жет быть» — the one calibrated hedge","Placed the formulas on a certainty scale (вряд ли / не исключено́ / по всей ви́димости / скоре́е всего́)","Distanced your own view without going soft (скло́нен полага́ть / рискну́ предположи́ть / представля́ется)","Heard what a hedge carries — «я́кобы» smuggles in the speaker's doubt"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you learned to win gracefully — concede the strong point, then rebut it («согла́сен, но…», «позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться»). Hold that poise; today we soften not the opponent but the claim itself.","introAudio":"Yesterday you learned to win gracefully — concede the strong point, then rebut it ('soglasen, no', 'pozvolyu sebe ne soglasitsya'). Hold that poise; today we soften not the opponent but the claim itself.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's graceful disagreement.","q":"In a serious debate, the graceful way to disagree with a respected opponent is —","opts":["«Ты про́сто не прав».","«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться».","«Что за чушь ты несёшь?»"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться» — firm disagreement inside courtesy. The other two attack the person; that loses the room."},{"type":"choice","q":"Granting your opponent's strong point before you counter it is called —","opts":["усту́пка (a concession)","антите́за (antithesis)","града́ция (gradation)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Усту́пка</b> — you concede («допу́стим», «согла́сен, но…»), then rebut on the merits. Answer the argument, not the person."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the graceful turn.","q":"Concede, then turn: “I agree, but the question is more complex.”","want":"Согла́сен, но вопро́с сложне́е","answer":"Согла́сен, но вопро́с сложне́е"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The measured claim","title":"Not blunt, not timid — the <em>calibrated</em> hedge","paras":["English at C2 tends to fall off one of two cliffs. Either it stays blunt — «Э́то доказыва́ет…», «очеви́дно, что…» — and lands like a fist on the table in a register that prizes restraint. Or it panics and scatters «мо́жет быть» over every sentence, and sounds sure of nothing. Russian scholarly and diplomatic speech does neither.","It hedges — but with <b>calibrated</b> formulas, each carrying a precise degree of certainty and distance. <b>Хеджи́рование</b> (hedging) is softening a claim by exactly the amount you mean: enough to be honest about your doubt, not so much that you vanish. The skill is choosing the formula whose weight matches yours.","So the C2 question returns: not «is this correct» (all these formulas are), but «how much certainty does this one <i>carry</i>, and is that mine?» «Вряд ли» is a quiet no; «не исключено́» leaves the door ajar; «по всей ви́димости» is nearly sure, on the visible evidence. Pick the one that measures your real confidence."],"audio":"English at C2 tends to fall off one of two cliffs. Either it stays blunt — 'Eto dokazyvayet', 'ochevidno, chto' — and lands like a fist on the table in a register that prizes restraint. Or it panics and scatters 'mozhet byt' over every sentence, and sounds sure of nothing. Russian scholarly and diplomatic speech does neither. It hedges, but with calibrated formulas, each carrying a precise degree of certainty and distance. 'Khedzhirovaniye', hedging, is softening a claim by exactly the amount you mean: enough to be honest about your doubt, not so much that you vanish. The skill is choosing the formula whose weight matches yours. So the C2 question returns: not is this correct — all these formulas are — but how much certainty does this one carry, and is that mine? 'Vryad li' is a quiet no; 'ne isklyucheno' leaves the door ajar; 'po vsey vidimosti' is nearly sure, on the visible evidence. Pick the one that measures your real confidence.","table":{"rows":[["вряд ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вряд ли\">🔊</button>","unlikely — a soft, polite “probably not”"],["не исключено́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не исключено\">🔊</button>","it can't be ruled out — possible, door left ajar"],["по всей ви́димости <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по всей видимости\">🔊</button>","in all likelihood — nearly sure, on the evidence"],["скоре́е всего́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скорее всего\">🔊</button>","most likely — high odds, everyday-neutral"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In an article you don't write “this proves”; you write “this, in all likelihood, indicates”.","en":"В статье́ не пи́шут «э́то доказыва́ет» — пи́шут «э́то, по всей ви́димости, свиде́тельствует».","say":"в статье не пишут это доказывает — пишут это, по всей видимости, свидетельствует."},{"ru":"«Unlikely» here isn't weakness — it's a precise, quiet “no”.","en":"«Вряд ли» здесь не сла́бость, а то́чное, ти́хое «нет».","say":"вряд ли здесь не слабость, а точное, тихое нет."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Мо́жет быть, э́то, мо́жет, ве́рно, но, мо́жет, и нет».","right":"«Не исключено́, что э́то ве́рно».","why":"Three «мо́жет быть» in a row doesn't read as careful — it reads as sure of nothing. One calibrated formula, <b>не исключено́</b>, hedges once, precisely, and stops. Over-hedging is as foreign as being blunt."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"In a scholarly article, a flat «Э́то доказыва́ет, что…» sounds —","opts":["appropriately confident","too categorical for the register","too timid"],"answer":1,"explain":"In a register that prizes restraint, a bare «доказыва́ет» lands like a fist on the table. A native softens it — «по всей ви́димости, свиде́тельствует». Neither blunt nor timid."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which hedge leaves the possibility open without committing?","opts":["вряд ли","не исключено́","несомне́нно"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Не исключено́» — <i>it can't be ruled out</i> — deliberately leaves the door ajar. «Вряд ли» leans to no; «несомне́нно» (undoubtedly) is the opposite of a hedge."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The certainty scale","title":"From <em>вряд ли</em> to <em>несомне́нно</em> — pick your rung","paras":["Line the formulas up and they form a ladder of certainty, from a soft no to near-proof. A native doesn't grab any word that means «probably» — they step onto the exact rung.","Low, leaning to no: <b>вряд ли</b> (unlikely — a polite «probably not»). Open: <b>не исключено́</b> (can't be ruled out), <b>возмо́жно</b> (possibly). Leaning yes: <b>вполне́ вероя́тно</b> (quite probably), <b>по-ви́димому</b> (apparently), <b>по всей ви́димости</b> (in all likelihood), <b>скоре́е всего́</b> (most likely). At the top, no hedge at all: <b>несомне́нно, безусло́вно</b> (undoubtedly).","The evidential ones — <b>по-ви́димому</b>, <b>по всей ви́димости</b> — carry an extra shade beyond «probably»: they mean «by the visible signs», you're reading evidence, not guessing. That «by the look of it» is exactly what English «apparently» carries too. Use them where you're inferring from what you can see."],"audio":"Line the formulas up and they form a ladder of certainty, from a soft no to near-proof. A native doesn't grab any word that means probably — they step onto the exact rung. Low, leaning to no: 'vryad li', unlikely, a polite probably not. Open: 'ne isklyucheno', can't be ruled out, and 'vozmozhno', possibly. Leaning yes: 'vpolne veroyatno', quite probably; 'po-vidimomu', apparently; 'po vsey vidimosti', in all likelihood; 'skoreye vsego', most likely. At the top, no hedge at all: 'nesomnenno, bezuslovno', undoubtedly. The evidential ones — 'po-vidimomu', 'po vsey vidimosti' — carry an extra shade beyond probably: they mean by the visible signs, you're reading evidence, not guessing. That by the look of it is exactly what English apparently carries too. Use them where you're inferring from what you can see.","table":{"rows":[["по-ви́димому <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-видимому\">🔊</button>","apparently — inferring from visible signs"],["по всей ви́димости <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по всей видимости\">🔊</button>","in all likelihood — the same, a touch more formal"],["вполне́ вероя́тно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вполне вероятно\">🔊</button>","quite probably — leaning yes, no evidence claim"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"By the look of it he's already left — the light's off.","en":"По-ви́димому, он уже́ ушёл: свет не гори́т.","say":"по-видимому, он уже ушёл: свет не горит."},{"ru":"He's unlikely to agree — but it can't be ruled out.","en":"Он вряд ли согласи́тся, но не исключено́.","say":"он вряд ли согласится, но не исключено."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«По всей ви́димости, я тебя́ люблю́».","right":"«Я тебя́ люблю́».","why":"You don't hedge what you're certain of. «По всей ви́димости» (in all likelihood) on your own feeling is absurd — it reads as cold, or as irony. Hedge inferences, not convictions; over-hedging a certainty flips the meaning."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Of these three, which marks the LEAST certainty that the thing is true?","opts":["скоре́е всего́","вряд ли","по всей ви́димости"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Вряд ли» (unlikely) sits lowest — a soft no. «По всей ви́димости» and «скоре́е всего́» both lean yes."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the evidential hedge meaning “apparently, by the visible signs” (hint: по-в…):","accept":["по-видимому","повидимому"],"placeholder":"по-в…","explain":"<b>По-ви́димому</b> — you're inferring from what you can see, not guessing blindly. That «by the look of it» is the shade it carries."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Distancing your own view","title":"«I think» is too flat — <em>скло́нен полага́ть</em>, <em>рискну́ предположи́ть</em>","paras":["The blunt cliff has a first-person version. «Я ду́маю, что…» / «Я счита́ю, что…» are fine in speech, but in an article or a careful discussion they can sound flat, even a little self-important — as if your opinion settled the matter.","Russian distances the speaker from the claim with a set of bookish formulas. Impersonal: <b>мне представля́ется, что…</b> (it seems to me), <b>сдаётся мне</b> (I've a feeling — warm, colloquial). Modest-firm: <b>я скло́нен полага́ть</b> (I'm inclined to think — a considered view, held lightly), <b>рискну́ предположи́ть</b> (I'll venture to suppose — flags it as a bold guess, so you're not overclaiming).","Each carries its own posture. <b>Рискну́ предположи́ть</b> carries modesty — it says «this may be wrong». <b>Скло́нен полага́ть</b> carries a weighed, deliberate lean. And <b>как бу́дто бы</b> (as if, seemingly) hedges a perception: «он как бу́дто бы не рад» — he seems it, but I won't swear to it. В нау́ке: choose the formula whose posture is yours."],"audio":"The blunt cliff has a first-person version. 'Ya dumayu, chto', 'ya schitayu, chto' are fine in speech, but in an article or a careful discussion they can sound flat, even a little self-important — as if your opinion settled the matter. Russian distances the speaker from the claim with a set of bookish formulas. Impersonal: 'mne predstavlyayetsya, chto', it seems to me; 'sdayotsya mne', I've a feeling, warm and colloquial. Modest and firm: 'ya sklonen polagat', I'm inclined to think, a considered view held lightly; 'risknu predpolozhit', I'll venture to suppose, which flags it as a bold guess so you're not overclaiming. Each carries its own posture. 'Risknu predpolozhit' carries modesty — it says this may be wrong. 'Sklonen polagat' carries a weighed, deliberate lean. And 'kak budto by', as if, seemingly, hedges a perception: 'on kak budto by ne rad', he seems it, but I won't swear to it. In scholarship: choose the formula whose posture is yours.","examples":[{"ru":"“It seems to me the author is being ironic here” — softer than “I think”.","en":"Мне представля́ется, что а́втор здесь ирони́чен.","say":"мне представляется, что автор здесь ироничен."},{"ru":"I'll venture to suppose the manuscript is a later forgery.","en":"Рискну́ предположи́ть, что ру́копись — по́здняя подде́лка.","say":"рискну предположить, что рукопись — поздняя подделка."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я счита́ю, что а́втор, безусло́вно, ошиба́ется». (в реце́нзии)","right":"«Мне представля́ется, что а́втор здесь не вполне́ прав».","why":"In a review, «я счита́ю» + «безусло́вно» stacks two blunt moves — flat opinion, then a hammer. The measured version distances («мне представля́ется») and softens the verdict («не вполне́ прав»). Same disagreement, scholarly poise."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«…, что ру́копись — подде́лка» — which opening marks it as a bold guess you might be wrong about?","opts":["«Я то́чно зна́ю, …»","«Рискну́ предположи́ть, …»","«Несомне́нно, …»"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Рискну́ предположи́ть» (I'll venture to suppose) carries modesty — it flags the claim as bold and possibly wrong. The other two carry certainty."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the distanced opinion.","q":"Distance your opinion in a review: “It seems to me the ending is weak.”","want":"Мне представля́ется, что фина́л сла́бый","answer":"Мне представля́ется, что фина́л сла́бый"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Whose judgment is it? — and the week ahead","title":"<em>Я́кобы</em> carries doubt — attributing a claim you don't vouch for","paras":["One last move: handing the claim to someone else, so you're reporting it, not backing it. Neutral attribution: <b>по слова́м (а́второв), как счита́ется, как утвержда́ется</b> (according to…, it is held that…). These just name the source.","But <b>я́кобы</b> (supposedly, allegedly) does more — it carries your <i>doubt</i>. «Он я́кобы боле́л» means «he was supposedly ill» with an audible eyebrow: I'm told so, and I don't quite buy it. Drop it into a neutral report and you've accidentally called someone a liar. The reported particles <b>мол, де́скать</b> carry the same skeptical wink. That «extra» — doubt smuggled into an attribution — is pure C2: the word says more than it means.","So the whole day is one dial: soften your claim by exactly the amount you mean — no fist, no fog. Tomorrow the sentence itself gets longer — the <b>пери́од</b>, a sixty-word thought held in perfect balance. Then Sunday we gather all of week three together."],"audio":"One last move: handing the claim to someone else, so you're reporting it, not backing it. Neutral attribution: 'po slovam avtorov', 'kak schitayetsya', 'kak utverzhdayetsya' — according to, it is held that. These just name the source. But 'yakoby', supposedly, allegedly, does more — it carries your doubt. 'On yakoby bolel' means he was supposedly ill, with an audible eyebrow: I'm told so, and I don't quite buy it. Drop it into a neutral report and you've accidentally called someone a liar. The reported particles 'mol, deskat' carry the same skeptical wink. That extra — doubt smuggled into an attribution — is pure C2: the word says more than it means. So the whole day is one dial: soften your claim by exactly the amount you mean, no fist, no fog. Tomorrow the sentence itself gets longer — the 'period', a sixty-word thought held in perfect balance. Then Sunday we gather all of week three together.","examples":[{"ru":"“He was supposedly ill” — the «я́кобы» says: I don't believe it.","en":"Он я́кобы боле́л — «я́кобы» здесь зна́чит «не ве́рю».","say":"он якобы болел — якобы здесь значит не верю."},{"ru":"Neutral attribution: “according to the authors, the method is new”.","en":"Нейтра́льно: по слова́м а́второв, ме́тод нов.","say":"нейтрально: по словам авторов, метод нов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«По́езд я́кобы прибыва́ет в шесть». (про́сто расписа́ние)","right":"«По́езд прибыва́ет в шесть».","why":"«Я́кобы» smuggles in doubt — «supposedly, but I don't trust it». On a plain timetable it's absurd, as if the schedule were lying. For neutral fact use «по расписа́нию» or nothing at all."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Он я́кобы всё сде́лал сам.» — what does «я́кобы» add?","opts":["polite respect","the speaker's doubt (“supposedly — but I doubt it”)","extra certainty"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Я́кобы» carries skepticism — you're reporting the claim and quietly refusing to vouch for it. That doubt is baked into the word."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the one-word particle meaning “supposedly / allegedly” that carries the speaker's disbelief (hint: я́к…):","accept":["якобы"],"placeholder":"як…","explain":"<b>Я́кобы</b> — it attributes a claim and, in the same breath, doubts it. The word carries more than «supposedly»."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is editing a professor's article at Ле́на's café. Ива́н drops by and watches her soften sentence after sentence. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты уже́ час смягча́ешь ка́ждую фра́зу профе́ссора. Он же зна́ет, что пи́шет.","ru":"You've spent an hour softening every sentence of the professor's. He knows what he's writing."},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́ет. Но он пи́шет «э́то доказыва́ет» там, где че́стно — «по всей ви́димости».","ru":"He does. But he writes “this proves” where the honest word is “in all likelihood”."},{"who":"B","en":"А ра́зница есть?","ru":"Is there a difference?"},{"who":"A","en":"Огро́мная. «Доказыва́ет» — кула́к по столу́. «По всей ви́димости» — тот же смысл, но че́стный.","ru":"Huge. “Proves” is a fist on the table. “In all likelihood” — the same sense, but honest."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, нау́ка вся така́я осторо́жная?","ru":"So scholarship is all this cautious?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не осторо́жная — то́чная. Сказа́ть ро́вно сто́лько, ско́лько зна́ешь. Ни бо́льше, ни ме́ньше.","ru":"Not cautious — precise. To say exactly as much as you know. No more, no less."},{"who":"B","en":"А е́сли напи́сать «я́кобы доказыва́ет»?","ru":"And if you wrote “supposedly proves”?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ ты назовёшь профе́ссора лжецо́м. Одно́ сло́во — и весь тон друго́й.","ru":"Then you'd be calling the professor a liar. One word, and the whole tone changes."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve tools for saying it with care — the calibrated formulas of hedging. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"хеджи́рование","ru":"hedging (softening a claim)","ex":"Хеджи́рование — иску́сство сказа́ть ро́вно сто́лько, ско́лько зна́ешь."},{"en":"вряд ли","ru":"hardly, unlikely (a soft “no”)","ex":"Он вряд ли успе́ет к сро́ку."},{"en":"не исключено́","ru":"it can't be ruled out","ex":"Не исключено́, что пойдёт дождь."},{"en":"по всей ви́димости","ru":"in all likelihood, apparently","ex":"По всей ви́димости, реше́ние уже́ при́нято."},{"en":"по-ви́димому","ru":"apparently, evidently","ex":"По-ви́димому, они́ уже́ ушли́."},{"en":"скоре́е всего́","ru":"most likely","ex":"Скоре́е всего́, он согласи́тся."},{"en":"скло́нен","ru":"inclined (to think)","ex":"Я скло́нен полага́ть, что а́втор прав."},{"en":"предположи́ть","ru":"to suppose, conjecture","ex":"Рискну́ предположи́ть, что текст по́здний."},{"en":"представля́ться","ru":"to seem (impersonal, bookish)","ex":"Мне представля́ется, что вы́вод спо́рен."},{"en":"как бу́дто бы","ru":"as if, seemingly","ex":"Он как бу́дто бы не рад встре́че."},{"en":"я́кобы","ru":"supposedly, allegedly (skeptical)","ex":"Он я́кобы за́нят, но я не ве́рю."},{"en":"смягчи́ть","ru":"to soften (a claim's edge)","ex":"На́до смягчи́ть категори́чность вы́вода."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each hedge to what it carries — the exact degree of certainty and distance.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each formula on the left onto what it carries — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["вряд ли","a soft, polite “no” — low odds"],["не исключено́","the door left ajar — possible, non-committal"],["по всей ви́димости","nearly sure — inferred from the evidence"],["я́кобы","supposedly — and the speaker doesn't buy it"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Russian scholarly style hedges a claim —","opts":["by scattering «мо́жет быть» everywhere","with one calibrated formula, sized to your certainty","never — it stays blunt"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which formula leans politely toward «no»?","opts":["несомне́нно","вряд ли","скоре́е всего́"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Рискну́ предположи́ть, что…» marks the claim as —","opts":["certain and proven","a bold guess that might be wrong","an official order"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он я́кобы всё сде́лал сам» — the «я́кобы» carries —","opts":["the speaker's doubt","polite respect","added certainty"],"answer":0},{"q":"«По всей ви́димости» differs from plain «возмо́жно» because it —","opts":["is ruder","signals you're inferring from visible evidence","means «never»"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Two versions of the same claim from Эмма's article — the author's blunt draft, and her measured edit. Read both aloud and hear the temperature drop.","title":"Ро́вно сто́лько, ско́лько зна́ешь","sentences":["А́втор написа́л: «Э́то несомне́нно дока́зывает по́лный прова́л рефо́рмы».","Э́мма прочла́ фра́зу три́жды и покача́ла голово́й.","Она́ испра́вила: «Э́то, по всей ви́димости, ста́вит успе́х рефо́рмы под вопро́с».","Смысл оста́лся тот же, но исче́з уда́р кулако́м по столу́.","«Вряд ли» вме́сто «никогда́», «не исключено́» вме́сто «безусло́вно» — и текст стал че́стнее.","Хеджи́рование — не тру́сость, а то́чность: сказа́ть ро́вно сто́лько, ско́лько зна́ешь."],"translation":"The author had written: “This undoubtedly proves the complete failure of the reform.” Emma read the sentence three times and shook her head. She corrected it: “This, in all likelihood, calls the reform's success into question.” The sense stayed the same, but the fist on the table was gone. “Unlikely” instead of “never”, “it can't be ruled out” instead of “certainly” — and the text became more honest. Hedging isn't cowardice but precision: to say exactly as much as you know."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to hedge with a jeweller's hand.","prompt":"Take one blunt, over-sure claim — «Э́тот фильм — шеде́вр», «Прое́кт прова́лится», anything categorical — and rewrite it THREE ways, each with a differently calibrated hedge: one leaning to «no» (вряд ли), one leaving the door open (не исключено́ / рискну́ предположи́ть), one nearly sure on the evidence (по всей ви́димости / скло́нен полага́ть). Then name, in a few words, how much certainty each version carries. 4–5 sentences.","hint":"Ask the C2 question of each formula: how much doubt does it carry, and is that yours? Remember the two cliffs — the blunt «доказано» and the fog of five «мо́жет быть». Land between them 🙂","example":"Категори́чно: «Прое́кт прова́лится». — Мя́гкое «нет»: «Вряд ли прое́кт бу́дет лёгким». — Дверь приоткры́та: «Не исключено́, что прое́кт столкнётся с тру́дностями». — Почти́ уве́ренно: «По всей ви́димости, прое́кту не хвата́ет сро́ков». Пе́рвое бьёт кулако́м, после́днее — че́стная оце́нка по фа́ктам: тот же смысл, ра́зная ме́ра уве́ренности."}},"19":{"day":19,"week":"03","themeRu":"The long, controlled sentence","themeEn":"Пери́од: дли́нное предложе́ние под контро́лем","intro":"Yesterday you learned to dose a claim; today you learn to sustain one. Open any page of Толсто́й and you meet a sentence that runs sixty words and never loses you — clause folding into clause, the tension mounting, until the main thought lands at the end like a key turning in a lock. That is a <b>пери́од</b> (a period — a long sentence held in balance). English at C2 either loses the thread on a long run or chops everything into short, breathless bursts. A native master holds the arch. Today you build one — and read Эмма's beloved Толсто́й without drowning.","introAudio":"Yesterday you learned to dose a claim; today you learn to sustain one. Open any page of Tolstoy and you meet a sentence that runs sixty words and never loses you — clause folding into clause, the tension mounting, until the main thought lands at the end like a key turning in a lock. That is a 'period', a long sentence held in balance. English at C2 either loses the thread on a long run or chops everything into short, breathless bursts. A native master holds the arch. Today you build one — and read Emma's beloved Tolstoy without drowning.","goals":["Tell a <b>пери́од</b> from a run-on: a long sentence built as an arch — tension up through the clauses, resolution in the main","Use <b>parallelism</b> as the skeleton: a repeated shape («когда́… когда́… — тогда́…») that keeps the reader oriented","Steer emphasis by word order: <b>те́ма</b> (the known) opens, <b>ре́ма</b> (the new) lands at the end, in the stress position","Build one 40-word period yourself and keep it clear from first word to last"],"learned":["Told a пери́од from a run-on — an arch that mounts through the clauses and resolves in the main","Used parallelism as the skeleton — a repeated shape that keeps the reader oriented","Steered emphasis by word order — те́ма opens, ре́ма lands last, in the strongest position","Built a controlled 40-word period and held it clear from first word to last"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday: calibrated hedging — softening a claim by exactly the amount you mean. One minute to keep it, then we make the sentence itself grow.","introAudio":"Yesterday: calibrated hedging — softening a claim by exactly the amount you mean. One minute to keep it, then we make the sentence itself grow.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's calibrated hedging.","q":"The measured way to open a claim you hold but might be wrong about is —","opts":["«Я то́чно зна́ю, что…»","«Рискну́ предположи́ть, что…»","«Ежу́ поня́тно, что…»"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Рискну́ предположи́ть» flags it as a bold guess held lightly. «Я то́чно зна́ю» overclaims; «ежу́ поня́тно» is slangy and blunt."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он я́кобы согласи́лся» carries —","opts":["the speaker's doubt","certainty","politeness"],"answer":0,"explain":"«Я́кобы» attributes the claim and quietly refuses to vouch for it — supposedly, but I don't believe it."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the evidential hedge.","q":"Hedge on the evidence: “In all likelihood the decision has already been made.”","want":"По всей ви́димости, реше́ние уже́ при́нято","answer":"По всей ви́димости, реше́ние уже́ при́нято"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The arch, not the run-on","title":"A <em>пери́од</em> is a long sentence held in <em>balance</em>","paras":["A run-on sentence sprawls — clauses pinned together with «и… и… и…», going nowhere in particular. A <b>пери́од</b> is the opposite: a long sentence with <i>architecture</i>. It has two halves. The first mounts — subordinate clause stacked on subordinate clause, tension rising (<b>нагнета́ние</b> — a building-up). The second releases: the main clause lands and resolves everything (<b>разреше́ние</b> — resolution).","Say it aloud and your voice draws the arch: it rises through the subordinate clauses (повыше́ние), pauses at the top, then falls into the main clause (пониже́ние). «Когда́ … , когда́ … , когда́ … , — тогда́ …». The reader waits, held, for the main thought — and it arrives at the end with all the weight.","This is the load-bearing sentence of Russian prose — Толсто́й, scholarship, oratory. English at C2 tends to lose the thread halfway and chop into fragments, or let it sprawl into a run-on. The mastery is holding forty, sixty words in one clear span."],"audio":"A run-on sentence sprawls — clauses pinned together with 'i, i, i', going nowhere in particular. A 'period' is the opposite: a long sentence with architecture. It has two halves. The first mounts — subordinate clause stacked on subordinate clause, tension rising: 'nagnetaniye', a building-up. The second releases: the main clause lands and resolves everything: 'razresheniye', resolution. Say it aloud and your voice draws the arch: it rises through the subordinate clauses, 'povysheniye', pauses at the top, then falls into the main clause, 'ponizheniye'. 'Kogda, kogda, kogda, — togda'. The reader waits, held, for the main thought — and it arrives at the end with all the weight. This is the load-bearing sentence of Russian prose — Tolstoy, scholarship, oratory. English at C2 tends to lose the thread halfway and chop into fragments, or let it sprawl into a run-on. The mastery is holding forty, sixty words in one clear span.","table":{"rows":[["нагнета́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нагнетание\">🔊</button>","the build-up — subordinate clauses mount the tension"],["разреше́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"разрешение\">🔊</button>","the resolution — the main clause lands and closes it"],["повыше́ние → пониже́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"повышение, понижение\">🔊</button>","the voice rises through the clauses, then falls into the main"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In prose: “when… when… when… — then…”: the clauses mount, the main clause resolves.","en":"В про́зе: когда́ смолка́ет го́род, когда́ га́снут о́кна, когда́ пусте́ет у́лица, — тогда́ он выхо́дит ду́мать.","say":"в прозе: когда смолкает город, когда гаснут окна, когда пустеет улица, — тогда он выходит думать."},{"ru":"A period keeps you waiting — and pays off in the last clause.","en":"Пери́од заставля́ет ждать — и вознагражда́ет в после́дней ча́сти.","say":"период заставляет ждать — и вознаграждает в последней части."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я пришёл домо́й, и я по́ел, и я лёг, и я усну́л».","right":"«Верну́вшись домо́й, поу́жинав и пога́сив свет, я нако́нец усну́л».","why":"The first is a run-on — «и… и… и…» with no arch. The rewrite makes a small пери́од: three parallel clauses mount the action, and «я нако́нец усну́л» resolves it. Same events, one controlled span."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"A пери́од differs from a run-on because it —","opts":["is simply longer","builds tension in parallel clauses and resolves in the main","uses only «и… и… и…»"],"answer":1,"explain":"A run-on just piles up; a пери́од is an arch — the clauses mount, the main clause resolves. Length alone isn't a period."},{"type":"choice","q":"In «Когда́ …, когда́ …, — тогда́ он ушёл», the resolution (the main thought) is —","opts":["the «когда́» clauses","«тогда́ он ушёл»","there is none"],"answer":1,"explain":"The «когда́» clauses are the build-up (нагнета́ние); «тогда́ он ушёл» is the resolution (разреше́ние) — it lands last and closes the arch."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Parallelism holds it up","title":"Repeat the <em>shape</em> and the reader never gets lost","paras":["How does a native keep you oriented through forty words? <b>Паралле́лизм</b> (parallelism) — repeating the same grammatical shape for each part. Once your ear catches the pattern — «кто…, тот…», «что́бы…, что́бы…, что́бы…», «чем…, тем…» — every new clause slots into a frame you already hold. The repetition is a handrail.","<b>Ана́фора</b> (anaphora — the same word opening each part) is parallelism's sharpest form: «Чем бо́льше он молча́л, чем да́льше отходи́л, чем ре́же звони́л…». The echo binds the clauses and drives the rhythm toward the close. This is why a long Russian sentence reads as one thought, not a heap.","Break the parallel and the period collapses into a list. Keep it, and you can stack clause on clause — the shape itself carries the reader up to the resolution. В нау́ке: parallel «с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́…» does the same steadying work in an argument."],"audio":"How does a native keep you oriented through forty words? 'Parallelizm', parallelism — repeating the same grammatical shape for each part. Once your ear catches the pattern — 'kto, tot', 'chtoby, chtoby, chtoby', 'chem, tem' — every new clause slots into a frame you already hold. The repetition is a handrail. 'Anafora', anaphora, the same word opening each part, is parallelism's sharpest form: 'Chem bolshe on molchal, chem dalshe otkhodil, chem rezhe zvonil'. The echo binds the clauses and drives the rhythm toward the close. This is why a long Russian sentence reads as one thought, not a heap. Break the parallel and the period collapses into a list. Keep it, and you can stack clause on clause — the shape itself carries the reader up to the resolution. In scholarship: parallel 's odnoy storony, s drugoy storony' does the same steadying work in an argument.","examples":[{"ru":"Anaphora: each clause opens the same way and drives to the end.","en":"Чем бо́льше он молча́л, чем да́льше отходи́л, чем ре́же звони́л, — тем ясне́е станови́лось всё.","say":"чем больше он молчал, чем дальше отходил, чем реже звонил, — тем яснее становилось всё."},{"ru":"“Who doesn't risk — doesn't win”: the «кто…, тот…» frame.","en":"Кто не риску́ет, тот не выи́грывает.","say":"кто не рискует, тот не выигрывает."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Что́бы вы́жить, а та́кже для того́, что́бы победи́ть, и ещё жела́я жить…»","right":"«Что́бы вы́жить, что́бы победи́ть, что́бы жить…»","why":"The first breaks the parallel — three different shapes for three parallel ideas — and the reader stumbles. Repeat one shape («что́бы… что́бы… что́бы…») and the clauses lock into a rhythm. Parallelism is the skeleton; don't bend it mid-sentence."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Anaphora (ана́фора) in a period means —","opts":["each clause is a different shape","each part opens with the same word or frame","the sentence has no main clause"],"answer":1,"explain":"Anaphora repeats the opening — «чем… чем… чем…» — binding the clauses into one driving rhythm."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the term. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the Russian term for repeating the same grammatical shape across parts of a sentence (hint: паралле…):","accept":["параллелизм"],"placeholder":"паралле…","explain":"<b>Паралле́лизм</b> — parallelism. The repeated shape is the handrail that carries a reader through a long period."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Тема and рема","title":"Word order is free so the <em>new</em> can land last","paras":["Why can Russian move words around so freely? Because word order does a job English does with «the/a» and stress: it marks what's <b>given</b> and what's <b>new</b>. The opening is the <b>те́ма</b> (the theme — what we're already talking about); the end is the <b>ре́ма</b> (the rheme — the new information, the payload). Russian saves the new for last, in the sentence's strongest position.","«В ко́мнату вошёл <b>Ива́н</b>» answers «who came in?» — Ива́н is new, so he lands last. «Ива́н вошёл <b>в ко́мнату</b>» answers «where did Иван go?» — now the room is new. Same words, opposite focus, steered purely by order. This is <b>актуа́льное члене́ние</b> (theme-rheme division — literally «actual division»).","In a period this is everything: the mounting clauses are те́ма, laying the ground; the resolution carries the ре́ма, the point, arriving last with full weight. Put the payload early and the long sentence deflates. Save it for the end and the whole arch pays off."],"audio":"Why can Russian move words around so freely? Because word order does a job English does with 'the' and 'a' and with stress: it marks what's given and what's new. The opening is the 'tema', the theme — what we're already talking about; the end is the 'rema', the rheme — the new information, the payload. Russian saves the new for last, in the sentence's strongest position. 'V komnatu voshyol Ivan' answers who came in? — Ivan is new, so he lands last. 'Ivan voshyol v komnatu' answers where did Ivan go? — now the room is new. Same words, opposite focus, steered purely by order. This is 'aktualnoye chleneniye', theme-rheme division, literally actual division. In a period this is everything: the mounting clauses are theme, laying the ground; the resolution carries the rheme, the point, arriving last with full weight. Put the payload early and the long sentence deflates. Save it for the end and the whole arch pays off.","table":{"rows":[["те́ма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тема\">🔊</button>","the theme — the known, given information (opens)"],["ре́ма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рема\">🔊</button>","the rheme — the new information, the payload (lands last)"],["актуа́льное члене́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"актуальное членение\">🔊</button>","theme-rheme division — steering focus by word order"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"“Who came in?” — the new one lands last: …came in Иван.","en":"На вопро́с «кто вошёл?» отвеча́ют: в ко́мнату вошёл Ива́н.","say":"на вопрос кто вошёл отвечают: в комнату вошёл Иван."},{"ru":"“Where did Ivan go?” — now the new part is the room, and it lands last.","en":"На вопро́с «куда́ он пошёл?»: Ива́н вошёл в ко́мнату.","say":"на вопрос куда он пошёл: Иван вошёл в комнату."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ива́н в ко́мнату вошёл». (в отве́т на «кто вошёл?»)","right":"«В ко́мнату вошёл Ива́н».","why":"If «who came in?» is the question, Ива́н is the new information (рема) and must land last. Opening with «Ива́н» makes him the given theme — right words, wrong focus. Word order is how Russian points the spotlight."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In «В ко́мнату вошёл Ива́н», the new information (рема) is —","opts":["в ко́мнату","Ива́н","вошёл"],"answer":1,"explain":"The new part lands last: «Ива́н». It's the рема, in the sentence's strongest position."},{"type":"choice","q":"In a пери́од, where does the payload (рема, the resolution) belong?","opts":["at the very start","at the end, in the strongest position","it doesn't matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"Save the new information for the end — that's where the arch pays off. Put it early and the long sentence deflates."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Building one — and the week ahead","title":"Stack, parallel, resolve — then a word that won't translate","paras":["To build a пери́од without losing control, hold three things. <b>One</b> main thought, and only one — that's your resolution, saved for the end. <b>Parallel</b> subordinate clauses leading up to it — same shape, stacked («когда́… когда́…»; «что́бы… что́бы…»). <b>The payload last</b> — тема first, рема at the close. Everything before the dash prepares; everything after it pays off.","Read it aloud as you build: if your voice can draw the arch — rising through the clauses, falling into the main — the period holds. If you run out of breath or forget where you started, it's a run-on; prune it back to one clean span.","Tomorrow closes the week's hardest door: <b>безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика</b> — words like <b>тоска́</b> and <b>по́шлость</b> that no single English word can hold, which you learn to unpack rather than translate. Then Sunday we gather all of week three — rhetoric, oratory, argument, hedging, the period, the untranslatable — into one review."],"audio":"To build a 'period' without losing control, hold three things. One main thought, and only one — that's your resolution, saved for the end. Parallel subordinate clauses leading up to it — same shape, stacked: 'kogda, kogda'; 'chtoby, chtoby'. The payload last — theme first, rheme at the close. Everything before the dash prepares; everything after it pays off. Read it aloud as you build: if your voice can draw the arch — rising through the clauses, falling into the main — the period holds. If you run out of breath or forget where you started, it's a run-on; prune it back to one clean span. Tomorrow closes the week's hardest door: 'bezekvivalentnaya leksika', non-equivalent lexis — words like 'toska' and 'poshlost' that no single English word can hold, which you learn to unpack rather than translate. Then Sunday we gather all of week three — rhetoric, oratory, argument, hedging, the period, the untranslatable — into one review.","examples":[{"ru":"Build it: three parallel clauses mount, one main clause resolves.","en":"Что́бы поня́ть текст, что́бы услы́шать его́ ритм, что́бы не потеря́ть ни отте́нка, — она́ чита́ла его́ вслух.","say":"чтобы понять текст, чтобы услышать его ритм, чтобы не потерять ни оттенка, — она читала его вслух."},{"ru":"One main thought, saved for the end — that's the whole discipline.","en":"Одна́ гла́вная мысль, припасённая на коне́ц, — вот вся дисципли́на.","say":"одна главная мысль, припасённая на конец, — вот вся дисциплина."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Она́ чита́ла текст вслух, что́бы поня́ть его́, и ещё ритм, и отте́нки то́же».","right":"«Что́бы поня́ть текст, что́бы услы́шать ритм, что́бы не потеря́ть отте́нки, — она́ чита́ла его́ вслух».","why":"The first puts the resolution («она́ чита́ла») first, then trails off into afterthoughts — the arch deflates. Move the main clause to the end and make the «что́бы» clauses parallel: now the sentence mounts and resolves."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The safest way to keep a long пери́од under control is —","opts":["one main thought, parallel clauses, the payload at the end","as many «и» as possible","never use subordinate clauses"],"answer":0,"explain":"One resolution saved for the end, parallel clauses mounting to it — that's the arch. It's not about length, it's about control."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it aloud, drawing the arch with your voice.","target":"Когда́ смолка́ет го́род, когда́ га́снут после́дние о́кна, когда́ у́лица наконе́ц пусте́ет, — то́лько тогда́ она́ сади́тся писа́ть.","sub":"let your voice rise through the three «когда́» clauses, then fall into «она́ сади́тся писа́ть»","want":"когда смолкает город, когда гаснут последние окна, когда улица наконец пустеет, — только тогда она садится писать"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is translating a single long sentence from Толсто́й. Ива́н reads over her shoulder and reaches for the scissors. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́то одно́ предложе́ние? Здесь со́рок слов! Я бы разби́л на три.","ru":"This is one sentence? Forty words! I'd break it into three."},{"who":"A","en":"Разобью́ — и убью́ его́. У Толсто́го э́то пери́од: напряже́ние растёт, растёт — и разряжа́ется в конце́.","ru":"If I break it, I kill it. In Tolstoy it's a period: the tension mounts and mounts — and releases at the end."},{"who":"B","en":"А чита́тель не потеря́ется?","ru":"Won't the reader get lost?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не потеря́ется, е́сли ча́сти паралле́льны. Слы́шишь: «когда́… когда́… когда́…»? Э́то по́ручни.","ru":"No — if the parts are parallel. Hear it: “when… when… when…”? Those are the handrails."},{"who":"B","en":"И вся мысль — в са́мом конце́?","ru":"And the whole thought is right at the end?"},{"who":"A","en":"В са́мом конце́. Всё до тире́ — разбе́г. По́сле тире́ — прыжо́к.","ru":"Right at the end. Everything before the dash is the run-up. After the dash — the jump."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, перево́дчик не име́ет пра́ва руби́ть.","ru":"So the translator has no right to chop."},{"who":"A","en":"Не име́ет. Дли́нное — не зна́чит запу́танное. Дли́нное — зна́чит уде́ржанное.","ru":"No right. Long doesn't mean tangled. Long means held."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the architecture of the long sentence. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"пери́од","ru":"a period (a long, balanced sentence)","ex":"Толсто́вский пери́од де́ржит со́рок слов."},{"en":"прида́точное","ru":"a subordinate clause","ex":"В пери́оде мно́го прида́точных."},{"en":"гла́вное","ru":"the main clause","ex":"Гла́вное предложе́ние разреша́ет пери́од."},{"en":"нагнета́ние","ru":"the build-up (of tension)","ex":"Нагнета́ние гото́вит развя́зку."},{"en":"разреше́ние","ru":"the resolution (of a sentence)","ex":"Разреше́ние прихо́дит в конце́."},{"en":"паралле́лизм","ru":"parallelism","ex":"Паралле́лизм де́ржит дли́нную фра́зу."},{"en":"ана́фора","ru":"anaphora (same opening repeated)","ex":"Ана́фора повторя́ет пе́рвое сло́во."},{"en":"те́ма","ru":"the theme (the given information)","ex":"Те́ма — то, что уже́ изве́стно."},{"en":"ре́ма","ru":"the rheme (the new information)","ex":"Ре́ма встаёт в коне́ц фра́зы."},{"en":"актуа́льное члене́ние","ru":"theme-rheme division","ex":"Поря́док слов задаёт актуа́льное члене́ние."},{"en":"равнове́сие","ru":"balance, equilibrium","ex":"Пери́од — фра́за в равнове́сии."},{"en":"уде́ржанный","ru":"held, kept under control","ex":"Дли́нное, но уде́ржанное предложе́ние."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each part of the period to the job it does in the arch.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its job — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["нагнета́ние","the build-up — subordinate clauses mount the tension"],["разреше́ние","the resolution — the main clause lands and closes it"],["те́ма","the known part — opens the sentence"],["ре́ма","the new part, the payload — lands at the very end"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A пери́од is —","opts":["a run-on held together by «и… и…»","a long sentence built as an arch: clauses mount, the main clause resolves","any sentence over ten words"],"answer":1},{"q":"Parallelism (паралле́лизм) helps a long sentence by —","opts":["making it shorter","repeating a shape so the reader stays oriented","removing the main clause"],"answer":1},{"q":"In «В ко́мнату вошёл Ива́н» (answering «who came in?»), the рема is —","opts":["Ива́н","в ко́мнату","вошёл"],"answer":0},{"q":"In a period, the resolution (the main thought) belongs —","opts":["at the start","at the end, in the strongest position","anywhere"],"answer":1},{"q":"Russian word order is relatively free because it is used to —","opts":["mark given (те́ма) vs new (ре́ма)","save ink","avoid verbs"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"One sentence, forty words — and not a reader lost. Watch the clauses mount and the main thought land at the end. Read it aloud and let your voice draw the arch.","title":"Одно́ предложе́ние на со́рок слов","sentences":["Когда́ за окно́м смолка́л после́дний трамва́й, когда́ сосе́ди га́сли за стено́й, когда́ весь дом погружа́лся в тишину́, — то́лько тогда́ Э́мма сади́лась за перево́д.","Она́ зна́ла: то, что она́ и́щет, придёт не сра́зу и не по зака́зу.","Чем до́льше она́ вчи́тывалась, чем ме́дленнее шла по стро́чке, чем ти́ше станови́лось вокру́г, — тем я́вственнее проступа́л го́лос а́втора.","Гла́вное сло́во она́ всегда́ припаса́ла на коне́ц — туда́, где оно́ ждёт чита́теля с по́лным ве́сом.","Дли́нное предложе́ние не пуга́ло её: дли́нное — зна́чит уде́ржанное, а не запу́танное."],"translation":"When the last tram fell silent beyond the window, when the neighbours went dark behind the wall, when the whole building sank into quiet — only then did Emma sit down to translate. She knew that what she was looking for would not come at once, and not on demand. The longer she read into it, the slower she moved along the line, the quieter it grew around her — the more distinctly the author's voice emerged. The key word she always saved for the end — the place where it waits for the reader at full weight. A long sentence did not frighten her: long means held, not tangled."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to hold the arch.","prompt":"Build ONE пери́од — a single long sentence, 30–45 words — on any theme (a city at night, a decision, a translation). Mount it with THREE parallel subordinate clauses of the same shape («когда́… когда́… когда́…» or «что́бы… что́бы… что́бы…»), put a dash, then resolve it in one main clause that carries the new information (рема) at the very end. Then, in one line, mark where your build-up ends and the resolution begins.","hint":"Read it aloud. If your voice rises through the clauses and falls cleanly into the main thought, the arch holds. If you run out of breath or forget the start, prune it back — long means held, not tangled 🙂","example":"Когда́ го́род затиха́ет, когда́ ла́мпы га́снут одна́ за друго́й, когда́ да́же ве́тер сти́хнет за стекло́м, — то́лько тогда́ я слы́шу со́бственные мы́сли. Разбе́г — три «когда́»; разреше́ние — «я слы́шу со́бственные мы́сли», где «мы́сли» ста́вится в са́мый коне́ц, под уда́р."}},"20":{"day":20,"week":"03","themeRu":"The words that won't translate","themeEn":"Безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика","intro":"There is a Russian word Набо́ков said no single English word can render: <b>тоска́</b>. Not sadness, not boredom, not melancholy — «a dull ache of the soul, a longing with nothing to long for», he wrote, spending half a page where one word should go. Some words have no equivalent because the other language never carved that exact shape out of experience. Today isn't about finding the English word — there isn't one. It's about learning to <b>unpack</b> such a word, and to feel the cultural weight it carries. This is the deepest room in thinking-in-Russian.","introAudio":"There is a Russian word Nabokov said no single English word can render: 'toska'. Not sadness, not boredom, not melancholy — 'a dull ache of the soul, a longing with nothing to long for', he wrote, spending half a page where one word should go. Some words have no equivalent because the other language never carved that exact shape out of experience. Today isn't about finding the English word — there isn't one. It's about learning to unpack such a word, and to feel the cultural weight it carries. This is the deepest room in thinking-in-Russian.","goals":["Understand <b>безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика</b> (culture-bound words with no one-word equivalent) — and why a one-for-one translation always lies","Unpack the anchor word <b>тоска́</b> — an aching, objectless longing, not «sadness» or «boredom»","Feel <b>по́шлость</b> — self-satisfied vulgarity-and-triteness at once, a verdict on soul and taste","Hold a few more that carry a whole worldview: <b>аво́сь · у́даль · со́весть · меща́нство · интеллиге́нт</b>"],"learned":["Understood безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика — words with no one-word twin, why a flat translation lies","Unpacked тоска́ — an aching, objectless longing, not «sadness» or «boredom»","Felt по́шлость — smug triteness posing as taste, a verdict on soul and taste at once","Held words that carry a worldview: аво́сь, у́даль, со́весть, меща́нство, интеллиге́нт, неприка́янный"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday: the пери́од — a long sentence built as an arch, mounting through parallel clauses to a resolution at the end. One minute to keep it, then a different kind of depth — words instead of sentences.","introAudio":"Yesterday: the 'period' — a long sentence built as an arch, mounting through parallel clauses to a resolution at the end. One minute to keep it, then a different kind of depth — words instead of sentences.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's controlled period.","q":"In a пери́од, the main thought (the resolution) lands —","opts":["at the very start","at the end, carrying the new information","in the middle"],"answer":1,"explain":"The build-up mounts through the clauses; the resolution (with the рема, the new information) lands last, in the strongest position."},{"type":"choice","q":"Parallel clauses («когда́… когда́… когда́…») in a period serve to —","opts":["confuse the reader","keep the reader oriented as the tension mounts","shorten the sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The repeated shape is a handrail — the reader holds the pattern and never loses the thread as the clauses stack up."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to open a period with three parallel clauses.","q":"Open a period: “When the city falls silent, when the windows go dark, when the street empties…”","want":"Когда́ смолка́ет го́род, когда́ га́снут о́кна, когда́ пусте́ет у́лица","answer":"Когда́ смолка́ет го́род, когда́ га́снут о́кна, когда́ пусте́ет у́лица"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · No word for it","title":"<em>Безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика</em> — you unpack it, you don't translate it","paras":["Some words have no equivalent — not «a hard-to-translate» word, but a word the other language simply never made, because it never carved that piece of experience off as a single thing. These are <b>безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика</b> (non-equivalent lexis) and <b>реа́лии</b> (realia — culture-bound things and concepts). The C2 move isn't to hunt an equivalent — there isn't one. It's to <b>unpack</b> (разверну́ть) the word: name what it holds, and feel its weight.","The anchor is <b>тоска́</b>. English offers «sadness, melancholy, boredom, yearning, anguish» — and each is a corner of it, none the whole. Набо́ков's own gloss: «at its deepest, a dull ache of the soul, a longing with no object; at its lightest, boredom, ennui». It's a longing that doesn't know what it longs for. Translate it as «sadness» and you've thrown the depth away.","So the discipline flips. B1 asked «what's the English for this word?»; C2 asks «what does this word <b>hold</b> that English never packed into one?» You keep the Russian word, and you unfold it — because the concept, not just the word, is Russian."],"audio":"Some words have no equivalent — not a hard-to-translate word, but a word the other language simply never made, because it never carved that piece of experience off as a single thing. These are 'bezekvivalentnaya leksika', non-equivalent lexis, and 'realii', realia — culture-bound things and concepts. The C2 move isn't to hunt an equivalent — there isn't one. It's to unpack, 'razvernut', the word: name what it holds, and feel its weight. The anchor is 'toska'. English offers sadness, melancholy, boredom, yearning, anguish — and each is a corner of it, none the whole. Nabokov's own gloss: at its deepest, a dull ache of the soul, a longing with no object; at its lightest, boredom, ennui. It's a longing that doesn't know what it longs for. Translate it as sadness and you've thrown the depth away. So the discipline flips. B1 asked what's the English for this word; C2 asks what does this word hold that English never packed into one? You keep the Russian word, and you unfold it — because the concept, not just the word, is Russian.","table":{"rows":[["тоска́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тоска\">🔊</button>","an aching, objectless longing — not «sadness» or «boredom»"],["разверну́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"развернуть\">🔊</button>","to unpack, unfold (a concept) — instead of translating it"],["реа́лия <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"реалия\">🔊</button>","a realia — a culture-bound thing with no foreign match"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“It's not sadness — it's тоска, a longing with no name.”","en":"Э́то не грусть — э́то тоска́, тя́га без и́мени.","say":"это не грусть — это тоска, тяга без имени."},{"ru":"You don't translate тоска — you unfold what it holds.","en":"Тоску́ не перево́дят — её развора́чивают.","say":"тоску не переводят — её разворачивают."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Мне ску́чно» = «У меня́ тоска́».","right":"«Мне ску́чно» ≠ «У меня́ тоска́».","why":"«Ску́чно» is plain boredom — an empty afternoon. <b>Тоска́</b> is an ache of the soul, a longing with no object; boredom is only its faintest edge. Flattening тоска́ to «bored» (or English «sad») throws away exactly the weight that makes it untranslatable."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика» is best handled by —","opts":["unpacking what the word holds and keeping it","finding the closest single English word","avoiding it"],"answer":0,"explain":"There's no equivalent to hunt. You unpack what the word holds and keep it — the concept itself is Russian."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Тоска́» is closest to —","opts":["mild boredom, and nothing more","an aching, objectless longing (with boredom only as its faintest edge)","physical pain"],"answer":1,"explain":"Тоска́ is a longing that doesn't know its object — an ache of the soul. Boredom is only its lightest edge, «sadness» misses the depth."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The verdict with no name","title":"<em>По́шлость</em> — vulgarity and triteness in one judgment","paras":["The hardest of all is <b>по́шлость</b>. It is <i>not</i> «vulgarity» (that's rude, crude — гру́бость) and <i>not</i> «banality» (that's merely dull). По́шлость is both at once and more: self-satisfied triteness that mistakes itself for taste, spiritual cheapness dressed up as the good life — the smug, the tacky-sublime, the cliché delivered with a wink as if it were profound.","Набо́ков spent an essay on it: «not only the obviously trashy but also the falsely important, the falsely beautiful, the falsely clever». A gift-shop cupid, a greeting-card sentiment, a man performing sensitivity to impress — all по́шлость. It's a moral-aesthetic verdict: the speaker judges soul <i>and</i> taste in one word.","That's why no English word fits: English splits the judgment across «vulgar / tacky / corny / trite / kitsch / smug», and по́шлость fuses them into a single, quietly devastating charge. To use it is to have an opinion English needs a sentence to state. В разгово́ре: «Кака́я по́шлость» lands like a verdict, not a description."],"audio":"The hardest of all is 'poshlost'. It is not vulgarity — that's rude, crude, 'grubost' — and not banality — that's merely dull. 'Poshlost' is both at once and more: self-satisfied triteness that mistakes itself for taste, spiritual cheapness dressed up as the good life — the smug, the tacky-sublime, the cliché delivered with a wink as if it were profound. Nabokov spent an essay on it: not only the obviously trashy but also the falsely important, the falsely beautiful, the falsely clever. A gift-shop cupid, a greeting-card sentiment, a man performing sensitivity to impress — all 'poshlost'. It's a moral-aesthetic verdict: the speaker judges soul and taste in one word. That's why no English word fits: English splits the judgment across vulgar, tacky, corny, trite, kitsch, smug, and 'poshlost' fuses them into a single, quietly devastating charge. To use it is to have an opinion English needs a sentence to state. In conversation: 'kakaya poshlost' lands like a verdict, not a description.","examples":[{"ru":"In conversation: that toast about “the main thing is health” is pure по́шлость.","en":"В разгово́ре: э́тот тост про «гла́вное — здоро́вье» — чи́стая по́шлость.","say":"в разговоре: этот тост про главное — здоровье — чистая пошлость."},{"ru":"It's not crude and not merely banal — it's по́шло, both at once.","en":"Э́то не гру́бо и не про́сто ба́нально — э́то по́шло, всё сра́зу.","say":"это не грубо и не просто банально — это пошло, всё сразу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он вы́ругался — кака́я по́шлость».","right":"«Он вы́ругался — кака́я гру́бость».","why":"Swearing is <b>гру́бость</b> (crudeness), not <b>по́шлость</b>. По́шлость isn't rudeness — it's smug, self-satisfied triteness that thinks it's refined: the tacky sentiment, the cliché worn as taste. Mixing the two is the classic slip, because English «vulgar» covers both."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«По́шлость» is —","opts":["simple rudeness or swearing","physical dirtiness","self-satisfied triteness posing as taste (a moral-aesthetic verdict)"],"answer":2,"explain":"По́шлость fuses «vulgar» and «trite» into one verdict on soul and taste. Rudeness is гру́бость; dirt is something else entirely."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the untranslatable noun for an aching, objectless longing (hint: тос…):","accept":["тоска"],"placeholder":"тос…","explain":"<b>Тоска́</b> — the soul's ache, a longing with no object. No single English word holds it; you unpack it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Words that carry a worldview","title":"<em>Аво́сь</em>, <em>у́даль</em>, <em>интеллиге́нт</em> — a culture in one word","paras":["Some untranslatable words carry a whole attitude to life. <b>Аво́сь</b> — reliance on blind luck, «it'll somehow work out» raised to a life-stance («де́лать что-то на аво́сь», acting on a hope with no plan). <b>У́даль</b> — reckless, joyous daring, bravado with a wide-open Russian sweep to it. Neither has an English word, because neither is quite an English idea.","Others name a moral or social type. <b>Со́весть</b> — conscience, but heavier: the inner voice you answer to («жить по со́вести», to live by your conscience). <b>Интеллиге́нт</b> — <i>not</i> «intelligent» (a false friend!) but a member of the educated, conscientious, morally-engaged class. <b>Меща́нство</b> — petty-bourgeois narrowness of spirit, comfort without ideals. <b>Неприка́янный</b> — restless, unable to find your place in the world.","You'll never render these one-for-one. In a translation you unpack them, or keep the word and gloss it — the way English simply borrowed «intelligentsia». Feeling their weight, knowing when a plain word won't do and only <b>тоска́</b> or <b>по́шлость</b> will — that's the last, deepest layer of thinking in Russian."],"audio":"Some untranslatable words carry a whole attitude to life. 'Avos' — reliance on blind luck, it'll somehow work out raised to a life-stance: 'delat chto-to na avos', acting on a hope with no plan. 'Udal' — reckless, joyous daring, bravado with a wide-open Russian sweep to it. Neither has an English word, because neither is quite an English idea. Others name a moral or social type. 'Sovest' — conscience, but heavier: the inner voice you answer to, 'zhit po sovesti', to live by your conscience. 'Intelligent' — not intelligent, a false friend, but a member of the educated, conscientious, morally-engaged class. 'Meshchanstvo' — petty-bourgeois narrowness of spirit, comfort without ideals. 'Neprikayanny' — restless, unable to find your place in the world. You'll never render these one-for-one. In a translation you unpack them, or keep the word and gloss it — the way English simply borrowed intelligentsia. Feeling their weight, knowing when a plain word won't do and only 'toska' or 'poshlost' will — that's the last, deepest layer of thinking in Russian.","table":{"rows":[["аво́сь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"авось\">🔊</button>","reliance on blind luck — «it'll somehow work out»"],["у́даль <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"удаль\">🔊</button>","reckless, joyous daring — bravado with a wide sweep"],["со́весть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"совесть\">🔊</button>","conscience — the inner voice you answer to"],["интеллиге́нт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интеллигент\">🔊</button>","the educated, conscientious type — NOT «intelligent»"],["неприка́янный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"неприкаянный\">🔊</button>","restless — unable to find one's place"]],"star":4},"examples":[{"ru":"He did it on a wing and a prayer — «на аво́сь», no plan at all.","en":"Он сде́лал э́то на аво́сь, без вся́кого пла́на.","say":"он сделал это на авось, без всякого плана."},{"ru":"“Intelligent” it is not: интеллиге́нт means the conscientious, educated class.","en":"«Интеллиге́нт» — не «у́мный»: э́то со́вестливый образо́ванный класс.","say":"интеллигент — не умный: это совестливый образованный класс."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он о́чень интеллиге́нтный челове́к — реши́л зада́чу за мину́ту».","right":"«Он о́чень у́мный челове́к — реши́л зада́чу за мину́ту».","why":"<b>Интеллиге́нтный</b> is a false friend — it means cultured, well-mannered, of the conscientious educated class, not «intelligent/clever». For solving a problem fast the word is <b>у́мный</b>. This is the trap English hears backwards."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Интеллиге́нт» means —","opts":["a clever, intelligent person","a member of the educated, conscientious class","a university student"],"answer":1,"explain":"False friend: интеллиге́нт names the conscientious, cultured, educated class — not «clever». For «clever» you'd say у́мный."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Де́лать что-то на аво́сь» means doing it —","opts":["trusting to blind luck, without a plan","carefully, with a full plan","under strict orders"],"answer":0,"explain":"Аво́сь is trust in blind luck — «it'll somehow work out». Doing something «на аво́сь» is acting on hope, with no plan behind it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Rendering the untranslatable — and the week ahead","title":"Unpack, borrow, or gloss — never flatten","paras":["So how does Эмма put <b>тоска́</b> or <b>по́шлость</b> into English? Three honest moves, never a flat one-word swap. <b>Unpack</b> — spend the sentence the concept needs («a longing with nothing to long for»). <b>Borrow</b> — keep the Russian and italicise it, the way English took <i>intelligentsia</i> and <i>kitsch</i> the other way. <b>Gloss</b> — keep the word and add a short unfolding beside it.","What you never do is substitute «sad» for тоска́ or «vulgar» for по́шлость and move on — that's the flattening that tells a reader a foreigner translated it. The word survives only if its weight does.","That closes week three's new ground. Tomorrow we gather it all — rhetorical figures, the art of the speech, graceful argument, calibrated hedging, the controlled period, and these untranslatable words — into one review. You've spent the week learning not just to be correct, but to <b>move</b> a Russian reader."],"audio":"So how does Emma put 'toska' or 'poshlost' into English? Three honest moves, never a flat one-word swap. Unpack — spend the sentence the concept needs: a longing with nothing to long for. Borrow — keep the Russian and italicise it, the way English took intelligentsia and kitsch the other way. Gloss — keep the word and add a short unfolding beside it. What you never do is substitute sad for 'toska' or vulgar for 'poshlost' and move on — that's the flattening that tells a reader a foreigner translated it. The word survives only if its weight does. That closes week three's new ground. Tomorrow we gather it all — rhetorical figures, the art of the speech, graceful argument, calibrated hedging, the controlled period, and these untranslatable words — into one review. You've spent the week learning not just to be correct, but to move a Russian reader.","examples":[{"ru":"You don't swap «sad» for тоска — you keep its weight or lose the word.","en":"Не подставля́й «грусть» вме́сто тоски́ — ина́че поте́ряешь сло́во.","say":"не подставляй грусть вместо тоски — иначе потеряешь слово."},{"ru":"English borrowed «intelligentsia» whole — sometimes that's the honest way.","en":"Англи́йский взял «intelligentsia» целико́м — иногда́ э́то и есть че́стный путь.","say":"английский взял intelligentsia целиком — иногда это и есть честный путь."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Перево́д: «I felt sad» для «Меня́ одолева́ла тоска́».","right":"Перево́д: «I was seized by toska — a longing with no name».","why":"«I felt sad» throws the word away. <b>Тоска́</b> earns unpacking — keep it, or unfold it («a longing with no name»). Flattening it to «sad» is the surrender that marks a translation as foreign."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The honest ways to render an untranslatable word are unpack, borrow, or gloss — but never —","opts":["keep the Russian word","flatten it to one near-English word","explain it in a footnote"],"answer":1,"explain":"Flattening тоска́ to «sad» or по́шлость to «vulgar» throws away the weight. Unpack, borrow, or gloss — but keep what the word holds."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it aloud, letting the untranslatable word sit heavy.","target":"Э́то не про́сто грусть — э́то тоска́, для кото́рой нет сло́ва в друго́м языке́.","sub":"let «тоска́» sit heavy — the whole line leans on that one word","want":"это не просто грусть — это тоска, для которой нет слова в другом языке"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is stuck on a single word in her translation. Ка́тя, Ива́н's friend, finds her staring at the page. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты бле́дная. Что случи́лось?","ru":"You're pale. What happened?"},{"who":"A","en":"Одно́ сло́во. «Тоска́». Перево́жу уже́ час — и не могу́.","ru":"One word. “Toska.” I've been translating for an hour — and I can't."},{"who":"B","en":"А что тут сло́жного? Ну, «грусть», «печа́ль».","ru":"What's hard about it? Well — “sadness,” “sorrow.”"},{"who":"A","en":"«Грусть» — про поте́рю. А тоска́ — ни про что. Тя́нет ду́шу, а по чему́ — сам не зна́ешь.","ru":"“Grust” is about a loss. Toska is about nothing. It pulls at your soul, and toward what — you don't even know."},{"who":"B","en":"И как ты э́то напи́шешь по-англи́йски?","ru":"And how will you write that in English?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ника́к — одни́м сло́вом. Оста́влю «toska» и разверну́ ря́дом. Как англича́не взя́ли «intelligentsia».","ru":"No way — not in one word. I'll keep “toska” and unpack it beside it. The way the English took “intelligentsia.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, есть слова́, кото́рые не перево́дятся?","ru":"So there are words that don't translate?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не перево́дятся — развора́чиваются. И в э́том ро́вно та Росси́я, кото́рую не вы́учишь по словарю́.","ru":"They don't translate — they unfold. And in that is exactly the Russia you won't learn from a dictionary."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words the dictionary can't quite hold — you unpack them, not translate them. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"тоска́","ru":"an aching, objectless longing","ex":"Тоска́ — тя́га без и́мени."},{"en":"по́шлость","ru":"smug triteness posing as taste","ex":"Э́тот пода́рок — сплошна́я по́шлость."},{"en":"аво́сь","ru":"reliance on blind luck","ex":"Он всё де́лает на аво́сь."},{"en":"у́даль","ru":"reckless, joyous daring","ex":"В та́нце была́ ру́сская у́даль."},{"en":"со́весть","ru":"conscience (the inner voice)","ex":"Он живёт по со́вести."},{"en":"меща́нство","ru":"petty-bourgeois narrowness of spirit","ex":"Она́ презира́ла меща́нство."},{"en":"интеллиге́нт","ru":"the conscientious, educated type (NOT «intelligent»)","ex":"Ста́рый интеллиге́нт, ти́хий и че́стный."},{"en":"неприка́янный","ru":"restless, unable to find one's place","ex":"Он броди́л неприка́янный по го́роду."},{"en":"безэквивале́нтный","ru":"non-equivalent, untranslatable","ex":"Безэквивале́нтное сло́во не перево́дят."},{"en":"реа́лия","ru":"a realia (a culture-bound thing)","ex":"«Ва́ленки» — ру́сская реа́лия."},{"en":"разверну́ть","ru":"to unpack, unfold (a concept)","ex":"Сло́во ну́жно разверну́ть, а не перевести́."},{"en":"непереводи́мый","ru":"untranslatable","ex":"Тоска́ — почти́ непереводи́мое сло́во."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each untranslatable word to what it really holds — the shade no single English word packs in.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word on the left onto what it holds — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["тоска́","an aching longing with no object — not «sadness»"],["по́шлость","smug triteness posing as taste — a verdict on soul and taste"],["аво́сь","trusting to blind luck — «it'll somehow work out»"],["интеллиге́нт","the conscientious, educated type — NOT «intelligent»"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика» is handled by —","opts":["a single close English word","unpacking the concept and keeping the word","skipping it"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Тоска́» differs from «грусть» and from «boredom» because it is —","opts":["a longing with no object, an ache of the soul","a physical illness","a kind of anger"],"answer":0},{"q":"«По́шлость» is —","opts":["self-satisfied triteness mistaking itself for taste","crude swearing","dust and dirt"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Интеллиге́нт» is a false friend — it means —","opts":["an intelligent person","a member of the conscientious, educated class","a schoolteacher"],"answer":1},{"q":"To render an untranslatable word faithfully, you should NOT —","opts":["flatten it to one near-English word","unpack it","borrow and gloss it"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A page from Эмма's notebook — the words she stopped trying to translate and learned to unfold instead. Read it aloud and feel the weight each one carries.","title":"Слова́, кото́рые не перево́дятся","sentences":["Есть слова́, над кото́рыми перево́дчик остана́вливается наперёд, зна́я, что англи́йского двойника́ у них нет.","«Тоска́» — не грусть и не ску́ка, а глуха́я тя́га души́, кото́рая сама́ не зна́ет, чего́ хо́чет.","«По́шлость» — не гру́бость: э́то самодово́льная безвку́сица, вы́данная за то́нкий вкус.","«Аво́сь» — це́лая филосо́фия: де́лать науда́чу и ве́рить, что как-нибу́дь обойдётся.","Таки́е слова́ Э́мма не перево́дит — она́ их развора́чивает, оставля́я ру́сское сло́во ды́шать.","И и́менно здесь, а не в грамма́тике, начина́ется настоя́щее «ду́мать по-ру́сски»."],"translation":"There are words a translator slows down before, knowing they have no English twin. “Toska” is not sadness and not boredom, but a muffled pull of the soul that doesn't itself know what it wants. “Poshlost” is not crudeness: it's a self-satisfied tastelessness passed off as fine taste. “Avos” is a whole philosophy: to act at random and trust that somehow it'll work out. Such words Emma does not translate — she unfolds them, leaving the Russian word to breathe. And it's here, not in the grammar, that real “thinking in Russian” begins."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — not to translate, but to unfold.","prompt":"Choose ONE untranslatable word from today — тоска́, по́шлость, аво́сь, у́даль, со́весть, меща́нство, интеллиге́нт, неприка́янный — and unpack it in Russian in 3–4 sentences: say what it is NOT (the near-English word that flattens it), what it actually holds (the shade, the feeling, the worldview), and one situation where only this word will do. Do not give an English one-word translation — unfold it.","hint":"Ask the C2 question: what does this word carry that no single English word packs in? Name the false friend it's mistaken for, then the weight it really holds 🙂","example":"«Тоска́» — э́то не «грусть» и не «ску́ка». Грусть быва́ет о чём-то — о поте́ре, о про́шлом; тоска́ же ни о чём: она́ тя́нет ду́шу, а куда́ — не зна́ет и сама́. Она́ прихо́дит без причи́ны в дождли́вый ве́чер, когда́ всё вро́де хорошо́, а на се́рдце глуха́я тя́жесть. Э́то сло́во не перево́дят — его́ то́лько развора́чивают."}},"21":{"day":21,"week":"03","themeRu":"Review 3","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 3","intro":"Week three, done — and what a week. You built figures that strike (ана́фора, града́ция, антите́за), stood up to give a speech that rises to its climax, learned to win an argument by conceding first, to hedge a claim by exactly the amount you mean, to hold a sixty-word пери́од in one clear arch, and to unpack words no English can carry (тоска́, по́шлость). Today we gather it — the whole rhetorical, argumentative, precise, untranslatable week — into one place. No new ground; just everything, firmer.","introAudio":"Week three, done — and what a week. You built figures that strike ('anafora', 'gradatsiya', 'antiteza'), stood up to give a speech that rises to its climax, learned to win an argument by conceding first, to hedge a claim by exactly the amount you mean, to hold a sixty-word 'period' in one clear arch, and to unpack words no English can carry ('toska', 'poshlost'). Today we gather it — the whole rhetorical, argumentative, precise, untranslatable week — into one place. No new ground; just everything, firmer.","goals":["Wield rhetorical figures as tools — <b>ана́фора, града́ция, антите́за</b>, the rhetorical question — and shape a speech that rises to a climax","Win gracefully: concede the strong point, then rebut — and hedge with calibrated formulas (<b>вряд ли, не исключено́, скло́нен полага́ть</b>)","Hold a <b>пери́од</b>: parallel clauses mount, the resolution lands last (те́ма → ре́ма)","Unpack the untranslatable — <b>тоска́, по́шлость, аво́сь</b> — feeling the weight instead of flattening it"],"learned":["Wielded rhetorical figures as tools and shaped a speech that rises to its climax","Won gracefully — conceded, then rebutted — and hedged with calibrated formulas","Held a пери́од — parallel clauses mounting to a resolution that lands last","Unpacked the untranslatable (тоска́, по́шлость, аво́сь), feeling the weight, not flattening it"],"review":{"intro":"One last look back before we sweep the week together — yesterday's untranslatable words, the deepest room of all.","introAudio":"One last look back before we sweep the week together — yesterday's untranslatable words, the deepest room of all.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's untranslatable lexis.","q":"«Тоска́» is best rendered by —","opts":["«boredom»","unpacking it: an aching, objectless longing","«anger»"],"answer":1,"explain":"No single English word holds тоска́ — you unpack it. Boredom is only its faintest edge."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Интеллиге́нт» means —","opts":["a member of the conscientious, educated class","an intelligent person","an office worker"],"answer":0,"explain":"A false friend: интеллиге́нт names the cultured, conscientious, educated class — not «clever». For «clever» you'd say у́мный."},{"type":"choice","q":"«По́шлость» is —","opts":["crude swearing","self-satisfied triteness posing as taste","a spelling mistake"],"answer":1,"explain":"По́шлость fuses «vulgar» and «trite» into one verdict on soul and taste. Swearing is гру́бость — a different word."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Rhetoric & the speech (days 15–16)","title":"Figures that <em>strike</em>, a speech that <em>rises</em>","paras":["Week three opened with rhetoric as a <b>tool</b>, not decoration. <b>Ана́фора</b> (the same opening, repeated) drives rhythm; <b>града́ция</b> (mounting intensity: «пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л») climbs; <b>антите́за</b> sets opposites against each other; the <b>рито́рический вопро́с</b> makes the listener answer inside their own head. Used in measure, they land; piled on for their own sake, they turn to noise.","Then the whole speech: an arch from зачи́н (the opening) through те́зис and argument to a <b>кульмина́ция</b> (climax) and a clean concо́вка (close). Russian public speech isn't afraid of <b>па́фос</b> — genuine high emotion, lifted toward the climax — where a dry corporate pitch would stay flat. You learned to raise the register on the way up, and to land it."],"audio":"Week three opened with rhetoric as a tool, not decoration. 'Anafora', the same opening repeated, drives rhythm; 'gradatsiya', mounting intensity — 'prishyol, uvidel, pobedil' — climbs; 'antiteza' sets opposites against each other; the 'ritoricheskiy vopros' makes the listener answer inside their own head. Used in measure, they land; piled on for their own sake, they turn to noise. Then the whole speech: an arch from 'zachin', the opening, through 'tezis' and argument to a 'kulminatsiya', climax, and a clean 'kontsovka', close. Russian public speech isn't afraid of 'pafos' — genuine high emotion, lifted toward the climax — where a dry corporate pitch would stay flat. You learned to raise the register on the way up, and to land it.","examples":[{"ru":"Gradation: three verbs, mounting — “came, saw, conquered”.","en":"Града́ция: пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л.","say":"градация: пришёл, увидел, победил."},{"ru":"A rhetorical question makes the hall answer for itself.","en":"Рито́рический вопро́с заставля́ет зал отве́тить самому́ себе́.","say":"риторический вопрос заставляет зал ответить самому себе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Пришёл, и уви́дел, и та́кже победи́л».","right":"«Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л».","why":"The mounting three-verb figure (града́ция) strikes precisely because the conjunctions are dropped. Adding «и… и…» deflates the rhythm — the тройка should land as three quick blows, not a list."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л» is an example of —","opts":["антите́за","града́ция (mounting intensity)","хеджи́рование"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three verbs climbing to a peak — that's <b>града́ция</b> (gradation). Антите́за sets opposites; хеджи́рование softens a claim."},{"type":"choice","q":"Russian oratory, unlike a dry corporate pitch, isn't afraid of —","opts":["па́фос — genuine high emotion toward the climax","correct grammar","short words"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Па́фос</b> is genuine, lifted emotion — not pity — raised toward the climax. A flat pitch leaves it out and sounds poorer for it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Arguing & hedging (days 17–18)","title":"Concede, then rebut — and <em>measure</em> the claim","paras":["You learned to win gracefully: <b>усту́пка</b> (concession) first — grant the opponent's strong point («согла́сен, но…», «допу́стим») — then rebut on the merits, firmly inside politeness: «позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться». Twist the person and you lose; answer the argument and you win.","Then <b>хеджи́рование</b> — dosing a claim by exactly the amount you mean. English falls off two cliffs: blunt («доказано») or a fog of «мо́жет быть». Russian calibrates: <b>вряд ли</b> (a soft no), <b>не исключено́</b> (door ajar), <b>по всей ви́димости</b> (nearly sure, on the evidence), <b>скло́нен полага́ть</b> (a considered lean). And <b>я́кобы</b> carries doubt — attribute a claim with it and you've refused to vouch for it."],"audio":"You learned to win gracefully: 'ustupka', concession, first — grant the opponent's strong point, 'soglasen, no', 'dopustim' — then rebut on the merits, firmly inside politeness: 'pozvolyu sebe ne soglasitsya'. Twist the person and you lose; answer the argument and you win. Then 'khedzhirovaniye' — dosing a claim by exactly the amount you mean. English falls off two cliffs: blunt, 'dokazano', or a fog of 'mozhet byt'. Russian calibrates: 'vryad li', a soft no; 'ne isklyucheno', door ajar; 'po vsey vidimosti', nearly sure on the evidence; 'sklonen polagat', a considered lean. And 'yakoby' carries doubt — attribute a claim with it and you've refused to vouch for it.","examples":[{"ru":"“I'll allow myself to disagree” — firm, but inside courtesy.","en":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться» — твёрдо, но в ве́жливости.","say":"позволю себе не согласиться — твёрдо, но в вежливости."},{"ru":"In scholarship: not “this proves” but “this, in all likelihood, indicates”.","en":"В нау́ке: не «э́то дока́зывает», а «э́то, по всей ви́димости, свиде́тельствует».","say":"в науке: не это доказывает, а это, по всей видимости, свидетельствует."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ты про́сто не прав, и всё».","right":"«Допу́стим, в чём-то вы пра́вы, но…»","why":"Attacking the person («ты не прав») loses the room. Concede the strong point first (усту́пка — «допу́стим»), then counter on the merits. Firmness lives inside courtesy, not instead of it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Granting your opponent's strong point before you counter it is —","opts":["усту́пка (a concession)","града́ция","тоска́"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Усту́пка</b> — concede («допу́стим», «согла́сен, но…»), then rebut. Answer the argument, not the person."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the hedge. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the hedge that leaves a possibility open — “it can't be ruled out” (hint: не искл…):","accept":["не исключено"],"placeholder":"не искл…","explain":"<b>Не исключено́</b> — the door left deliberately ajar. Neither blunt nor foggy: a claim dosed to exactly your certainty."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The period & the untranslatable (days 19–20)","title":"Hold the long arch — then the words that won't translate","paras":["The <b>пери́од</b>: a long sentence built as an arch. Parallel subordinate clauses mount the tension («когда́… когда́… когда́…»), a dash marks the top, and the main clause resolves it — with the new information (<b>ре́ма</b>) landing last, in the strongest position, while the given (<b>те́ма</b>) opened. Long doesn't mean tangled; long means held.","And the deepest room: <b>безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика</b> — words with no one-word twin. <b>Тоска́</b> (an aching, objectless longing, not «sadness»), <b>по́шлость</b> (smug triteness posing as taste), <b>аво́сь</b> (trust in blind luck), <b>интеллиге́нт</b> (the conscientious, educated class — not «intelligent»). You don't translate them — you unpack them, and feel the weight. That's thinking in Russian."],"audio":"The 'period': a long sentence built as an arch. Parallel subordinate clauses mount the tension — 'kogda, kogda, kogda' — a dash marks the top, and the main clause resolves it, with the new information, the 'rema', landing last, in the strongest position, while the given, the 'tema', opened. Long doesn't mean tangled; long means held. And the deepest room: 'bezekvivalentnaya leksika' — words with no one-word twin. 'Toska', an aching, objectless longing, not sadness; 'poshlost', smug triteness posing as taste; 'avos', trust in blind luck; 'intelligent', the conscientious, educated class, not intelligent. You don't translate them — you unpack them, and feel the weight. That's thinking in Russian.","examples":[{"ru":"Clauses mount, the main thought resolves — and «мысль» lands last.","en":"Когда́ всё сти́хнет, когда́ пога́снет свет, — тогда́ прихо́дит мысль.","say":"когда всё стихнет, когда погаснет свет, — тогда приходит мысль."},{"ru":"You don't translate тоска — you unfold what it holds.","en":"Тоску́ не перево́дят — её развора́чивают.","say":"тоску не переводят — её разворачивают."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он сде́лал э́то, и я усну́л, и пришла́ мысль».","right":"«Когда́ он сде́лал э́то, когда́ всё сти́хло, — пришла́ мысль».","why":"The first is a run-on — «и… и…» with no arch. The rewrite is a small пери́од: two parallel «когда́» clauses mount, the main thought («пришла́ мысль») resolves last. Long, but held."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a пери́од, the new information (рема) lands —","opts":["at the start","at the very end, in the strongest position","in the middle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Save the payload (рема) for the end — that's where the arch pays off, with the resolution."},{"type":"choice","q":"You handle «по́шлость» in translation by —","opts":["swapping in «vulgar» and moving on","unpacking what it holds and keeping its weight","deleting it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Flattening по́шлость to «vulgar» throws away the verdict on soul and taste. Unpack it, or keep and gloss it."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is rehearsing a short talk for a translators' meetup. Ива́н is her one-man audience. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну, чита́й своё выступле́ние. Я — весь зал.","ru":"Go on, read your talk. I'm the whole audience."},{"who":"A","en":"«Когда́ мы перево́дим, когда́ мы и́щем сло́во, когда́ мы бои́мся его́ потеря́ть, — мы, по су́ти, спаса́ем смысл».","ru":"“When we translate, when we search for the word, when we fear to lose it — we are, in essence, saving meaning.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Краси́во. Э́то ведь пери́од — как ты учи́ла?","ru":"Beautiful. That's a period, isn't it — the way you taught me?"},{"who":"A","en":"Он са́мый. Три «когда́» — разбе́г, гла́вное — в конце́. А да́льше я не рублю́ с плеча́: «Вряд ли есть то́чный перево́д для „тоски́“».","ru":"The very one. Three “when” — the run-up, the main thought at the end. And then I don't cut from the shoulder: “There's hardly an exact translation for ‘toska’.”"},{"who":"B","en":"«Вряд ли» вме́сто «нет»? Мя́гче.","ru":"“Hardly” instead of “no”? Softer."},{"who":"A","en":"И че́стнее. А в конце́ — усту́пка за́лу: «Допу́стим, я не права́, — но попро́буйте перевести́ „по́шлость“ одни́м сло́вом».","ru":"And more honest. And at the end — a concession to the hall: “Suppose I'm wrong — but try translating ‘poshlost’ in one word.”"},{"who":"B","en":"На э́том они́ и сда́дутся.","ru":"That's where they'll give up."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и вся неде́ля: фигу́ра, пери́од, хедж и сло́во, кото́рое не перевести́. Всё сра́зу.","ru":"That's the whole week: a figure, a period, a hedge, and a word you can't translate. All at once."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words to carry out of week three — rhetoric, argument, the period, the untranslatable. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ана́фора","ru":"anaphora (same opening repeated)","ex":"Ана́фора задаёт ритм ре́чи."},{"en":"града́ция","ru":"gradation (mounting intensity)","ex":"Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л — града́ция."},{"en":"кульмина́ция","ru":"the climax (of a speech)","ex":"Речь поднима́ется к кульмина́ции."},{"en":"па́фос","ru":"pathos, genuine high emotion","ex":"Ру́сская речь не бои́тся па́фоса."},{"en":"усту́пка","ru":"a concession (in argument)","ex":"Усту́пка располага́ет оппоне́нта."},{"en":"хеджи́рование","ru":"hedging (softening a claim)","ex":"Хеджи́рование — то́чная ме́ра уве́ренности."},{"en":"вряд ли","ru":"hardly, unlikely (a soft “no”)","ex":"Вряд ли э́то возмо́жно."},{"en":"не исключено́","ru":"it can't be ruled out","ex":"Не исключено́, что он прав."},{"en":"пери́од","ru":"a period (a long, balanced sentence)","ex":"Пери́од де́ржит мысль до конца́."},{"en":"ре́ма","ru":"the rheme (new info, lands last)","ex":"Ре́ма встаёт в коне́ц фра́зы."},{"en":"тоска́","ru":"an aching, objectless longing","ex":"Тоска́ — тя́га без и́мени."},{"en":"по́шлость","ru":"smug triteness posing as taste","ex":"По́шлость вы́дана за вкус."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each term from week three to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["града́ция","a mounting, rising series — intensity climbing to the last"],["не исключено́","the door left ajar — a calibrated hedge"],["пери́од","a long sentence held as an arch — clauses mount, main resolves"],["тоска́","an aching, objectless longing — unpack it, don't translate"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — all of week three. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Пришёл, уви́дел, победи́л» is —","opts":["антите́за","града́ция (mounting intensity)","хеджи́рование"],"answer":1},{"q":"Russian public speech, unlike a dry pitch, embraces —","opts":["па́фос — genuine high emotion toward the climax","only bullet points","silence"],"answer":0},{"q":"To disagree gracefully with a respected opponent, you first —","opts":["insult them","concede their strong point (усту́пка), then rebut","change the subject"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which hedge leans politely toward «no»?","opts":["несомне́нно","безусло́вно","вряд ли"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Он я́кобы согласи́лся» — «я́кобы» adds —","opts":["the speaker's doubt","politeness","certainty"],"answer":0},{"q":"In a пери́од, the resolution (main thought, рема) lands —","opts":["first","at the very end","never"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Тоска́» is —","opts":["mild boredom, nothing more","an aching, objectless longing you unpack rather than translate","a kind of joy"],"answer":1},{"q":"«По́шлость» means —","opts":["self-satisfied triteness posing as taste","crude swearing","a factual error"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"The week in a single passage — a figure, a speech that rises, a graceful concession, a measured hedge, a held period, and a word that won't translate. Read it aloud and hear them all working together.","title":"Вся неде́ля в одно́м абза́це","sentences":["Когда́ говори́шь пе́ред за́лом, когда́ и́щешь еди́нственно ве́рное сло́во, когда́ хо́чешь, что́бы тебя́ услы́шали, — ты по́льзуешься всем сра́зу.","Ты стро́ишь пери́од так, что́бы гла́вное упа́ло в са́мый коне́ц, под уда́р.","Ты не руби́шь с плеча́: не «э́то до́лжно быть так», а «по всей ви́димости, э́то так».","Ты усту́пишь оппоне́нту си́льное ме́сто — и то́лько пото́м, споко́йно, возрази́шь по существу́.","И е́сли в те́ксте встре́тится «тоска́» и́ли «по́шлость», ты не переведёшь их одни́м сло́вом — ты их развернёшь.","Вот и вся неде́ля: фигу́ра, речь, усту́пка, хедж, пери́од и сло́во, кото́рое не име́ет двойника́."],"translation":"When you speak before a hall, when you search for the one right word, when you want to be heard — you use all of it at once. You build the period so that the main thing falls at the very end, on the stress. You don't cut from the shoulder: not “this must be so,” but “in all likelihood, this is so.” You concede your opponent the strong point — and only then, calmly, object on the merits. And if “toska” or “poshlost” turns up in the text, you won't translate them in one word — you'll unfold them. That's the whole week: a figure, a speech, a concession, a hedge, a period, and a word that has no twin."},"essay":{"intro":"One paragraph to show the whole week at once.","prompt":"Write a short paragraph (4–6 sentences) that USES at least four of week three's skills together: a rhetorical figure (ана́фора or града́ция), a пери́од with the payload at the end, a calibrated hedge (вряд ли / не исключено́ / по всей ви́димости), a graceful concession (допу́стим… / согла́сен, но…), and — if it fits — one untranslatable word (тоска́ / по́шлость / аво́сь) left unpacked, not translated. Theme: free. Then name which skill each sentence used.","hint":"Don't cram all six in mechanically — let them serve one thought. Build to a climax, hedge where you're honestly unsure, concede before you counter, and let the period land its last word hard 🙂","example":"Когда́ я начина́ла, когда́ я пу́тала падежи́, когда́ ка́ждое сло́во дава́лось с бо́ем, — я вряд ли ве́рила, что дойду́ сюда́. Допу́стим, я и сейча́с не всё зна́ю, — но, по всей ви́димости, гла́вное уже́ произошло́: ру́сский стал не предме́том, а го́лосом. И да́же «тоску́» я тепе́рь не перевожу́ — я её чу́вствую. (Ана́фора — три «когда́»; пери́од с гла́вным в конце́; хедж «вряд ли / по всей ви́димости»; усту́пка «допу́стим»; безэквивале́нтное сло́во.)"}},"22":{"day":22,"week":"04","themeRu":"Reading the classics unadapted","themeEn":"Кла́ссика без адапта́ции","intro":"Every learner hits the same wall. Your modern Russian is effortless — news, novels, chat — and then you open Толсто́го or Достое́вского in the original, unadapted, no footnotes, and the paragraph fights back. The words are familiar; the <b>sentence</b> coils the wrong way, and three small words — «сей», «дабы́», «о́чи» — belong to a Russian no one has spoken in a hundred years. Эмма crossed that wall this year: she now reads the nineteenth century for pleasure, feeling in a breath what is merely <i>old</i> and what is still alive. Today, so do you.","introAudio":"Every learner hits the same wall. Your modern Russian is effortless — news, novels, chat — and then you open Tolstogo or Dostoevskogo in the original, unadapted, no footnotes, and the paragraph fights back. The words are familiar; the sentence coils the wrong way, and three small words — 'sey', 'daby', 'ochi' — belong to a Russian no one has spoken in a hundred years. Emma crossed that wall this year: she now reads the nineteenth century for pleasure, feeling in a breath what is merely old and what is still alive. Today, so do you.","goals":["Read unadapted nineteenth-century prose — Пу́шкин, Го́голь, Достое́вский — without stumbling on its old syntax","Recognise the classic archaisms and their plain modern twins: <b>о́чи → глаза́, уста́ → гу́бы, чело́ → лоб, сей → э́тот</b>","Tell a <b>dead</b> archaism (сей, дабы́, о́чи) from a still-<b>living</b> bookish word (ве́сьма, и́бо, дитя́)","Feel what an archaism <b>carries</b> — solemnity, distance, irony — not just what it means"],"learned":["Read unadapted nineteenth-century prose without stumbling on its syntax","Matched classic archaisms to their modern twins (о́чи → глаза́, чело́ → лоб, сей → э́тот)","Told a dead archaism (сей, дабы́) from a living bookish word (ве́сьма, и́бо)","Felt what an archaism carries — solemnity, distance, or irony"],"review":{"intro":"Before the classics, one minute back on last week's rhetoric — the long period, the graceful concession, the words that won't translate. Hold it; today the nineteenth century tests all three.","introAudio":"Before the classics, one minute back on last week's rhetoric — the long period, the graceful concession, the words that won't translate. Hold it; today the nineteenth century tests all three.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is last week's period — an old friend.","q":"A <b>пери́од</b> — the long, controlled sentence you built — works by —","opts":["stacking subordinate clauses that build tension, then resolving in the main clause","using no commas at all","keeping every sentence under five words"],"answer":0,"explain":"That's the период: the subordinate parts pile up the tension, the main clause discharges it. You'll meet the same architecture in the classics — only denser."},{"type":"choice","q":"«по́шлость» has no one-word English match. The C2 speaker —","opts":["unfolds it — vulgar, self-satisfied triteness — instead of forcing «banality»","just says «banality» and moves on","refuses to use the word"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-equivalent words like по́шлость, тоска́, у́даль are <b>unfolded</b>, not translated. Feeling their weight is the «think in Russian» layer."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the concession.","q":"Build a graceful concession (уступка): «Granted, you're right — but not in everything.»","want":"Допу́стим, вы пра́вы, но не во всём","answer":"Допу́стим, вы пра́вы, но не во всём"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The wall of the original","title":"The words you know — in a Russian <em>no one speaks anymore</em>","paras":["You can read anything modern. The nineteenth-century page resists for two reasons, and neither is vocabulary you're missing: its <b>syntax</b> is built differently (long, inverted, clause piled on clause), and it carries a thin layer of <b>archaic words</b> — words the living language has quietly replaced.","The C2 skill is not to translate each old word but to <b>recognise its register</b> — to feel, instantly, «this is old / elevated» — and glide on. Meaning arrives from context; the flavour of oldness is the point.","And there are two kinds of «old». Truly <b>dead</b> archaisms — «сей» (= э́тот), «дабы́» (= что́бы), «о́чи» (= глаза́) — are gone from living speech; you meet them only in classics or as a deliberate wink. But «ве́сьма» (= о́чень), «и́бо» (= потому́ что), «дитя́» — are <b>bookish yet alive</b>: rare, elevated, still written today. Mistaking one for the other is the classic slip."],"audio":"You can read anything modern. The nineteenth-century page resists for two reasons, and neither is vocabulary you're missing: its syntax is built differently — long, inverted, clause piled on clause — and it carries a thin layer of archaic words, words the living language has quietly replaced. The C2 skill is not to translate each old word but to recognise its register — to feel, instantly, 'this is old, elevated' — and glide on. Meaning arrives from context; the flavour of oldness is the point. And there are two kinds of old. Truly dead archaisms — 'sey' equals 'etot', 'daby' equals 'chtoby', 'ochi' equals 'glaza' — are gone from living speech; you meet them only in classics or as a deliberate wink. But 'vesma' equals 'ochen', 'ibo' equals 'potomu chto', 'ditya' — are bookish yet alive: rare, elevated, still written today. Mistaking one for the other is the classic slip.","table":{"rows":[["сей <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сей\">🔊</button>","= э́тот (this) — <b>dead</b> archaism, gone from living speech"],["о́чи <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"очи\">🔊</button>","= глаза́ (eyes) — <b>dead / poetic</b>, only in verse or irony"],["ве́сьма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"весьма\">🔊</button>","= о́чень (very) — <b>bookish but alive</b>, still written today"],["и́бо <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ибо\">🔊</button>","= потому́ что (for, because) — <b>bookish but alive</b>"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"«In this very instant» — but «сию́» (an old form of «this») rings antique; today you'd say «в э́ту мину́ту».","en":"В сию́ мину́ту он всё по́нял.","say":"В сию минуту он всё понял."},{"ru":"«весьма» is no fossil — a modern report still reaches for it.","en":"Результа́ты иссле́дования ве́сьма любопы́тны.","say":"Результаты исследования весьма любопытны."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Напиши́ мне, дабы́ я знал, во ско́лько сей по́езд».","right":"«Напиши́ мне, что́бы я знал, во ско́лько э́тот по́езд».","why":"«сей» and «дабы́» are museum pieces — perfect on an 1840 page, absurd in a text to a friend. <i>Reading</i> them is C2; <i>using</i> them straight (not as a wink) marks a foreigner who learned Russian from Пу́шкин. The living twins are «э́тот» and «что́бы»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You open Достое́вского unadapted and hit «сей», «дабы́», «о́чи». The C2 move is to —","opts":["recognise them as archaic and glide on, feeling the old flavour","translate each one and start using it in your own speech","assume the book has typos"],"answer":0,"explain":"Recognise the register, feel the age, read on. Meaning comes from context — the flavour of oldness is what the author put there."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is <b>not</b> a dead archaism but a bookish word still alive today?","opts":["о́чи","ве́сьма","дабы́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ве́сьма</b> (= о́чень) is bookish but perfectly current — a report today can use it. о́чи and дабы́ are fossils."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The archaic shelf","title":"<em>О́чи, уста́, чело́</em> — the body in the old high style","paras":["The classics keep a whole parallel vocabulary, especially for the body and for high feeling. A native reads it without a dictionary and hears the solemnity underneath. These are <b>поэти́змы</b> (poetic words) and <b>церковнославяни́змы</b> (Church-Slavonic words) — high, old, used now only in verse, solemn prose, or irony.","The body shelf: <b>о́чи</b> = глаза́ (eyes), <b>уста́</b> = гу́бы, рот (lips, mouth), <b>чело́</b> = лоб (brow, forehead), <b>перст</b> = па́лец (finger), <b>ла́ниты</b> = щёки (cheeks), <b>длань</b> = ладо́нь, рука́ (palm, hand).","And they don't just mean the body part — they <b>carry</b> it lifted, sanctified. «о́чи» aren't eyes but eyes raised to heaven; «уста́» is the mouth that prays or speaks truth. Say «протяну́л длань» about a friend passing the salt and you've made a joke — the solemnity crashes into the everyday."],"audio":"The classics keep a whole parallel vocabulary, especially for the body and for high feeling. A native reads it without a dictionary and hears the solemnity underneath. These are poetizmy, poetic words, and tserkovnoslavyanizmy, Church-Slavonic words — high, old, used now only in verse, solemn prose, or irony. The body shelf: 'ochi' equals 'glaza', eyes; 'usta' equals 'guby, rot', lips and mouth; 'chelo' equals 'lob', brow; 'perst' equals 'palets', finger; 'lanity' equals 'shchyoki', cheeks; 'dlan' equals 'ladon, ruka', palm or hand. And they don't just mean the body part — they carry it lifted, sanctified. 'Ochi' aren't eyes but eyes raised to heaven; 'usta' is the mouth that prays or speaks truth. Say 'protyanul dlan' about a friend passing the salt and you've made a joke — the solemnity crashes into the everyday.","table":{"rows":[["о́чи → глаза́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"очи\">🔊</button>","eyes — but lifted, solemn, poetic"],["уста́ → гу́бы, рот <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уста\">🔊</button>","lips / mouth — the mouth that speaks truth or prays"],["чело́ → лоб <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чело\">🔊</button>","brow, forehead — noble, thoughtful"],["перст → па́лец <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"перст\">🔊</button>","finger — survives in «перст судьбы́», the finger of fate"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"A real proverb that keeps two archaisms alive: уста́ (mouth) and глаго́лет (speaks).","en":"Уста́ми младе́нца глаго́лет и́стина.","say":"Устами младенца глаголет истина."},{"ru":"He raised his eyes to heaven — «о́чи», not «глаза́», makes the gesture solemn.","en":"Он торже́ственно подня́л о́чи к не́бу.","say":"Он торжественно поднял очи к небу."},{"ru":"«the finger of fate» — «перст» (finger) survives only in this frozen, weighty phrase.","en":"В э́том он уви́дел перст судьбы́.","say":"В этом он увидел перст судьбы."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Переда́й мне соль свое́й дла́нью».","right":"«Переда́й мне соль руко́й».","why":"«длань» is a solemn, poetic «hand» — for oaths and prayers, not the dinner table; over a saltshaker it turns unintentionally comic. Neutral is «рука́» / «ладо́нь». Reading «длань» in a classic: fine. Using it plainly: a register crash."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The old, poetic word for «eyes» that a classic lifts «к не́бу» — type it:","accept":["очи"],"placeholder":"о…","explain":"<b>о́чи</b> — eyes, but solemn and poetic. Modern neutral: глаза́."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он торже́ственно подня́л … к не́бу.» — in solemn nineteenth-century narration, which is exact?","opts":["глаза́","о́чи","гляде́лки"],"answer":1,"explain":"Solemn, lifted → <b>о́чи</b>. глаза́ is neutral (flattens the tone); гляде́лки is jokey slang (crashes it)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The long sentence of the century","title":"Old <em>syntax</em>: inversion, the piled clause, the classic period","paras":["The bigger obstacle isn't the words — it's word order and sentence length. Nineteenth-century authors invert freely (object or adverb before the verb, adjective after its noun), stack subordinate clauses, and hold one sentence for half a page. It's the период you built last week, only denser.","Read for the skeleton: find the <b>граммати́ческая осно́ва</b> (the grammatical core — subject + verb), and let everything else hang off it. Inversion like «пришёл он по́здно» or «челове́к он был до́брый» is normal old (and still emphatic modern) order, not a mistake to patch.","The joinery looks old too: «дабы́» (= что́бы, so that), «е́жели», «ко́ли» (= е́сли, if), «и́бо» (= потому́ что). The whole phrasing feels weighed, unhurried, formal — that's the century speaking, not an error."],"audio":"The bigger obstacle isn't the words — it's word order and sentence length. Nineteenth-century authors invert freely — object or adverb before the verb, adjective after its noun — stack subordinate clauses, and hold one sentence for half a page. It's the period you built last week, only denser. Read for the skeleton: find the grammaticheskaya osnova, the grammatical core, subject plus verb, and let everything else hang off it. Inversion like 'prishyol on pozdno' or 'chelovek on byl dobryy' is normal old, and still emphatic modern, order — not a mistake to patch. The joinery looks old too: 'daby' equals 'chtoby', so that; 'ezheli', 'koli' equal 'esli', if; 'ibo' equals 'potomu chto'. The whole phrasing feels weighed, unhurried, formal — that's the century speaking, not an error.","table":{"rows":[["Челове́к он был до́брый. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"человек он был добрый\">🔊</button>","«A kind man he was» — noun fronted for emphasis"],["Пришёл он по́здно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пришёл он поздно\">🔊</button>","verb before subject — old / emphatic order"],["дабы́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дабы\">🔊</button>","= что́бы (so that) — archaic purpose conjunction"],["е́жели, ко́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ежели, коли\">🔊</button>","= е́сли (if) — dated / folksy conditional"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Достоевский, the first sentence of «Crime and Punishment» — three stacked phrases before the man even appears. Find the core: «челове́к вы́шел».","en":"В нача́ле ию́ля, в чрезвыча́йно жа́ркое вре́мя, под ве́чер, оди́н молодо́й челове́к вы́шел из свое́й камо́рки на у́лицу.","say":"В начале июля, в чрезвычайно жаркое время, под вечер, один молодой человек вышел из своей каморки на улицу."},{"ru":"Гоголь, «Dead Souls» — a rhetorical question in the grand old voice; word order and rhythm are the whole point.","en":"И како́й же ру́сский не лю́бит бы́строй езды́?","say":"И какой же русский не любит быстрой езды?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Челове́к, он был до́брый».","right":"«Челове́к он был до́брый».","why":"The inverted «Челове́к он был до́брый» (a kind man, he was) is correct, emphatic nineteenth-century — and still-alive colloquial — order, not a broken run-on to fix with a comma. Read for the core «он был до́брый» and let the fronted «челове́к» carry the emphasis. Misreading old inversion as error is the C2 stumble."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In «В нача́ле ию́ля … оди́н молодо́й челове́к вы́шел…», the reader's job is to —","opts":["find the core «челове́к вы́шел» and let the phrases hang off it","translate every phrase before reading on","assume Достое́вский forgot the verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"Locate the граммати́ческая осно́ва — «челове́к вы́шел» — and the three front phrases fall into place as scene-setting. That's how you read a long period without drowning."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the old-style clause.","q":"Build the archaic purpose clause: «so that I would know» (old style, with «дабы́»):","want":"дабы́ я знал","answer":"дабы́ я знал"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · How to read it — and the week ahead","title":"Don't decode — <em>recognise the flavour</em> and glide","paras":["The method is one habit. When an old word or twist appears, don't stop to translate it — <b>recognise its flavour</b> (archaic? poetic? the officialese of 1850?), register that flavour, and read on. The meaning almost always arrives from the sentence around it; the flavour was the author's whole intent.","And notice what archaisms <b>carry</b>: age, solemnity, distance — and, dropped into a modern sentence on purpose, <b>irony</b>. A writer today reaches for «сей» or «о́чи» only as a deliberate, mock-grand wink («в сей нелёгкий час»). Catching that wink — knowing it's a costume, not a mistake — is pure C2.","The week ahead climbs to mastery. Tomorrow: <b>verse</b> — meter, rhyme, and the poetic licence that bends word order even further (день 23). Then the professional genres — writing a <b>scholarly article</b> (24) and the editor's craft of stylistic <b>правка</b> (25). By Friday you'll read, and write, essentially anything."],"audio":"The method is one habit. When an old word or twist appears, don't stop to translate it — recognise its flavour: archaic? poetic? the officialese of 1850? Register that flavour, and read on. The meaning almost always arrives from the sentence around it; the flavour was the author's whole intent. And notice what archaisms carry: age, solemnity, distance — and, dropped into a modern sentence on purpose, irony. A writer today reaches for 'sey' or 'ochi' only as a deliberate, mock-grand wink — 'v sey nelyogkiy chas', in this trying hour. Catching that wink, knowing it's a costume and not a mistake, is pure C2. The week ahead climbs to mastery. Tomorrow: verse — meter, rhyme, and the poetic licence that bends word order even further, day 23. Then the professional genres — writing a scholarly article, 24, and the editor's craft of stylistic pravka, 25. By Friday you'll read, and write, essentially anything.","examples":[{"ru":"«сей» dropped into modern speech on purpose — a mock-solemn wink, «in this trying hour».","en":"И что же вы предлага́ете в сей нелёгкий час?","say":"И что же вы предлагаете в сей нелёгкий час?"},{"ru":"«to petition» — literally «to beat one's brow»; the archaism «чело́» survives frozen in the idiom.","en":"Крестья́не пришли́ би́ть чело́м царю́.","say":"Крестьяне пришли бить челом царю."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В сей рестора́н мы хо́дим ка́ждую пя́тницу».","right":"«В э́тот рестора́н мы хо́дим ка́ждую пя́тницу».","why":"Plain modern speech takes «э́тот». «сей» here isn't broken grammar — it's a fossil worn straight-faced, so it reads either as a foreigner's slip or as unmotivated mock-grandeur. Keep «сей» for the deliberate ironic wink; the default is «э́тот»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A modern writer drops «о́чи» or «сей» into a plain sentence on purpose. Most often it signals —","opts":["a deliberate ironic, mock-solemn wink","that they don't know modern Russian","a spelling reform"],"answer":0,"explain":"Used on purpose, an archaism is a costume — a mock-grand, ironic wink. Reading it straight (missing the irony) is the classic C2 stumble."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it aloud, letting the old words ring solemn.","target":"Уста́ми младе́нца глаго́лет и́стина.","sub":"let «уста́ми» and «глаго́лет» sound weighty — the archaisms carry the solemnity","want":"устами младенца глаголет истина"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"A word that's old and gone from living speech is an … . Type the Russian noun (hint: арх…):","accept":["архаизм","архаизмы"],"placeholder":"арх…","explain":"<b>архаи́зм</b> — an archaism. The C2 skill is reading it without a dictionary and hearing what it carries."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is reading Достое́вского in the original at Ле́на's café. Ка́тя, curious, peeks at the old page. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты чита́ешь э́то без слова́рика? Тут же «сей», «дабы́»…","ru":"You're reading this without a dictionary? It's full of «сей», «дабы́»…"},{"who":"A","en":"Не перевожу́ ка́ждое сло́во. Я про́сто слы́шу, что оно́ ста́рое, — и чита́ю да́льше.","ru":"I don't translate each word. I just hear that it's old — and read on."},{"who":"B","en":"А «о́чи», «уста́» — э́то же не глаза́ и гу́бы?","ru":"And «о́чи», «уста́» — those aren't just eyes and lips?"},{"who":"A","en":"По смы́слу — они́. Но они́ несу́т торже́ственность: не глаза́, а о́чи, по́днятые к не́бу.","ru":"In meaning — yes. But they carry solemnity: not eyes, but о́чи lifted to heaven."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, е́сли я скажу́ подру́ге «твои́ уста́ прекра́сны»…","ru":"So if I tell a friend «your уста́ are beautiful»…"},{"who":"A","en":"…она́ реши́т, что ты шу́тишь. В жи́зни — гу́бы. О́чи и уста́ — для стихо́в и́ли для иро́нии.","ru":"…she'll decide you're joking. In real life — гу́бы. О́чи and уста́ are for verse or for irony."},{"who":"B","en":"А «ве́сьма»? То́же музе́й?","ru":"And «ве́сьма»? Also a museum piece?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, «ве́сьма» — живо́е сло́во, про́сто кни́жное. Вот э́то и есть чутьё: что умерло́, а что про́сто вы́сокое.","ru":"No, «ве́сьма» is a living word, just bookish. That's the whole feel: what's died, and what's merely elevated."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the nineteenth-century page: the archaic shelf plus the tools for reading it. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"архаи́зм","ru":"an archaism (a word gone from living speech)","ex":"В э́том рома́не мно́го архаи́змов."},{"en":"о́чи","ru":"eyes (poetic, solemn)","ex":"Она́ подняла́ о́чи к не́бу."},{"en":"уста́","ru":"lips, mouth (poetic, elevated)","ex":"Его́ уста́ шепта́ли моли́тву."},{"en":"чело́","ru":"brow, forehead (elevated)","ex":"Высо́кое чело́ выдава́ло мысли́теля."},{"en":"сей","ru":"this (archaic = э́тот; alive in «на сей раз»)","ex":"На сей раз ему́ повезло́."},{"en":"дабы́","ru":"so that, in order to (archaic = что́бы)","ex":"Он спеши́л, дабы́ успе́ть к сро́ку."},{"en":"ве́сьма","ru":"very, highly (bookish, alive)","ex":"Докла́д ве́сьма любопы́тен."},{"en":"и́бо","ru":"for, because (bookish, alive)","ex":"Он молча́л, и́бо не́чего бы́ло сказа́ть."},{"en":"перст","ru":"finger (archaic; «перст судьбы́»)","ex":"В э́том он уви́дел перст судьбы́."},{"en":"неадапти́рованный","ru":"unadapted (original, unsimplified)","ex":"Она́ чита́ет кла́ссику в неадапти́рованном ви́де."},{"en":"инве́рсия","ru":"inversion (reversed word order)","ex":"Инве́рсия де́лает фра́зу торже́ственной."},{"en":"пери́од","ru":"a period (a long, balanced sentence)","ex":"Толсто́вский пери́од тя́нется на полстрани́цы."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each archaism to its plain modern twin — the everyday word that took its place.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each old word on the left onto its modern twin — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["о́чи","eyes (глаза́)"],["уста́","lips, mouth (гу́бы, рот)"],["чело́","brow, forehead (лоб)"],["сей","this (э́тот)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"You hit «сей», «дабы́», «о́чи» in unadapted Достое́вского. The C2 move is to —","opts":["recognise them as archaic and read on","start using them in your own texts","assume the book is misprinted"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which word is <b>still alive</b> today, just bookish — not a dead archaism?","opts":["дабы́","ве́сьма","о́чи"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он торже́ственно подня́л … к не́бу.» — in solemn narration, which is exact?","opts":["глаза́","о́чи","зе́нки"],"answer":1},{"q":"The plain modern twin of «уста́» is —","opts":["у́ши","гу́бы","но́ги"],"answer":1},{"q":"A writer today drops «сей» into a plain modern line. Usually it signals —","opts":["a deliberate ironic, mock-solemn wink","a grammar error","a regional dialect"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's own notes on the evening she stopped needing footnotes. The half-page sentence she quotes is the real opening of Dostoevsky's «Crime and Punishment». Read each line aloud and feel the old Russian settle.","title":"Сей стра́нный, ста́рый ру́сский","sentences":["Э́мма откры́ла Достое́вского — настоя́щее изда́ние, без примеча́ний и без адапта́ции.","Пе́рвая фра́за тяну́лась на полстрани́цы: «В нача́ле ию́ля… оди́н молодо́й челове́к вы́шел из свое́й камо́рки на у́лицу».","Слова́ бы́ли ей знако́мы, но поря́док их — стари́нный, ва́жный, неторопли́вый.","Тут и там мелька́ли «сей», «дабы́», «о́чи» — и Э́мма уже́ не спотыка́лась о них.","Она́ не переводи́ла их — она́ слы́шала, что они́ ста́рые, и скользи́ла да́льше.","И тогда́ давно́ уме́рший язы́к вдруг зазвуча́л — торже́ственно, чу́ждо и жи́во."],"translation":"Emma opened Dostoevsky — a real edition, no notes, nothing adapted. The first sentence stretched half a page: «In early July… a young man stepped out of his cupboard of a room into the street» (the opening of «Crime and Punishment»). The words were familiar to her, but their order was old, weighty, unhurried. Here and there flickered «сей», «дабы́», «о́чи» — and Emma no longer stumbled on them. She didn't translate them — she heard that they were old, and glided on. And then a long-dead language suddenly sounded — solemn, alien, and alive."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to step into the nineteenth century.","prompt":"Take one plain modern sentence of your own — about your morning, in neutral Russian. Then rewrite the SAME sentence «под старину́» (in mock-nineteenth-century style): swap in one or two archaisms (сей, о́чи, уста́, дабы́, ве́сьма) and invert the word order once. Finally, in one line, name what the old version <b>carries</b> that the modern one doesn't. 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Don't archaise everything — one or two old words and a single inversion already change the whole flavour. Ask the C2 question: what does «о́чи» carry that «глаза́» doesn't — solemnity? distance? a wink? 🙂","example":"Нейтра́льно: Сего́дня у́тром я откры́ла глаза́ и посмотре́ла в окно́. Под старину́: В сие́ у́тро отве́рзла я о́чи свои́ и обрати́ла взор к окну́. «О́чи» и «отве́рзла» несу́т торже́ственность и лёгкую иро́нию, кото́рых нет в про́стом «откры́ла глаза́». Одно́ ста́рое сло́во — и бу́дничное у́тро вдруг звучи́т как строка́ из кла́ссики."}},"23":{"day":23,"week":"04","themeRu":"Reading verse","themeEn":"Поэ́зия: ритм и во́льность","intro":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил…» Read that flat, as prose, and it's a mild remark about an uncle. Read it as Эмма reads it now — feeling the pulse under the words, the rhyme waiting at the line's end, the word order tipped on purpose — and it's the opening of «Евгений Онегин», the most quoted line in the language. Verse asks a different ear than prose: it keeps a beat, it rhymes, and it earns the right to bend syntax and reach for words no one says out loud anymore. Today you stop reading poems as sentences with odd line breaks — and start hearing them.","introAudio":"'Moy dyadya samykh chestnykh pravil…' Read that flat, as prose, and it's a mild remark about an uncle. Read it as Emma reads it now — feeling the pulse under the words, the rhyme waiting at the line's end, the word order tipped on purpose — and it's the opening of 'Yevgeniy Onegin', the most quoted line in the language. Verse asks a different ear than prose: it keeps a beat, it rhymes, and it earns the right to bend syntax and reach for words no one says out loud anymore. Today you stop reading poems as sentences with odd line breaks — and start hearing them.","goals":["Read Russian verse <b>as verse</b> — for its beat and line, not as prose with odd breaks","Hear the two commonest meters by ear: <b>ямб</b> (da-DUM) and <b>хоре́й</b> (DUM-da)","Recognise the rhyme schemes: <b>перекрёстная</b> (а-б-а-б), <b>па́рная</b> (а-а-б-б), <b>кольцева́я</b> (а-б-б-а)","Accept poetic licence: inversion (<b>Петра́ творе́нье</b>) and поэти́змы (<b>брег, зла́то, глас, о́чи</b>)"],"learned":["Read verse for its beat and line, not as broken prose","Heard ямб (da-DUM) and хоре́й (DUM-da) by ear","Named the rhyme schemes: перекрёстная, па́рная, кольцева́я","Accepted poetic licence — inversion and поэти́змы (брег, зла́то, глас)"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday's classics — the archaic shelf (о́чи, уста́, сей) and the long, inverted period. Verse leans on both, so keep them warm.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday's classics — the archaic shelf (ochi, usta, sey) and the long, inverted period. Verse leans on both, so keep them warm.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — yesterday's classics.","q":"Yesterday's rule: «сей», «дабы́», «о́чи» are —","opts":["dead archaisms — read them, don't use them straight","modern slang","spelling mistakes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dead archaisms: perfect on a nineteenth-century page, out of place in your own speech unless you mean them as a wink."},{"type":"choice","q":"The plain modern twin of «о́чи» is —","opts":["у́ши","глаза́","ру́ки"],"answer":1,"explain":"«о́чи» = <b>глаза́</b> (eyes), but lifted and solemn. Verse will use «о́чи» precisely for that lift."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the old-style inversion.","q":"Build the emphatic, old-style inversion: «A kind man he was.»","want":"Челове́к он был до́брый","answer":"Челове́к он был до́брый"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A poem is not a paragraph","title":"Read for the <em>line</em> and the <em>beat</em> — not the sentence","paras":["Your prose habit is to chase the sentence and ignore where lines break. Verse punishes that. The <b>строка́</b> (line) is a unit in its own right; a single sentence may spill across several lines (<b>перено́с</b>, enjambment); and the whole thing rides a steady beat you're meant to feel.","Three things a poem has that prose doesn't: a <b>метр</b> (a fixed pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables), <b>ри́фма</b> (rhyme, usually at the line's end), and the licence to bend word order and word choice to serve both.","So you read it differently: feel the pulse, honour the line-end, and expect the syntax to tip and rare words to surface. Read a poem as flat prose and you strip out exactly what makes it a poem."],"audio":"Your prose habit is to chase the sentence and ignore where lines break. Verse punishes that. The stroka, the line, is a unit in its own right; a single sentence may spill across several lines — perenos, enjambment — and the whole thing rides a steady beat you're meant to feel. Three things a poem has that prose doesn't: a metr, a fixed pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables; rifma, rhyme, usually at the line's end; and the licence to bend word order and word choice to serve both. So you read it differently: feel the pulse, honour the line-end, and expect the syntax to tip and rare words to surface. Read a poem as flat prose and you strip out exactly what makes it a poem.","examples":[{"ru":"Read as prose it's a flat remark; read as verse it keeps a beat and a rhyme waiting at the end.","en":"Стихи́ живу́т ри́тмом и ри́фмой, а не то́лько смы́слом.","say":"Стихи живут ритмом и рифмой, а не только смыслом."},{"ru":"When a sentence runs on past the line's end, that's перено́с (enjambment) — hold the line, then continue.","en":"Фра́за иногда́ перехо́дит из строки́ в строку́ — э́то перено́с.","say":"Фраза иногда переходит из строки в строку — это перенос."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил когда́ не в шу́тку занемо́г он уважа́ть себя́ заста́вил».","right":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил, / когда́ не в шу́тку занемо́г, / он уважа́ть себя́ заста́вил…»","why":"Run together as one prose line, the beat and the rhyme (пра́вил–заста́вил) vanish. Verse is read line by line, with the pulse and the pause at each line-end; the line is a unit, not an accident of the page width."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"To read a poem well, you should —","opts":["read for the line and its beat, accepting bent word order","ignore the line breaks and read it as one paragraph","translate every word to English before reading on"],"answer":0,"explain":"The line and the beat are the poem's body. Honour them, and the inversions and rare words fall into place instead of tripping you."},{"type":"choice","q":"A sentence that runs on past the end of a line into the next is —","opts":["перено́с (enjambment)","ри́фма (rhyme)","оши́бка (a mistake)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>перено́с</b> (enjambment) — the syntax crosses the line-end. Hold the line, then let the sentence finish; it's a device, not a slip."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Two beats you can hear","title":"<em>Ямб</em> is da-DUM; <em>хоре́й</em> is DUM-da","paras":["You don't need to scan on paper — you need an ear. Two meters cover most of the classics. <b>Ямб</b> (iamb): the stress lands on the <b>even</b> beats — da-DUM da-DUM. <b>Хоре́й</b> (trochee): stress on the <b>odd</b> beats — DUM-da DUM-da.","Tap it out. «Мой дя́-дя са́-мых че́ст-ных пра́-вил» — the taps land 2-4-6-8: <b>ямб</b>, four feet (четырёхсто́пный ямб), the meter of «Онегин». «Бу́-ря мгло́-ю не́-бо кро́-ет» — taps on 1-3-5-7: <b>хоре́й</b>.","The unit of the beat is the <b>стопа́</b> (foot). Naming the meter matters less than <i>feeling</i> it: once you catch the pulse, the odd word order stops feeling wrong — it's just the line keeping time."],"audio":"You don't need to scan on paper — you need an ear. Two meters cover most of the classics. Yamb, the iamb: the stress lands on the even beats — da-DUM da-DUM. Khorey, the trochee: stress on the odd beats — DUM-da DUM-da. Tap it out. 'Moy dya-dya sa-mykh che-stnykh pra-vil' — the taps land two, four, six, eight: yamb, four feet, chetyryohstopnyy yamb, the meter of Onegin. 'Bu-rya mglo-yu ne-bo kro-yet' — taps on one, three, five, seven: khorey. The unit of the beat is the stopa, the foot. Naming the meter matters less than feeling it: once you catch the pulse, the odd word order stops feeling wrong — it's just the line keeping time.","table":{"rows":[["Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мой дядя самых честных правил\">🔊</button>","da-DUM da-DUM… — <b>ямб</b> (iamb), stress on even beats (Пу́шкин)"],["Бу́ря мгло́ю не́бо кро́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"буря мглою небо кроет\">🔊</button>","DUM-da DUM-da… — <b>хоре́й</b> (trochee), stress on odd beats (Пу́шкин)"],["стопа́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стопа\">🔊</button>","a foot — one unit of the beat (two syllables here)"],["четырёхсто́пный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"четырёхстопный\">🔊</button>","four-foot — four beats to the line"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Пушкин, «Eugene Onegin» — iambic tetrameter, the most famous opening in Russian verse.","en":"Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил, когда́ не в шу́тку занемо́г.","say":"Мой дядя самых честных правил, когда не в шутку занемог."},{"ru":"Пушкин, «The Tale of Tsar Saltan» — trochaic tetrameter; feel the DUM-da rock of it.","en":"Три деви́цы под окно́м пря́ли по́здно вечерко́м.","say":"Три девицы под окном пряли поздно вечерком."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил» — хоре́й.","right":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил» — ямб.","why":"The taps fall on the even syllables (дя́-, са́-, че́-, пра́-) — that's da-DUM, <b>ямб</b>, not DUM-da. Mixing up the two is the commonest beginner slip; trust where your finger lands."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил» — stress on the even beats (da-DUM). The meter is —","opts":["хоре́й","ямб","верли́бр"],"answer":1,"explain":"Even-beat stress, da-DUM → <b>ямб</b> (iamb). хоре́й is DUM-da; верли́бр (free verse) has no fixed beat at all."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Бу́ря мгло́ю не́бо кро́ет» — stress on the odd beats (DUM-da). The meter is —","opts":["ямб","хоре́й","про́за"],"answer":1,"explain":"Odd-beat stress, DUM-da → <b>хоре́й</b> (trochee). It rocks; the iamb rises."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Rhyme and the shape of a stanza","title":"How lines pair off: <em>перекрёстная, па́рная, кольцева́я</em>","paras":["Rhyme (ри́фма) usually sits at the line ends and binds lines into a <b>строфа́</b> (stanza). Three patterns cover most classic verse. <b>Перекрёстная</b> (cross): а-б-а-б — lines 1 & 3, 2 & 4. <b>Па́рная</b> / сме́жная (couplet): а-а-б-б — neighbours rhyme. <b>Кольцева́я</b> (envelope): а-б-б-а — the outer pair hugs the inner.","Rhyme also has «genders»: <b>мужска́я</b> (masculine — stress on the last syllable: «окно́ — давно́») and <b>же́нская</b> (feminine — stress on the second-to-last: «пра́вил — заста́вил»). You needn't name them; hearing which it is tells you where the line's beat settles.","Пушкин even invented a whole 14-line stanza for «Онегин» (the <b>онегинская строфа́</b>). You needn't memorise its scheme — just know a stanza has a designed shape, and the rhyme is its architecture."],"audio":"Rhyme, rifma, usually sits at the line ends and binds lines into a strofa, a stanza. Three patterns cover most classic verse. Perekryostnaya, cross rhyme: a-b-a-b, lines one and three, two and four. Parnaya, or smezhnaya, the couplet: a-a-b-b, neighbours rhyme. Koltsevaya, the envelope: a-b-b-a, the outer pair hugs the inner. Rhyme also has genders: muzhskaya, masculine, stress on the last syllable, 'okno — davno'; and zhenskaya, feminine, stress on the second-to-last, 'pravil — zastavil'. You needn't name them; hearing which it is tells you where the line's beat settles. Pushkin even invented a whole fourteen-line stanza for Onegin, the oneginskaya strofa. You needn't memorise its scheme — just know a stanza has a designed shape, and the rhyme is its architecture.","table":{"rows":[["перекрёстная: а-б-а-б <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"перекрёстная рифма\">🔊</button>","cross rhyme — lines 1 & 3, 2 & 4 (the most common)"],["па́рная: а-а-б-б <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"парная рифма\">🔊</button>","couplet — neighbouring lines rhyme"],["кольцева́я: а-б-б-а <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кольцевая рифма\">🔊</button>","envelope — the outer pair hugs the inner"],["строфа́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"строфа\">🔊</button>","a stanza — lines bound into a unit by rhyme"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Pushkin, «Eugene Onegin» — a cross rhyme (а-б-а-б): line 1 rhymes with line 3, and line 2 with line 4.","en":"Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил, когда́ не в шу́тку занемо́г, он уважа́ть себя́ заста́вил и лу́чше вы́думать не мог.","say":"Мой дядя самых честных правил, когда не в шутку занемог, он уважать себя заставил и лучше выдумать не мог."},{"ru":"Pushkin — a couplet (па́рная rhyme): the two neighbouring lines chime with each other.","en":"Три деви́цы под окно́м пря́ли по́здно вечерко́м.","say":"Три девицы под окном пряли поздно вечерком."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«пра́вил — заста́вил»: мужска́я ри́фма.","right":"«пра́вил — заста́вил»: же́нская ри́фма.","why":"The stress sits on the second-to-last syllable (пра́-вил, заста́-вил), so it's a <b>же́нская</b> (feminine) rhyme. Masculine (мужска́я) rhymes end on a stressed final syllable, like «окно́ — давно́»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Lines 1 & 3 rhyme, and lines 2 & 4 — the pattern а-б-а-б. That's —","opts":["перекрёстная ри́фма","па́рная ри́фма","кольцева́я ри́фма"],"answer":0,"explain":"а-б-а-б is <b>перекрёстная</b> (cross rhyme) — the commonest scheme in Russian verse, the one «Онегин» rides."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The Russian word for a stanza — a group of lines bound by rhyme (hint: стр…):","accept":["строфа","строфы"],"placeholder":"стр…","explain":"<b>строфа́</b> — a stanza. Пушкин's famous 14-line one is the онегинская строфа́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Poetic licence — and the week ahead","title":"Verse earns the right to <em>bend</em>: inversion and поэти́змы","paras":["To keep the meter and the rhyme, poets take liberties prose never would — <b>поэти́ческая во́льность</b> (poetic licence). The two big ones: <b>inversion</b> (word order flipped — «Петра́ творе́нье» for «творе́нье Петра́», Peter's creation) and <b>поэти́змы</b> (elevated, often archaic words: «брег» for бе́рег, «зла́то» for зо́лото, «глас» for го́лос, «о́чи», «вне́мли», «виждь»).","These aren't errors and they aren't random — each is chosen for the beat, the rhyme, and the lift. «Петра́ творе́нье» sits in the line and rings grander than plain «творе́нье Петра́». Read it as prose and you'd «correct» it; read it as verse and you hear why the poet flipped it.","The C2 move: accept the bend, feel what it buys (solemnity, emphasis, music), and read the poem aloud with the beat and the pause at each line-end (<b>каданс</b>, cadence). The week ahead turns to writing: tomorrow the <b>scholarly article</b> and те́зисы (день 24), then the editor's craft of stylistic <b>правка</b> (день 25). From reading anything — to writing anything."],"audio":"To keep the meter and the rhyme, poets take liberties prose never would — poeticheskaya volnost, poetic licence. The two big ones: inversion, word order flipped — 'Petra tvorenye' for 'tvorenye Petra', Peter's creation — and poetizmy, elevated, often archaic words: 'breg' for 'bereg', 'zlato' for 'zoloto', 'glas' for 'golos', 'ochi', 'vnemli', 'vizhd'. These aren't errors and they aren't random — each is chosen for the beat, the rhyme, and the lift. 'Petra tvorenye' sits in the line and rings grander than plain 'tvorenye Petra'. Read it as prose and you'd 'correct' it; read it as verse and you hear why the poet flipped it. The C2 move: accept the bend, feel what it buys — solemnity, emphasis, music — and read the poem aloud with the beat and the pause at each line-end, kadans, cadence. The week ahead turns to writing: tomorrow the scholarly article and tezisy, day 24, then the editor's craft of stylistic pravka, day 25. From reading anything — to writing anything.","examples":[{"ru":"Пушкин, «The Bronze Horseman» — inversion: «Петра́ творе́нье» (Peter's creation), fronted for grandeur and meter.","en":"Люблю́ тебя́, Петра́ творе́нье, люблю́ твой стро́гий, стро́йный вид.","say":"Люблю тебя, Петра творенье, люблю твой строгий, стройный вид."},{"ru":"Пушкин, «The Prophet» — «глаго́лом» (with the word) is a поэти́зм / archaism; the line commands the poet to set hearts ablaze with the word.","en":"Глаго́лом жги сердца́ люде́й.","say":"Глаголом жги сердца людей."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Люблю́ тебя́, творе́нье Петра́».","right":"«Люблю́ тебя́, Петра́ творе́нье».","why":"«Corrected» to plain order, the line loses its meter and its grandeur. In verse «Петра́ творе́нье» (inversion) is right on purpose — fronting «Петра́» lifts the tone and fits the beat. Don't un-invert verse."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In verse, «Петра́ творе́нье» instead of «творе́нье Петра́» is —","opts":["poetic inversion, chosen for meter and grandeur","a grammatical error","a typo"],"answer":0,"explain":"Inversion is poetic licence — chosen for the beat and the lift, not a mistake to fix. Un-inverting it flattens the line."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it aloud — keep the iambic beat and pause at the comma.","target":"Люблю́ тебя́, Петра́ творе́нье, люблю́ твой стро́гий, стро́йный вид.","sub":"let the inversion «Петра́ творе́нье» sound grand, and hold the beat","want":"люблю тебя петра творенье люблю твой строгий стройный вид"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Elevated, often archaic poetic words like «брег», «зла́то», «глас» are called … (hint: поэт…):","accept":["поэтизмы","поэтизм"],"placeholder":"поэт…","explain":"<b>поэти́змы</b> — poeticisms. They lift the line and often help the rhyme; hearing their flavour is the C2 skill."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is reading Пу́шкина aloud when Макси́м, her most no-nonsense engineer friend, frowns at the strange word order. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Чита́ю Пу́шкина — и не понима́ю, почему́ слова́ сто́ят так стра́нно.","ru":"I'm reading Pushkin — and I can't get why the words stand so oddly."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то не стра́нно — э́то стихи́. Поря́док слов подчиня́ется ри́тму, а не привы́чке.","ru":"It's not odd — it's verse. Word order obeys the rhythm, not habit."},{"who":"B","en":"«Петра́ творе́нье» — почему́ не «творе́нье Петра́»?","ru":"Take «Петра́ творе́нье» — why is it in that reversed order, and not the plain one?"},{"who":"A","en":"Инве́рсия. В стро́ку ло́жится ина́че — и зву́чит торже́ственнее.","ru":"Inversion. It sits differently in the line — and sounds more solemn."},{"who":"B","en":"А как ты слы́шишь, где ямб, а где хоре́й?","ru":"And how do you hear where it's iamb and where trochee?"},{"who":"A","en":"По уда́рам. «Мой дя́дя» — та-та́, та-та́: ямб. «Бу́ря» — та́-та, та́-та: хоре́й.","ru":"By the beats. «Мой дя́дя» — da-DUM: iamb. «Бу́ря» — DUM-da: trochee."},{"who":"B","en":"А «брег», «зла́то» — э́то оши́бки и́ли наро́чно?","ru":"And «брег», «зла́то» — are those errors or on purpose?"},{"who":"A","en":"Наро́чно. Э́то поэти́змы — высо́кие, ста́рые слова́. Стих и́ми ды́шит.","ru":"On purpose. They're poeticisms — high, old words. Verse breathes through them."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for reading verse — the machinery of meter and rhyme, plus a few poeticisms. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"стих","ru":"a line of verse; verse","ex":"В ка́ждом стихе́ свой ритм и своя́ ри́фма."},{"en":"строфа́","ru":"a stanza","ex":"Стихотворе́ние из четырёх строф."},{"en":"ритм","ru":"rhythm","ex":"Ритм — э́то бие́ние стро́ки."},{"en":"метр","ru":"meter (the stress pattern)","ex":"Метр — схе́ма уда́рных и безуда́рных сло́гов."},{"en":"ямб","ru":"iamb (da-DUM, stress on even beats)","ex":"«Мой дя́дя» напи́сан я́мбом."},{"en":"хоре́й","ru":"trochee (DUM-da, stress on odd beats)","ex":"«Бу́ря мгло́ю» — э́то хоре́й."},{"en":"ри́фма","ru":"rhyme","ex":"Перекрёстная ри́фма: а-б-а-б."},{"en":"стопа́","ru":"a metrical foot","ex":"В строке́ четы́ре стопы́ — четырёхсто́пный ямб."},{"en":"инве́рсия","ru":"inversion (reversed word order)","ex":"Инве́рсия — душа́ поэти́ческой ре́чи."},{"en":"поэти́зм","ru":"a poeticism (elevated poetic word)","ex":"«Брег» и «зла́то» — поэти́змы."},{"en":"во́льность","ru":"licence, liberty (poetic licence)","ex":"Поэти́ческая во́льность гнёт поря́док слов."},{"en":"внима́ть","ru":"to heed, hearken (elevated; source of «вне́мли»)","ex":"Внима́ть стиха́м — не то́ же, что чита́ть газе́ту."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each поэти́зм to the plain modern word it stands in for.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each poetic word on the left onto its everyday twin — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["брег","shore (бе́рег)"],["зла́то","gold — the modern зо́лото"],["глас","voice (го́лос)"],["младо́й","young — the modern молодо́й"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Verse breaks a prose habit. To read it well you —","opts":["read for the line and its beat, accepting bent word order","ignore the line breaks and read it as a paragraph","translate every word to English first"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил» — stress on the even beats. That meter is —","opts":["ямб (iamb)","хоре́й (trochee)","про́за"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Бу́ря мгло́ю не́бо кро́ет» — stress on the odd beats. That meter is —","opts":["ямб","хоре́й","ри́фма"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Люблю́ тебя́, Петра́ творе́нье» puts «Петра́» before «творе́нье». This reversed order is —","opts":["a grammatical mistake","poetic inversion — normal in verse","a misprint"],"answer":1},{"q":"«брег», «зла́то», «глас» instead of «бе́рег», «зо́лото», «го́лос» are —","opts":["spelling errors","поэти́змы — elevated poetic words","regional slang"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"The real opening stanza of Пу́шкин's «Зимнее утро» (Winter Morning, 1829) — iambic tetrameter throughout. Read it aloud line by line, keeping the beat and pausing at each line-end.","title":"Моро́з и со́лнце; день чуде́сный!","sentences":["Моро́з и со́лнце; день чуде́сный!","Ещё ты дре́млешь, друг преле́стный —","Пора́, краса́вица, просни́сь:","Откро́й сомкну́ты не́гой взо́ры","Навстре́чу се́верной Авро́ры,","Звездо́ю се́вера яви́сь!"],"translation":"«Frost and sun; a wondrous day! / Still you doze, my lovely friend — / It's time, my beauty, wake: / Open your eyes, sealed shut by bliss, / To meet the northern Aurora, / As the star of the north appear!» — the opening of Pushkin's «Winter Morning» (1829). Note the inversion «сомкну́ты не́гой взо́ры» (eyes sealed by bliss) and the poeticism «взо́ры» for «eyes»: read flat as prose it stumbles; read as verse, on the iambic beat, it sings."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to read like a poet — and to try a line yourself.","prompt":"Take a two-line fragment of Russian verse you like (or one from today — «Беле́ет па́рус одино́кий…»). In Russian: name its meter (ямб or хоре́й) and point to one poetic liberty in it — an inversion or a поэти́зм. Then attempt two lines of your own in the same meter, letting word order bend if the rhythm asks. 3–4 sentences, plus your two lines.","hint":"Tap out the beats with your finger: da-DUM = ямб, DUM-da = хоре́й. Don't force natural word order — verse has earned the right to invert, and a single поэти́зм («о́чи», «брег») can lift a whole line 🙂","example":"«Беле́ет па́рус одино́кий» — э́то четырёхсто́пный ямб: та-та́, та-та́, та-та́, та-та́. Инве́рсия «па́рус одино́кий» (не «одино́кий па́рус») ста́вит одино́чество в си́льную, уда́рную пози́цию. Мои́ две строки́: «Плывёт кора́блик по волне́, / И па́рус све́тится в огне́». Ри́фма па́рная — волне́ и огне́, — и я не «поправля́л» поря́док слов, а слу́шал ритм."}},"24":{"day":24,"week":"04","themeRu":"The scholarly article","themeEn":"Нау́чная статья́ и те́зисы","intro":"There's one phrase that instantly outs a foreigner in a Russian academic journal: «Я счита́ю, что я докажу́…» — «I believe I will prove…». The grammar is flawless. The register is wrong for the genre: Russian scholarship speaks not in the first person but impersonally — «в рабо́те пока́зано, что…», «it is shown in this work that…». This week you've read anything; now you <b>write</b> in the hardest register to fake — the нау́чная статья́ (scholarly article) and its те́зисы (abstract theses). Эмма does this for a living; today she hands you the blueprint.","introAudio":"There's one phrase that instantly outs a foreigner in a Russian academic journal: 'Ya schitayu, chto ya dokazhu…' — 'I believe I will prove…'. The grammar is flawless. The register is wrong for the genre: Russian scholarship speaks not in the first person but impersonally — 'v rabote pokazano, chto…', 'it is shown in this work that…'. This week you've read anything; now you write in the hardest register to fake — the nauchnaya statya, the scholarly article, and its tezisy, the abstract theses. Emma does this for a living; today she hands you the blueprint.","goals":["Lay out a Russian scholarly article by its parts: <b>аннота́ция, введе́ние, ме́тод, результа́ты, вы́воды</b>","Write in the impersonal, objective register — «в рабо́те пока́зано, что…», not «я счита́ю»","Repeat the <b>exact term</b> and use the nominal style научный prose expects — precision over variety","Cite a source and compress an argument into те́зисы (theses) and an аннота́ция (abstract)"],"learned":["Laid out a Russian article by its parts (аннота́ция → вы́воды)","Wrote in the impersonal register — «в рабо́те пока́зано», not «я счита́ю»","Kept the exact term and the nominal style of научный prose","Cited a source and compressed an argument into те́зисы"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on yesterday's verse — the two meters and poetic licence — before we swap the poet's freedom for the scientist's discipline.","introAudio":"One minute on yesterday's verse — the two meters and poetic licence — before we swap the poet's freedom for the scientist's discipline.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — yesterday's verse.","q":"«Мой дя́дя са́мых че́стных пра́вил» — the meter is —","opts":["ямб (da-DUM)","хоре́й (DUM-da)","верли́бр (free verse)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Even-beat stress, da-DUM → <b>ямб</b>. That's the meter of «Онегин»."},{"type":"choice","q":"In verse, «Петра́ творе́нье» instead of «творе́нье Петра́» is —","opts":["a grammatical error","poetic inversion — normal in verse","regional slang"],"answer":1,"explain":"Inversion is poetic licence, chosen for meter and grandeur. Today's genre is the opposite — no licence at all."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to un-invert the phrase.","q":"Build the plain, un-inverted order of «Петра́ творе́нье»:","want":"творе́нье Петра́","answer":"творе́нье Петра́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The shape of an article","title":"From <em>пробле́ма</em> to <em>вы́вод</em> — a fixed скеле́т","paras":["A Russian нау́чная статья́ isn't free-form; it has an expected skeleton and editors look for each part. The arc: <b>постано́вка пробле́мы</b> (state the problem — and its актуа́льность, why it matters now) → <b>обзо́р</b> (what's already known) → <b>ме́тод</b> (how you studied it) → <b>результа́ты</b> → <b>вы́воды / заключе́ние</b> (conclusions).","Wrapped around that arc: at the top an <b>аннота́ция</b> (a 3–5-sentence abstract) and <b>ключевы́е слова́</b> (keywords); at the bottom a <b>спи́сок литерату́ры</b> (reference list). The аннота́ция says, in miniature, what the whole article does.","Knowing the skeleton is half the register — each section has its own set phrases: «В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается…» to open, «Актуа́льность обусло́влена…» for relevance, «Таки́м о́бразом, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод…» to close."],"audio":"A Russian nauchnaya statya isn't free-form; it has an expected skeleton and editors look for each part. The arc: postanovka problemy, state the problem, and its aktualnost, why it matters now; then obzor, what's already known; then metod, how you studied it; then rezultaty; then vyvody or zaklyucheniye, the conclusions. Wrapped around that arc: at the top an annotatsiya, a three-to-five-sentence abstract, and klyuchevye slova, keywords; at the bottom a spisok literatury, the reference list. The annotatsiya says, in miniature, what the whole article does. Knowing the skeleton is half the register — each section has its own set phrases: 'V nastoyashchey statye rassmatrivaetsya…' to open, 'Aktualnost obuslovlena…' for relevance, 'Takim obrazom, mozhno sdelat vyvod…' to close.","table":{"rows":[["Аннота́ция + ключевы́е слова́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"аннотация, ключевые слова\">🔊</button>","abstract (3–5 sentences) + keywords — at the top"],["Введе́ние: постано́вка пробле́мы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"введение, постановка проблемы\">🔊</button>","the problem and its актуа́льность (relevance)"],["Ме́тод и результа́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"метод и результаты\">🔊</button>","how it was studied, and what was found"],["Вы́воды; спи́сок литерату́ры <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"выводы, список литературы\">🔊</button>","conclusions; then the reference list"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"A standard opener — «This article examines the problem of…»: impersonal, present tense, naming the topic.","en":"В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма перево́да безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики.","say":"В настоящей статье рассматривается проблема перевода безэквивалентной лексики."},{"ru":"A standard closer — «Thus, one may conclude that…».","en":"Таки́м о́бразом, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод о необходи́мости да́льнейшего иссле́дования.","say":"Таким образом, можно сделать вывод о необходимости дальнейшего исследования."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я хочу́ рассказа́ть о том, как я перево́жу слова́».","right":"«В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма перево́да безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики».","why":"A нау́чная статья́ doesn't open with «Я хочу́ рассказа́ть…» (I want to tell you about…) — that's a school essay. The genre opens impersonally, naming the пробле́ма and its актуа́льность: «В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается…»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"A Russian scholarly article is expected to move —","opts":["пробле́ма → обзо́р → ме́тод → результа́ты → вы́воды","straight to the author's personal opinion","in whatever order feels natural on the day"],"answer":0,"explain":"State the problem, review what's known, give the method, the results, the conclusions. Editors read for exactly that skeleton."},{"type":"choice","q":"The short 3–5-sentence summary at the top of an article is the —","opts":["спи́сок литерату́ры","аннота́ция","эпи́граф"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>аннота́ция</b> — the abstract: the whole article in miniature. спи́сок литерату́ры is the reference list; эпи́граф belongs to literature, not science."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Take yourself out of it","title":"Not «<em>я счита́ю</em>» but «<em>в рабо́те пока́зано, что</em>»","paras":["English scholarship is personal and assertive: «I argue that…», «In this paper I show…», «I believe…». Russian нау́чный style does the opposite — it hides the author and lets the work speak. The single loudest tell of an article translated from English is a parade of «я».","The tools: the <b>impersonal passive</b> — «в рабо́те пока́зано, что…» (it is shown in the work that…), «бы́ло устано́влено» (it was established), «рассма́тривается» (is examined); and the authorial <b>«мы»</b> — «мы полага́ем», «на́ми проведён ана́лиз» (we hold; the analysis was carried out by us) — a modest «we» even for a lone author.","Modality stays hedged and objective (last week's хеджи́рование, now inside a genre): «представля́ется целесообра́зным» (it appears reasonable), «сле́дует отме́тить» (it should be noted), «по-ви́димому». Not «я то́чно зна́ю» — научный prose claims with care."],"audio":"English scholarship is personal and assertive: 'I argue that…', 'In this paper I show…', 'I believe…'. Russian nauchnyy style does the opposite — it hides the author and lets the work speak. The single loudest tell of an article translated from English is a parade of 'ya', I. The tools: the impersonal passive — 'v rabote pokazano, chto…', it is shown in the work that; 'bylo ustanovleno', it was established; 'rassmatrivaetsya', is examined; and the authorial 'my', we — 'my polagaem', 'nami proveden analiz', the analysis was carried out by us — a modest 'we' even for a lone author. Modality stays hedged and objective — last week's khedzhirovaniye, now inside a genre: 'predstavlyaetsya tselesoobraznym', it appears reasonable; 'sleduet otmetit', it should be noted; 'po-vidimomu'. Not 'ya tochno znayu', I know for sure — nauchnyy prose claims with care.","table":{"rows":[["в рабо́те пока́зано, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в работе показано, что\">🔊</button>","«it is shown in this work that…» — impersonal passive"],["на́ми устано́влено, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нами установлено, что\">🔊</button>","«it was established by us…» — the authorial «мы»"],["сле́дует отме́тить, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следует отметить, что\">🔊</button>","«it should be noted that…» — impersonal, hedged"],["представля́ется целесообра́зным <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"представляется целесообразным\">🔊</button>","«it appears reasonable» — objective modality"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«This work shows that…» — the finding stated impersonally, the author invisible.","en":"В да́нной рабо́те пока́зано, что вы́бор сло́ва зави́сит от конте́кста.","say":"В данной работе показано, что выбор слова зависит от контекста."},{"ru":"«An analysis of a hundred translations was carried out by us» — the modest authorial «мы», not «я».","en":"На́ми был проведён ана́лиз ста перево́дов.","say":"Нами был проведён анализ ста переводов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я счита́ю, что я доказа́л свою́ гипо́тезу, и я ду́маю, что я прав».","right":"«В рабо́те пока́зано, что вы́двинутая гипо́теза подтвержда́ется».","why":"Four «я» in one line — flawless grammar, wrong genre. Russian нау́чный prose retreats behind «в рабо́те пока́зано» / «гипо́теза подтвержда́ется». The impersonal isn't modesty for its own sake; it's the register's core signal of objectivity."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a Russian scholarly article, «I have shown that the result holds» is best written —","opts":["Я показа́л, что результа́т ве́рен","В рабо́те пока́зано, что результа́т ве́рен","Я вам щас докажу́, что всё ве́рно"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>В рабо́те пока́зано, что…</b> — impersonal, objective, the научный norm. «Я показа́л» imports English rhetoric; «щас докажу́» is chat slang in the wrong universe entirely."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the one word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Fill the impersonal formula: «В рабо́те …, что…» (it is shown that…). Type the word (hint: пока…):","accept":["показано"],"placeholder":"пока…","explain":"«В рабо́те <b>пока́зано</b>, что…» — the impersonal passive that keeps the author out of the научный text."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The exact term, repeated","title":"In science, <em>repeat the term</em> — variety is a vice here","paras":["Everyday prose avoids repeating a word — you reach for synonyms. Нау́чный prose does the opposite: a <b>те́рмин</b> (term) is repeated exactly, every time, because swapping «перево́д» for «перело́жение» or «интерпрета́ция» would imply a <i>different</i> concept. Precision beats elegance; the same thing keeps the same name.","The style is also <b>именно́й</b> (nominal): it leans on nouns, especially <b>отглаго́льные существи́тельные</b> (verbal nouns) — «проведе́ние ана́лиза» (the carrying-out of analysis), «в це́лях выявле́ния» (for the purpose of revealing). In this genre that density is correct — compact and objective. (Tomorrow you'll meet the very same nominal style as a <i>vice</i> — канцеляри́т — outside science.)","Terms are defined once and then used unchanged; a new term gets a definition — «под X мы понима́ем…» («by X we mean…»). The reader must never have to wonder whether two words point to the same thing."],"audio":"Everyday prose avoids repeating a word — you reach for synonyms. Nauchnyy prose does the opposite: a termin, a term, is repeated exactly, every time, because swapping 'perevod' for 'perelozheniye' or 'interpretatsiya' would imply a different concept. Precision beats elegance; the same thing keeps the same name. The style is also imennoy, nominal: it leans on nouns, especially otglagolnye sushchestvitelnye, verbal nouns — 'provedeniye analiza', the carrying-out of analysis; 'v tselyakh vyyavleniya', for the purpose of revealing. In this genre that density is correct — compact and objective. Tomorrow you'll meet the very same nominal style as a vice, kantselyarit, outside science. Terms are defined once and then used unchanged; a new term gets a definition — 'pod iks my ponimaem…', by X we mean. The reader must never have to wonder whether two words point to the same thing.","table":{"rows":[["те́рмин — оди́н и тот же <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"термин один и тот же\">🔊</button>","repeat the exact term — don't «vary» it with synonyms"],["проведе́ние ана́лиза <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"проведение анализа\">🔊</button>","«the carrying-out of analysis» — nominal (a verbal noun)"],["в це́лях выявле́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в целях выявления\">🔊</button>","«for the purpose of revealing» — nominal, научный"],["под X мы понима́ем… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"под икс мы понимаем\">🔊</button>","«by X we mean…» — defining a term once"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«By connotation we mean…» — a term defined once, then reused unchanged.","en":"Под конно́тацией мы понима́ем скры́тую оце́ночную окра́ску сло́ва.","say":"Под коннотацией мы понимаем скрытую оценочную окраску слова."},{"ru":"«For the carrying-out of the analysis, a hundred contexts were selected» — nominal and impersonal at once.","en":"Для проведе́ния ана́лиза бы́ло ото́брано сто конте́кстов.","say":"Для проведения анализа было отобрано сто контекстов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ана́лиз показа́л ку́чу интере́сных моме́нтов».","right":"«Ана́лиз вы́явил ряд значи́мых закономе́рностей».","why":"«ку́ча интере́сных моме́нтов» (a bunch of interesting bits) is fine in chat, jarring in a нау́чная статья́; the register wants «ряд значи́мых закономе́рностей» (a number of significant regularities). One colloquial phrase breaks the objective register the whole article must hold."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a научная статья, if «перево́д» is your term, on the next page you write —","opts":["«перево́д» again — the exact same term","a synonym like «перело́жение», for variety","whatever sounds most elegant that day"],"answer":0,"explain":"Repeat the term. In science a synonym signals a <i>different</i> concept; sameness is precision, not clumsiness."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which phrase holds the objective register of a научная статья?","opts":["ана́лиз вы́явил ряд закономе́рностей","ана́лиз показа́л ку́чу интере́сного","коро́че, там и так всё поня́тно"],"answer":0,"explain":"«вы́явил ряд закономе́рностей» keeps the научный register. «ку́чу интере́сного» and «коро́че… всё поня́тно» are chat register — they shatter it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Citing, the abstract — and the week ahead","title":"Point to your sources; then <em>compress</em> into те́зисы","paras":["Science stands on other work, so you <b>cite</b>. In Russian articles: a bracketed reference «[Ивано́в 2019: 45]» or «[1, с. 45]», and inside the sentence «как отмеча́ет Н. Н. Ивано́в…» / «по мне́нию…» / «согла́сно да́нным…». Every claim that isn't your own is pinned to a source in the спи́сок литерату́ры.","The <b>аннота́ция</b> (abstract) and <b>те́зисы</b> (theses) are compression exercises. The аннота́ция: 3–5 sentences — problem, method, result — no water. Те́зисы: each one a single finished claim, stripped to the bone («В докла́де пока́зано, что…»), the way you submit to a conference.","The home stretch of the week: tomorrow the editor's craft — cutting канцеляри́т, tautology, redundancy (стилисти́ческая пра́вка, день 25), where that dense nominal style flips into a fault to fix; then перево́д (день 26). Read it, write it — and repair it."],"audio":"Science stands on other work, so you cite. In Russian articles: a bracketed reference — 'Ivanov 2019, page 45', or 'one, page 45' — and inside the sentence 'kak otmechaet N. N. Ivanov…', as Ivanov notes; 'po mneniyu…', in the opinion of; 'soglasno dannym…', according to the data. Every claim that isn't your own is pinned to a source in the spisok literatury. The annotatsiya, abstract, and tezisy, theses, are compression exercises. The annotatsiya: three to five sentences — problem, method, result — no water. Tezisy: each one a single finished claim, stripped to the bone — 'V doklade pokazano, chto…' — the way you submit to a conference. The home stretch of the week: tomorrow the editor's craft — cutting kantselyarit, tautology, redundancy, stilisticheskaya pravka, day 25, where that dense nominal style flips into a fault to fix; then perevod, day 26. Read it, write it — and repair it.","examples":[{"ru":"A citation in the sentence, plus the bracket — «As N. N. Ivanov notes, context determines word choice [Ivanov 2019: 45]».","en":"Как отмеча́ет Н. Н. Ивано́в, конте́кст определя́ет вы́бор сло́ва [Ивано́в 2019: 45].","say":"Как отмечает Н. Н. Иванов, контекст определяет выбор слова [Иванов 2019: 45]."},{"ru":"A single thesis — one finished claim, stripped to the bone, ready to submit.","en":"В докла́де пока́зано, что конно́тация сло́ва зави́сит от конте́кста.","say":"В докладе показано, что коннотация слова зависит от контекста."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В э́той о́чень интере́сной и ва́жной статье́ а́втор хо́чет рассказа́ть мно́го поле́зного о перево́де».","right":"«В статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма перево́да безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики; предло́жен ме́тод её реше́ния».","why":"An аннота́ция has no room for «о́чень интере́сной и ва́жной» or «хо́чет рассказа́ть мно́го поле́зного» — that's water (вода́). It states, impersonally and precisely, the пробле́ма and what the article does."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A good те́зис (a submitted thesis) is —","opts":["one finished claim, stripped to its bones","a whole paragraph of background","your personal feelings about the topic"],"answer":0,"explain":"One claim, self-contained, no water — «В докла́де пока́зано, что…». That's what a conference committee reads."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it aloud — flat and even, no drama.","target":"В настоя́щей статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма перево́да безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики.","sub":"the научный voice is level and impersonal — let it sound objective, not excited","want":"в настоящей статье рассматривается проблема перевода безэквивалентной лексики"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The 3–5-sentence summary at the top of an article is the … (hint: анно…):","accept":["аннотация"],"placeholder":"анно…","explain":"<b>аннота́ция</b> — the abstract: problem, method, result, no water."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя is drafting her first нау́чную статью́ and shows Эмме the opening. Эмма reaches for her editor's pen. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Я написа́ла так: «Я счита́ю, что я докажу́ свою́ иде́ю».","ru":"I wrote it like this: «I believe I'll prove my idea»."},{"who":"A","en":"Грамма́тика идеа́льна. Но в нау́чной статье́ так не пи́шут: сли́шком мно́го «я».","ru":"The grammar's perfect. But a scholarly article isn't written that way — too many «я»."},{"who":"B","en":"А как на́до?","ru":"So how should it be?"},{"who":"A","en":"Безли́чно: «В рабо́те пока́зано, что…». А́втор ухо́дит в тень — говори́т сама́ рабо́та.","ru":"Impersonally: «It is shown in the work that…». The author steps into the shadow — the work itself speaks."},{"who":"B","en":"И ещё: я везде́ по-ра́зному называ́ю одно́ и то́ же — то «перево́д», то «перело́жение».","ru":"Also: I keep naming the same thing differently — now «перево́д», now «перело́жение»."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот э́того не на́до. Те́рмин — оди́н и тот же, всегда́. В нау́ке повто́р — не оши́бка, а то́чность.","ru":"Don't do that. One term, the same one, always. In science, repetition isn't an error — it's precision."},{"who":"B","en":"А в конце́ я написа́ла: «в о́бщем, всё кла́ссно получи́лось».","ru":"And at the end I wrote: «basically, it all turned out great»."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то — в чат, не в статью́. В вы́водах: «Таки́м о́бразом, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод, что…».","ru":"That's for a chat, not an article. In the conclusions: «Thus, one may conclude that…»."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for writing science — the parts of an article and the machinery of its register. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"нау́чная статья́","ru":"a scholarly article","ex":"Она́ пи́шет нау́чную статью́ о перево́де."},{"en":"те́зисы","ru":"theses (a conference abstract)","ex":"Те́зисы — сжа́тые основны́е положе́ния."},{"en":"аннота́ция","ru":"an abstract (summary of an article)","ex":"Аннота́ция — вся статья́ в миниатю́ре."},{"en":"те́рмин","ru":"a term","ex":"Ка́ждый те́рмин определя́ется одна́жды."},{"en":"постано́вка пробле́мы","ru":"statement of the problem","ex":"Введе́ние начина́ется с постано́вки пробле́мы."},{"en":"актуа́льность","ru":"relevance, topicality","ex":"А́втор обосно́вывает актуа́льность те́мы."},{"en":"обзо́р литерату́ры","ru":"a literature review","ex":"В обзо́ре литерату́ры — что уже́ изве́стно."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"a conclusion","ex":"Гла́вный вы́вод — в после́днем абза́це."},{"en":"безли́чный","ru":"impersonal","ex":"Нау́чный стиль — подчёркнуто безли́чный."},{"en":"объекти́вность","ru":"objectivity","ex":"Безли́чность слу́жит объекти́вности изложе́ния."},{"en":"ссы́лка","ru":"a reference, a citation","ex":"По́сле цита́ты ста́вят ссы́лку на исто́чник."},{"en":"спи́сок литерату́ры","ru":"the reference list","ex":"Спи́сок литерату́ры — в конце́ статьи́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each научный set phrase to the job it does in an article.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each formula on the left onto its function — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["в рабо́те пока́зано, что…","states a finding impersonally (no «я»)"],["сле́дует отме́тить, что…","flags a point, hedged and objective"],["как отмеча́ет Ивано́в…","cites another author's view"],["таки́м о́бразом, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод…","opens the conclusion"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The loudest tell of an English-to-Russian scholarly article is —","opts":["a parade of «я» («я счита́ю», «я докажу́»)","too many citations","using the past tense"],"answer":0},{"q":"«I have shown that the result holds», in научный style, is —","opts":["Я показа́л, что результа́т ве́рен","В рабо́те пока́зано, что результа́т ве́рен","Я вам докажу́, что я прав"],"answer":1},{"q":"If your term is «перево́д», later in the article you write —","opts":["a synonym, for variety","«перево́д» again — the exact term","whatever reads most elegantly"],"answer":1},{"q":"The 3–5-sentence summary at the top of an article is the —","opts":["спи́сок литерату́ры","аннота́ция","эпи́граф"],"answer":1},{"q":"A good те́зис is —","opts":["one finished claim, stripped to the bones","a paragraph of background","your feelings about the topic"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"The opening of Эмма's own conference те́зисы, on translating words that have no equivalent. This is научный prose at full strength — read it and hear the level, impersonal voice.","title":"Из те́зисов Э́ммы: перево́д безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики","sentences":["В настоя́щей рабо́те рассма́тривается пробле́ма перево́да безэквивале́нтной ле́ксики.","Актуа́льность те́мы обусло́влена ро́стом интере́са к культу́рно-специфи́чным поня́тиям.","Под безэквивале́нтной ле́ксикой понима́ются слова́, не име́ющие прямо́го соотве́тствия в друго́м языке́.","На́ми был проведён ана́лиз ста конте́кстов из совреме́нной про́зы.","В рабо́те пока́зано, что вы́бор приёма перево́да зави́сит от конте́кста и реги́стра.","Таки́м о́бразом, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод о необходи́мости конте́кстного подхо́да."],"translation":"This work examines the problem of translating non-equivalent vocabulary. The topic's relevance is driven by the growing interest in culturally specific concepts. By non-equivalent vocabulary we mean words that have no direct correspondence in another language. An analysis of a hundred contexts from contemporary prose was carried out. The work shows that the choice of translation technique depends on context and register. Thus, one may conclude that a context-based approach is needed. — Every sentence is impersonal, the term «безэквивале́нтная ле́ксика» repeated exactly, not a single «я»."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to write like a scholar.","prompt":"Write the opening of a tiny «scholarly article» on any topic you know — three sentences in the научный register: (1) state the пробле́ма impersonally («В рабо́те рассма́тривается…»); (2) give a ме́тод or обзо́р («на́ми проведён…»); (3) a вы́вод («таки́м о́бразом…»). Rule: not a single «я», and the same key term repeated exactly. Then, in one line, show the «я счита́ю» draft you had to fix.","hint":"Hide yourself. Every time you reach for «я», swap in «в рабо́те пока́зано» / «на́ми устано́влено» / «представля́ется». Repeat your key term instead of varying it — in science, sameness is precision 🙂","example":"В настоя́щей рабо́те рассма́тривается влия́ние ко́фе на концентра́цию внима́ния. На́ми был проведён о́прос ста студе́нтов; в рабо́те пока́зано, что сосредото́ченность повыша́ется по́сле ча́шки ко́фе. Таки́м о́бразом, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод о положи́тельном эффе́кте. (Черново́й, «англи́йский» вариа́нт был: «Я ду́маю, что ко́фе помога́ет ду́мать», — и его́ пришло́сь переписа́ть.)"}},"25":{"day":25,"week":"04","themeRu":"The editor's hand","themeEn":"Стилисти́ческая пра́вка","intro":"Read this line and find the mistake: «В це́лях улучше́ния ка́чества обслу́живания в ме́сяце ма́рте…». Grammatically? There isn't one — every ending is correct. And yet Эмма's editor's pen is already crossing things out: «в це́лях» wants to be «что́бы», «в ме́сяце ма́рте» wants to be just «в ма́рте». This is the last thing an English speaker learns to see — not grammar errors, but <b>style</b> errors: sentences that are correct and still <i>bad</i>. Repairing them is Эмма's core craft as an editor — and today it's yours.","introAudio":"Read this line and find the mistake: 'V tselyakh uluchsheniya kachestva obsluzhivaniya v mesyatse marte…'. Grammatically? There isn't one — every ending is correct. And yet Emma's editor's pen is already crossing things out: 'v tselyakh' wants to be 'chtoby', 'v mesyatse marte' wants to be just 'v marte'. This is the last thing an English speaker learns to see — not grammar errors, but style errors: sentences that are correct and still bad. Repairing them is Emma's core craft as an editor — and today it's yours.","goals":["See the C2-level fault: a sentence that's grammatically <b>correct</b> and stylistically <b>bad</b>","Cut <b>канцеляри́т</b> (bureaucratese): «в це́лях улучше́ния» → «что́бы улу́чшить»","Strip <b>плеона́зм</b> and <b>тавтоло́гию</b>: «ме́сяц март» → «март», «спроси́ть вопро́с» → «зада́ть вопро́с»","Un-stack a chain of genitives (нани́зывание падеже́й) and choose the verb over the noun"],"learned":["Spotted the «correct but bad» sentence — style, not grammar","Cut канцеляри́т: «в це́лях улучше́ния» → «что́бы улу́чшить»","Stripped плеона́зм and тавтоло́гию («ме́сяц март» → «март»)","Un-stacked genitive chains and chose verbs over verbal nouns"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on yesterday's научный register — the impersonal voice and the repeated term — because today you'll learn where that same nominal style turns into a fault.","introAudio":"One minute on yesterday's nauchnyy register — the impersonal voice and the repeated term — because today you'll learn where that same nominal style turns into a fault.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — yesterday's научный register.","q":"In a scholarly article, «I have shown that…» is written —","opts":["В рабо́те пока́зано, что…","Я показа́л, что…","Щас докажу́, что…"],"answer":0,"explain":"Impersonal «В рабо́те пока́зано» is the научный norm — the author stays invisible."},{"type":"choice","q":"In a научная статья, if «перево́д» is your term, later you use —","opts":["a synonym, for variety","«перево́д» — the exact same term","a slang word"],"answer":1,"explain":"Repeat the term. In science a synonym would hint at a different concept; sameness is precision."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the impersonal opener.","q":"Build the impersonal научный opener: «This article examines the problem.»","want":"В статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма","answer":"В статье́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Correct — and still bad","title":"The editor sees <em>style</em>, not just grammar — start with канцеляри́т","paras":["Until now, «wrong» meant a broken rule. The editor's eye adds a second question: this is <b>correct</b> — but is it <b>good</b>? A sentence can be grammatically flawless and still limp, bloated, lifeless. Fixing that is <b>стилисти́ческая пра́вка</b> (stylistic editing) — Эмма's daily bread.","The commonest disease is <b>канцеляри́т</b> — deadening bureaucratese. The writer Корне́й Чуко́вский coined the word (in «Живо́й как жизнь», 1962), naming it like an illness; the great editor Но́ра Галь made hunting it a craft. Its symptoms: verbs turned into noun-clusters, empty prepositional turns, everything heavier than it needs to be.","The first cure is almost always the same — <b>put the verb back</b>. «В це́лях улучше́ния» → «что́бы улу́чшить». «Осуществля́ть прове́рку» → «проверя́ть». «Оказа́ть содействие» → «помо́чь». Where officialese reaches for a noun, living Russian reaches for a verb."],"audio":"Until now, 'wrong' meant a broken rule. The editor's eye adds a second question: this is correct — but is it good? A sentence can be grammatically flawless and still limp, bloated, lifeless. Fixing that is stilisticheskaya pravka, stylistic editing — Emma's daily bread. The commonest disease is kantselyarit, deadening bureaucratese. The writer Korney Chukovskiy coined the word, in 'Zhivoy kak zhizn', 1962, naming it like an illness; the great editor Nora Gal made hunting it a craft. Its symptoms: verbs turned into noun-clusters, empty prepositional turns, everything heavier than it needs to be. The first cure is almost always the same — put the verb back. 'V tselyakh uluchsheniya' becomes 'chtoby uluchshit'. 'Osushchestvlyat proverku' becomes 'proveryat'. 'Okazat sodeystviye' becomes 'pomoch'. Where officialese reaches for a noun, living Russian reaches for a verb.","table":{"rows":[["в це́лях улучше́ния → что́бы улу́чшить <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в целях улучшения, чтобы улучшить\">🔊</button>","kill the noun-cluster — put the verb back"],["осуществля́ть прове́рку → проверя́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"осуществлять проверку, проверять\">🔊</button>","канцеляри́т → a plain verb"],["в связи́ с тем, что → потому́ что <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в связи с тем что, потому что\">🔊</button>","empty bureaucratic turn → a simple conjunction"],["име́ет ме́сто → есть, быва́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"имеет место, есть, бывает\">🔊</button>","«there takes place» → «there is»"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"«For the purpose of raising the effectiveness of the work» — pure bureaucratese; the cure is a verb.","en":"Канцеляри́т: «в це́лях повыше́ния эффекти́вности». Живо́е: «что́бы рабо́тать эффекти́внее».","say":"Канцелярит: в целях повышения эффективности. Живое: чтобы работать эффективнее."},{"ru":"«Storage of data is carried out» → «Data is stored» — one verb swallows the whole cluster.","en":"Канцеляри́т: «осуществля́ется хране́ние да́нных». Пра́вка: «да́нные храня́тся».","say":"Канцелярит: осуществляется хранение данных. Правка: данные хранятся."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В це́лях улучше́ния ка́чества мы произвели́ ремо́нт помеще́ния».","right":"«Что́бы улу́чшить ка́чество, мы отремонти́ровали помеще́ние».","why":"Grammar is perfect; style is bureaucratic. «В це́лях улучше́ния» → «что́бы улу́чшить», «произвели́ ремо́нт» → «отремонти́ровали». Living Russian prefers the verb; канцеляри́т buries it in nouns."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«В це́лях улучше́ния» in living Russian becomes —","opts":["что́бы улу́чшить","в направле́нии улучше́ния","по вопро́су улучше́ния"],"answer":0,"explain":"Free the verb: «что́бы улу́чшить». The other two just swap one bureaucratic turn for another."},{"type":"choice","q":"The deadening bureaucratese Чуко́вский named «канцеляри́т» is cured mainly by —","opts":["putting the verb back in place of noun-clusters","adding more nouns for precision","making the sentences longer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Канцеляри́т hides verbs inside noun-clusters. Free the verb and the sentence breathes again."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Say it once","title":"<em>Плеона́зм</em> and <em>тавтоло́гия</em> — cut the twin","paras":["Two faults double a meaning that's already present. <b>Плеона́зм</b> (pleonasm): one word already contains the other, so the second is dead weight — «ме́сяц ма́рт» (март <i>is</i> a month), «свобо́дная вака́нсия» (a вака́нсия is free by definition), «пе́рвый дебю́т», «ме́стный абориге́н», «бо́лее лу́чший».","<b>Тавтоло́гия</b> (tautology): the same root repeated to no purpose — «спроси́ть вопро́с», «рассказа́ть расска́з», «проливно́й ли́вень», «умножа́ть во мно́го раз». The cure is to swap one word — «зада́ть вопро́с», «рассказа́ть исто́рию».","The editor's test is one question: does the second word add anything? Russians nickname this fault «ма́сло ма́сляное» (buttery butter). If a word only repeats what's already said, cut it — the meaning doesn't move an inch."],"audio":"Two faults double a meaning that's already present. Pleonazm, pleonasm: one word already contains the other, so the second is dead weight — 'mesyats mart', March is a month; 'svobodnaya vakansiya', a vacancy is free by definition; 'pervyy debyut', first debut; 'mestnyy aborigen'; 'boleye luchshiy'. Tavtologiya, tautology: the same root repeated to no purpose — 'sprosit vopros', 'rasskazat rasskaz', 'prolivnoy liven', 'umnozhat vo mnogo raz'. The cure is to swap one word — 'zadat vopros', 'rasskazat istoriyu'. The editor's test is one question: does the second word add anything? Russians nickname this fault 'maslo maslyanoye', buttery butter. If a word only repeats what's already said, cut it — the meaning doesn't move an inch.","table":{"rows":[["ме́сяц март → март <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"месяц март, март\">🔊</button>","плеона́зм — «март» is already a month"],["свобо́дная вака́нсия → вака́нсия <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"свободная вакансия, вакансия\">🔊</button>","плеона́зм — a вака́нсия is free by definition"],["спроси́ть вопро́с → зада́ть вопро́с <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спросить вопрос, задать вопрос\">🔊</button>","тавтоло́гия — same root; swap one word"],["бо́лее лу́чший → лу́чший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"более лучший, лучший\">🔊</button>","плеона́зм — «лу́чший» already means «more good»"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Two pleonasms in one line — «ме́сяце апре́ле» and «свобо́дная вака́нсия»; the trimmed version says exactly as much.","en":"Бы́ло: «в ме́сяце апре́ле откры́лась свобо́дная вака́нсия». Ста́ло: «в апре́ле откры́лась вака́нсия».","say":"Было: в месяце апреле открылась свободная вакансия. Стало: в апреле открылась вакансия."},{"ru":"«спроси́ть вопро́с» repeats the idea of asking; a question is «зада́ть» (posed), not «спроси́ть».","en":"Не «спра́шивают вопро́с», а «задаю́т вопро́с».","say":"Не спрашивают вопрос, а задают вопрос."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В свои́х мемуа́рах о свое́й жи́зни а́втор рассказа́л расска́з о своём пе́рвом дебю́те».","right":"«В свои́х мемуа́рах а́втор рассказа́л о дебю́те».","why":"«мемуа́ры о свое́й жи́зни» (memoirs are about one's life), «пе́рвый дебю́т» (a дебю́т is a first appearance), «рассказа́л расска́з» (same root) — three redundancies. Each twin is cut; the meaning is untouched."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«В ме́сяце ма́рте» should be edited to —","opts":["в ма́рте","в тече́ние ме́сяца ма́рта","в ма́рте ме́сяце"],"answer":0,"explain":"«март» is already a month, so «ме́сяце» is a плеона́зм. Just «в ма́рте»."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"«Спроси́ть вопро́с» repeats a root (тавтоло́гия). The right verb with «вопро́с» is (hint: зад…):","accept":["задать","задавать"],"placeholder":"зад…","explain":"«<b>Зада́ть</b> вопро́с» — a question is posed, not «asked» with a twin root. «спроси́ть» takes a person, not «вопро́с»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Un-stack the nouns","title":"The genitive chain — and choosing the verb","paras":["A special strain of канцеляри́т: <b>нани́зывание падеже́й</b> (case-stacking) — a train of nouns all in the genitive, each hanging off the last: «реше́ние вопро́са улучше́ния ка́чества обслу́живания населе́ния». Grammatically legal, humanly unreadable — five genitives in a row.","The cure: break the chain with verbs and prepositions. «реше́ние вопро́са улучше́ния ка́чества обслу́живания» → «как улу́чшить обслу́живание». Ask what actually <i>happens</i>, and let a verb carry it.","A milder cousin is <b>расщепле́ние сказу́емого</b> (splitting the predicate): «оказа́ть по́мощь» for «помо́чь», «приня́ть реше́ние» for «реши́ть», «произвести́ ремо́нт» for «отремонти́ровать». In an official document these are fine; in living prose the plain verb almost always wins. The choice, as ever, is by register."],"audio":"A special strain of kantselyarit: nanizyvaniye padezhey, case-stacking — a train of nouns all in the genitive, each hanging off the last: 'resheniye voprosa uluchsheniya kachestva obsluzhivaniya naseleniya'. Grammatically legal, humanly unreadable — five genitives in a row. The cure: break the chain with verbs and prepositions. 'Resheniye voprosa uluchsheniya kachestva obsluzhivaniya' becomes 'kak uluchshit obsluzhivaniye'. Ask what actually happens, and let a verb carry it. A milder cousin is rasshchepleniye skazuemogo, splitting the predicate: 'okazat pomoshch' for 'pomoch', 'prinyat resheniye' for 'reshit', 'proizvesti remont' for 'otremontirovat'. In an official document these are fine; in living prose the plain verb almost always wins. The choice, as ever, is by register.","table":{"rows":[["реше́ние вопро́са улучше́ния ка́чества <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"решение вопроса улучшения качества\">🔊</button>","нани́зывание — a chain of genitives (unreadable)"],["→ как улу́чшить ка́чество <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как улучшить качество\">🔊</button>","the cure — a verb breaks the chain"],["оказа́ть по́мощь → помо́чь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оказать помощь, помочь\">🔊</button>","расщепле́ние — the plain verb is lighter"],["приня́ть реше́ние → реши́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принять решение, решить\">🔊</button>","noun-cluster → one verb (choose by register)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Four stacked genitives unravel into one clear verb.","en":"Канцеляри́т: «для реше́ния вопро́са повыше́ния у́ровня подгото́вки». Пра́вка: «что́бы лу́чше подгото́вить».","say":"Канцелярит: для решения вопроса повышения уровня подготовки. Правка: чтобы лучше подготовить."},{"ru":"«A cleaning of the premises was carried out» → «The premises were cleaned» — the split predicate collapses into one verb.","en":"Канцеляри́т: «была́ произведена́ убо́рка помеще́ния». Пра́вка: «помеще́ние убра́ли».","say":"Канцелярит: была произведена уборка помещения. Правка: помещение убрали."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Был осуществлён проце́сс реше́ния вопро́са организа́ции убо́рки».","right":"«Реши́ли, как организова́ть убо́рку».","why":"«осуществлён проце́сс реше́ния вопро́са организа́ции убо́рки» stacks five nouns where two verbs do the job: «реши́ли, как организова́ть». A case-chain reads as fog; verbs read as sense."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Реше́ние вопро́са улучше́ния ка́чества» is a chain of genitives. The fix is to —","opts":["break it with a verb: «как улу́чшить ка́чество»","add one more noun to the chain","make it even more formal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ask what happens and let a verb carry it: «как улу́чшить ка́чество». That un-stacks the genitives into sense."},{"type":"choice","q":"In living (non-official) prose, «оказа́ть по́мощь» is usually better as —","opts":["помо́чь","произвести́ оказа́ние по́мощи","осуществи́ть подде́ржку"],"answer":0,"explain":"The plain verb «помо́чь» is lighter and more alive. The other two pile on even more nouns — deeper into канцеляри́т."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The editor's method — and the week ahead","title":"Read aloud, cut, verb over noun, hold the register","paras":["Эмма's checklist, in order: <b>read it aloud</b> (the ear catches what the eye forgives); <b>cut</b> every word that repeats what's already said (плеона́зм, тавтоло́гия); <b>put the verb back</b> wherever a noun-cluster hides one (канцеляри́т, нани́зывание); and <b>hold one register</b> — no chat word in an official line, no officialese in a warm one (<b>стилево́й сбо́й</b>, a register clash).","The deepest skill is «correct but bad». A native editor doesn't reach for a rulebook — they <i>hear</i> that «в ме́сяце ма́рте» is heavier than «в ма́рте», that «осуществля́ть де́ятельность по подгото́вке» is begging to become «гото́вить». You hear it now too.","The week's last steps: tomorrow <b>перево́д</b> — carrying tone, register and irony across languages, not just words (день 26); then the essay in your own voice (день 27), toward a text no native could tell from their own. From reading, to writing, to <b>repairing</b> Russian."],"audio":"Emma's checklist, in order: read it aloud — the ear catches what the eye forgives; cut every word that repeats what's already said, pleonazm, tavtologiya; put the verb back wherever a noun-cluster hides one, kantselyarit, nanizyvaniye; and hold one register — no chat word in an official line, no officialese in a warm one, stilevoy sboy, a register clash. The deepest skill is 'correct but bad'. A native editor doesn't reach for a rulebook — they hear that 'v mesyatse marte' is heavier than 'v marte', that 'osushchestvlyat deyatelnost po podgotovke' is begging to become 'gotovit'. You hear it now too. The week's last steps: tomorrow perevod — carrying tone, register and irony across languages, not just words, day 26; then the essay in your own voice, day 27, toward a text no native could tell from their own. From reading, to writing, to repairing Russian.","examples":[{"ru":"A register clash (стилево́й сбо́й): lofty «соизво́лил» smashed against chat «ну чё, как оно́» in one breath.","en":"Дире́ктор соизво́лил зайти́ и говори́т: «Ну чё, как оно́?»","say":"Директор соизволил зайти и говорит: ну чё, как оно?"},{"ru":"The great editor Nora Gal's rule — test every word: is it alive or dead?","en":"Но́ра Галь учи́ла: проверя́й ка́ждое сло́во — живо́е оно́ и́ли мёртвое.","say":"Нора Галь учила: проверяй каждое слово — живое оно или мёртвое."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что я, коро́че, зайду́ ве́чером».","right":"«Уведомля́ю, что зайду́ ве́чером».","why":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю» (officialese) and «коро́че» (chat filler) collide — a стилево́й сбо́й (register clash). Pick one register and hold it; here, drop the chat word (or drop the officialese, depending on who's reading)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The first, simplest tool an editor uses to catch «correct but bad» prose is to —","opts":["read it aloud — the ear catches what the eye forgives","count the words","check the spelling and nothing else"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reading aloud is the editor's oldest trick: a heavy, bureaucratic line trips your tongue even when it passes your eye."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read the edited, living version aloud.","target":"Что́бы улу́чшить ка́чество, мы отремонти́ровали помеще́ние.","sub":"light and verb-driven — this is the «after», with the канцеляри́т cut out","want":"чтобы улучшить качество мы отремонтировали помещение"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The deadening bureaucratese Чуко́вский named — noun-clusters, «в це́лях», split predicates — is called … (hint: канц…):","accept":["канцелярит"],"placeholder":"канц…","explain":"<b>канцеляри́т</b> — bureaucratese. Корне́й Чуко́вский coined it; the cure is nearly always a living verb."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н wrote an announcement for his office and asks Эмму to «just check the grammar». She checks the style instead. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Прове́рь, пожа́луйста, грамма́тику. Оши́бок нет?","ru":"Check the grammar, please. Any errors?"},{"who":"A","en":"Оши́бок нет. Но чита́ть невозмо́жно. Слу́шай: «в це́лях улучше́ния проце́сса…».","ru":"No errors. But it's impossible to read. Listen: «for the purpose of improving the process…»."},{"who":"B","en":"А что не так? Всё же пра́вильно.","ru":"What's wrong with it? It's all correct."},{"who":"A","en":"Пра́вильно — не зна́чит хорошо́. «В це́лях улучше́ния» — э́то «что́бы улу́чшить». Коро́че и живе́е.","ru":"Correct doesn't mean good. «В це́лях улучше́ния» means «to improve». Shorter and more alive."},{"who":"B","en":"А «свобо́дная вака́нсия» — то́же убра́ть?","ru":"And «свобо́дная вака́нсия» — should I cut that one as well?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да: вака́нсия и так свобо́дна. Э́то плеона́зм — ли́шнее сло́во.","ru":"Yes: a вака́нсия is free by definition. That's a pleonasm — an extra word."},{"who":"B","en":"Тогда́ тут же ещё: «в ме́сяце ма́рте», «произвели́ ремо́нт»…","ru":"Then here's more of it: «in the month of March», «carried out repairs»…"},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́дишь — ты уже́ сам слы́шишь. «В ма́рте», «отремонти́ровали». Вот э́то и есть пра́вка.","ru":"See — you already hear it yourself. «In March», «repaired». That's what editing is."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the editor's desk — the faults you cut and the tools you cut them with. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"пра́вка","ru":"editing, correction","ex":"Хоро́шая пра́вка незаме́тна для чита́теля."},{"en":"канцеляри́т","ru":"bureaucratese (deadening officialese)","ex":"Канцеляри́т убива́ет живу́ю фра́зу."},{"en":"плеона́зм","ru":"pleonasm (redundant repetition)","ex":"«Ме́сяц март» — типи́чный плеона́зм."},{"en":"тавтоло́гия","ru":"tautology (same root repeated)","ex":"«Спроси́ть вопро́с» — тавтоло́гия."},{"en":"избы́точность","ru":"redundancy, excess","ex":"Речева́я избы́точность — вра́г я́сности."},{"en":"стилево́й сбо́й","ru":"a register clash, a style slip","ex":"Стилево́й сбо́й ре́жет слух."},{"en":"отглаго́льное существи́тельное","ru":"a verbal noun","ex":"Отглаго́льные существи́тельные утяжеля́ют текст."},{"en":"ре́дактор","ru":"an editor","ex":"Ре́дактор ви́дит не оши́бки, а стиль."},{"en":"многосло́вие","ru":"verbosity, wordiness","ex":"Многосло́вие — при́знак нея́сной мы́сли."},{"en":"сокраща́ть","ru":"to shorten, to cut","ex":"Сокраща́й: коро́че зна́чит я́снее."},{"en":"нани́зывание падеже́й","ru":"case-stacking (a genitive chain)","ex":"Нани́зывание падеже́й де́лает фра́зу нечита́емой."},{"en":"ли́шний","ru":"superfluous, extra","ex":"Вы́черкни ка́ждое ли́шнее сло́во."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each «correct but bad» phrase to the fault it commits.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each phrase on the left onto its fault — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["в це́лях улучше́ния","bureaucratese — needs a plain verb"],["ме́сяц март","pleonasm — one word is redundant"],["спроси́ть вопро́с","tautology — the same root repeated"],["реше́ние вопро́са ка́чества","a genitive chain — un-stack it"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The editor's extra question, beyond «is it correct?», is —","opts":["«is it good?» — style, not just grammar","«is it long enough?»","«are there enough nouns?»"],"answer":0},{"q":"«В це́лях улучше́ния», edited into living Russian, is —","opts":["что́бы улу́чшить","в направле́нии улучше́ния","по вопро́су улучше́ния"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Ме́сяц март» is an example of —","opts":["тавтоло́гии","плеона́зма","инве́рсии"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Спроси́ть вопро́с» should be edited to —","opts":["зада́ть вопро́с","спроси́ть отве́т","задава́ть спро́с"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Реше́ние вопро́са улучше́ния ка́чества обслу́живания» is fixed by —","opts":["stacking one more genitive","breaking the chain with a verb","making it more formal"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's own note on the day Иван handed her a «grammatically perfect» text. Watch a paragraph lose a third of its weight and come alive. Read each line aloud.","title":"Пра́вило Эммы: пра́вильно — не зна́чит хорошо́","sentences":["Ива́н принёс текст и попроси́л прове́рить то́лько грамма́тику.","Оши́бок в нём не́ было — а чита́ть его́ бы́ло невозмо́жно.","«В це́лях улучше́ния» преврати́лось в «что́бы улу́чшить».","«Свобо́дная вака́нсия» ста́ла про́сто «вака́нсией», а «ме́сяц март» — «ма́ртом».","Дли́нная цепо́чка роди́тельных падеже́й распа́лась на просты́е глаго́лы.","Текст стал коро́че на треть — и впервы́е зазвуча́л по-живо́му."],"translation":"Ivan brought a text and asked her to check only the grammar. There were no errors in it — and yet it was impossible to read. «В це́лях улучше́ния» turned into «что́бы улу́чшить». «Свобо́дная вака́нсия» became simply «вака́нсия», and «ме́сяц март» became «март». A long chain of genitives broke apart into plain verbs. The text got a third shorter — and for the first time it sounded alive."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to hold the editor's pen.","prompt":"Find or invent one «correct but bad» Russian sentence — bloated with канцеляри́т, a плеона́зм, or a chain of genitives. Write it as-is; then edit it: put the verb back, cut the redundant word, un-stack the nouns. Finally, in one line, name what was wrong («канцеляри́т», «плеона́зм», «тавтоло́гия», «стилево́й сбо́й»). 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Read your sentence aloud — if it's heavier than how you'd say it to a friend, it needs пра́вка. The reflex: wherever a noun-cluster hides a verb, free the verb 🙂","example":"Бы́ло: «В це́лях улучше́ния ка́чества обслу́живания в ме́сяце ма́рте была́ произведена́ прове́рка». Ста́ло: «В ма́рте мы прове́рили, как улу́чшить обслу́живание». Убра́л канцеляри́т («в це́лях», «была́ произведена́ прове́рка») и плеона́зм («ме́сяц март»). Оши́бок не́ было ни в одно́м вариа́нте — но второ́й живо́й, а пе́рвый мёртвый."}},"26":{"day":26,"week":"04","themeRu":"Rendering it faithfully","themeEn":"Перево́д и то́чность","intro":"«Перево́дчик в про́зе — раб, перево́дчик в поэ́зии — сопе́рник», wrote Жуко́вский two centuries ago. Эмма keeps that line pinned above her desk. Today it stops being a quote and becomes the job. You already choose the exact word <i>inside</i> Russian; now you watch two languages at once. A translator who swaps word for word makes something correct and dead. A translator who <b>renders</b> — передаёт — keeps the tone, the register, the irony, the charge, and lets the literal word go when it must. That's today: перево́д и то́чность — precision that lives above the words, not inside them.","introAudio":"'Perevodchik v proze — rab, perevodchik v poezii — sopernik,' wrote Zhukovsky two centuries ago. Emma keeps that line pinned above her desk. Today it stops being a quote and becomes the job. You already choose the exact word inside Russian; now you watch two languages at once. A translator who swaps word for word makes something correct and dead. A translator who renders — 'peredayot' — keeps the tone, the register, the irony, the charge, and lets the literal word go when it must. That's today: 'perevod i tochnost', precision that lives above the words, not inside them.","goals":["See the leap from «translate the words» to «render the meaning» — <b>переда́ть</b>, not <b>подста́вить</b>","Catch the false friends of the bookish layer: <b>претендова́ть · актуа́льный · симпати́чный · патети́ческий · корре́ктный</b>","Keep tone, register and irony alive across the gap — a flat rendering of an ironic line is a mistranslation","Feel what won't cross without unpacking (<b>тоска́ · по́шлость</b>) — render the idea, don't swap the word"],"learned":["Felt the leap from swapping words to rendering meaning (переда́ть, not подста́вить)","Caught the bookish false friends (претендова́ть / актуа́льный / симпати́чный / патети́ческий / корре́ктный)","Kept tone, register and irony alive across the two languages","Unpacked the untranslatable instead of swapping it (тоска́, по́шлость)"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you held the editor's red pen — cutting канцеляри́т and tautology so a text reads clean. One step further today: rendering someone else's meaning into Russian. First, half a minute back on the editor's ear and last week's untranslatable words.","introAudio":"Yesterday you held the editor's red pen — cutting kantselyarit and tautology so a text reads clean. One step further today: rendering someone else's meaning into Russian. First, half a minute back on the editor's ear and last week's untranslatable words.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the clean edit. This is yesterday's editor's ear.","q":"«в це́лях экономи́и» is канцеляри́т (bureaucratese). The clean edit is —","opts":["что́бы сэконо́мить","в це́лях эконо́мии сре́дств","на предме́т эконо́мии"],"answer":0,"explain":"«в це́лях экономи́и» is padded officialese → plain <b>что́бы сэконо́мить</b>. The other two only pile on more канцеляри́т."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which of these words can't be swapped for one English word without lying a little?","opts":["печа́ль","тоска́","гру́сть"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>тоска́</b> is an existential longing with no English one-word match; печа́ль and гру́сть map cleanly to «sorrow / sadness». Last week's lesson — and today's problem."},{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> pick the native collocation.","q":"«… влия́ние на реше́ние» — “to influence a decision”. Which verb keeps company with влия́ние?","opts":["сде́лать","оказа́ть","сформирова́ть"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b> — the fixed collocation (week 1). «сде́лать влия́ние» is a word-for-word calque from English, exactly the translationese we're hunting today."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Render, don't substitute","title":"<em>Переда́ть</em> the meaning — not <em>подста́вить</em> the words","paras":["For years you translated to <b>understand</b> — swap each Russian word for its English one and read off the sense. A working translator does the opposite thing. The goal isn't to move the words across; it's to make a Russian reader feel exactly what the original reader felt. The words are a means, and a means you're allowed to change.","So the master question is never «what does this word equal?» but «what does this sentence <b>do</b> — and how do I make Russian do the same?». <b>Передава́ть</b> — to render, to carry meaning over — is the verb for it. Its opposite is <b>подставля́ть</b>, to substitute, to plug word into slot. Substitution gives you something correct and lifeless; rendering gives you the living thing.","That's why «сло́во в сло́во» — word for word — is a warning, not a compliment. A literal перево́д can be grammatically perfect and still betray the original, because it drops everything the words were <i>carrying</i> — the tone, the speed, the charge you spent this whole month learning to hear."],"audio":"For years you translated to understand — swap each Russian word for its English one and read off the sense. A working translator does the opposite thing. The goal isn't to move the words across; it's to make a Russian reader feel exactly what the original reader felt. The words are a means, and a means you're allowed to change. So the master question is never 'what does this word equal' but 'what does this sentence do, and how do I make Russian do the same'. 'Peredavat', to render, to carry meaning over, is the verb for it. Its opposite is 'podstavlyat', to substitute, to plug word into slot. Substitution gives you something correct and lifeless; rendering gives you the living thing. That's why 'slovo v slovo', word for word, is a warning, not a compliment. A literal 'perevod' can be grammatically perfect and still betray the original, because it drops everything the words were carrying — the tone, the speed, the charge you spent this whole month learning to hear.","table":{"rows":[["бу́квальный перево́д <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"буквальный перевод\">🔊</button>","literal, word-for-word — often correct and dead"],["во́льный перево́д <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вольный перевод\">🔊</button>","free, loose — can drift from the original"],["то́чный перево́д <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"точный перевод\">🔊</button>","faithful — same sense, same tone, same register"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The translator carries meaning across, not words.","en":"Перево́дчик перено́сит смысл, а не слова́.","say":"Переводчик переносит смысл, а не слова."},{"ru":"Word for word — correct, and lifeless.","en":"Сло́во в сло́во — пра́вильно, но безжи́зненно.","say":"Слово в слово — правильно, но безжизненно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Она́ сде́лала оши́бку в перево́де.","right":"Она́ допусти́ла оши́бку в перево́де.","why":"«сде́лать оши́бку» is a calque of English «made a mistake». The Russian collocation is <b>допусти́ть</b> (or <b>соверши́ть</b>) <b>оши́бку</b>. Even one substituted verb leaves the fingerprint of translationese — this is rendering, not swapping."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap.","q":"A faithful translation is judged by —","opts":["whether every word has a match","whether the reader feels what the original reader felt","whether it is longer than the source"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fidelity is to the <b>effect</b>, not the word count. <b>Передава́ть</b> the meaning; the literal word is only a means."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Render “she made a mistake” with the native collocation:","want":"она́ допусти́ла оши́бку","answer":"она́ допусти́ла оши́бку"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The false friends that fool a C2","title":"<em>Претендова́ть</em> isn't «pretend» — the bookish traps","paras":["The false friends that trip a beginner are concrete — «магази́н» isn't a magazine. The ones that trip a near-native are the <b>bookish, Latinate</b> pair: words that look identical to an English word, sit in the same educated register, and mean something else entirely. They're dangerous precisely because you feel sure you know them.","Five that catch translators daily. <b>Претендова́ть на</b> — to lay claim to, to aspire to (a post, a title) — <i>not</i> «to pretend», which is <b>притворя́ться</b>. <b>Актуа́льный</b> — topical, relevant, current — <i>not</i> «actual», which is <b>факти́ческий</b> or <b>действи́тельный</b>. <b>Симпати́чный</b> — likeable, nice-looking — <i>not</i> «sympathetic», which is <b>сочу́вствующий</b>.","And two with a literary sting. <b>Патети́ческий</b> — lofty, full of high pathos — <i>not</i> «pathetic» in the pitiful sense (<b>жа́лкий</b>). <b>Корре́ктный</b> — polite, proper, tactful in manner — <i>not</i> «correct» meaning right (<b>пра́вильный</b>). Reach for the look-alike and you don't make an error — you say something you never meant, in perfect Russian."],"audio":"The false friends that trip a beginner are concrete — 'magazin' isn't a magazine. The ones that trip a near-native are the bookish, Latinate pair: words that look identical to an English word, sit in the same educated register, and mean something else entirely. They're dangerous precisely because you feel sure you know them. Five that catch translators daily. 'Pretendovat na' — to lay claim to, to aspire to — not 'to pretend', which is 'pritvoryatsya'. 'Aktualnyy' — topical, relevant, current — not 'actual', which is 'fakticheskiy'. 'Simpatichnyy' — likeable, nice-looking — not 'sympathetic', which is 'sochuvstvuyushchiy'. And two with a literary sting. 'Pateticheskiy' — lofty, full of high pathos — not 'pathetic' in the pitiful sense, 'zhalkiy'. 'Korrektnyy' — polite, proper, tactful in manner — not 'correct' meaning right, 'pravilnyy'. Reach for the look-alike and you don't make an error — you say something you never meant, in perfect Russian.","table":{"rows":[["претендова́ть на … <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"претендовать на\">🔊</button>","to lay claim to, aspire to — NOT «pretend» (притворя́ться)"],["актуа́льный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"актуальный\">🔊</button>","topical, relevant, current — NOT «actual» (факти́ческий)"],["симпати́чный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"симпатичный\">🔊</button>","likeable, nice-looking — NOT «sympathetic» (сочу́вствующий)"],["патети́ческий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"патетический\">🔊</button>","lofty, full of pathos — NOT «pathetic» (жа́лкий)"],["корре́ктный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"корректный\">🔊</button>","polite, proper — NOT «correct/right» (пра́вильный)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This question is very topical right now.","en":"Э́тот вопро́с сейча́с о́чень актуа́лен.","say":"Этот вопрос сейчас очень актуален."},{"ru":"He was coldly correct — polite, and not one degree warmer.","en":"Он был хо́лодно корре́ктен — ве́жлив, и ни гра́дусом тепле́е.","say":"Он был холодно корректен — вежлив, и ни градусом теплее."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он претендова́л, что всё в поря́дке.","right":"Он притворя́лся, что всё в поря́дке.","why":"«претендова́ть» looks like «pretend» but means <b>to lay claim</b>. «To pretend, to feign» is <b>притворя́ться</b>. The two are unrelated — the resemblance is the whole trap."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́тот вопро́с сейча́с о́чень …» — “this question is very topical”. Which word?","opts":["факти́ческий","актуа́льный","действи́тельный"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>актуа́льный</b> = topical, relevant, current. факти́ческий / действи́тельный mean «actual, real» — the false-friend trap."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"“To pretend, to feign” — NOT the look-alike «претендова́ть». Type the real verb (hint: прит…):","accept":["притворяться","притворяюсь","притвориться"],"placeholder":"прит…","explain":"<b>притворя́ться</b> — to pretend, to feign. «претендова́ть» is the false friend: it means to lay claim."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The tone has to travel","title":"A flat rendering of an <em>ironic</em> line is a mistranslation","paras":["Fidelity to sense is only half the job. A line also has a <b>register</b> and often a <b>charge</b> — irony, warmth, contempt, a wink — and those have to cross the gap too. Render an ironic jab as a straight compliment and you've been «accurate» about the words while getting the meaning exactly backwards.","So the translator matches register on purpose. Colloquial stays colloquial; bookish stays bookish; ironic stays ironic. <b>Разг.</b> (colloquial): «Ну ты и вы́думал!». <b>Кн.</b> (bookish): «Како́е неле́пое измышле́ние». Same idea, two different worlds — and a rendering that flattens one into the other has lost the meaning even with every word «right».","This is where the whole month pays off: connotation, subtext, irony. The translator is the reader who can least afford to take an ironic line literally — because they have to build it again, with the sting intact, in another language."],"audio":"Fidelity to sense is only half the job. A line also has a register and often a charge — irony, warmth, contempt, a wink — and those have to cross the gap too. Render an ironic jab as a straight compliment and you've been 'accurate' about the words while getting the meaning exactly backwards. So the translator matches register on purpose. Colloquial stays colloquial; bookish stays bookish; ironic stays ironic. Colloquial: 'Nu ty i vydumal!'. Bookish: 'Kakoye nelepoye izmyshleniye'. Same idea, two different worlds — and a rendering that flattens one into the other has lost the meaning even with every word 'right'. This is where the whole month pays off: connotation, subtext, irony. The translator is the reader who can least afford to take an ironic line literally — because they have to build it again, with the sting intact, in another language.","examples":[{"ru":"Sarcastic “thanks” — keep the sting, don't turn it into gratitude.","en":"Ирони́чное «спаси́бо» — сохрани́ уко́л, не де́лай из него́ благода́рность.","say":"Ироничное спасибо — сохрани укол, не делай из него благодарность."},{"ru":"Bookish: “what an absurd notion.” Colloquial: “what nonsense you've dreamed up.”","en":"Кни́жно: «Како́е неле́пое измышле́ние». Разгово́рно: «Ну и чушь ты приду́мал».","say":"Книжно: какое нелепое измышление. Разговорно: ну и чушь ты придумал."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"(sarcastic “Great, thanks.”) → «Отли́чно, благодарю́ вас».","right":"(sarcastic “Great, thanks.”) → «Ну спаси́бо, удружи́л».","why":"The source is sarcastic and colloquial. Rendering it in polite, bookish Russian («благодарю́ вас») is «correct» yet wrong: it erases the irony and lifts the register. The faithful rendering keeps both the sting and the casual tone."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"An ironic “Ну ты ге́ний!” rendered as a sincere “You're a genius!” is —","opts":["a mistranslation — the irony is the meaning","fine, the words match","more polite, so better"],"answer":0,"explain":"Literal accuracy with the irony stripped out reverses the meaning. The <b>charge</b> has to cross the gap too."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it with the irony audible — let the «спаси́бо» curdle.","target":"Ну спаси́бо, удружи́л — тако́го я не ожида́л.","sub":"say it so the «спаси́бо» sounds anything but grateful","want":"ну спасибо удружил — такого я не ожидал"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The words you unpack — and the road ahead","title":"Some words you <em>unpack</em>, not translate — and the finish line","paras":["Sometimes there's no word to render, because the concept itself doesn't live in the other language. <b>Тоска́</b> isn't «sadness» or «boredom»; <b>по́шлость</b> isn't «vulgarity» or «triteness». Swap in a single English word and you've told a small lie. Here the translator stops substituting and starts <b>unpacking</b>: rendering the idea with a phrase, or keeping the Russian word and letting the sentence around it explain.","That's the whole art in one move — knowing when to find the exact equivalent, when to render the effect by other means, and when to surrender the word and carry the <i>concept</i> instead. Faithful never meant literal. It meant: the reader on the far side receives what the reader on this side received.","This week now turns from taking texts in to putting your own out, at native level. Tomorrow: the essay with a <b>voice</b> (со́бственный го́лос) — one register, modulated on purpose. Then the whole vertical range of Russian (jargon, slang, dialect), then everything at once in one native-grade text, and finally the capstone — the whole road, A1 to here, in your own voice."],"audio":"Sometimes there's no word to render, because the concept itself doesn't live in the other language. 'Toska' isn't 'sadness' or 'boredom'; 'poshlost' isn't 'vulgarity' or 'triteness'. Swap in a single English word and you've told a small lie. Here the translator stops substituting and starts unpacking: rendering the idea with a phrase, or keeping the Russian word and letting the sentence around it explain. That's the whole art in one move — knowing when to find the exact equivalent, when to render the effect by other means, and when to surrender the word and carry the concept instead. Faithful never meant literal. It meant: the reader on the far side receives what the reader on this side received. This week now turns from taking texts in to putting your own out, at native level. Tomorrow: the essay with a voice, 'sobstvennyy golos' — one register, modulated on purpose. Then the whole vertical range of Russian; then everything at once in one native-grade text; and finally the capstone — the whole road, A1 to here, in your own voice.","examples":[{"ru":"“тоска” isn't sadness — it's a longing with no English name.","en":"«Тоска́» — не гру́сть, а тоска́, кото́рой нет и́мени по-англи́йски.","say":"Тоска — не грусть, а тоска, которой нет имени по-английски."},{"ru":"Don't swap the word — carry the idea.","en":"Не подставля́й сло́во — передава́й поня́тие.","say":"Не подставляй слово — передавай понятие."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В его́ слова́х была́ по́шлость» → “there was vulgarity in his words.”","right":"«В его́ слова́х была́ по́шлость» — unpack it: a smug, tasteless triteness that fancies itself clever.","why":"«по́шлость» has no one-word English match: it's a moral-aesthetic verdict — banal, self-satisfied and in poor taste all at once. The faithful move isn't a synonym, it's <b>unpacking</b> the concept."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Faced with «по́шлость», the C2 translator —","opts":["writes “banality” and moves on","unpacks the concept — no single English word holds it","deletes the sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"Words like <b>по́шлость</b> and <b>тоска́</b> are unpacked, not swapped. Rendering the idea beats any single false equivalent."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (dictionary form). Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The verb for “to render / carry meaning across”, the translator's core move (hint: перед…):","accept":["передавать","передать","передаёт","передает"],"placeholder":"перед…","explain":"<b>передава́ть</b> / <b>переда́ть</b> — to render, to carry meaning over. Its opposite, <b>подставля́ть</b> (to substitute), is what betrays a translation."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is finishing a translation at home; Ива́н reads over her shoulder and trips on a false friend. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"«Он претенду́ет на э́то ме́сто» — он что, притворя́ется, бу́дто уже́ рабо́тает здесь?","ru":"“He претендует to this position” — what, he's pretending he already works here?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет-нет. «Претендова́ть» — не «притворя́ться». Он на ме́сто претенду́ет — то есть хо́чет его́ заня́ть.","ru":"No, no. «Претендовать» isn't «to pretend». He's laying claim to the position — that is, he wants to take it."},{"who":"B","en":"Ло́жный друг, зна́чит. А я был уве́рен, что по́нял.","ru":"A false friend, then. And I was sure I'd understood."},{"who":"A","en":"Са́мые опа́сные — вот таки́е, кни́жные. Похо́жи на англи́йские до бу́квы, а смысл друго́й.","ru":"The most dangerous ones are exactly these — bookish. Identical to the English down to the letter, but the meaning's different."},{"who":"B","en":"Ну ты про́сто ходя́чий слова́рь лову́шек.","ru":"You're a walking dictionary of traps."},{"who":"A","en":"Не слова́рь. Про́сто на ка́ждой из них я уже́ раз оши́блась — и запо́мнила.","ru":"Not a dictionary. I've just tripped on each of them once — and remembered."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то предложе́ние ты как переведёшь — сло́во в сло́во?","ru":"And this sentence — how will you translate it, word for word?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ни за что́. Переведу́ смысл. Сло́во в сло́во — э́то ещё не перево́д.","ru":"Never. I'll render the meaning. Word for word isn't translation yet."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the translator's table — the craft of rendering, plus the false friends you met today. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"перево́д","ru":"translation","ex":"То́чный перево́д передаёт смысл, а не слова́."},{"en":"передава́ть","ru":"to render, convey (meaning)","ex":"Ва́жно передава́ть тон, а не то́лько смысл."},{"en":"исхо́дный","ru":"source, original (text)","ex":"В исхо́дном те́ксте была́ иро́ния."},{"en":"эквивале́нт","ru":"equivalent","ex":"У э́того сло́ва нет то́чного эквивале́нта."},{"en":"бу́квальный","ru":"literal, word-for-word","ex":"Бу́квальный перево́д здесь звучи́т неле́по."},{"en":"во́льный","ru":"free, loose (translation)","ex":"Э́то сли́шком во́льный перево́д."},{"en":"ло́жный друг","ru":"false friend (look-alike word)","ex":"«Актуа́льный» — класси́ческий ло́жный друг."},{"en":"претендова́ть","ru":"to lay claim, aspire (not «pretend»)","ex":"Он претенду́ет на пе́рвое ме́сто."},{"en":"актуа́льный","ru":"topical, relevant (not «actual»)","ex":"Э́то о́чень актуа́льная те́ма."},{"en":"корре́ктный","ru":"polite, proper (not «correct»)","ex":"Он был холо́дно корре́ктен."},{"en":"патети́ческий","ru":"lofty, full of pathos (not «pathetic»)","ex":"Речь вы́шла сли́шком патети́ческой."},{"en":"то́чность","ru":"precision, exactness","ex":"Хоро́ший перево́д тре́бует то́чности."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each bookish false friend to what it REALLY means — not its English look-alike.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word on the left onto its true meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["претендова́ть","to lay claim to, aspire to — not «pretend»"],["актуа́льный","topical, relevant, current — not «actual»"],["патети́ческий","lofty, full of pathos — not «pathetic»"],["корре́ктный","polite, proper in manner — not «correct»"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The C2 translator's core move is to —","opts":["substitute each word for its dictionary match","render the meaning — переда́ть, not подста́вить","keep the source word order at all costs"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он … на пре́мьерский пост» — “he lays claim to the premier's post”. Which verb?","opts":["претенду́ет","притворя́ется","претворя́ет"],"answer":0},{"q":"«актуа́льный» means —","opts":["actual, real","accurate, exact","topical, relevant, current"],"answer":2},{"q":"An ironic line rendered flat and literal is —","opts":["a mistranslation — the charge was the meaning","fine, as long as the words match","automatically more precise"],"answer":0},{"q":"«тоска́» in translation is best —","opts":["replaced by “boredom”","unpacked — no single English word holds it","left out to save space"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short meditation on the craft, the way Эмма would frame it to a beginner. Read each line aloud and feel where fidelity really lives.","title":"Сло́во в сло́во — ещё не перево́д","sentences":["Плохо́й перево́дчик перено́сит слова́ — хоро́ший перено́сит смысл.","Е́сли в по́длиннике зву́чит иро́ния, а в перево́де её нет, перево́дчик потеря́л гла́вное.","Есть слова́ — тоска́, по́шлость, — кото́рые нельзя́ подмени́ть одни́м чужи́м сло́вом.","Их не перево́дят, а развора́чивают: объясня́ют, обхо́дят, передаю́т наме́ком.","То́чность здесь не в бу́кве, а в том, что чита́тель почу́вствует то же са́мое.","Вот почему́ перево́дчик — не раб словаря́, а сопе́рник а́втора."],"translation":"A bad translator carries words across — a good one carries meaning. If irony sounds in the original and it's gone from the translation, the translator has lost the main thing. There are words — тоска, пошлость — that can't be replaced by a single foreign word. You don't translate them, you unpack them: explain, go around, render by hint. Precision here isn't in the letter, but in the reader feeling the very same thing. That is why a translator is not the slave of a dictionary, but a rival of the author."},"essay":{"intro":"Now you render, not swap.","prompt":"Take one short, loaded English line — something ironic, or colloquial, or carrying a feeling with no one-word Russian match. Render it into Russian TWICE: once <b>word for word</b> (the literal, dead version), once <b>faithfully</b> (keeping the tone, the register, the irony, the charge). Then say in a phrase what the faithful version saved that the literal one lost. 3–4 sentences.","hint":"Ask the translator's question: what does this line DO to its reader — make them laugh, sting them, move them? Your faithful version must do the same, even if not one word matches 🙂","example":"Бу́квально: «Спаси́бо большо́е» — е́сли по́нял в лоб. То́чно: «Ну спаси́бо, удружи́л» — е́сли на де́ле э́то упрёк. Второ́е сохраня́ет иро́нию, кото́рую пе́рвое убива́ет напова́л. Перево́дчик передаёт не слова́, а тот уда́р, кото́рый они́ несу́т."}},"27":{"day":27,"week":"04","themeRu":"The essay with a voice","themeEn":"Эссе́ с со́бственным го́лосом","intro":"Эмма reads two essays for a magazine. Both are flawless — clean grammar, tidy paragraphs, not an error between them. She recommends one and forgets the other by lunch. The difference isn't correctness; it's that one had a <b>voice</b> and the other didn't. Anyone can now write Russian that's right. Today you write Russian that could only be <i>yours</i>: a single, controlled register you bend on purpose — a wink of irony here, a rhetorical question there, an allusion dropped exactly where it lands. Эссе́ с со́бственным го́лосом — the essay with a voice.","introAudio":"Emma reads two essays for a magazine. Both are flawless — clean grammar, tidy paragraphs, not an error between them. She recommends one and forgets the other by lunch. The difference isn't correctness; it's that one had a voice and the other didn't. Anyone can now write Russian that's right. Today you write Russian that could only be yours: a single, controlled register you bend on purpose — a wink of irony here, a rhetorical question there, an allusion dropped exactly where it lands. 'Esse s sobstvennym golosom' — the essay with a voice.","goals":["See the leap from a «correct» essay to one with a <b>voice</b> — unmistakably yours","Hold one base register and <b>modulate on purpose</b> — irony, a rhetorical question, an allusion to the point","Land a strong <b>зачи́н</b> (opening) and a strong <b>концо́вка</b> (closing) — hook and resonance","Tell deliberate modulation from a register <b>slip</b> — control, not accident"],"learned":["Felt the leap from a correct essay to one with a voice","Held a base register and modulated it on purpose (irony / rhetorical question / allusion)","Built a strong opening and a resonant closing (зачи́н / концо́вка)","Told deliberate modulation apart from an accidental register slip"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you rendered someone else's meaning; today you put out your own. First, half a minute on the tools you'll modulate WITH — the rhetorical figure and the ironic turn you met earlier this month.","introAudio":"Yesterday you rendered someone else's meaning; today you put out your own. First, half a minute on the tools you'll modulate with — the rhetorical figure and the ironic turn you met earlier this month.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> name the figure. This is week 3 — an old tool.","q":"«Я не пла́кал. Я не проси́л. Я молча́л.» — which rhetorical figure?","opts":["ана́фора (same word begins each part)","антите́за (two ideas opposed)","града́ция (rising intensity)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The repeated «Я не…» at the head of each clause is <b>ана́фора</b> — one of the figures you'll now use to give an essay its beat."},{"type":"choice","q":"After a friend spills coffee on your laptop: «Ну ты и молоде́ц, ничего́ не ска́жешь!» This is —","opts":["sincere praise","иро́ния — it means the opposite","a neutral statement"],"answer":1,"explain":"The words praise; the situation reverses them — that's <b>иро́ния</b> (week 2). A voice modulates with turns exactly like this."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the rhetorical question.","q":"Build a rhetorical question: “Who among us hasn't made mistakes?”","want":"Кто из нас не ошиба́лся?","answer":"Кто из нас не ошиба́лся?"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Flawless isn't the same as yours","title":"A <em>correct</em> essay anyone can write — a <em>voice</em> is yours alone","paras":["An essay can be grammatically perfect, cleanly organised, argued without a slip — and still be <b>безли́кое</b>: faceless. You read it, you nod, you forget it. Correctness is the floor now; everyone in this room clears it. What separates one essay from a thousand identical ones is a person audible behind the words.","That person is a <b>voice</b> — and a voice is just the sum of recognisable choices: which word you reach for when three would do, how long you let a sentence run before you cut it short, what you find worth an ironic aside, what you refuse to spell out. None of it is decoration. It's you, deciding, on every line.","So the discipline of the day is double. A voice is <i>not</i> chaos — it isn't breaking rules for the thrill of it. It's control that lets a person show through. The faceless essay hides the writer; the essay with a voice puts them, calmly, in plain sight."],"audio":"An essay can be grammatically perfect, cleanly organised, argued without a slip — and still be 'bezlikoye': faceless. You read it, you nod, you forget it. Correctness is the floor now; everyone in this room clears it. What separates one essay from a thousand identical ones is a person audible behind the words. That person is a voice — and a voice is just the sum of recognisable choices: which word you reach for when three would do, how long you let a sentence run before you cut it short, what you find worth an ironic aside, what you refuse to spell out. None of it is decoration. It's you, deciding, on every line. So the discipline of the day is double. A voice is not chaos — it isn't breaking rules for the thrill of it. It's control that lets a person show through. The faceless essay hides the writer; the essay with a voice puts them, calmly, in plain sight.","table":{"rows":[["ве́рный текст <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"верный текст\">🔊</button>","correct — grammar and logic are clean (the floor)"],["безли́кий текст <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"безликий текст\">🔊</button>","faceless — correct, tidy, and forgettable"],["текст с го́лосом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"текст с голосом\">🔊</button>","with a voice — a person audible in every choice"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Correct writing is common; a voice is rare.","en":"Гра́мотно пи́шут мно́гие — со свои́м го́лосом еди́ницы.","say":"Грамотно пишут многие — со своим голосом единицы."},{"ru":"A voice isn't breaking the rules — it's breaking them on purpose.","en":"Го́лос — не наруша́ть пра́вила, а наруша́ть их наме́ренно.","say":"Голос — не нарушать правила, а нарушать их намеренно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В настоя́щем эссе́ рассма́тривается пробле́ма вре́мени.","right":"Вре́мя — еди́нственное, что нельзя́ ни купи́ть, ни верну́ть.","why":"The first line is correct and utterly faceless — the impersonal научный throat-clear «рассма́тривается пробле́ма». A voice opens with a claim it's ready to defend. Same topic, but now someone is speaking."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap.","q":"A grammatically perfect, tidy, but forgettable essay is —","opts":["безли́кое — faceless","the C2 goal","impossible to write"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>безли́кое</b> — faceless. Correct is the floor now; a <b>voice</b> is what you add on top."},{"type":"choice","q":"An authorial voice is best described as —","opts":["random rule-breaking for effect","the most formal register you can manage","the sum of recognisable, controlled choices"],"answer":2,"explain":"A voice is <b>control that shows a person</b> — your words, your rhythm, your humour — not chaos and not stiffness."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Bend the register — don't break it","title":"One base register, <em>modulated</em> — not a register that wobbles","paras":["An essayist picks a base register — usually <b>публицисти́ческий</b> (the essay register: educated, engaged, a shade literary) — and holds it. That steady base is what lets everything else register as a <i>choice</i>. Without it there's nothing to modulate against.","Then, deliberately, you depart. A sudden colloquial word for a wink; a rhetorical question that pulls the reader by the sleeve; an allusion that trusts them to catch it. This is <b>модуля́ция</b> — a controlled step away that comes back home. Think of a singer bending a note on purpose and returning to pitch.","The danger is its twin. The very same colloquial word, arriving by <i>accident</i> — because you lost the register, not because you chose to leave it — is a <b>slip</b> (сбой), and the reader feels the floor drop. Same word, opposite effect: one is a bent note, the other is a wrong one. The whole skill is knowing, and showing, that you meant it."],"audio":"An essayist picks a base register — usually 'publitsisticheskiy', the essay register: educated, engaged, a shade literary — and holds it. That steady base is what lets everything else register as a choice. Without it there's nothing to modulate against. Then, deliberately, you depart. A sudden colloquial word for a wink; a rhetorical question that pulls the reader by the sleeve; an allusion that trusts them to catch it. This is 'modulyatsiya' — a controlled step away that comes back home. Think of a singer bending a note on purpose and returning to pitch. The danger is its twin. The very same colloquial word, arriving by accident — because you lost the register, not because you chose to leave it — is a slip, 'sboy', and the reader feels the floor drop. Same word, opposite effect: one is a bent note, the other is a wrong one. The whole skill is knowing, and showing, that you meant it.","examples":[{"ru":"Base (publicistic): the argument holds together, but the reader is left cold.","en":"Ба́за (публ.): «Аргуме́нт стро́ен, но чита́тель остаётся равноду́шен».","say":"База: аргумент строен, но читатель остаётся равнодушен."},{"ru":"Modulation (a wink): and here, frankly, the whole clever construction falls apart.","en":"Модуля́ция (иро́ния): «И тут, что называ́ется, вся у́мная констру́кция сы́плется».","say":"Модуляция: и тут, что называется, вся умная конструкция сыплется."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Иссле́дование глубоко́ анализи́рует пробле́му, но места́ми а́втор открове́нно тупи́т.","right":"Иссле́дование глубоко́ анализи́рует пробле́му, хотя́ места́ми а́втор я́вно теря́ет нить.","why":"«тупи́т» is street slang crashing into a bookish, analytical base <i>by accident</i> — a register <b>slip</b> (сбой), not a modulation. If you didn't set it up and mean it, keep the neutral «теря́ет нить». The reader has to feel a choice, not a stumble."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A colloquial word you drop in <b>on purpose</b>, then return to your base register, is —","opts":["a register slip (сбой)","a deliberate modulation (модуля́ция)","always a mistake"],"answer":1,"explain":"That's <b>модуля́ция</b> — a controlled departure that comes home. The same word by accident would be a <b>сбой</b> (slip)."},{"type":"choice","q":"What tells a reader a colloquial jab was modulation, not a slip?","opts":["it's the longest word in the sentence","everything around it holds a steady base register","it appears in the first line"],"answer":1,"explain":"A steady base is what makes the departure read as deliberate. Wobble everywhere and the reader can't tell a choice from an accident."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Open with a hook, close with a resonance","title":"The <em>зачи́н</em> earns the read; the <em>концо́вка</em> stays with them","paras":["A voice announces itself in the first line. A <b>зачи́н</b> (opening) is a hook — a paradox, a sharp image, a question, a well-known line turned on its head — and never «В настоя́щем эссе́ я хочу́ рассказа́ть о…», the faceless throat-clear that promises a faceless text. If the first sentence doesn't make the reader want the second, nothing after it matters.","The <b>концо́вка</b> (close) is where the essay is won or lost. Don't summarise — resonate. Circle back to the opening image so the essay closes like a ring; or land a short, bare sentence right after a long one, so the silence after it does the work. A reader forgives a soft middle; they never forgive a limp last line.","Beginning and end carry more voice, sentence for sentence, than the whole body between them. Write them last, write them hardest, read them aloud."],"audio":"A voice announces itself in the first line. A 'zachin', an opening, is a hook — a paradox, a sharp image, a question, a well-known line turned on its head — and never 'V nastoyashchem esse ya khochu rasskazat o...', the faceless throat-clear that promises a faceless text. If the first sentence doesn't make the reader want the second, nothing after it matters. The 'kontsovka', the close, is where the essay is won or lost. Don't summarise — resonate. Circle back to the opening image so the essay closes like a ring; or land a short, bare sentence right after a long one, so the silence after it does the work. A reader forgives a soft middle; they never forgive a limp last line. Beginning and end carry more voice, sentence for sentence, than the whole body between them. Write them last, write them hardest, read them aloud.","examples":[{"ru":"A hook opening: we spend our whole life saving time — and lose it all the same.","en":"Зачи́н-крючо́к: «Всю жизнь мы копи́м вре́мя — и всё равно́ теря́ем его́».","say":"Зачин-крючок: всю жизнь мы копим время — и всё равно теряем его."},{"ru":"A resonant close: a short line after a long one — so it was never about time at all.","en":"Си́льная концо́вка: «Зна́чит, де́ло бы́ло не во вре́мени».","say":"Сильная концовка: значит, дело было не во времени."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В э́том эссе́ я хочу́ рассказа́ть о том, как ва́жно вре́мя.","right":"Вре́мя не отдаёт долго́в. О́тнятый час не вернётся ни за каки́е де́ньги.","why":"«В э́том эссе́ я хочу́ рассказа́ть о…» is the faceless opening — it announces the topic and kills the voice. Open with a claim, an image, a paradox: make the reader lean in, not check the word count."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is a strong <b>зачи́н</b> for an essay on time?","opts":["«В настоя́щем эссе́ я хочу́ рассказа́ть о вре́мени.»","«Те́ма вре́мени о́чень актуа́льна в на́ши дни.»","«Вре́мя — еди́нственное, чего́ нельзя́ ни купи́ть, ни верну́ть.»"],"answer":2,"explain":"A paradox / sharp claim hooks the reader. The throat-clearing «я хочу́ рассказа́ть» and the flat «о́чень актуа́льна» both promise a faceless text."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read this close so the short last line lands hard after the long one.","target":"Мы спо́рим о слова́х, забыва́я о смы́сле, — а вре́мя идёт. И его́ не верну́ть.","sub":"let the long clause run, then drop the last three words like a stone","want":"мы спорим о словах забывая о смысле — а время идёт. и его не вернуть"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · How a voice is built — and what's next","title":"Rhythm, stance, restraint — then the whole range","paras":["The method fits on a card. One: a <b>stance</b> you actually hold — an essay is a person thinking, not a fence sat on. Two: <b>rhythm</b> — alternate the long, controlled пери́од (period) with a short, ру́бленая (chopped) phrase that lands; monotony of length is the quiet killer of voice. Three: <b>restraint</b> — one ironic aside is a voice, five is a tic. Four: read it aloud, because a voice is something you can hear.","Notice that everything you modulate <i>with</i> — the irony, the rhetorical figure, the allusion — you already own from earlier this month. Today only changed the job: not recognising these tools in others, but wielding them, sparingly, as yours.","Tomorrow gives you the raw material for the boldest modulations: the whole vertical range of Russian — jargon, slang, dialect — and the taste to reach down into it without falling in. Then day 29 pulls every thread into one native-grade text, and day 30 is the capstone: the whole road, A1 to here, in the voice you've been building."],"audio":"The method fits on a card. One: a stance you actually hold — an essay is a person thinking, not a fence sat on. Two: rhythm — alternate the long, controlled 'period' with a short, chopped 'rublenaya' phrase that lands; monotony of length is the quiet killer of voice. Three: restraint — one ironic aside is a voice, five is a tic. Four: read it aloud, because a voice is something you can hear. Notice that everything you modulate with — the irony, the rhetorical figure, the allusion — you already own from earlier this month. Today only changed the job: not recognising these tools in others, but wielding them, sparingly, as yours. Tomorrow gives you the raw material for the boldest modulations: the whole vertical range of Russian — jargon, slang, dialect — and the taste to reach down into it without falling in. Then day 29 pulls every thread into one native-grade text, and day 30 is the capstone: the whole road, A1 to here, in the voice you've been building.","examples":[{"ru":"Rhythm: after a long, coiling sentence — a short one. It hits.","en":"Ритм: по́сле дли́нной, петля́ющей фра́зы — коро́ткая. Она́ бьёт.","say":"Ритм: после длинной, петляющей фразы — короткая. Она бьёт."},{"ru":"One irony is a voice, five in a row are a nervous tic.","en":"Одна́ иро́ния — го́лос, пять подря́д — не́рвный тик.","say":"Одна ирония — голос, пять подряд — нервный тик."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Эссе́ на одни́х дли́нных, ро́вных, оди́наковых предложе́ниях без переды́шки.","right":"Дли́нный пери́од — а пото́м коро́ткая фра́за. Ды́шит.","why":"Monotone length flattens even correct prose into a drone. Voice needs <b>rhythm</b>: let a period run, then cut short. The ear hears the breath — that's the person."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"How much modulation makes a voice?","opts":["as much as possible — pile it on","used sparingly — one ironic aside is a voice, five a tic","none — keep it perfectly flat throughout"],"answer":1,"explain":"Restraint is the taste. One well-placed turn reads as a person; a shower of them reads as a nervous habit."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The Russian for a text's <b>opening</b> — the hook that earns the read (hint: зач…):","accept":["зачин"],"placeholder":"зач…","explain":"<b>зачи́н</b> — the opening. Its partner is the <b>концо́вка</b>, the close that resonates instead of summarising."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя has written her first opinion piece and asks Эмма to look. It's clean — and Эмма's advice isn't about errors. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Оши́бок нет, ты сказа́ла. Почему́ тогда́ ты недово́льна?","ru":"No errors, you said. So why aren't you happy with it?"},{"who":"A","en":"Оши́бок нет — и лица́ нет. Про́читал и забы́л. Всё ве́рно, а чьё э́то?","ru":"No errors — and no face either. You read it and forget it. It's all correct, but whose is it?"},{"who":"B","en":"А как сде́лать, что́бы бы́ло «моё»?","ru":"And how do I make it «mine»?"},{"who":"A","en":"Начни́ не с «в э́том эссе́ я хочу́…», а с уда́ра. Вопро́с, о́браз, парадо́кс — что́-то твоё.","ru":"Start not with «in this essay I want to…» but with a punch. A question, an image, a paradox — something of yours."},{"who":"B","en":"А е́сли посереди́не я вдруг пошучу́ — э́то не собьёт стиль?","ru":"And if I suddenly crack a joke in the middle — won't that break the style?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не собьёт, е́сли ты пошути́ла наро́чно и верну́лась. Собьёт — е́сли сорвала́сь неча́янно.","ru":"It won't, if you joked on purpose and came back. It will, if you slipped by accident."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, го́лос — э́то не наруша́ть пра́вила, а наруша́ть их со смы́слом?","ru":"So a voice isn't breaking the rules — it's breaking them with intent?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Пра́вильно пи́шут все. А вот так, что́бы бы́ло слы́шно тебя́, — э́то уже́ мастерство́.","ru":"Exactly. Everyone writes correctly. But writing so that you can be heard — that's already mastery."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the essayist's desk — building a voice and controlling its register. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"эссе́","ru":"essay","ex":"Хоро́шее эссе́ узнаётся с пе́рвой строки́."},{"en":"го́лос","ru":"voice (an author's)","ex":"У ка́ждого настоя́щего а́втора свой го́лос."},{"en":"со́бственный","ru":"one's own","ex":"Пиши́ свои́м со́бственным го́лосом."},{"en":"зачи́н","ru":"opening (of a text)","ex":"Си́льный зачи́н — уже́ полде́ла."},{"en":"концо́вка","ru":"ending, close","ex":"Концо́вка должна́ звуча́ть, а не переска́зывать."},{"en":"публицисти́ческий","ru":"publicistic (the essay register)","ex":"Публицисти́ческий стиль допуска́ет иро́нию."},{"en":"модуля́ция","ru":"modulation (a deliberate register shift)","ex":"Иро́ния здесь — созна́тельная модуля́ция."},{"en":"безли́кий","ru":"faceless, impersonal","ex":"Гра́мотный, но безли́кий текст."},{"en":"а́вторский","ru":"authorial","ex":"Э́то сра́зу узнава́емый а́вторский го́лос."},{"en":"наме́ренный","ru":"deliberate, intentional","ex":"Э́то наме́ренный сдвиг, а не сбой."},{"en":"ру́бленый","ru":"chopped, short (of a phrase)","ex":"По́сле пери́ода — коро́ткая, ру́бленая фра́за."},{"en":"иро́ния","ru":"irony","ex":"Одна́ иро́ния — го́лос, пять — уже́ тик."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each essayist's term to what it does on the page.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its job — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["зачи́н","the opening — a hook that earns the read"],["концо́вка","the close — a resonance that stays, not a summary"],["модуля́ция","a deliberate bend of register — irony, a question, an allusion"],["безли́кий","faceless — correct, tidy, and forgettable"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A correct, tidy essay that reads like everyone else's is —","opts":["the C2 goal","безли́кое — faceless","grammatically wrong"],"answer":1},{"q":"A colloquial word dropped in on purpose and then abandoned as you return to your base register is —","opts":["модуля́ция — a deliberate bend","сбой — an accidental slip","always an error"],"answer":0},{"q":"The best <b>зачи́н</b> (opening) for an essay —","opts":["announces the topic: «В э́том эссе́ я расскажу́ о…»","states the topic is «о́чень актуа́льна»","hooks with a claim, image, question or paradox"],"answer":2},{"q":"A strong <b>концо́вка</b> (close) should —","opts":["resonate — circle back or land a short line","neatly summarise all the points made","introduce a brand-new argument"],"answer":0},{"q":"How much modulation makes a voice, not a tic?","opts":["as much as you can fit","none at all","sparingly — a turn or two that you meant"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short piece on voice — and it practises what it preaches: one steady register, with a rhetorical question modulated in and a short line to close. Read it aloud.","title":"Го́лос — э́то вы́бор","sentences":["Гра́мотно писа́ть уме́ет ка́ждый образо́ванный челове́к — и потому́ одна́ гра́мотность ещё не го́лос.","Го́лос начина́ется там, где а́втор выбира́ет: э́то сло́во, а не сосе́днее; здесь — коро́ткая фра́за, а да́льше — дли́нный пери́од.","Мо́жно ли писа́ть без лица́? Мо́жно. Но заче́м тогда́ вообще́ бра́ться за перо́?","Хоро́ший эссеи́ст де́ржит оди́н реги́стр — и наруша́ет его́ то́лько там, где хо́чет, а не там, где сорва́лся.","Иро́ния, вопро́с к чита́телю, аллю́зия к ме́сту — вот чем го́лос отлича́ется от про́сто ве́рного те́кста.","И е́сли пе́рвая фра́за не заста́вила чита́ть да́льше — остально́е уже́ непра́вда."],"translation":"Any educated person can write correctly — and that is exactly why correctness alone is not yet a voice. A voice begins where the author chooses: this word and not the neighbouring one; a short phrase here, a long period there. Can one write without a face? One can. But then why pick up the pen at all? A good essayist holds a single register — and breaks it only where they want to, not where they slipped. Irony, a question to the reader, an allusion in the right place — that is what separates a voice from a merely correct text. And if the first sentence hasn't made you read on, everything after it is already a lie."},"essay":{"intro":"Now write with a voice.","prompt":"Pick any small claim you actually believe. Write its opening line TWICE: once <b>faceless</b> (the «В э́том эссе́ я хочу́…» throat-clear), once <b>with a voice</b> — a hook: a paradox, a sharp image, a question, or a known line turned on its head. Then name the one modulation you'd use later in the piece (an ironic aside? a rhetorical question? an allusion?). 3–4 sentences.","hint":"Read both openings aloud. The faceless one tells the reader what's coming; the voiced one makes them lean in. Voice is audible — trust your ear 🙂","example":"Безли́ко: «В э́том эссе́ я хочу́ рассказа́ть о том, как мы теря́ем вре́мя». С го́лосом: «Мы э́кономим вре́мя всю жизнь — и всё равно́ умира́ем в долга́х». Да́льше я оди́н раз позво́лю себе́ иро́нию: назову́ на́ши «поле́зные» приложе́ния для эконо́мии вре́мени гла́вными его́ во́рами. Оди́н реги́стр, оди́н наме́ренный уко́л — и текст стано́вится мои́м."}},"28":{"day":28,"week":"04","themeRu":"The whole range of Russian","themeEn":"Весь диапазо́н: жарго́н, сленг, диале́кт","intro":"At the next table in Ле́на's café two students talk fast: «прико́льно», «доста́ло», «пойдём тусова́ться». Эмма catches every word — and would use none of them in the book review she's writing at her own table. That's the whole day. A master of Russian isn't the person who knows the most slang; it's the person who <b>understands the entire vertical</b> — bookish heights, neutral middle, colloquial floor, professional jargon, regional colour — and reaches into any layer <i>on purpose</i>, in measured doses. Весь диапазо́н: to hear all of it, and to switch with taste.","introAudio":"At the next table in Lena's café two students talk fast: 'prikolno', 'dostalo', 'poydyom tusovatsya'. Emma catches every word — and would use none of them in the book review she's writing at her own table. That's the whole day. A master of Russian isn't the person who knows the most slang; it's the person who understands the entire vertical — bookish heights, neutral middle, colloquial floor, professional jargon, regional colour — and reaches into any layer on purpose, in measured doses. 'Ves diapazon': to hear all of it, and to switch with taste.","goals":["Hear the whole vertical of Russian, top to bottom — and understand every layer, even the ones you'd never use","Recognise the lowered floor: <b>жарго́н</b> (jargon), <b>сленг</b> (slang), <b>просторе́чие</b> (substandard speech), dialect traces","Switch registers (code-switching) <b>on purpose and in small doses</b> — знать сленг ≠ им сы́пать","Catch the two opposite failures: not hearing the low layer, and over-using it once learned"],"learned":["Heard the whole vertical of Russian and understood every layer","Recognised jargon, slang, просторечие and dialect traces (о́канье, ба́ско)","Switched register on purpose, in measured doses — never sprinkling slang for its own sake","Avoided both failures: missing the low layer and over-using it"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday a voice modulated its register on purpose. Today you meet the raw material of those modulations — the whole range, high to low. First, half a minute back on the register scale you've owned since C1.","introAudio":"Yesterday a voice modulated its register on purpose. Today you meet the raw material of those modulations — the whole range, high to low. First, half a minute back on the register scale you've owned since C1.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is C1 register — an old friend.","q":"In an official document, “money” should be —","opts":["де́нежные сре́дства","ба́бки","деньжа́та"],"answer":0,"explain":"Official → <b>де́нежные сре́дства</b>. ба́бки is slang, деньжа́та is colloquial. The register scale, exactly as at C1."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is <b>просторе́чие</b> (substandard) rather than merely casual?","opts":["класть","ло́жить","положи́ть"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ло́жить</b> is substandard — an educated native never uses it. класть / положи́ть are the norm. Casual isn't the same as outside-the-norm — today's whole distinction."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the neutral version.","q":"Put the slang «меня́ э́то доста́ло» into neutral Russian: “I'm tired of this.”","want":"мне э́то надое́ло","answer":"мне э́то надое́ло"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Understand everything, top to bottom","title":"The master hears the <em>whole vertical</em> — and lives mostly in the middle","paras":["Picture Russian as a tall building. Top floors: <b>высо́кое / кни́жное</b> — lofty and bookish («взира́ть» for look, «дабы́» for so that). The middle floor, where most of life happens: <b>нейтра́льное</b> («смотре́ть», «что́бы»). Below it, <b>разгово́рное</b> — casual but still standard, the speech of any educated person at home. Lower still, <b>просторе́чие</b> — substandard, uneducated speech, a different thing from casual. And off to the sides: <b>сленг</b> and <b>жарго́н</b> (street and in-group), and <b>диале́кт</b> (regional).","The C2 target for all of it is one word: <b>comprehension</b>. You understand a line from any floor instantly — the professor, the teenager, the village grandmother. There is no ceiling on understanding, and at this level a word you <i>don't</i> know because it's «too low» is a real gap, not a virtue.","Producing is a separate question, and taste answers it. You understand the whole building; you keep your own quarters in the middle and visit the other floors on purpose. Knowing a lowered word is simply not the same act as using one."],"audio":"Picture Russian as a tall building. Top floors: 'vysokoye', 'knizhnoye' — lofty and bookish, 'vzirat' for look, 'daby' for so that. The middle floor, where most of life happens: 'neytralnoye', 'smotret', 'chtoby'. Below it, 'razgovornoye' — casual but still standard, the speech of any educated person at home. Lower still, 'prostorechiye' — substandard, uneducated speech, a different thing from casual. And off to the sides: 'sleng' and 'zhargon', street and in-group, and 'dialekt', regional. The C2 target for all of it is one word: comprehension. You understand a line from any floor instantly — the professor, the teenager, the village grandmother. There is no ceiling on understanding, and at this level a word you don't know because it's 'too low' is a real gap, not a virtue. Producing is a separate question, and taste answers it. You understand the whole building; you keep your own quarters in the middle and visit the other floors on purpose. Knowing a lowered word is simply not the same act as using one.","table":{"rows":[["высо́кое / кни́жное <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"высокое, книжное\">🔊</button>","lofty / bookish — «взира́ть», «дабы́», «сокрове́нный»"],["нейтра́льное <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нейтральное\">🔊</button>","neutral — «смотре́ть», «что́бы» — home base"],["разгово́рное <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"разговорное\">🔊</button>","colloquial — casual but still standard («гляде́ть», «кла́ссно»)"],["просторе́чие <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"просторечие\">🔊</button>","substandard — «ло́жить», «и́хний» — uneducated, not just casual"],["жарго́н / сленг / диале́кт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жаргон, сленг, диалект\">🔊</button>","in-group / street / regional — «рыба́», «тусо́вка», «ба́ско»"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"A master understands every floor — and chooses where to stand.","en":"Ма́стер понима́ет ка́ждый эта́ж — и сам выбира́ет, где стоя́ть.","say":"Мастер понимает каждый этаж — и сам выбирает, где стоять."},{"ru":"Knowing a low word isn't the same as using it.","en":"Знать сни́женное сло́во — не то же, что употребля́ть его́.","say":"Знать сниженное слово — не то же, что употреблять его."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он говори́т на сле́нге — зна́чит, э́то непра́вильный ру́сский.","right":"Он говори́т на сле́нге — э́то то́же ру́сский, про́сто друго́й эта́ж.","why":"Slang, просторечие and dialect aren't «wrong Russian» — they're real floors of the same building, and a master understands them all. What's «wrong» is only using the wrong floor for the room. Comprehension covers the whole vertical; production is chosen by taste."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap.","q":"At C2, the aim for the whole vertical of Russian — high to street — is —","opts":["to speak in every layer all the time","to avoid the low layers entirely","to understand all of it; to speak only your measured part"],"answer":2,"explain":"Comprehension has no ceiling — the professor, the teenager and the village grandmother alike. What you <i>produce</i> is chosen by taste."},{"type":"choice","q":"«просторе́чие» differs from «разгово́рное» because it —","opts":["marks a speaker as outside the standard, not just casual","is simply a more polite way to talk","means exactly the same thing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Colloquial (разгово́рное) is standard-but-relaxed — anyone educated uses it at home. Просторе́чие («ло́жить», «и́хний») marks a speaker as outside the norm. Hearing the difference is a C2 skill."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Slang, jargon, and the words that date","title":"<em>Сленг и жарго́н</em> — understand it all, wear it lightly","paras":["The colloquial-and-slang floor is where language moves fastest. A warning built in: <b>slang dates</b>. Last decade's cool word is this decade's cringe, and a foreigner reaching for yesterday's slang sounds odder than one who stays neutral. So the safe stance is: understand it fully, use it rarely, and never chase the very newest word.","Durable colloquial pieces worth recognising: <b>тусо́вка / ту́сить</b> (a hangout / to hang out), <b>прико́л / прико́льный</b> (a laugh / cool, funny), <b>заморо́чки</b> (fuss, hassle, needless complications), <b>халя́ва</b> (a freebie, something for nothing). These aren't substandard — they're the relaxed speech of educated people among friends.","Separate from slang is <b>жарго́н</b> — professional in-group shorthand. In Эмма's publishing world: <b>рыба́</b> (dummy placeholder text), <b>вы́читка</b> (a proofreading pass), <b>самотёк</b> (the slush pile of unsolicited manuscripts). <b>Жарг. (изд.):</b> «В самотёке одна́ рыба́, но попада́ются и жемчу́жины». Jargon is efficient and precise inside the group, and completely opaque outside it — that's the point of it."],"audio":"The colloquial-and-slang floor is where language moves fastest. A warning built in: slang dates. Last decade's cool word is this decade's cringe, and a foreigner reaching for yesterday's slang sounds odder than one who stays neutral. So the safe stance is: understand it fully, use it rarely, and never chase the very newest word. Durable colloquial pieces worth recognising: 'tusovka' and 'tusit', a hangout and to hang out; 'prikol', 'prikolnyy', a laugh, cool, funny; 'zamorochki', fuss, hassle, needless complications; 'halyava', a freebie, something for nothing. These aren't substandard — they're the relaxed speech of educated people among friends. Separate from slang is 'zhargon' — professional in-group shorthand. In Emma's publishing world: 'ryba', dummy placeholder text; 'vychitka', a proofreading pass; 'samotyok', the slush pile of unsolicited manuscripts. Publishing jargon: 'v samotyoke odna ryba, no popadayutsya i zhemchuzhiny'. Jargon is efficient and precise inside the group, and completely opaque outside it — that's the point of it.","table":{"rows":[["тусо́вка / ту́сить <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тусовка, тусить\">🔊</button>","colloquial — a hangout / to hang out"],["прико́л / прико́льный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прикол, прикольный\">🔊</button>","colloquial — a laugh, a funny thing / cool"],["заморо́чки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"заморочки\">🔊</button>","colloquial — fuss, complications, hassle"],["халя́ва <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"халява\">🔊</button>","slang — a freebie, something for nothing"],["рыба́ / самотёк <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рыба, самотёк\">🔊</button>","pro jargon (publishing) — dummy text / the slush pile"]],"star":4},"examples":[{"ru":"Slang: understand all of it, use almost none of it.","en":"Сленг: понима́й весь, употребля́й почти́ никако́й.","say":"Сленг: понимай весь, употребляй почти никакой."},{"ru":"Jargon (publishing): the slush pile is mostly filler, but pearls do turn up.","en":"Жарг. (изд.): «В самотёке одна́ рыба́, но попада́ются и жемчу́жины».","say":"В самотёке одна рыба, но попадаются и жемчужины."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Уважа́емые колле́ги, спаси́бо за клёвую и прико́льную рабо́ту.","right":"Уважа́емые колле́ги, спаси́бо за отли́чную и увлека́тельную рабо́ту.","why":"«клёвую», «прико́льную» are colloquial-slang crashing into a formal address — you understand the words, but they're the wrong floor for «уважа́емые колле́ги». Neutral-positive («отли́чную», «увлека́тельную») fits the room. Knowing the slang was never the problem; placing it was."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Professional <b>жарго́н</b> like «рыба́» (dummy text) or «самотёк» (slush pile) is —","opts":["efficient shorthand inside a group, opaque outside it","substandard, uneducated Russian","exactly the same thing as dialect"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Жарго́н</b> is in-group shorthand — precise and quick among insiders, baffling to everyone else. It isn't «wrong»; it's addressed to one specific room."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read this casual line the way a friend would — light and quick.","target":"Ве́чером тусо́вка у Анто́на — приходи́, бу́дет прико́льно.","sub":"relaxed and colloquial — this is a floor you visit, not where you live","want":"вечером тусовка у антона — приходи будет прикольно"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Substandard speech and regional colour","title":"<em>Просторе́чие</em> is a layer to read — not a mistake to copy","paras":["<b>Просторе́чие</b> is the speech of the uneducated urban layer — real, expressive, and crucially <i>not</i> the same as casual. Markers you must recognise on sight but never adopt: <b>ло́жить</b> (for класть), <b>и́хний</b> (for их), <b>зво́нит</b> with front stress (for звони́т), «оплати́ть <b>за</b> прое́зд» (for оплати́ть прое́зд). These tell you something social about a speaker; an educated native hears them as a marker, not as informality.","Off to the side stands <b>диале́кт</b> — regional Russian. You'll hear <b>о́канье</b> (the North keeping a full unstressed «о»: «мо-ло-ко́» not «ма-ла-ко́»), southern <b>гэ́канье</b> (a soft, fricative «г»), and local words like <b>ба́ско</b> (northern for «краси́во, хорошо́»). Dialect is geographic colour — it tells you <i>where</i> a person is from, the way просторечие tells you a social layer.","The C2 move is the same for both: read them as signals, keep your own Russian standard. The trap is «learning» a просторечие form as if it were merely relaxed style — importing an error while thinking you're loosening up."],"audio":"'Prostorechiye' is the speech of the uneducated urban layer — real, expressive, and crucially not the same as casual. Markers you must recognise on sight but never adopt: 'lozhit' for 'klast'; 'ikhniy' for 'ikh'; 'zvonit' with front stress for 'zvonit'; 'oplatit za proyezd' for 'oplatit proyezd'. These tell you something social about a speaker; an educated native hears them as a marker, not as informality. Off to the side stands 'dialekt' — regional Russian. You'll hear 'okanye', the North keeping a full unstressed o, 'moloko' not 'malako'; southern 'gekanye', a soft fricative g; and local words like 'basko', northern for beautiful, good. Dialect is geographic colour — it tells you where a person is from, the way prostorechiye tells you a social layer. The C2 move is the same for both: read them as signals, keep your own Russian standard. The trap is learning a prostorechiye form as if it were merely relaxed style — importing an error while thinking you're loosening up.","examples":[{"ru":"Substandard: “where do I put the books?” — never say it this way.","en":"Просторе́чие: «Куда́ ло́жить кни́ги?» — так говори́ть нельзя́.","say":"Просторечие: куда ложить книги? — так говорить нельзя."},{"ru":"Dialect (northern): “what a lovely house you have.”","en":"Диале́кт (се́вер): «Ба́ско у вас изба́, хозя́ева».","say":"Диалект: баско у вас изба, хозяева."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ты не зна́ешь, где ло́жить кни́ги? И где и́хние ве́щи?","right":"Ты не зна́ешь, куда́ класть кни́ги? И где их ве́щи?","why":"«ло́жить» and «и́хний» are textbook <b>просторе́чие</b> (substandard), not «informal» Russian — an educated native never uses them. The standard forms are <b>класть</b> (to put) and <b>их</b> (their). Recognise просторечие; never adopt it as your casual layer."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is <b>просторе́чие</b> — a marker to recognise but never adopt?","opts":["класть на по́лку","ло́жить на по́лку","положи́ть на по́лку"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ло́жить</b> is substandard; the norm is <b>класть</b> (imperfective) / <b>положи́ть</b> (perfective). Educated Russians hear «ло́жить» as a social marker, not as casual style."},{"type":"choice","q":"«ба́ско» (for «краси́во, хорошо́») and northern о́канье are examples of —","opts":["жарго́на (in-group slang)","диале́кта (regional speech)","кни́жного сти́ля (bookish style)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regional pronunciation (о́канье) and local words (<b>ба́ско</b>) are <b>диале́кт</b> — geographic colour that tells you where a speaker is from."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Reach down on purpose — then integration","title":"<em>Переключа́ться</em> with taste — знать сленг ≠ им сы́пать","paras":["Now the whole point: <b>переключе́ние ко́дов</b> — code-switching. The master drops into a lower layer <i>deliberately</i>, for a precise effect. One slang word in an otherwise clean text lands like a single spice; a handful is a spilled rack. The register-scale you've owned since C1 plus the whole vertical you heard today are exactly what tomorrow's essay-voice modulates with.","Two opposite failures mark the non-native. One: not hearing the low layer at all — you miss half of a real conversation and every joke in it. Two: having learned some slang, sprinkling it everywhere to «sound Russian» — which, precisely, sounds the opposite, because natives don't sprinkle; they place.","The rule is a dose. <b>Разг.:</b> «Прое́кт, че́стно говоря́, — та ещё моро́ка». One relaxed word inside a controlled sentence — a voice. Ten — a mess. Tomorrow: everything at once, one native-grade text; and the day after, the capstone — the whole road in your own voice."],"audio":"Now the whole point: 'pereklyucheniye kodov' — code-switching. The master drops into a lower layer deliberately, for a precise effect. One slang word in an otherwise clean text lands like a single spice; a handful is a spilled rack. The register-scale you've owned since C1 plus the whole vertical you heard today are exactly what tomorrow's essay-voice modulates with. Two opposite failures mark the non-native. One: not hearing the low layer at all — you miss half of a real conversation and every joke in it. Two: having learned some slang, sprinkling it everywhere to 'sound Russian' — which, precisely, sounds the opposite, because natives don't sprinkle; they place. The rule is a dose. Colloquial: 'proyekt, chestno govorya, — ta yeshchyo moroka'. One relaxed word inside a controlled sentence — a voice. Ten — a mess. Tomorrow: everything at once, one native-grade text; and the day after, the capstone — the whole road in your own voice.","examples":[{"ru":"Colloquial, one dose: the project, honestly, is a real bother.","en":"Разг.: «Прое́кт, че́стно говоря́, — та ещё моро́ка».","say":"Проект, честно говоря, — та ещё морока."},{"ru":"Knowing the slang isn't the skill — placing it is.","en":"Уме́ние — не знать сленг, а ста́вить его́ к ме́сту.","say":"Умение — не знать сленг, а ставить его к месту."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Рома́н кла́ссный, сюже́т огонь, геро́й вообще́ бо́мба, ну про́сто ва́у.","right":"Рома́н удиви́тельно живо́й, и оди́н геро́й — про́сто нахо́дка.","why":"Slang piled on to «sound native» does the opposite. A single well-placed relaxed word can lift a review; a shower of «огонь / бо́мба / ва́у» buries the judgement and reads as trying too hard. Natives place; they don't sprinkle."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Знать сленг ≠ им сы́пать» means —","opts":["never learn slang at all","understand the low layer, but reach into it only on purpose and in small doses","use as much slang as possible to sound native"],"answer":1,"explain":"Taste is the dose. One slang word can spice a text; a handful spoils it. The over-user sounds <i>less</i> native, not more."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The word for deliberately dropping between registers — “code-switching / switching” (hint: переключ…):","accept":["переключение","переключение кодов"],"placeholder":"переключ…","explain":"<b>переключе́ние</b> (ко́дов) — code-switching. Done on purpose and in small doses, it's a mark of mastery; done constantly, it's noise."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ле́на, behind the café counter, teases Эмма for «talking like a book». Эмма explains why she hears all the slang but rarely uses it. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты понима́ешь всё, что говоря́т вон те студе́нты? Я полови́ны не разбира́ю.","ru":"Do you understand everything those students are saying? I catch half of it."},{"who":"A","en":"Понима́ю ка́ждое сло́во. Про́сто сама́ так не говорю́ — не моё э́то.","ru":"I understand every word. I just don't talk that way myself — it's not me."},{"who":"B","en":"Стра́нно: зна́ешь сленг, а сы́плешь им ре́дко. Почему́?","ru":"Strange: you know the slang, but you rarely sprinkle it. Why?"},{"who":"A","en":"Потому́ что носи́тель им не сы́плет — он его́ ста́вит. Одно́ сло́во к ме́сту — и хва́тит.","ru":"Because a native doesn't sprinkle it — they place it. One word in the right spot — and that's enough."},{"who":"B","en":"А «ло́жить», «и́хний» — э́то то́же про́сто разгово́рное?","ru":"And «ложить», «ихний» — is that just colloquial too?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, э́то уже́ просторе́чие. Понима́ть — да, повторя́ть — никогда́.","ru":"No, that's already substandard. Understand it — yes; repeat it — never."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, ты слы́шишь весь язы́к, от «дабы́» до «прико́ла»?","ru":"So you hear the whole language, from «дабы» to «прикол»?"},{"who":"A","en":"В э́том и де́ло. Слы́шать всё — а говори́ть свои́м го́лосом, со свои́м вку́сом.","ru":"That's the whole thing. Hear it all — but speak in your own voice, with your own taste."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the whole vertical — the layers of Russian and the art of moving between them. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"диапазо́н","ru":"range (the whole span)","ex":"Носи́тель владе́ет всем диапазо́ном языка́."},{"en":"жарго́н","ru":"jargon (in-group shorthand)","ex":"У ка́ждой профе́ссии свой жарго́н."},{"en":"сленг","ru":"slang","ex":"Сленг устарева́ет о́чень бы́стро."},{"en":"просторе́чие","ru":"substandard, uneducated speech","ex":"«Ло́жить» — э́то просторе́чие, а не разгово́рное."},{"en":"диале́кт","ru":"dialect (regional speech)","ex":"В его́ ре́чи слы́шен се́верный диале́кт."},{"en":"переключа́ться","ru":"to switch (between registers)","ex":"Ма́стер легко́ переключа́ется ме́жду реги́страми."},{"en":"сни́женный","ru":"lowered (of register)","ex":"Э́то сло́во из сни́женного пласта́."},{"en":"уме́стно","ru":"appropriately, to the point","ex":"Оди́н сленг здесь уме́стен, де́сять — нет."},{"en":"тусо́вка","ru":"a hangout, get-together (colloquial)","ex":"Ве́чером у друзе́й больша́я тусо́вка."},{"en":"заморо́чки","ru":"fuss, hassle, complications (colloquial)","ex":"Без ли́шних заморо́чек, про́сто сде́лай."},{"en":"о́канье","ru":"okanye (northern full unstressed «о»)","ex":"На Се́вере слы́шно я́сное о́канье."},{"en":"переключе́ние","ru":"switching, code-switching","ex":"Уме́стное переключе́ние — при́знак ма́стера."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each layer or move to what it is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its description — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["жарго́н","in-group shorthand — efficient inside, opaque outside"],["просторе́чие","substandard speech — marks a speaker outside the norm"],["диале́кт","regional colour — о́канье, ба́ско, local words"],["переключе́ние","code-switching — dropping a layer on purpose, in doses"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"At C2, understanding the low layers of Russian (slang, просторечие, dialect) is —","opts":["unnecessary — only the high style matters","essential — comprehension has no ceiling","a sign you talk badly"],"answer":1},{"q":"«ло́жить» and «и́хний» are —","opts":["просторе́чие — substandard, never to adopt","neutral, standard Russian","professional jargon"],"answer":0},{"q":"«рыба́» (dummy text) and «самотёк» (slush pile) in publishing are —","opts":["dialect words","просторе́чие","жарго́н — in-group shorthand"],"answer":2},{"q":"Northern о́канье and the word «ба́ско» are —","opts":["диале́кт — regional colour","slang that's dating fast","bookish, high style"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Знать сленг ≠ им сы́пать» tells you to —","opts":["avoid ever learning slang","use slang in every sentence","understand the low layer, but place it on purpose and in doses"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short reflection on range — from the top floor to the street. Read each line aloud and place it on the vertical.","title":"Слы́шать все этажи́","sentences":["Настоя́щее владе́ние языко́м — э́то не то́лько высо́кий стиль, но и у́лица, и ку́хня, и цехово́й жарго́н.","Ма́стер понима́ет и профе́ссора, и подро́стка, и дереве́нскую ба́бушку — все тро́е говоря́т по-ру́сски.","Но понима́ть — не зна́чит подража́ть: чужо́е сни́женное сло́во легко́ узна́ть и не́зачем повторя́ть.","Оди́н то́чный сленг в стро́гом те́ксте — как спе́ция; горсть — как испо́рченное блю́до.","Кто зна́ет весь диапазо́н, тот и промолчи́т к ме́сту, и сни́зит реги́стр к ме́сту.","Вот почему́ сленг легко́ вы́учить — и тру́дно употребля́ть со вку́сом."],"translation":"Real command of a language isn't only the high style, but also the street, the kitchen, and the trade jargon. A master understands the professor, the teenager and the village grandmother alike — all three are speaking Russian. But to understand is not to imitate: someone else's lowered word is easy to recognise and needless to repeat. One exact bit of slang in a strict text is like a single spice; a fistful is like a spoiled dish. Whoever knows the whole range knows both when to fall silent and when to drop the register. That is why slang is easy to learn — and hard to use with taste."},"essay":{"intro":"Now play the whole range yourself.","prompt":"Take one everyday message — say, telling a friend a plan fell through. Write it at THREE levels with the SAME content: <b>bookish</b> (высо́кое/кни́жное), <b>neutral</b> (нейтра́льное), and <b>colloquial/slang</b> (разгово́рное). Then say which one you'd actually send, to whom, and why. 3–4 sentences total.","hint":"Change only the register, not the facts. Notice how much the SAME news shifts as it moves down the floors — and that the «right» one depends entirely on who's reading 🙂","example":"Кни́жно: «Вы́нужден с сожале́нием сообщи́ть, что за́мысел не осуществи́лся». Нейтра́льно: «К сожале́нию, план не сложи́лся». Разг.: «Слу́шай, зате́я накры́лась». Дру́гу я напишу́ тре́тье — коро́тко и по-живо́му; нача́льнику пе́рвое; а нейтра́льное подойдёт почти́ кому́ уго́дно."}},"29":{"day":29,"week":"04","themeRu":"Putting it all together","themeEn":"Всё вме́сте: текст у́ровня носи́теля","intro":"Here's the strangest failure at this level. A text can be right on every single count — the exact word, an idiom in place, the register held and modulated, a rhetorical figure that lands, even an allusion dropped just so — and still read, as a whole, <i>almost</i> native. Every part passes; the sum doesn't quite. Today is that last inch. Not one more skill, but all of them at once under a single controlling taste — and then the editor's pass over your own text that catches the one word still wearing an accent. Всё вме́сте: a text at native level.","introAudio":"Here's the strangest failure at this level. A text can be right on every single count — the exact word, an idiom in place, the register held and modulated, a rhetorical figure that lands, even an allusion dropped just so — and still read, as a whole, almost native. Every part passes; the sum doesn't quite. Today is that last inch. Not one more skill, but all of them at once under a single controlling taste — and then the editor's pass over your own text that catches the one word still wearing an accent. 'Vsyo vmeste': a text at native level.","goals":["Bring every C2 thread together at once — exact word, subtext, idiom, figure, register + modulation, allusion","Feel the last trap: «correct on every point, yet the whole is still not quite native»","Run a self-edit (<b>саморедакту́ра</b>) with the editor's ear — catch the one word still «почти́ то»","Make the seams vanish — a text that reads as written, not assembled"],"learned":["Brought every C2 thread together into one text","Named the last trap: right in every part, not-quite-native as a whole","Ran a self-edit and caught the near-miss word","Made the seams vanish — assembled, but reading as native"],"review":{"intro":"Today is the gathering of everything. So the review is a quick round of the tools themselves — the exact word, the hidden charge, the meaning between the lines — before you fold them all into one text.","introAudio":"Today is the gathering of everything. So the review is a quick round of the tools themselves — the exact word, the hidden charge, the meaning between the lines — before you fold them all into one text.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the exact word. This is day 1 — the hunt where it all began.","q":"«Едва́ живо́й от го́ря, он … домо́й.» — which verb is exact?","opts":["плёлся","шага́л","семени́л"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barely alive with grief → <b>плёлся</b> (to drag oneself along). шага́л strides; семени́л minces. The day-1 lesson: the exact word, not the near one."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word carries a hidden negative charge — «infamous», not just «famous»?","opts":["знамени́тый","пресло́вутый","изве́стный"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>пресло́вутый</b> = «that notorious / so-called» — connotation (day 2). знамени́тый / изве́стный are neutral «famous / known»."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the idiom.","q":"Build the idiom for “to beat around the bush”:","want":"ходи́ть вокру́г да о́коло","answer":"ходи́ть вокру́г да о́коло"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Not one skill — all of them, together","title":"Integration: the <em>whole</em> is judged, not the parts","paras":["For a month you've installed instruments one at a time — precision, connotation, collocation, the shades of aspect, particles, subtext, irony, allusion, rhetoric, the whole register-range. Each was a separate lesson with a separate test. Today they stop being a list.","Native fluency isn't any one of these; it's all of them playing at once, governed by a single taste. And here is the last trap of the level: a text can score full marks on every item of a checklist and still fail as a whole. Why? Because a native doesn't <b>assemble</b> a sentence from correct parts — they <b>compose</b> it. The parts serve one voice, not the other way round.","So integration is a shift, not an addition. Not «one more skill» — the move from a row of correct decisions to a single coherent thing. It's the difference between a pile of right notes and a piece of music."],"audio":"For a month you've installed instruments one at a time — precision, connotation, collocation, the shades of aspect, particles, subtext, irony, allusion, rhetoric, the whole register-range. Each was a separate lesson with a separate test. Today they stop being a list. Native fluency isn't any one of these; it's all of them playing at once, governed by a single taste. And here is the last trap of the level: a text can score full marks on every item of a checklist and still fail as a whole. Why? Because a native doesn't assemble a sentence from correct parts — they compose it. The parts serve one voice, not the other way round. So integration is a shift, not an addition. Not 'one more skill' — the move from a row of correct decisions to a single coherent thing. It's the difference between a pile of right notes and a piece of music.","table":{"rows":[["пра́вильно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"правильно\">🔊</button>","correct — the grammar works (A1–B1)"],["уме́стно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уместно\">🔊</button>","appropriate — fits the room (C1)"],["то́чно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"точно\">🔊</button>","exact — the right shade in each word (C2, day 1)"],["це́льно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"цельно\">🔊</button>","whole — all of it as one voice (C2, today)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"A native doesn't assemble a phrase — they say it whole.","en":"Носи́тель не собира́ет фра́зу — он произно́сит её целико́м.","say":"Носитель не собирает фразу — он произносит её целиком."},{"ru":"Correct in every part, and still not quite one voice.","en":"Ве́рно в ка́ждой ча́сти — и всё же ещё не оди́н го́лос.","say":"Верно в каждой части — и всё же ещё не один голос."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Textbook-perfect essay where every sentence is correct but they don't add up to one voice.","right":"Оди́н го́лос, оди́н вкус — и все приёмы рабо́тают на це́лое.","why":"A checklist can be fully ticked and the text still reads «assembled». The fix isn't another correct part — it's subordinating every device to one voice. That's <b>це́льность</b> (coherence), the last quality native readers feel."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap.","q":"A text that is correct on every separate point can still —","opts":["never be written at all","always pass as native automatically","fail as a whole — a native composes, not assembles"],"answer":2,"explain":"Full marks on a checklist ≠ one coherent voice. That gap between «assembled» and «composed» is exactly today's work."},{"type":"choice","q":"Integration means —","opts":["all the skills at once, governed by a single taste","the plain sum of your correct moves","one more separate skill to add to the list"],"answer":0,"explain":"Not a list — a <b>сплав</b> (fusion) of every instrument, ruled by taste. That's the leap from near-native to native."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Run the checklist, then forget it","title":"Six questions a master asks — <em>without asking them</em>","paras":["To make integration teachable, make it conscious first. Over any sentence you write, six questions: (1) Is every word the <b>exact</b> one, not the near one? (2) Are the <b>collocations</b> native (оказа́ть влия́ние, not сде́лать)? (3) Is the <b>register</b> held — and any departure a modulation you meant? (4) Is the <b>subtext / irony</b> landing the way you intend? (5) Does each <b>figure or allusion</b> earn its place, or show off? (6) Does it <b>flow</b> — rhythm varied, seams hidden?","Run them deliberately today, one by one, slow. It will feel mechanical, and it should — that's the price of making them automatic.","Because the destination isn't a person forever ticking six boxes. It's an ear that has swallowed the list whole and now hears a wrong note before the conscious mind can name which box it failed. You run the checklist now so that one day you never have to again."],"audio":"To make integration teachable, make it conscious first. Over any sentence you write, six questions: one, is every word the exact one, not the near one? Two, are the collocations native — 'okazat vliyaniye', not 'sdelat'? Three, is the register held, and any departure a modulation you meant? Four, is the subtext or irony landing the way you intend? Five, does each figure or allusion earn its place, or show off? Six, does it flow — rhythm varied, seams hidden? Run them deliberately today, one by one, slow. It will feel mechanical, and it should — that's the price of making them automatic. Because the destination isn't a person forever ticking six boxes. It's an ear that has swallowed the list whole and now hears a wrong note before the conscious mind can name which box it failed. You run the checklist now so that one day you never have to again.","table":{"rows":[["то́чное сло́во? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"точное слово\">🔊</button>","the exact word, not the near-synonym (day 1)"],["своя́ коллока́ция? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"своя коллокация\">🔊</button>","native word-pairings, not calques (day 3)"],["реги́стр под контро́лем? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"регистр под контролем\">🔊</button>","held, and modulated only on purpose (day 27)"],["подте́кст и иро́ния? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"подтекст и ирония\">🔊</button>","the second layer landing right (week 2)"],["фигу́ра / аллю́зия к ме́сту? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"фигура, аллюзия к месту\">🔊</button>","each device earning its place (week 2–3)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Run the checklist by hand today — so your ear runs it tomorrow.","en":"Сего́дня прове́рь по спи́ску вручну́ю — что́бы за́втра проверя́л слух.","say":"Сегодня проверь по списку вручную — чтобы завтра проверял слух."},{"ru":"The ear hears a wrong note before you can name the rule.","en":"Слух слы́шит фальши́вую но́ту ра́ньше, чем ты назовёшь пра́вило.","say":"Слух слышит фальшивую ноту раньше, чем ты назовёшь правило."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ходить по списку и ставить галочки, но не слушать целое.","right":"Прове́рить ка́ждый пункт — и в конце́ прочита́ть всё вслух одни́м го́лосом.","why":"The checklist is a scaffold, not the building. Tick every box and you can still miss the one thing no box holds — whether the whole <i>sounds</i> like one person. Always end on the ear: read it aloud, all at once."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The six-question checklist (exact word? collocation? register? subtext? figure? flow?) is something a master —","opts":["runs consciously, forever, box by box","has internalised — the ear now runs it","ignores completely"],"answer":1,"explain":"You make it conscious now precisely so it can become automatic. The goal is an ear that checks without checking."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"One Russian word for the finest difference in meaning between near-synonyms — the whole level lives in it (hint: отт…):","accept":["оттенок","оттенки"],"placeholder":"отт…","explain":"<b>отте́нок</b> — a shade, a nuance. From day 1 to day 30, exactness lives in these оттенки."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Be your own editor","title":"<em>Саморедакту́ра</em> — read your own text with a stranger's ear","paras":["This is Эмма's craft turned on herself, and now on you: <b>саморедакту́ра</b>, self-editing. The rule of thumb — write hot, edit cold. Draft in one heat, then come back later as if the text were a stranger's, red pen ready.","On the cold pass you hunt specific game: the word that's «почти́ то» but not <b>то</b> (day 1); the collocation that's a quiet calque from English; the register that wobbled where you didn't mean it; the one figure too many; the idiom you forced in because you liked it. The near-native text is usually just one or two words from native — and this pass is how you find them.","Read it aloud. The ear catches what the eye forgives — a rhythm that drones, a seam that shows, a «спаси́бо» that should have curdled and didn't. Your eye approves the correct; your ear judges the whole."],"audio":"This is Emma's craft turned on herself, and now on you: 'samoredaktura', self-editing. The rule of thumb — write hot, edit cold. Draft in one heat, then come back later as if the text were a stranger's, red pen ready. On the cold pass you hunt specific game: the word that's 'pochti to' but not 'to', from day 1; the collocation that's a quiet calque from English; the register that wobbled where you didn't mean it; the one figure too many; the idiom you forced in because you liked it. The near-native text is usually just one or two words from native — and this pass is how you find them. Read it aloud. The ear catches what the eye forgives — a rhythm that drones, a seam that shows, a 'spasibo' that should have curdled and didn't. Your eye approves the correct; your ear judges the whole.","examples":[{"ru":"Write hot, edit cold — the second pass finds the near-miss word.","en":"Пиши́ на горячу́ю, пра́вь на холо́дную — второ́й прохо́д ло́вит «почти́».","say":"Пиши на горячую, правь на холодную — второй проход ловит почти."},{"ru":"The eye approves what's correct; the ear judges the whole.","en":"Глаз одобря́ет ве́рное — у́хо су́дит це́лое.","say":"Глаз одобряет верное — ухо судит целое."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он сде́лал большо́е влия́ние на мою́ жизнь».","right":"«Он оказа́л большо́е влия́ние на мою́ жизнь».","why":"A quiet calque that a hot draft slips past: «сде́лать влия́ние» copies English «made an influence». Self-editing catches it and restores the native collocation <b>оказа́ть влия́ние</b>. One word between «почти́» and «то»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Саморедакту́ра» works best when you —","opts":["edit in the very same heat as you wrote","write hot and edit cold, with a stranger's ear","never re-read your own text"],"answer":1,"explain":"Write hot, edit cold. On the cold pass you catch the word that's «почти́ то» — the calque, the wobble, the device too many."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it as one natural line — three tools folded into a single breath.","target":"Он, что называ́ется, ка́нул в Ле́ту — и никто́ не хвати́лся.","sub":"an idiom (ка́нул в Ле́ту), a colloquial wink (что называ́ется), dry understatement — as speech, not assembly","want":"он что называется канул в лету — и никто не хватился"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · No seams — and tomorrow, the whole road","title":"A text should read as <em>written</em>, not <em>assembled</em>","paras":["The final quality has no name on a checklist because it's the <i>absence</i> of something: no visible effort. The reader feels a person speaking, not a set of techniques being demonstrated. When the seams show — a brilliant idiom stranded next to a flat clause, an allusion that struts, ten sentences of the same length — the text says «assembled», however correct each piece.","The cure is subordination: every device serves the whole, and the whole is one voice. A show-off allusion, cut. A perfect but pointless figure, cut. What's left reads as though it could only have come out that way — which is exactly how a native's Russian reads.","Tomorrow is the capstone. Everything you've built — from «меня́ зову́т…» on day one of A1 to this — turned on your own story: the road from nothing to a voice, told <i>in</i> that voice. Not a report about yourself. You, finally, in Russian."],"audio":"The final quality has no name on a checklist because it's the absence of something: no visible effort. The reader feels a person speaking, not a set of techniques being demonstrated. When the seams show — a brilliant idiom stranded next to a flat clause, an allusion that struts, ten sentences of the same length — the text says 'assembled', however correct each piece. The cure is subordination: every device serves the whole, and the whole is one voice. A show-off allusion, cut. A perfect but pointless figure, cut. What's left reads as though it could only have come out that way — which is exactly how a native's Russian reads. Tomorrow is the capstone. Everything you've built — from 'menya zovut' on day one of A1 to this — turned on your own story: the road from nothing to a voice, told in that voice. Not a report about yourself. You, finally, in Russian.","examples":[{"ru":"A master hides mastery — the reader sees only the thought.","en":"Ма́стер пря́чет мастерство́ — чита́тель ви́дит одну́ лишь мысль.","say":"Мастер прячет мастерство — читатель видит одну лишь мысль."},{"ru":"When only the meaning shows and not the work — it's finished.","en":"Когда́ видна́ то́лько мысль, а не рабо́та, — зна́чит, гото́во.","say":"Когда видна только мысль, а не работа, — значит, готово."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ка́ждая фра́за — с идио́мой, аллю́зией и фигу́рой сра́зу, напока́з.","right":"Одна́ то́чная идио́ма на абза́ц — и та рабо́тает на смысл, а не на себя́.","why":"Piling every device into every sentence is the surest seam of all — it shouts «look what I can do». Mastery is restraint: one figure that serves the thought beats five that serve your ego. Hide the work; show the meaning."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A native-grade text should read as —","opts":["a clever display of every device you know","a checklist you can see being satisfied","written, not assembled — the seams invisible"],"answer":2,"explain":"Effort should disappear. The reader feels a person speaking; every device is subordinated to the one voice."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The cold second pass over your OWN text, hunting the near-miss word (hint: самореда́к…):","accept":["саморедактура"],"placeholder":"самореда́к…","explain":"<b>саморедакту́ра</b> — self-editing. Emma's craft turned on your own page: it finds the one or two words between «почти́» and «то»."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м — Ива́н and Ле́на's brother, the engineer — picks up Эмма's finished translation and can't believe it wasn't written in Russian to begin with. She tells him the secret is the seams. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Чита́ю и не ве́рю, что э́то перево́д. Как бу́дто сра́зу по-ру́сски напи́сано.","ru":"I'm reading it and I can't believe it's a translation. As if it were written straight in Russian."},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́чит, полу́чилось. Са́мое тру́дное — что́бы не́ было ви́дно швов.","ru":"Then it worked. The hardest thing is for the seams not to show."},{"who":"B","en":"Каки́х швов? Я ничего́ тако́го не заме́тил.","ru":"What seams? I didn't notice anything like that."},{"who":"A","en":"И не до́лжен был. Шов — э́то когда́ идио́ма блести́т, а ря́дом фра́за пуста́я. Ви́дно, что со́брано.","ru":"And you weren't meant to. A seam is when an idiom sparkles and a flat phrase sits right beside it. You can tell it was assembled."},{"who":"B","en":"А как ты их убира́ешь?","ru":"And how do you get rid of them?"},{"who":"A","en":"Пишу́ на горячу́ю, пра́влю на холо́дную. И вслух чита́ю — у́хо слы́шит то, что глаз прости́л.","ru":"I write hot and edit cold. And I read aloud — the ear hears what the eye forgave."},{"who":"B","en":"То есть ма́стер — не тот, кто мно́го уме́ет, а тот, кто пря́чет уме́ние?","ru":"So a master isn't the one who can do a lot, but the one who hides the skill?"},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и́менно. Когда́ ви́дно то́лько мысль, а не рабо́ту, — зна́чит, гото́во.","ru":"Exactly. When only the thought shows, not the labour — it's done."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the final synthesis — making the whole, and hiding the joins. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"интегра́ция","ru":"integration, bringing together","ex":"Интегра́ция — не су́мма уме́ний, а их сплав."},{"en":"це́лое","ru":"the whole","ex":"Це́лое ва́жнее ка́ждой отде́льной ча́сти."},{"en":"це́льность","ru":"coherence, wholeness","ex":"Гла́вное в те́ксте — его́ це́льность."},{"en":"сплав","ru":"a fusion, alloy","ex":"Мастерство́ — сплав всех уме́ний сра́зу."},{"en":"саморедакту́ра","ru":"self-editing","ex":"Саморедакту́ра — второ́й, холо́дный взгляд."},{"en":"неотличи́мый","ru":"indistinguishable","ex":"Текст, неотличи́мый от носи́тельского."},{"en":"шов","ru":"a seam, join","ex":"Ма́стер пря́чет шов ме́жду фра́зами."},{"en":"незаме́тно","ru":"seamlessly, imperceptibly","ex":"Все приёмы рабо́тают незаме́тно."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"shade, nuance","ex":"И на э́том у́ровне реша́ет отте́нок."},{"en":"подте́кст","ru":"subtext","ex":"Под ка́ждой фра́зой — свой подте́кст."},{"en":"вкус","ru":"taste, judgement","ex":"Всем в те́ксте управля́ет вкус."},{"en":"носи́тель","ru":"native speaker","ex":"Цель — писа́ть так, как пи́шет носи́тель."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each term of the final synthesis to what it means.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["интегра́ция","all skills at once, governed by one taste"],["саморедакту́ра","the cold second pass over your own text"],["шов","a seam — the visible join a master hides"],["це́льность","coherence — the whole reading as one, not a list"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The last trap of C2 is a text that is —","opts":["wrong in most of its parts","correct in every part, yet not quite native as a whole","too short to judge"],"answer":1},{"q":"Native fluency is best described as —","opts":["all the instruments at once, ruled by one taste","the biggest possible vocabulary","one final skill you haven't learned yet"],"answer":0},{"q":"The six-question checklist is meant to become —","opts":["a form you fill in forever","automatic — swallowed by the ear","something you throw away unlearned"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Write hot, edit cold» is the rule of —","opts":["саморедакту́ра — self-editing","translation","memorising vocabulary"],"answer":0},{"q":"A finished, native-grade text hides —","opts":["its meaning","the author entirely","the work — you see the thought, not the labour"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short piece on the last inch — and it tries to practise it: every device present, none of them showing off. Read it aloud and listen for the seams that aren't there.","title":"Це́лое, а не су́мма","sentences":["Мо́жно всё сде́лать пра́вильно — и всё же оста́ться в шаге́ от носи́теля.","То́чное сло́во, ве́рная коллока́ция, подте́кст, фигу́ра, аллю́зия — ка́ждое на ме́сте, а це́лое скрипи́т.","Потому́ что носи́тель не собира́ет фра́зу из дета́лей — он произно́сит её целико́м.","Мастерство́ здесь не в ча́стях, а в швах, кото́рых не ви́дно.","Напиши́ горячо́, а пото́м перечита́й хо́лодно — и найди́ то одно́ сло́во, что ещё то́лько «почти́».","Когда́ исче́знет после́дний шов, текст переста́нет быть перево́дом с себя́ — и ста́нет ре́чью."],"translation":"You can do everything correctly — and still stay a step short of a native. The exact word, the right collocation, the subtext, the figure, the allusion — each in its place, yet the whole creaks. Because a native doesn't assemble a phrase from details — they say it whole. Mastery here isn't in the parts, but in the seams you can't see. Write hot, then reread cold — and find that one word that's still only «almost». When the last seam vanishes, the text stops being a translation of yourself — and becomes speech."},"essay":{"intro":"Now, everything at once.","prompt":"Write ONE short paragraph (4–5 sentences) on any topic you like, and deliberately fold in at least THREE tools from this month: an exact word, an idiom in place, a modulation or ironic turn, an allusion, a rhetorical figure. Then add a one-line <b>саморедакту́ра</b> note: name the single word or phrase you'd still change to close the last inch. Say which tools you used.","hint":"Don't let the devices show off — subordinate each to the whole. Read the paragraph aloud once: if a seam sings out, that's your samoredaktura target 🙂","example":"Мы все, что называ́ется, «в отве́те за тех, кого́ приручи́ли», — вот то́лько ко́шка мое́й сестры́ я́вно счита́ет наоборо́т. Она́ не про́сит — она́ снисхо́дит; не мяу́кает — оглаша́ет ультима́тум. Коро́че, хозя́ева здесь не мы. (Са́мое сла́бое — «оглаша́ет ультима́тум»: чуть напока́з; заменю́ на «предъявля́ет счёт». Приёмы: аллю́зия к Экзюпери́, иро́ния, града́ция.)"}},"30":{"day":30,"week":"04","themeRu":"About me — mastery (capstone)","themeEn":"О себе́: мастерство́ и во́льность","intro":"Look back down the road. On day one of A1, «Приве́т, меня́ зову́т…» was a whole achievement — a handful of words held together by sheer will. Today you wrote a paragraph with an allusion, an idiom and an ironic aside folded in so smoothly no seam showed. That's the distance you've come. This last day isn't a new lesson; it's the finish line — a look back over the whole climb, and one final idea: <b>мастерство́ и во́льность</b>, mastery and licence. Because somewhere on this road Russian stopped being your <i>subject</i>. It became your <i>voice</i>.","introAudio":"Look back down the road. On day one of A1, 'Privet, menya zovut...' was a whole achievement — a handful of words held together by sheer will. Today you wrote a paragraph with an allusion, an idiom and an ironic aside folded in so smoothly no seam showed. That's the distance you've come. This last day isn't a new lesson; it's the finish line — a look back over the whole climb, and one final idea: 'masterstvo i volnost', mastery and licence. Because somewhere on this road Russian stopped being your subject. It became your voice.","goals":["Look back over the whole road — A1 «меня́ зову́т» to C2 virtuosity — and feel the distance","See the real shift: from talking <i>about</i> yourself (A1) to saying anything at all, <i>as your own</i>","Earn <b>во́льность</b> (licence) — the master breaks a rule on purpose, because now every choice is meant","Write the capstone: your own path from nothing to a voice, told in that voice"],"learned":["Looked back over the whole road, A1 to C2, and named the distance","Understood the shift from talking about yourself to saying anything as your own","Claimed во́льность — deliberate rule-breaking, earned by control","Wrote the capstone reflection in your own native-grade voice"],"review":{"intro":"One last look back before the finish — not a test, a tour. A word from each stage of the road you've walked, A1 to C2.","introAudio":"One last look back before the finish — not a test, a tour. A word from each stage of the road you've walked, A1 to C2.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is A1 — the very first steps.","q":"Where it all began: «меня́ зову́т …» means —","opts":["I would like…","my name is…","excuse me…"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>меня́ зову́т…</b> — «my name is…». One of your first five words of Russian — and still load-bearing under everything you say now."},{"type":"choice","q":"A stop at C1: in an official letter, “money” is —","opts":["де́нежные сре́дства","ба́бки","деньжа́та"],"answer":0,"explain":"Official → <b>де́нежные сре́дства</b>; ба́бки is slang, деньжа́та colloquial. The register scale you made second nature at C1."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the phrase.","q":"Build the phrase for the whole journey: “from zero to a master”:","want":"от нуля́ до ма́стера","answer":"от нуля́ до ма́стера"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Thirty days, and a whole road","title":"From «<em>меня́ зову́т</em>» to a voice — the distance you came","paras":["Let's walk the road once, without re-teaching a step of it. <b>A1</b>: your name, your numbers, «где вокза́л?» — pure survival, every word a small victory. <b>A2–B1</b>: the past and the future, opinions, real back-and-forth conversation. <b>B2</b>: participles, gerunds, the deep machinery of aspect — the engine of complex thought.","<b>C1</b>: the five functional styles and the whole register scale — the <i>right</i> word for the room. <b>C2</b>, this month: the <i>exact</i> word, connotation, subtext, irony, rhetoric, the entire vertical range, and finally integration — the level of taste.","And here's the quiet truth of it: no level ever replaced the one beneath it. Each stood on the last. That beginner's «меня́ зову́т» is still down there, load-bearing, at the base of every effortless thing you now do — <b>A1:</b> «Меня́ зову́т Э́мма». <b>C2:</b> «Позво́льте предста́виться — и, боюсь, э́то займёт весь ве́чер». Same act; a whole language between them."],"audio":"Let's walk the road once, without re-teaching a step of it. A1: your name, your numbers, 'gde vokzal', where's the station — pure survival, every word a small victory. A2 to B1: the past and the future, opinions, real back-and-forth conversation. B2: participles, gerunds, the deep machinery of aspect — the engine of complex thought. C1: the five functional styles and the whole register scale — the right word for the room. C2, this month: the exact word, connotation, subtext, irony, rhetoric, the entire vertical range, and finally integration — the level of taste. And here's the quiet truth of it: no level ever replaced the one beneath it. Each stood on the last. That beginner's 'menya zovut' is still down there, load-bearing, at the base of every effortless thing you now do. A1: 'Menya zovut Emma'. C2: 'Pozvolte predstavitsya — i, boyus, eto zaymyot ves vecher'. Same act; a whole language between them.","table":{"rows":[["A1–B1 <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"меня зовут, я хочу, где вокзал\">🔊</button>","survival & conversation — «меня́ зову́т», «я хочу́», «где вокза́л»"],["B2 <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"причастия, деепричастия, вид\">🔊</button>","the machinery — participles, gerunds, deep aspect"],["C1 <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стили и регистр\">🔊</button>","the right word for the room — styles & register"],["C2 <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"точное слово, оттенок, вкус\">🔊</button>","the exact word, the shade, the taste — this month"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"You didn't swap levels — you built each on the last.","en":"Ты не меня́л у́ровни — ты стро́ил ка́ждый на пре́жнем.","say":"Ты не менял уровни — ты строил каждый на прежнем."},{"ru":"The first five words still hold up everything you say.","en":"Пе́рвые пять слов до сих пор де́ржат всё остально́е.","say":"Первые пять слов до сих пор держат всё остальное."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Тепе́рь, когда́ я всё зна́ю, ста́рое мо́жно забы́ть».","right":"«Всё ста́рое — фунда́мент; на нём и стои́т всё но́вое».","why":"Nothing you learned got left behind — the levels stack. The A1 base carries the C2 taste. «Master» never means «done with the basics»; it means the basics run themselves while you attend to the shade."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap.","q":"Across A1→C2, each new level —","opts":["replaced the one before it","stood on the one before — the beginner's words still load-bearing","made the earlier levels useless"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nothing was thrown away. «Меня́ зову́т» from A1 still sits at the base of everything you now do without thinking."},{"type":"choice","q":"What did C2 add on top of C1's «the right word for the room»?","opts":["the exact word, connotation, subtext, irony — taste","the basics of grammar","the alphabet"],"answer":0,"explain":"C1 gave you register — the appropriate word. C2 gave you the <b>exact</b> word and all it carries: the level of taste."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The real shift","title":"A1 talked <em>about</em> you — now you say <em>anything</em>, as your own","paras":["Notice that this capstone shares its title with a lesson from the very beginning: «О себе́», about myself. But the two are opposite motions. The A1 «О себе́» was <i>facts about you</i>, laid out in the few words you had — name, age, city, «я люблю́ ко́фе». The subject was you; Russian was the fragile tool.","This «О себе́» flips it. The subject is no longer <i>you</i>, served up in careful Russian. The subject is <b>anything at all</b> — an idea, a joke, a grief, a quarrel about a comma — said in a Russian that could only be yours. Language stopped being the thing you describe and became the thing you describe <i>with</i>.","That's the whole journey in one line: Russian went from <b>предме́т</b> (a subject you study) to <b>сре́дство</b> (a medium you think in). When you dream in it, joke in it, lose your temper in it without reaching for English — the tool has quietly become a voice."],"audio":"Notice that this capstone shares its title with a lesson from the very beginning: 'O sebe', about myself. But the two are opposite motions. The A1 'O sebe' was facts about you, laid out in the few words you had — name, age, city, 'ya lyublyu kofe'. The subject was you; Russian was the fragile tool. This 'O sebe' flips it. The subject is no longer you, served up in careful Russian. The subject is anything at all — an idea, a joke, a grief, a quarrel about a comma — said in a Russian that could only be yours. Language stopped being the thing you describe and became the thing you describe with. That's the whole journey in one line: Russian went from 'predmet', a subject you study, to 'sredstvo', a medium you think in. When you dream in it, joke in it, lose your temper in it without reaching for English — the tool has quietly become a voice.","examples":[{"ru":"Once: you, in Russian. Now: anything, in your Russian.","en":"Ра́ньше: ты — по-ру́сски. Тепе́рь: что уго́дно — на твоём ру́сском.","say":"Раньше: ты — по-русски. Теперь: что угодно — на твоём русском."},{"ru":"The language went from subject to medium.","en":"Язы́к прошёл путь от предме́та к сре́дству.","say":"Язык прошёл путь от предмета к средству."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я хорошо́ зна́ю ру́сский язы́к» — как факт о себе́.","right":"«Я про́сто ду́маю и шучу́ на ру́сском» — и об э́том бо́льше не говорю́.","why":"«Knowing Russian» as a fact about yourself is the A1 frame — you as subject. The C2 frame doesn't announce the tool at all; it just uses it. When you stop mentioning that you speak Russian and simply speak, the shift is complete."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The A1 «О себе́» and this capstone «О себе́» differ because —","opts":["they are exactly the same task","A1 talked ABOUT you in few words; now you say ANYTHING, as your own","the capstone is the easier of the two"],"answer":1,"explain":"A1 served <i>you</i> up in the few words you had. Now the subject can be anything — and the Russian is unmistakably yours. Subject became medium."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The course's whole journey: Russian went from a <b>subject</b> you studied to a … you think with (hint: сре́д…):","accept":["средство"],"placeholder":"сре́д…","explain":"<b>сре́дство</b> — a means, a medium. Russian went from предме́т (subject) to сре́дство (medium): the one-sentence story of the whole road."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Mastery earns licence","title":"<em>Во́льность</em>: the right to break a rule — because now you mean it","paras":["One last idea, and it's a gift the earlier levels couldn't give you: <b>во́льность</b> — licence, the poet's freedom to break a rule on purpose. A beginner who drops a case ending has made an <i>error</i>. A master who drops the same ending has made a <i>choice</i> — a fragment for punch, a «wrong» word for irony, a lurch of register for effect. <b>Оши́бка (нача́ло):</b> a rule broken by accident. <b>Приём (ма́стер):</b> the very same rule broken on purpose.","The whole difference is intention — and intention is exactly what a month of taste has built in you. You've earned the licence precisely because you can no longer break a rule by accident without <i>hearing</i> it. Freedom, it turns out, isn't knowing every rule; it's being able to break one and mean it.","And so the send-off. Эмма arrived unable to order a coffee without breaking a sweat; she edits novels now, and argues about a comma for the joy of it. Your road is your own, but it runs the same way — from surviving in Russian to living in it. This isn't the end of the language. It's the end of the course. The voice is yours now. Спаси́бо за доро́гу — и говори́."],"audio":"One last idea, and it's a gift the earlier levels couldn't give you: 'volnost' — licence, the poet's freedom to break a rule on purpose. A beginner who drops a case ending has made an error. A master who drops the same ending has made a choice — a fragment for punch, a 'wrong' word for irony, a lurch of register for effect. Error, at the beginning: a rule broken by accident. Device, at mastery: the very same rule broken on purpose. The whole difference is intention — and intention is exactly what a month of taste has built in you. You've earned the licence precisely because you can no longer break a rule by accident without hearing it. Freedom, it turns out, isn't knowing every rule; it's being able to break one and mean it. And so the send-off. Emma arrived unable to order a coffee without breaking a sweat; she edits novels now, and argues about a comma for the joy of it. Your road is your own, but it runs the same way — from surviving in Russian to living in it. This isn't the end of the language. It's the end of the course. The voice is yours now. 'Spasibo za dorogu' — and speak.","examples":[{"ru":"A beginner breaks a rule and errs; a master breaks it and means it.","en":"Новичо́к нару́шит пра́вило — и оши́бка; ма́стер нару́шит — и приём.","say":"Новичок нарушит правило — и ошибка; мастер нарушит — и приём."},{"ru":"Freedom isn't knowing every rule — it's breaking one on purpose.","en":"Свобо́да — не знать все пра́вила, а нару́шить одно́ наро́чно.","say":"Свобода — не знать все правила, а нарушить одно нарочно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я бо́льше не де́лаю оши́бок» — как коне́ц пути́.","right":"«Я и́ногда наруша́ю пра́вила — но тепе́рь наро́чно».","why":"Mastery isn't the absence of rule-breaking — it's rule-breaking with intent. «Never making mistakes» is a small ambition; hearing every departure and choosing it is во́льность, the real prize. The road doesn't end at «correct»; it opens into «yours»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Во́льность» (a master's licence) is —","opts":["the beginner's freedom to make mistakes","breaking a rule on purpose — a choice, because now you'd hear the accident","never breaking any rule at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"A beginner breaks a rule and errs; a master breaks it and means it. Intention — built by a month of taste — is the whole difference."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read your send-off line aloud — slowly, like you mean it. This one's yours.","target":"Доро́га зако́нчилась, а го́лос оста́лся — и он тепе́рь мой.","sub":"the course ends here; the voice doesn't — let the last two words land","want":"дорога закончилась а голос остался — и он теперь мой"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"A year on from her first fumbled coffee order, Эмма sits with Ива́н — the friend who heard her very first Russian sentences. He's noticed something has changed for good. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"По́мнишь свой пе́рвый разгово́р со мной? Ты иска́ла ка́ждое сло́во по мину́те.","ru":"Remember your first conversation with me? You searched a whole minute for every word."},{"who":"A","en":"По́мню. А тепе́рь не ищу́ — про́сто говорю́. Са́мое стра́нное: почти́ не ду́маю по-англи́йски.","ru":"I remember. And now I don't search — I just speak. The strangest thing: I barely think in English anymore."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, вы́училась. Мо́жешь поста́вить га́лочку.","ru":"So you've learned it. You can tick the box."},{"who":"A","en":"Не «вы́училась». Э́то не то́чка, а го́лос, кото́рый тепе́рь мой. Учи́ться бу́ду всегда́.","ru":"Not «learned it». It's not a full stop — it's a voice that's now mine. I'll always be learning."},{"who":"B","en":"А по́мнишь, как ты боя́лась, что никогда́ не заговори́шь свобо́дно?","ru":"And remember how afraid you were that you'd never speak fluently?"},{"who":"A","en":"Боя́лась. А ока́зывается, свобо́да — э́то не когда́ зна́ешь все пра́вила, а когда́ мо́жешь нару́шить их наро́чно.","ru":"I was. And it turns out freedom isn't knowing all the rules — it's being able to break them on purpose."},{"who":"B","en":"Кра́сиво ска́зано. Пря́мо как в кни́ге.","ru":"Beautifully put. Just like in a book."},{"who":"A","en":"Не как в кни́ге, Ива́н. Как я. В э́том и вся суть.","ru":"Not like in a book, Ivan. Like me. That's the whole point."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the finish line — mastery, licence, and the road behind you. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"мастерство́","ru":"mastery, craft","ex":"Мастерство́ — э́то во́ля, ста́вшая привы́чкой."},{"en":"во́льность","ru":"licence, freedom (to break a rule)","ex":"Во́льность — награ́да за контро́ль."},{"en":"путь","ru":"path, road, journey","ex":"Твой путь — от нуля́ до го́лоса."},{"en":"доро́га","ru":"road, the way","ex":"До́лгая была́ доро́га — и стои́ла того́."},{"en":"ито́г","ru":"the sum, the upshot","ex":"Ито́г ме́сяца: ру́сский стал го́лосом."},{"en":"рост","ru":"growth","ex":"Ка́ждый у́ровень был но́вым ро́стом."},{"en":"свобо́да","ru":"freedom","ex":"Свобо́да — не хао́с, а осо́знанный вы́бор."},{"en":"го́лос","ru":"voice","ex":"Тепе́рь у тебя́ есть свой го́лос."},{"en":"предме́т","ru":"subject (what is studied)","ex":"Ру́сский был предме́том — а стал сре́дством."},{"en":"сре́дство","ru":"means, medium","ex":"Язы́к — сре́дство, а не цель."},{"en":"го́рдость","ru":"pride","ex":"Твоя́ го́рдость — заслу́женная."},{"en":"благода́рность","ru":"gratitude","ex":"Оста́лась и благода́рность — к самому́ пути́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each word of the finish line to what it means.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each term on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["мастерство́","the whole craft, become second nature"],["во́льность","licence — breaking a rule on purpose, and meaning it"],["предме́т → сре́дство","the shift: Russian from subject to medium"],["ито́г","the sum of the road — a voice of your own"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — the whole road, A1 to here. A last tour; let it feel like a victory lap.","items":[{"q":"The C2 leap from C1 was from the «appropriate» word to —","opts":["the longest word","the exact word — and what it carries","the rarest word"],"answer":1},{"q":"The hidden charge a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning is its —","opts":["коннота́ция (connotation)","synonym","spelling"],"answer":0},{"q":"A faithful translation keeps, above all —","opts":["the exact word count","the tone, register and irony — not just the words","the source word order"],"answer":1},{"q":"An essay «with a voice» holds one base register and —","opts":["never changes it at all","modulates it on purpose","collapses into slang"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Знать сленг ≠ им сы́пать» tells you to —","opts":["sprinkle slang into every sentence","never learn slang at all","understand it, but place it on purpose and in doses"],"answer":2},{"q":"The last trap of integration is a text that is —","opts":["correct in every part, yet not quite native as a whole","full of grammar errors","far too short to read"],"answer":0},{"q":"The one-sentence story of the whole course: Russian went from —","opts":["hard to easy","subject (предме́т) to medium (сре́дство)","spoken to written"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Во́льность» — a master's licence — is —","opts":["breaking a rule by accident","breaking a rule on purpose, earned by control","following every rule forever"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"The last reading of the course — a look back over the whole climb, in the free voice you built to read it with. Read it aloud, slowly. You've earned it.","title":"От нуля́ до го́лоса","sentences":["Ты на́чал с пяти́ слов: «приве́т», «спаси́бо», «меня́ зову́т» — и ка́ждое дава́лось с бо́ю.","Пото́м пришли́ времена́, паде́жи, ви́ды — маши́на языка́, кото́рую ты собра́л по винти́ку.","На C1 ты научи́лся выбира́ть сло́во по ко́мнате, а на C2 — по то́нчайшему отте́нку.","И вот стра́нность: чем лу́чше ты говори́шь, тем ме́ньше ду́маешь о ру́сском — и тем бо́льше про́сто говори́шь.","Ру́сский переста́л быть предме́том изуче́ния и стал тем, чем ты ду́маешь и шу́тишь.","Доро́га зако́нчилась. Го́лос оста́лся. Он тепе́рь твой."],"translation":"You began with five words: «hello», «thank you», «my name is» — and each one came at a cost. Then came the tenses, the cases, the aspects — the machine of the language, which you assembled screw by screw. At C1 you learned to choose a word for the room; at C2, for the finest shade. And here's the strange part: the better you speak, the less you think about Russian — and the more you simply speak. Russian stopped being a subject of study and became the thing you think and joke in. The road has ended. The voice remains. It's yours now."},"essay":{"intro":"The last one. Not «about me» facts — you, thinking freely, in Russian.","prompt":"Write your own short reflection (5–6 sentences) on the road from your very first Russian word to now — in your OWN voice, using the tools you've earned: an exact word, maybe an idiom or an ironic aside, a strong opening and a strong close. This isn't the A1 «facts about me»; it's you, thinking freely, in Russian. End on what Russian has become for you.","hint":"Don't list your levels like a CV — that's the faceless version. Open with an image or a paradox, close with a line that resonates. Let your voice do the talking — it's a voice now 🙂","example":"Год наза́д я не могла́ заказа́ть ко́фе, не вспоте́в. Сего́дня я пра́влю чужи́е рома́ны и спо́рю о запято́й — и, что называ́ется, вошла́ во вкус. Ру́сский переста́л быть ле́стницей, по кото́рой я кара́бкалась; тепе́рь э́то ко́мната, в кото́рой я живу́. Я всё ещё учу́сь — но учу́сь уже́ не языку́, а себе́ на нём. Доро́га была́ дли́нной. Го́лос стал мои́м."}}};
